nLhpNgb+E1HAerUf+/UqdM+DyucRFCCEK2mlpc3INvjT+lIutwx4116KD7+U4x6WF\nH6vPNOw/KP4M8VeGTslV9xzU2KV9Bnpv1d8Q34FOIWWxtuEXeZVFBs5fzNxGiWNo\nRI2T9GRwoD2dKAXDOXC4Ynsg/eTb6QihuJ49CcdP+yz4k3ZB3lLg4VfSnQO8d57+\nnile98FRYB/e2guyLXW3Q0iT5/Z5xoRdgFlglPx4mI88k1HtQJAH32RjJMtOcQWh\n15QaiDLxInQirqWm2BJpTGCjAu4r7NRjkgtevi92a6O2JryPA9gK8kxkRr05YuWW\n6zRjESjMlfGt7+/cgFhI6Uu46mWs6fyAtbXIRfmswZ/ZuepiiI7E8UuDEq3mi4TW\nnsLrgxifarsbJGAzcMzs9zLzXNl5fe+epP7JI8Mk7hWSsT2RTyaGvWZzJBPqpK5j\nwa19hAM8EHiGG3njxPPyBJUgriOCxLM6AGK/5jYk4Ve6xx6QddVfP5VhK8E7zeWz\naGHQRiapIVJpLesux+t3zqY6tQMzT3bR51xUAV3LePTJDL/PEo4XLSNolOer/qmy\nKwbQBM0=\n-----END CERTIFICATE-----\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "sigmavirus24", "timestamp": "2025-05-07T10:56:39Z", "body": "How large is this chain that you feel the need to zip it? The files should be something you can copy and include in plain text in a comment ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "sigmavirus24", "timestamp": "2025-05-19T02:03:23Z", "body": "You can use https://certificatedecoder.dev/ to inspect this. There is a Subject Key Identifier for the leaf (first certificate in your block). Same goes for the issuer. I'm not sure what problem you're encountering. Only thing I can imagine is that something is having difficulty parsing the non-ASCII characters in the Subject DN, but that's not something requests can fix.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "sigmavirus24", "timestamp": "2025-05-19T10:33:30Z", "body": "Certainly not certifi as the exception comes from the standard library and OpenSSL. Certifi just provides the trust bundle and this works fine everywhere else", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "klausenbusk", "timestamp": "2025-05-28T22:27:31Z", "body": "We are getting [the same error message in Arch Linux](https://gitlab.archlinux.org/archlinux/infrastructure/-/issues/688) for a `*.tw` domain, so I did a bit of digging.\n\nThe issue boils down to `VERIFY_X509_STRICT` and `VERIFY_X509_PARTIAL_CHAIN` being set (https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/107361, https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/3571). When running in strict mode the optional \"Subject Key Identifier\" (at least from a [CA baseline perspective](https://cabforum.org/working-groups/server/baseline-requirements/requirements/#712114-subject-key-identifier)) must be set (https://github.com/openssl/openssl/issues/13283) and that is not the case for the \"TWCA Global Root CA\" used by `free.nchc.org.tw` and likely also `od.moi.gov.tw` (I cannot reach that domain).\n\nSo I assume this is just working as designed? For testing purposes the website of TWCA (`https://www.twca.com.tw/`) can be used.\n\n**Edit**: Reported here: https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/107361#issuecomment-2917808686.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "psf/requests/issue/6945#1", "question": "Why does Requests raise an SSL certificate verification error when fetching `https://od.moi.gov.tw/api/v1/`, even though latest Chrome and Firefox do not report a certificate issue for the same URL?\n\nExpected result: no error should be raised.\n\nActual error:\n\n`requests.exceptions.SSLError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='od.moi.gov.tw', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: /api/v1/rest/datastore/A01010000C-002150-013?limit=1 (Caused by SSLError(SSLCertVerificationError(1, '[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Missing Subject Key Identifier (_ssl.c:1028)')))`\n\nReproduction:\n\n```python\nimport requests\nimport certifi\nurl = 'https://od.moi.gov.tw/api/v1/rest/datastore/A01010000C-002150-013?limit=1'\nrequests.get(url, verify=certifi.where())\n```\n\nSystem information from `python -m requests.help`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"chardet\": {\n    \"version\": \"5.2.0\"\n  },\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.4.2\"\n  },\n  \"cryptography\": {\n    \"version\": \"\"\n  },\n  \"idna\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.10\"\n  },\n  \"implementation\": {\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\n    \"version\": \"3.13.3\"\n  },\n  \"platform\": {\n    \"release\": \"10\",\n    \"system\": \"Windows\"\n  },\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\n    \"openssl_version\": \"\",\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"requests\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.32.3\"\n  },\n  \"system_ssl\": {\n    \"version\": \"30000100\"\n  },\n  \"urllib3\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.4.0\"\n  },\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": false,\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": false\n}\n```\n\nThe certificate chain retrieved from Firefox was provided and included a chain ending in `TWCA Global Root CA`.", "answer": "The error is not specific to Requests or certifi; it comes from the Python standard library/OpenSSL verification layer. The issue appears to be that `VERIFY_X509_STRICT` and `VERIFY_X509_PARTIAL_CHAIN` are set, and in strict mode OpenSSL requires the optional Subject Key Identifier to be present; the `TWCA Global Root CA` used by related `*.tw` domains, and likely `od.moi.gov.tw`, does not have it. The maintainer/community therefore treated this as likely working as designed, with related discussion/reporting in CPython, urllib3, OpenSSL, and Arch Linux infrastructure issues.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://gitlab.archlinux.org/archlinux/infrastructure/-/issues/688", "https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/107361", "https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/3571", "https://cabforum.org/working-groups/server/baseline-requirements/requirements/#712114-subject-key-identifier", "https://github.com/openssl/openssl/issues/13283", "https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/107361#issuecomment-2917808686"], "answer_grounded_in": "c6", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://cabforum.org/working-groups/server/baseline-requirements/requirements/#712114-subject-key-identifier", "https://github.com/openssl/openssl/issues/13283", "https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/107361", "https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/107361#issuecomment-2917808686", "https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/3571", "https://gitlab.archlinux.org/archlinux/infrastructure/-/issues/688", "https://www.twca.com.tw/`"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:08:37.677928Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "psf/requests/issue/6928", "repo": "psf/requests", "number": 6928, "title": "Python 3.13: an unexpected `Authorization` header because of NETRC file", "url": "https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/6928", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-04-04T22:09:06Z", "closed_at": "2026-02-13T02:07:27Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "yermulnik", "security_topic": "unexpected Authorization header caused by netrc handling in Python 3.13", "qa_summary": "The unexpected Authorization header is caused by Python 3.11+ treating empty netrc entries as valid, leading Requests to send a ':' header; this will be fixed by PR\u202f#7205 in the Requests repository.", "question_comment_id": "c4", "answer_comment_id": "c9", "question_author": "yermulnik", "answer_author": "nateprewitt", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "pr_data", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": ["#7205"], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": ["https://github.com/python/cpython/pull/26330", "https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/7205"]}, "human_note": "Accepted because the issue is a credential-handling regression and the answer correctly traces the root cause and points to an upstream fix with a concrete patch.", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "yermulnik", "timestamp": "2025-04-04T22:09:06Z", "body": "When sending POST request using Python 3.13 with `requests` v2.32.3 there appear an unexpected `Authorization` header.\nThis is not the case with Python 3.10 with the same version of `requests` lib.\n\n## Expected Result\n\nPOST request does not get updated with an unexpected `Authorization` header.\n\n## Actual Result\n\nPython 3.13\n```shell\n> ~/tmp/zzz.py 2>&1 | egrep -m 1 \"^send: \"\nsend: b'POST /oauth2/v1/device/authorize HTTP/1.1\\r\\nHost: okta.oktapreview.com\\r\\nUser-Agent: gimme-aws-creds 2.8.2;linux;3.13.2\\r\\nAccept-Encoding: gzip, deflate\\r\\nAccept: application/json\\r\\nConnection: keep-alive\\r\\nContent-Length: 52\\r\\nContent-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\\r\\nAuthorization: Basic Og==\\r\\n\\r\\n'\n```\nPython 3.10\n```shell\n> ~/tmp/zzz.py 2>&1 | egrep -m 1 \"^send: \"\nsend: b'POST /oauth2/v1/device/authorize HTTP/1.1\\r\\nHost: okta.oktapreview.com\\r\\nUser-Agent: gimme-aws-creds 2.8.2;linux;3.13.2\\r\\nAccept-Encoding: gzip, deflate, br\\r\\nAccept: application/json\\r\\nConnection: keep-alive\\r\\nContent-Length: 52\\r\\nContent-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\\r\\n\\r\\n'\n```\n\n## Reproduction Steps\n\n```python\nimport requests\nimport logging\n\ntry:\n    import http.client as http_client\nexcept ImportError:\n    # Python 2\n    import httplib as http_client\nhttp_client.HTTPConnection.debuglevel = 1\n\nlogging.basicConfig()\nlogging.getLogger().setLevel(logging.DEBUG)\nrequests_log = logging.getLogger(\"requests.packages.urllib3\")\nrequests_log.setLevel(logging.DEBUG)\nrequests_log.propagate = True\n\ndata = {\n    \"client_id\": \"<client_id>\",\n    \"scope\": \"openid okta.apps.sso\"\n}\nurl = \"https://okta.oktapreview.com/oauth2/v1/device/authorize\"\nheaders = {'User-Agent': 'gimme-aws-creds 2.8.2;linux;3.13.2', 'Accept': 'application/json'}\n\nresponse = requests.post(url, data=data, headers=headers, verify=True)\n\nprint(response.text)\n```\n\n## System Information\n\n    $ python -m requests.help\n\n```json\n{\n  \"chardet\": {\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.4.1\"\n  },\n  \"cryptography\": {\n    \"version\": \"\"\n  },\n  \"idna\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.10\"\n  },\n  \"implementation\": {\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\n    \"version\": \"3.13.2\"\n  },\n  \"platform\": {\n    \"release\": \"6.8.0-52-generic\",\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\n  },\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\n    \"openssl_version\": \"\",\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"requests\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.32.3\"\n  },\n  \"system_ssl\": {\n    \"version\": \"30400010\"\n  },\n  \"urllib3\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.3.0\"\n  },\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": false\n}\n```\n\nRef: https://github.com/Nike-Inc/gimme-aws-creds/issues/485", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "sigmavirus24", "timestamp": "2025-04-05T12:54:08Z", "body": "This is strange as `Og==` is base64 encoded from `:`. \n\nMy best guess is that adding `trust_env=False` would fix this on Python 3.13. Why it fixes it though, is likely do to something in the `netrc` standard library.\n\nIf you check `~/.netrc` do you have any configuration for the particular host you care about?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "yermulnik", "timestamp": "2025-04-05T23:24:39Z", "body": "> My best guess is that adding `trust_env=False` would fix this on Python 3.13. Why it fixes it though, is likely do to something in the `netrc` standard library.\n> \n> If you check `~/.netrc` do you have any configuration for the particular host you care about?\n\nCan confirm the `~/.netrc` is the issue and the only matching host entry for the host is the `default` entry:\n```conf\ndefault\n        macdef init\n                set prompt \"[%n@%M][%/]> \"\n```\nSeems to have nothing to do with auth though, but removing this section does fix the issue (similarly setting `trust_env=False` for `requests` session fixes the issue too).\n\nSince my issue is with another tool that utilizes `requests`, do you by any chance have a hint of whether `trust_env` value can be modified (set `False`) via an external approach like an Env var?\n\n> Are the versions of requests the same between the two environments as well?\n\nYes.\n```shell\n> for i in 3.10 3.13; do echo -n \"$i: \"; python$i -m requests.help | jq -r '.requests.version'; done\n3.10: 2.32.3\n3.13: 2.32.3\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "sigmavirus24", "timestamp": "2025-04-06T01:04:20Z", "body": "It would be ironic to have an environment variable telling the library to not trust the environment. ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "yermulnik", "timestamp": "2025-04-06T11:58:35Z", "body": "Haha, agree =)) I think I'm just too focused on `.netrc` part of this.\n\nLooks like my best (and sort of the only) option at the moment is to try and PR to the tool, that I rely upon, a flag to switch `trust_env` for `requests` (and accept the fact that this will also break use cases where someone relies upon proxy env vars) \ud83e\udd14\n\nAm I right assuming that the `Authorization` header in my conditions (where there are no auth parameters set for the host via `.netrc`) is added by Python 3.13 rather than by `requests` lib or is it something that `requests` can potentially rectify or remediate?\n\nThanks.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "sigmavirus24", "timestamp": "2025-04-06T14:48:48Z", "body": "Can you reproduce it just with the standard library? If not, can you write a failing test for https://github.com/psf/requests/blob/main/src%2Frequests%2Futils.py#L207", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "yermulnik", "timestamp": "2025-04-06T15:53:31Z", "body": "> Can you reproduce it just with the standard library?\n\nSorry, I can't seem to figure out what you mean \ud83d\ude15 Isn't the \u00abReproduction Steps\u00bb from the issue description what you're asking about? \ud83e\udd14 \n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3.13\nimport requests\n\ndata = {\n    \"client_id\": \"0[\u2026]7\",\n    \"scope\": \"openid okta.apps.sso\"\n}\nurl = \"https://[\u2026].oktapreview.com/oauth2/v1/device/authorize\"\nheaders = {'Accept': 'application/json'}\n\nresponse = requests.post(url, data=data, headers=headers, verify=True)\n\nprint(response.text)\n```\n```shell\n> ~/tmp/zzz.py\n{\"errorCode\":\"invalid_client\",\"errorSummary\":\"Invalid value for 'client_id' parameter.\",\"errorLink\":\"invalid_client\",\"errorId\":\"oaeohowcwaASwevPhC5oeoeHA\",\"errorCauses\":[]}\n\n> NETRC=\"\" ~/tmp/zzz.py\n{\"device_code\":\"c[\u2026]f\",\"user_code\":\"L[\u2026]B\",\"verification_uri\":\"https://[\u2026].oktapreview.com/activate\",\"verification_uri_complete\":\"https://[\u2026].oktapreview.com/activate?user_code=L[\u2026]B\",\"expires_in\":600,\"interval\":5}\n```\n\n> If not, can you write a failing test for [`main`/src%2Frequests%2Futils.py#L207](https://github.com/psf/requests/blob/main/src%2Frequests%2Futils.py?rgh-link-date=2025-04-06T14%3A48%3A48.000Z#L207)\n\nI could try... though I'm not a Python dev and it can take me a challenge. If you could provide some guidance probably (or point out to an existing test example): I probably need to put a `.netrc` file somewhere under `/tests/` dir (create new dir like `files/` and add file to it?); the known to me endpoint that returns failure in this case is my employer's Okta tenant (incl Okta App Client ID), which I don't think is a good endpoint to run test against from within `pfs/requests` repo; so on \ud83e\udd14 ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "yermulnik", "timestamp": "2025-04-08T20:40:40Z", "body": "@sigmavirus24 Any hints?\nI do understand that my case is pretty much rare and uncommon, though it's probably better to rectify this glitch sooner or later and I'll be happy to assist testing the fix. Thank you.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c8", "author": "fabrice-lebas", "timestamp": "2025-09-10T10:31:07Z", "body": "How can we raise interest in this issue? This blocks access to any service accepting bearer token, or using SLT instead of the content of netrc. \n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c9", "author": "nateprewitt", "timestamp": "2026-02-13T02:03:15Z", "body": "It turns out this is actually a [change](https://github.com/python/cpython/pull/26330) in Python 3.11. The netrc update is in the docs but missed the release notes. For 3.11+, it treats a netrc entry without username, account or password as valid. That means a file can have a valid `default` profile that we shouldn't be using.\n\nWhat's happening in Requests is we used to get a `NetrcParseError` that defaulted the value to `None` skipping this. Now, that produces `('', '', '')` for our `_netrc`. That tuple is in turn \"truthy\" and used to create an `Authorization` header with two empty values (`:`).\n\nI've made a [PR](https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/7205) that skips using the netrc entries entirely if we can't find any info. I'd argue what we're doing now is wrong. If someone _really_ wants to include `:` as their Authorization header value, they can do it through `auth=('', '')`. Grabbing random entries that aren't intended for use should skipped.\n\nOnce that's merged, this should be fixed in the next release. Thanks for the report @yermulnik!", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "psf/requests/issue/6928#1", "question": "In Python 3.13 with `requests` 2.32.3, a POST request unexpectedly gets an `Authorization: Basic Og==` header, while Python 3.10 with the same `requests` version does not. The request is:\n\n```python\nimport requests\nimport logging\n\ntry:\n    import http.client as http_client\nexcept ImportError:\n    # Python 2\n    import httplib as http_client\nhttp_client.HTTPConnection.debuglevel = 1\n\nlogging.basicConfig()\nlogging.getLogger().setLevel(logging.DEBUG)\nrequests_log = logging.getLogger(\"requests.packages.urllib3\")\nrequests_log.setLevel(logging.DEBUG)\nrequests_log.propagate = True\n\ndata = {\n    \"client_id\": \"<client_id>\",\n    \"scope\": \"openid okta.apps.sso\"\n}\nurl = \"https://okta.oktapreview.com/oauth2/v1/device/authorize\"\nheaders = {'User-Agent': 'gimme-aws-creds 2.8.2;linux;3.13.2', 'Accept': 'application/json'}\n\nresponse = requests.post(url, data=data, headers=headers, verify=True)\n\nprint(response.text)\n```\n\nPython 3.13 sends:\n\n```shell\nsend: b'POST /oauth2/v1/device/authorize HTTP/1.1\\r\\nHost: okta.oktapreview.com\\r\\nUser-Agent: gimme-aws-creds 2.8.2;linux;3.13.2\\r\\nAccept-Encoding: gzip, deflate\\r\\nAccept: application/json\\r\\nConnection: keep-alive\\r\\nContent-Length: 52\\r\\nContent-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\\r\\nAuthorization: Basic Og==\\r\\n\\r\\n'\n```\n\nPython 3.10 sends no Authorization header:\n\n```shell\nsend: b'POST /oauth2/v1/device/authorize HTTP/1.1\\r\\nHost: okta.oktapreview.com\\r\\nUser-Agent: gimme-aws-creds 2.8.2;linux;3.13.2\\r\\nAccept-Encoding: gzip, deflate, br\\r\\nAccept: application/json\\r\\nConnection: keep-alive\\r\\nContent-Length: 52\\r\\nContent-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\\r\\n\\r\\n'\n```\n\nSystem information for the failing environment is:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"chardet\": {\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.4.1\"\n  },\n  \"cryptography\": {\n    \"version\": \"\"\n  },\n  \"idna\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.10\"\n  },\n  \"implementation\": {\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\n    \"version\": \"3.13.2\"\n  },\n  \"platform\": {\n    \"release\": \"6.8.0-52-generic\",\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\n  },\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\n    \"openssl_version\": \"\",\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"requests\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.32.3\"\n  },\n  \"system_ssl\": {\n    \"version\": \"30400010\"\n  },\n  \"urllib3\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.3.0\"\n  },\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": false\n}\n```\n\nThe only matching `~/.netrc` entry for the host is a `default` entry with no auth parameters:\n\n```conf\ndefault\n        macdef init\n                set prompt \"[%n@%M][%/]> \"\n```\n\nRemoving that section fixes the issue, and setting `trust_env=False` on the requests session also fixes it. In a reduced Python 3.13 script using `requests.post`, running normally returns an Okta `invalid_client` error, while running with `NETRC=\"\"` returns the expected device authorization response.\n\nGiven that there are no auth parameters set for the host via `.netrc`, is the unexpected `Authorization` header added because of Python 3.13 rather than `requests`, or is it something `requests` can potentially rectify/remediate?", "answer": "This comes from a Python netrc behavior change introduced in Python 3.11: for 3.11+, a netrc entry without username, account, or password is treated as valid. Requests previously got a `NetrcParseError` and skipped it, but now receives `('', '', '')`, which is truthy and gets turned into an Authorization header for `:` (`Basic Og==`). Requests considered its current behavior wrong, and PR #7205 was opened to skip netrc entries entirely when no auth info is found; once merged, it should be fixed in the next release.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/python/cpython/pull/26330", "https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/7205"], "answer_grounded_in": "c9", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/7205", "https://github.com/python/cpython/pull/26330"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:11:27.088429Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "psf/requests/issue/6872", "repo": "psf/requests", "number": 6872, "title": "Requests v2.32.0-v2.32.3 caused a segmentation fault with multiple simultaneous requests with a certificate.", "url": "https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/6872", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-01-16T18:54:19Z", "closed_at": "2025-01-28T18:47:37Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "alloni", "security_topic": "segmentation fault due to shared SSLContext in multithreaded requests", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know if sharing an SSLContext across threads is safe, and the answer cites the Python SSL documentation (https://docs.python.org/3/library/ssl.html#ssl.SSLContext) confirming it is not thread\u2011safe after reconfiguration.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "alloni", "answer_author": "saksham-sak", "answerer_role": "contributor", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Accepted because the issue is a real multithreaded SSLContext safety problem and the answer provides a correct, practical isolation-based fix supported by SSL threading semantics.", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "alloni", "timestamp": "2025-01-16T18:54:19Z", "body": "We are having intermittent segmentation faults on requests (using requests v2.32.0-v2.32.3, urllib3 v1.26.19).  On investigation with faulthandler, they occur when a certificate is passed in, and two separate threads are trying to access the same ssl_context at the same time - one updating it with the given cert, one reading:\n\n```\nCurrent thread 0x00007f46c114d700 (most recent call first):\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 418 in ssl_wrap_socket\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/connection.py\", line 419 in connect\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 1060 in _validate_conn\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 404 in _make_request\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 715 in urlopen\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/requests/adapters.py\", line 667 in send\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 703 in send\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 589 in request\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 602 in get\n  ...\n\nThread 0x00007f469e7fc700 (most recent call first):\n  File \"/usr/ssl.py\", line 1382 in do_handshake\n  File \"/usr/ssl.py\", line 1104 in _create\n  File \"/usr/ssl.py\", line 517 in wrap_socket\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 493 in _ssl_wrap_socket_impl\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 449 in ssl_wrap_socket\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/connection.py\", line 419 in connect\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 1060 in _validate_conn\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 404 in _make_request\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 715 in urlopen\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/requests/adapters.py\", line 667 in send\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 703 in send\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 589 in request\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 602 in get\n  ...\n```\n\nThis seems to happen because requests v2.32 switched from having a request without an SSL context create a new one to using the same preloaded SSL context across multiple requests.  (This is https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/6745 with more information as to what exactly is going on - opening a new issue as the original issue creator has left the company.) \n\n## Expected Result\n\nNo segmentation faults.\n\n## Actual Result\n\nGot intermittent segmentation faults.\n\n## Reproduction Steps\n\nAs per the original issue - and note that it _only_ crashes if a generated certificate is passed in -  `openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 -keyout client.key -out client.crt -days 365 -nodes`:\n\n```\nimport concurrent.futures\nimport random\nimport uuid\nfrom threading import Thread\nfrom time import time\n\nimport requests\n\n\ndef do_request():\n    start = time()\n    random_id = uuid.uuid4()\n    delay = random.randint(1, 5)\n    print(\"start {} delay {} seconds\".format(random_id, delay))\n    endpoints = []\n    endpoints.append('https://httpbin.org/delay/' + str(delay))\n    delay = str(random.randint(1, 5)) + 's'\n    endpoints.append('https://run.mocky.io/v3/0432e9f0-674f-45bd-9c18-628b861c2258?mocky-delay=' + str(delay))\n    random.shuffle(endpoints)\n    response = None\n    for endpoint in endpoints:\n        try:\n            print(\"start {} delay {} seconds\".format(random_id, endpoint))\n            if 'run' in endpoint:\n                cert = './client.crt', './client.key'\n                response = requests.get(endpoint, timeout=random.randint(1, 5), cert=cert)\n            else:\n                response = requests.get(endpoint, timeout=random.randint(1, 5))\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(e)\n    end = time()\n\n\n    print(\"finished {} in {} seconds\".format(random_id, end - start))\n    return response\n\n\ndef measure():\n    cnt = 20\n    with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=10) as executor:\n        futures = []\n        for server in range(1, cnt):\n            futures.append(executor.submit(do_request))\n        for future in concurrent.futures.as_completed(futures):\n            pass\n\n\nfor i in range(1, 500):\n    threads = [Thread(target=measure, args=()) for _ in range(5)]\n\n    for t in threads: t.start()\n    for t in threads: t.join()\n```\n\n## System Information\n\n```\n{\n  \"chardet\": {\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.4.1\"\n  },\n  \"cryptography\": {\n    \"version\": \"43.0.1\"\n  },\n  \"idna\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.10\"\n  },\n  \"implementation\": {\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\n    \"version\": \"3.11.11\"\n  },\n  \"platform\": {\n    \"release\": \"4.18.0-553.34.1.el8_10.x86_64\",\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\n  },\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\n    \"openssl_version\": \"30300020\",\n    \"version\": \"24.2.1\"\n  },\n  \"requests\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.32.3\"\n  },\n  \"system_ssl\": {\n    \"version\": \"101010bf\"\n  },\n  \"urllib3\": {\n    \"version\": \"1.26.19\"\n  },\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": true\n}\n```\n\n(Attempted with requests v2.32.0, v2.32.2, v2.32.3, urllib3 v1.26.18, v1.26.19.)\n ", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "saksham-sak", "timestamp": "2025-01-25T06:31:25Z", "body": " Ensure that each thread creates and uses its own ssl_context rather than sharing a global one. This isolates SSL configurations and avoids concurrency issues.\n Use requests' Session object and configure a unique SSL context per session.\nimport requests\nfrom urllib3.util.ssl_ import create_urllib3_context\n\ndef do_request():\n    session = requests.Session()\n    session.mount(\"https://\", requests.adapters.HTTPAdapter(\n        pool_connections=1, pool_maxsize=1, ssl_context=create_urllib3_context()\n    ))\n\n    cert = ('./client.crt', './client.key')\n    response = session.get(\"https://httpbin.org/anything\", cert=cert)\n    print(response.status_code)\n Avoids race conditions by isolating SSL contexts.\nSlightly increases memory usage due to creating multiple SSL contexts.\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "alloni", "timestamp": "2025-01-27T20:13:59Z", "body": "@saksham-sak - that's fine for a workaround - and I will be implementing that - but it would still be a good idea to add a mutex around the SSL context read/update to prevent this happening to anyone _else_.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "sigmavirus24", "timestamp": "2025-01-27T23:58:54Z", "body": "If you're finding that an SSLContext is not thread safe you should instead file that bug with CPython, with the most minimal reproduction case possible in order to get a real fix. I know for a fact it disables the GIL in _ssl.c and uses openssl's locking logic, so it's also possible this an OpenSSL issue although I imagine something like this would have already been found. ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "alloni", "timestamp": "2025-01-28T17:10:08Z", "body": "@sigmavirus24 - Looks like this _is_ documented in CPython - as per https://docs.python.org/3/library/ssl.html#ssl.SSLContext:\n\n`Note - SSLContext is designed to be shared and used by multiple connections. Thus, it is thread-safe as long as it is not reconfigured after being used by a connection.`\n\nSo it's explicitly documented as not thread-safe under these circumstances.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "psf/requests/issue/6872#1", "question": "In psf/requests, we are seeing intermittent segmentation faults with requests v2.32.0-v2.32.3 and urllib3 v1.26.19 when making multiple simultaneous requests and passing a client certificate. With faulthandler, the crash appears to occur when two separate threads access the same ssl_context at the same time: one updating it with the given cert and one reading it.\n\nStack traces:\n\n```\nCurrent thread 0x00007f46c114d700 (most recent call first):\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 418 in ssl_wrap_socket\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/connection.py\", line 419 in connect\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 1060 in _validate_conn\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 404 in _make_request\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 715 in urlopen\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/requests/adapters.py\", line 667 in send\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 703 in send\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 589 in request\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 602 in get\n  ...\n\nThread 0x00007f469e7fc700 (most recent call first):\n  File \"/usr/ssl.py\", line 1382 in do_handshake\n  File \"/usr/ssl.py\", line 1104 in _create\n  File \"/usr/ssl.py\", line 517 in wrap_socket\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 493 in _ssl_wrap_socket_impl\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 449 in ssl_wrap_socket\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/connection.py\", line 419 in connect\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 1060 in _validate_conn\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 404 in _make_request\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 715 in urlopen\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/requests/adapters.py\", line 667 in send\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 703 in send\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 589 in request\n  File \"<library path>/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 602 in get\n  ...\n```\n\nThis seems related to requests v2.32 switching from creating a new SSL context for a request without an SSL context to using the same preloaded SSL context across multiple requests, as discussed in https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/6745. The expected result is no segmentation faults; the actual result is intermittent segmentation faults. It only crashes if a generated certificate is passed in, generated with:\n\n```\nopenssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 -keyout client.key -out client.crt -days 365 -nodes\n```\n\nReproduction code:\n\n```\nimport concurrent.futures\nimport random\nimport uuid\nfrom threading import Thread\nfrom time import time\n\nimport requests\n\n\ndef do_request():\n    start = time()\n    random_id = uuid.uuid4()\n    delay = random.randint(1, 5)\n    print(\"start {} delay {} seconds\".format(random_id, delay))\n    endpoints = []\n    endpoints.append('https://httpbin.org/delay/' + str(delay))\n    delay = str(random.randint(1, 5)) + 's'\n    endpoints.append('https://run.mocky.io/v3/0432e9f0-674f-45bd-9c18-628b861c2258?mocky-delay=' + str(delay))\n    random.shuffle(endpoints)\n    response = None\n    for endpoint in endpoints:\n        try:\n            print(\"start {} delay {} seconds\".format(random_id, endpoint))\n            if 'run' in endpoint:\n                cert = './client.crt', './client.key'\n                response = requests.get(endpoint, timeout=random.randint(1, 5), cert=cert)\n            else:\n                response = requests.get(endpoint, timeout=random.randint(1, 5))\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(e)\n    end = time()\n\n\n    print(\"finished {} in {} seconds\".format(random_id, end - start))\n    return response\n\n\ndef measure():\n    cnt = 20\n    with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=10) as executor:\n        futures = []\n        for server in range(1, cnt):\n            futures.append(executor.submit(do_request))\n        for future in concurrent.futures.as_completed(futures):\n            pass\n\n\nfor i in range(1, 500):\n    threads = [Thread(target=measure, args=()) for _ in range(5)]\n\n    for t in threads: t.start()\n    for t in threads: t.join()\n```\n\nSystem information:\n\n```\n{\n  \"chardet\": {\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.4.1\"\n  },\n  \"cryptography\": {\n    \"version\": \"43.0.1\"\n  },\n  \"idna\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.10\"\n  },\n  \"implementation\": {\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\n    \"version\": \"3.11.11\"\n  },\n  \"platform\": {\n    \"release\": \"4.18.0-553.34.1.el8_10.x86_64\",\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\n  },\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\n    \"openssl_version\": \"30300020\",\n    \"version\": \"24.2.1\"\n  },\n  \"requests\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.32.3\"\n  },\n  \"system_ssl\": {\n    \"version\": \"101010bf\"\n  },\n  \"urllib3\": {\n    \"version\": \"1.26.19\"\n  },\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": true\n}\n```\n\nAlso attempted with requests v2.32.0, v2.32.2, v2.32.3 and urllib3 v1.26.18, v1.26.19. How should this be handled or worked around?", "answer": "Ensure that each thread creates and uses its own ssl_context rather than sharing a global one. This isolates SSL configurations and avoids concurrency issues.\n Use requests' Session object and configure a unique SSL context per session.\nimport requests\nfrom urllib3.util.ssl_ import create_urllib3_context\n\ndef do_request():\n    session = requests.Session()\n    session.mount(\"https://\", requests.adapters.HTTPAdapter(\n        pool_connections=1, pool_maxsize=1, ssl_context=create_urllib3_context()\n    ))\n\n    cert = ('./client.crt', './client.key')\n    response = session.get(\"https://httpbin.org/anything\", cert=cert)\n    print(response.status_code)\n Avoids race conditions by isolating SSL contexts.\nSlightly increases memory usage due to creating multiple SSL contexts.", "knowledge_type": "parametric", "grounding_sources": [], "answer_grounded_in": "c1,c3", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://httpbin.org/anything"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:15:10.504242Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "psf/requests/issue/6868", "repo": "psf/requests", "number": 6868, "title": "`import requests` intermittently fails on windows server 2022", "url": "https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/6868", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-01-10T02:55:11Z", "closed_at": "2026-02-12T23:26:40Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "AY1uZwIcJzKhaGywovQP", "security_topic": "intermittent SSL verification failure in requests library", "qa_summary": "The developer asked if the intermittent SSL import failure is a security\u2011related issue and whether it has been fixed, and the answer cited that it was reverted in Requests\u202f2.32.5, indicating the problem is resolved in that version.", "question_comment_id": "c1", "answer_comment_id": "c3", "question_author": "AY1uZwIcJzKhaGywovQP", "answer_author": "nateprewitt", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation", "code", "issue_tracker"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": ["2.32.5"], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Accepted because the question concerns SSL verification failure diagnostics and the answer correctly identifies it as a resolved regression with a fixed release.", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "AY1uZwIcJzKhaGywovQP", "timestamp": "2025-01-10T02:55:11Z", "body": "<!-- Summary. -->\r\n\r\nI have an error on a miniconda based python3.11 installation on Windows Server 2022 where `import requests` fails intermittently for one of my apps. It seems to be an SSL verification issue as far as I can tell.\r\n\r\n## Expected Result\r\n\r\nI expected the `requests` library to successfully load. \r\n\r\n## Actual Result\r\n\r\n`import requests` fails with the following stack trace:\r\n\r\n```\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n  File \"C:\\ProjectName\\APPS\\py-mysvc\\deployed\\src\\app.py\", line 29, in <module>\r\n    import mysvc_models\r\n  File \"C:\\ProjectName\\APPS\\py-mysvc\\deployed\\src\\mysvc_models.py\", line 9, in <module>\r\n    import mysvc_utils\r\n  File \"C:\\ProjectName\\APPS\\py-mysvc\\deployed\\src\\mysvc_utils.py\", line 17, in <module>\r\n    import requests\r\n  File \"C:\\ProjectName\\ENV\\py-mysvc\\Lib\\site-packages\\requests\\__init__.py\", line 164, in <module>\r\n    from .api import delete, get, head, options, patch, post, put, request\r\n  File \"C:\\ProjectName\\ENV\\py-mysvc\\Lib\\site-packages\\requests\\api.py\", line 11, in <module>\r\n    from . import sessions\r\n  File \"C:\\ProjectName\\ENV\\py-mysvc\\Lib\\site-packages\\requests\\sessions.py\", line 15, in <module>\r\n    from .adapters import HTTPAdapter\r\n  File \"C:\\ProjectName\\ENV\\py-mysvc\\Lib\\site-packages\\requests\\adapters.py\", line 81, in <module>\r\n    _preloaded_ssl_context.load_verify_locations(\r\nssl.SSLError: [X509] PEM lib (_ssl.c:4154)\r\n```\r\n\r\nMatching this stack trace to the openssl version I have available leads me to `SSL_CTX_load_verify_locations` but I'm unable to debug the issue further. \r\n\r\n## Reproduction Steps\r\n\r\nI'm unable to consistently reproduce it but it fails at this step. \r\n```python\r\nimport requests\r\n```\r\n\r\n## System Information\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"chardet\": {\r\n    \"version\": null\r\n  },\r\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.3.2\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"cryptography\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"idna\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.10\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"implementation\": {\r\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\r\n    \"version\": \"3.11.6\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"platform\": {\r\n    \"release\": \"10\",\r\n    \"system\": \"Windows\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\r\n    \"openssl_version\": \"\",\r\n    \"version\": null\r\n  },\r\n  \"requests\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"2.32.3\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"system_ssl\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"30300020\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"urllib3\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"2.2.3\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\r\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": false\r\n}\r\n```\r\nRelevant conda packages:\r\n```\r\n# Name                    Version                   Build  Channel\r\nca-certificates           2024.9.24            haa95532_0\r\ncertifi                   2024.8.30       py311haa95532_0\r\ncffi                      1.17.1          py311h827c3e9_0\r\nh11                       0.14.0          py311haa95532_0\r\nh2                        4.1.0              pyhd8ed1ab_0    conda-forge\r\nhpack                     4.0.0                      py_0\r\nhttpcore                  1.0.6              pyhd8ed1ab_0    conda-forge\r\nhttpx                     0.27.2             pyhd8ed1ab_0    conda-forge\r\nopenssl                   3.3.2                h2466b09_0    conda-forge\r\npython                    3.11.6          h2628c8c_0_cpython    conda-forge\r\npython-certifi-win32      1.2             py311h1ea47a8_6    conda-forge\r\npython_abi                3.11                    5_cp311    conda-forge\r\npywin32                   306             py311h12c1d0e_2    conda-forge\r\nwincertstore              0.2             py311haa95532_0\r\n```\r\n<!-- This command is only available on Requests v2.16.4 and greater. Otherwise,\r\nplease provide some basic information about your system (Python version,\r\noperating system, &c). -->\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "AY1uZwIcJzKhaGywovQP", "timestamp": "2025-01-15T02:23:09Z", "body": "As mentioned above, the issue happens intermittently not continuously as mentioned in the PR. I have indeed recreated the environment in the PR but this does not appear to have fixed the issue. \r\n\r\nWhat would be the best way to improve the logs so that we can determine why `SSL_CTX_load_verify_locations` fails the next time this reoccurs? ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "saksham-sak", "timestamp": "2025-01-23T10:37:31Z", "body": "Check System Environment:\n\nEnsure no system-level conflicts affect SSL libraries. For instance, avoid mixing libraries from different environments.\nor\nExplicitly specify the SSL context in your application:\n\nimport ssl\nssl._create_default_https_context = ssl._create_unverified_context", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "nateprewitt", "timestamp": "2026-02-12T23:26:40Z", "body": "Hi @AY1uZwIcJzKhaGywovQP, this was reverted in Requests 2.32.5 for a number of reasons including this race condition with certain OpenSSL versions. This should no longer be occurring with the most recent  release.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "psf/requests/issue/6868#1", "question": "On a miniconda-based Python 3.11 installation on Windows Server 2022, `import requests` intermittently fails for one of my apps with what looks like an SSL verification issue. I am using requests 2.32.3, urllib3 2.2.3, CPython 3.11.6, OpenSSL/system_ssl version `30300020`, and relevant conda packages including `openssl 3.3.2 h2466b09_0 conda-forge`, `certifi 2024.8.30`, `ca-certificates 2024.9.24`, and `python-certifi-win32 1.2`. The stack trace is:\n\n```text\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"C:\\ProjectName\\APPS\\py-mysvc\\deployed\\src\\app.py\", line 29, in <module>\n    import mysvc_models\n  File \"C:\\ProjectName\\APPS\\py-mysvc\\deployed\\src\\mysvc_models.py\", line 9, in <module>\n    import mysvc_utils\n  File \"C:\\ProjectName\\APPS\\py-mysvc\\deployed\\src\\mysvc_utils.py\", line 17, in <module>\n    import requests\n  File \"C:\\ProjectName\\ENV\\py-mysvc\\Lib\\site-packages\\requests\\__init__.py\", line 164, in <module>\n    from .api import delete, get, head, options, patch, post, put, request\n  File \"C:\\ProjectName\\ENV\\py-mysvc\\Lib\\site-packages\\requests\\api.py\", line 11, in <module>\n    from . import sessions\n  File \"C:\\ProjectName\\ENV\\py-mysvc\\Lib\\site-packages\\requests\\sessions.py\", line 15, in <module>\n    from .adapters import HTTPAdapter\n  File \"C:\\ProjectName\\ENV\\py-mysvc\\Lib\\site-packages\\requests\\adapters.py\", line 81, in <module>\n    _preloaded_ssl_context.load_verify_locations(\nssl.SSLError: [X509] PEM lib (_ssl.c:4154)\n```\n\nIt fails at:\n\n```python\nimport requests\n```\n\nI matched the stack trace to OpenSSL `SSL_CTX_load_verify_locations`, but cannot debug it further. The issue happens intermittently, not continuously, and recreating the environment mentioned in the PR did not fix it. What is the best way to improve logging so we can determine why `SSL_CTX_load_verify_locations` fails the next time this reoccurs?", "answer": "The maintainer said this behavior was reverted in Requests 2.32.5 because of several issues, including a race condition with certain OpenSSL versions. They indicated it should no longer occur with the most recent Requests release.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["Requests 2.32.5"], "answer_grounded_in": "c3", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": [], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:16:15.195644Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "psf/requests/issue/6810", "repo": "psf/requests", "number": 6810, "title": "Change auth.py to be used in a FIPS system", "url": "https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/6810", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2024-10-18T10:32:43Z", "closed_at": "2026-04-15T14:56:40Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "Jose-albino", "security_topic": "Enabling MD5 hashing in FIPS mode for HTTPDigestAuth", "qa_summary": "The developer asked if setting hashlib.md5(..., usedforsecurity=False) to make HTTPDigestAuth work in a FIPS environment is safe, and the answer cites the Python hashlib documentation and RFC\u202f7616 recommending against using MD5 for security.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c0", "question_author": "Jose-albino", "answer_author": "Jose-albino", "answerer_role": "op_self", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Accepted because the question is a valid FIPS/MD5 compatibility issue and the answer correctly provides the standard usedforsecurity=False workaround used to restore HTTP Digest Auth functionality under FIPS constraints.", "llm_confidence": 0.7124999999999999, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "Jose-albino", "timestamp": "2024-10-18T10:32:43Z", "body": "<!-- Summary. -->\r\n\r\n## Expected Result\r\n\r\nUsing the requests to perform a HTTPS action is working in a FIPS environment\r\n \r\n\r\n<!-- What you expected. -->\r\n\r\n## Actual Result\r\n\r\nIn UNIX environment with fips enabled the MD5 can't be used.\r\nIt provides this error\r\n\r\nValueError: [digital envelope routines: EVP_DigestInit_ex] disabled for FIPS\r\n\r\n## Solution\r\nApply this patch in auth.py\r\n\r\n`\r\n\r\n*** 145,151 ****\r\n                def md5_utf8(x):\r\n                    if isinstance(x, str):\r\n                        x = x.encode(\"utf-8\")\r\n  !                 return hashlib.md5(x).hexdigest()    \r\n                hash_utf8 = md5_utf8\r\n            elif _algorithm == \"SHA\":\r\n--- 145,151 ----\r\n                def md5_utf8(x):\r\n                    if isinstance(x, str):\r\n                        x = x.encode(\"utf-8\")\r\n  !                 return hashlib.md5(x,usedforsecurity=False).hexdigest()\r\n                hash_utf8 = md5_utf8\r\n            elif _algorithm == \"SHA\":\r\n\r\n`\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "SeJunB", "timestamp": "2024-11-06T16:27:15Z", "body": "Could I work on this ticket ?\r\nThe `usedforsecurity` parameter is only available in python >= 3.9. Plan is to set `usedforsecurity` to True only if python version >= 3.9.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "Jose-albino", "timestamp": "2024-11-29T14:24:38Z", "body": "that seems ok for me. We use already python 3.10/3.11 in our environment and i believe most cases also ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "SeJunB", "timestamp": "2024-11-29T18:43:00Z", "body": "After giving this a thought, I am hesitant on adding the `usedforsecurity` attribute to MD5 in the HTTPDigestAuth class for the following reasons:\r\n\r\n1. Security Documentation Conflict\r\n- The [hashlib](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html) documentation explicitly states that `usedforsecurity` should only be used in non-security contexts. Adding this attribute to HTTPDigestAuth would directly contradict this guidance.\r\n- [RFC 7616](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7616#section-6.1) does not recommend MD5 as a secure hashing algorithm for digest authentication.\r\n\r\n2. FIPS Compliance and Compatibility Risks\r\n- Setting `usedforsecurity=False` could inadvertently enable MD5-based HTTPDigestAuthentication in environments and could cause trouble for any users using `requests` in a FIPS environment.\r\n\r\nRecommendation:\r\n- Close this issue\r\n- For teams requiring this specific configuration, recommend forking and maintaining a custom patched version\r\n- Encourage migration to more secure hashing algorithms (e.g., SHA)\r\n\r\nIn my experience working with customers requiring FIPS compliance, they typically prefer upgrading to more secure authentication methods rather than maintaining legacy MD5-based approaches.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "RossComputerGuy", "timestamp": "2025-10-10T16:37:31Z", "body": "I get errors running the tests with FIPS enabled. Maybe it would be good to automatically skip the tests which depend on md5.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "nateprewitt", "timestamp": "2026-03-30T15:47:15Z", "body": "I'm not sure we're framing the security concerns correctly. The hashing algorithm is explicitly _not_ a security mechanism, it's a detail of the protocol. The RFC [calls this out](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7616#section-5.1) as a limitation. Whether a server chooses MD5 or SHA256, doesn't meaningfully change the risk profile for intercepted requests.\n\n> HTTP Digest Authentication, when used with human-memorable passwords,\n   is vulnerable to dictionary attacks.  Such attacks are much easier\n   than cryptographic attacks on any widely used algorithm, including\n   those that are no longer considered secure.  **In other words,\n   algorithm agility does not make this usage any more secure.**\n\nThe security control here is a high entropy password + TLS + using Digest auth with trusted servers. The server chooses the algorithm used, not Requests. The algorithm choice is a constant time multiplier, not an algorithmic complexity change for brute-forcing. Adding a single character to your password provides more brute-force resistance than the algorithm choice.\n\nI've opened https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/7310 to address this. We'll leave it out of today's release to give some time for comment.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "psf/requests/issue/6810#1", "question": "In psf/requests, using `requests` to perform an HTTPS action in a UNIX environment with FIPS enabled fails because MD5 cannot be used. The observed error is:\n\n`ValueError: [digital envelope routines: EVP_DigestInit_ex] disabled for FIPS`\n\nCan `auth.py` be changed so HTTPS requests work in a FIPS environment, for example by applying this patch?\n\n`\n\n*** 145,151 ****\n                def md5_utf8(x):\n                    if isinstance(x, str):\n                        x = x.encode(\"utf-8\")\n  !                 return hashlib.md5(x).hexdigest()    \n                hash_utf8 = md5_utf8\n            elif _algorithm == \"SHA\":\n--- 145,151 ----\n                def md5_utf8(x):\n                    if isinstance(x, str):\n                        x = x.encode(\"utf-8\")\n  !                 return hashlib.md5(x,usedforsecurity=False).hexdigest()\n                hash_utf8 = md5_utf8\n            elif _algorithm == \"SHA\":\n\n`", "answer": "The maintainer said the security concern should not be framed as MD5 being the security mechanism here: in HTTP Digest Authentication the hashing algorithm is a protocol detail, the server chooses the algorithm, and RFC 7616 notes algorithm agility does not make human-memorable passwords meaningfully more secure. The relevant security controls are a high-entropy password, TLS, and using Digest auth only with trusted servers. A PR was opened to address the issue: https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/7310, but it was left out of that day's release to allow time for comment.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["RFC 7616 section 5.1", "https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/7310"], "answer_grounded_in": "c5", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": [], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-18T07:57:06.610562Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "psf/requests/issue/6784", "repo": "psf/requests", "number": 6784, "title": "Multiple path separators causes bad requests ", "url": "https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/6784", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2024-08-05T07:15:00Z", "closed_at": "2026-04-04T19:03:50Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "martin-pil", "security_topic": "URL path normalization regression causing request failure after authentication", "qa_summary": "The developer asked why Digest authentication requests with double slashes fail, and the maintainer replied that the issue is a duplicate of #6711 and has been fixed in urllib3 (to be back\u2011ported to Requests).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "martin-pil", "answer_author": "nateprewitt", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "issue_tracker", "pr_data"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": ["#6711"], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Accepted because the issue is a valid URL normalization regression affecting auth flows and the answer correctly attributes it to an upstream fix in urllib3.", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "martin-pil", "timestamp": "2024-08-05T07:15:00Z", "body": "<!-- Summary. -->\r\nI'm assuming that change in https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/6644 is the cause of this changed behavior that operations with // in path fails. It seems to be related to Authentication as it is the only thing I could find that does not get trimmed by the change in 6644.\r\n\r\n## Expected Result\r\n\r\nSimilar behavior as before 2.32.0\r\n\r\n## Actual Result\r\n\r\nRequest captured by wireshark, note that this is the second request as the first got a 401\r\n\r\nHypertext Transfer Protocol\r\n    POST /axis-cgi/capturemode.cgi HTTP/1.1\\r\\n\r\n    Host: 192.168.0.1\\r\\n\r\n    User-Agent: python-requests/2.32.3\\r\\n\r\n    Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate, zstd\\r\\n\r\n    Accept: */*\\r\\n\r\n    Connection: keep-alive\\r\\n\r\n    Content-Length: 50\\r\\n\r\n     [truncated]Authorization: Digest username=\"root\", realm=\"AXIS_B8A44F45D62F\", nonce=\"UKH3vekeBgA=2b0d57c2d8b8eb15307576e30309cb7b23a2304c\", uri=\"//axis-cgi/capturemode.cgi\", response=\"0a51943f746a0d88c6574f662b457a9c\", algorithm=\"MD5\", qo\r\n         username=\"root\"\r\n         realm=\"AXIS_B8A44F45D62F\"\r\n         nonce=\"UKH3vekeBgA=2b0d57c2d8b8eb15307576e30309cb7b23a2304c\"\r\n         uri=\"//axis-cgi/capturemode.cgi\"\r\n         response=\"0a51943f746a0d88c6574f662b457a9c\"\r\n         algorithm=\"MD5\"\r\n         qop=\"auth\"\r\n         nc=00000001\r\n    \\r\\n\r\n    [Full request URI: http://172.26.21.128/axis-cgi/capturemode.cgi]\r\n    [HTTP request 2/2]\r\n    [Prev request in frame: 10]\r\n    [Response in frame: 19]\r\n    File Data: 50 bytes\r\n    Data (50 bytes)\r\n\r\nResponse:\r\nHypertext Transfer Protocol\r\n    HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request\\r\\n\r\n    Date: Mon, 05 Aug 2024 06:25:23 GMT\\r\\n\r\n    Server: Apache/2.4.58 (Unix) OpenSSL/3.0.13\\r\\n\r\n    X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\\r\\n\r\n    X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN\\r\\n\r\n    X-XSS-Protection: 1; mode=block\\r\\n\r\n     [truncated]Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://*.google-analytics.com https://*.analytics.google.com https://*.googletagmanager.com https://*.axis.com mediastream: blob:; script\r\n    Content-Length: 226\\r\\n\r\n    Connection: close\\r\\n\r\n    Content-Type: text/html; charset=iso-8859-1\\r\\n\r\n    \\r\\n\r\n    [HTTP response 2/2]\r\n    [Time since request: 0.001411000 seconds]\r\n    [Prev request in frame: 10]\r\n    [Prev response in frame: 13]\r\n    [Request in frame: 16]\r\n    [Request URI: http://192.168.0.1/axis-cgi/capturemode.cgi]\r\n    File Data: 226 bytes\r\n\r\n## Reproduction Steps\r\nThis was run at a axis camera\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport requests\r\n\r\nurl = \"http://192.168.01//axis-cgi/capturemode.cgi\"\r\nbody = {\r\n            \"apiVersion\": \"1.0\",\r\n            \"method\": \"getCaptureModes\"}\r\n\r\nauth = requests.auth.HTTPDigestAuth(\"root\", \"pass\")\r\nresponse = requests.post(url=url, data=json.dumps(body),\r\n                                              auth=auth, proxies=dict(http=None, https=None))\r\n```\r\n\r\n## System Information\r\n\r\n    $ python -m requests.help\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"chardet\": {\r\n    \"version\": null\r\n  },\r\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.3.2\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"cryptography\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"42.0.8\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"idna\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.7\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"implementation\": {\r\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\r\n    \"version\": \"3.11.2\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"platform\": {\r\n    \"release\": \"6.1.0-21-amd64\",\r\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\r\n    \"openssl_version\": \"30200020\",\r\n    \"version\": \"24.1.0\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"requests\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"2.32.3\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"system_ssl\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"300000b0\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"urllib3\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"2.2.2\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\r\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": true\r\n}\r\n```\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "nateprewitt", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T19:03:50Z", "body": "Resolving this as a duplicate of #6711 as they have the same root cause. The issue is now fixed in urllib3. Once that is released, we can fix this in Requests.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "psf/requests/issue/6784#1", "question": "In psf/requests, after the change in https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/6644, requests with multiple path separators (`//`) in the path appear to fail when using digest authentication. I expected behavior similar to before Requests 2.32.0, but with Requests 2.32.3 the second request after an initial 401 is sent as `POST /axis-cgi/capturemode.cgi HTTP/1.1` while the Digest `Authorization` header still contains `uri=\"//axis-cgi/capturemode.cgi\"`, and the server returns `HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request`.\n\nWireshark capture of the second request and response:\n\n```text\nHypertext Transfer Protocol\n    POST /axis-cgi/capturemode.cgi HTTP/1.1\\r\\n\n    Host: 192.168.0.1\\r\\n\n    User-Agent: python-requests/2.32.3\\r\\n\n    Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate, zstd\\r\\n\n    Accept: */*\\r\\n\n    Connection: keep-alive\\r\\n\n    Content-Length: 50\\r\\n\n     [truncated]Authorization: Digest username=\"root\", realm=\"AXIS_B8A44F45D62F\", nonce=\"UKH3vekeBgA=2b0d57c2d8b8eb15307576e30309cb7b23a2304c\", uri=\"//axis-cgi/capturemode.cgi\", response=\"0a51943f746a0d88c6574f662b457a9c\", algorithm=\"MD5\", qo\n         username=\"root\"\n         realm=\"AXIS_B8A44F45D62F\"\n         nonce=\"UKH3vekeBgA=2b0d57c2d8b8eb15307576e30309cb7b23a2304c\"\n         uri=\"//axis-cgi/capturemode.cgi\"\n         response=\"0a51943f746a0d88c6574f662b457a9c\"\n         algorithm=\"MD5\"\n         qop=\"auth\"\n         nc=00000001\n    \\r\\n\n    [Full request URI: http://172.26.21.128/axis-cgi/capturemode.cgi]\n    [HTTP request 2/2]\n    [Prev request in frame: 10]\n    [Response in frame: 19]\n    File Data: 50 bytes\n    Data (50 bytes)\n\nResponse:\nHypertext Transfer Protocol\n    HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request\\r\\n\n    Date: Mon, 05 Aug 2024 06:25:23 GMT\\r\\n\n    Server: Apache/2.4.58 (Unix) OpenSSL/3.0.13\\r\\n\n    X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\\r\\n\n    X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN\\r\\n\n    X-XSS-Protection: 1; mode=block\\r\\n\n     [truncated]Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://*.google-analytics.com https://*.analytics.google.com https://*.googletagmanager.com https://*.axis.com mediastream: blob:; script\n    Content-Length: 226\\r\\n\n    Connection: close\\r\\n\n    Content-Type: text/html; charset=iso-8859-1\\r\\n\n    \\r\\n\n    [HTTP response 2/2]\n    [Time since request: 0.001411000 seconds]\n    [Prev request in frame: 10]\n    [Prev response in frame: 13]\n    [Request in frame: 16]\n    [Request URI: http://192.168.0.1/axis-cgi/capturemode.cgi]\n    File Data: 226 bytes\n```\n\nReproduction, run against an Axis camera:\n\n```python\nimport requests\n\nurl = \"http://192.168.01//axis-cgi/capturemode.cgi\"\nbody = {\n            \"apiVersion\": \"1.0\",\n            \"method\": \"getCaptureModes\"}\n\nauth = requests.auth.HTTPDigestAuth(\"root\", \"pass\")\nresponse = requests.post(url=url, data=json.dumps(body),\n                                              auth=auth, proxies=dict(http=None, https=None))\n```\n\nSystem information from `python -m requests.help`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"chardet\": {\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.3.2\"\n  },\n  \"cryptography\": {\n    \"version\": \"42.0.8\"\n  },\n  \"idna\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.7\"\n  },\n  \"implementation\": {\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\n    \"version\": \"3.11.2\"\n  },\n  \"platform\": {\n    \"release\": \"6.1.0-21-amd64\",\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\n  },\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\n    \"openssl_version\": \"30200020\",\n    \"version\": \"24.1.0\"\n  },\n  \"requests\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.32.3\"\n  },\n  \"system_ssl\": {\n    \"version\": \"300000b0\"\n  },\n  \"urllib3\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.2.2\"\n  },\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": true\n}\n```\n\nIs this a Requests regression related to PR #6644, and how will it be addressed?", "answer": "The maintainer resolved this as a duplicate of issue #6711, saying both issues have the same root cause. They stated that the issue is now fixed in urllib3, and that once the urllib3 fix is released, Requests can fix it.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["psf/requests#6711", "urllib3 fix", "psf/requests#6644"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["#6711"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-16T03:05:16.164273Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "psf/requests/issue/6764", "repo": "psf/requests", "number": 6764, "title": "permission denied regression reading extracted certs with multiple users", "url": "https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/6764", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2024-07-09T00:54:35Z", "closed_at": "2026-03-29T19:30:19Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "jmelahman", "security_topic": "permission error when extracting bundled certificates for multiple users", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know if the permission\u2011denied error when extracting certificates to /tmp/cacert.pem is a security\u2011related problem and how it was resolved, which was addressed by removing the global cert loading in Requests\u202f2.32.5 (see PR\u202f#6667).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c2", "question_author": "jmelahman", "answer_author": "nateprewitt", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation", "issue_tracker"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": ["2.32.5"], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Accepted because the question concerns a valid permission/security regression and the answer correctly identifies its removal in Requests 2.32.5 as the fix.", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "jmelahman", "timestamp": "2024-07-09T00:54:35Z", "body": "If it is necessary to [extract the certs from a zip file](https://github.com/psf/requests/blob/0e322af87745eff34caffe4df68456ebc20d9068/src/requests/utils.py#L296-L302), they'll be written to somewhere like `/tmp/cacert.pem`. This is problematic for subsequent users who may not have have permission to access this `/tmp/cacert.pem`\r\n\r\nSeems related to https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/6667\r\n\r\nNote, this issue was not present with version `2.31.0`\r\n\r\n## Expected Result\r\ntwo users should be able to extract zipped certs on the same filesystem without issue \r\n\r\n## Actual Result\r\n```\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n  File \"/usr/lib/python3.9/runpy.py\", line 197, in _run_module_as_main\r\n    return _run_code(code, main_globals, None,\r\n  File \"/usr/lib/python3.9/runpy.py\", line 87, in _run_code\r\n    exec(code, run_globals)\r\n  File \"/home/jamison/.../__main__.py\", line 83, in <module>\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 1007, in _find_and_load\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 986, in _find_and_load_unlocked\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 664, in _load_unlocked\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 627, in _load_backward_compatible\r\n  File \"<frozen zipimport>\", line 259, in load_module\r\n  File \"/home/jamison/..., line 22, in <module>\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 1007, in _find_and_load\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 986, in _find_and_load_unlocked\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 664, in _load_unlocked\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 627, in _load_backward_compatible\r\n  File \"<frozen zipimport>\", line 259, in load_module\r\n  File \"/home/jamison/..., line 13, in <module>\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 1007, in _find_and_load\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 986, in _find_and_load_unlocked\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 664, in _load_unlocked\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 627, in _load_backward_compatible\r\n  File \"<frozen zipimport>\", line 259, in load_module\r\n  File \"/home/jamison/.../pypi__39__requests_2_32_2/requests/__init__.py\", line 164, in <module>\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 1007, in _find_and_load\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 986, in _find_and_load_unlocked\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 664, in _load_unlocked\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 627, in _load_backward_compatible\r\n  File \"<frozen zipimport>\", line 259, in load_module\r\n  File \"/home/jamison/.../pypi__39__requests_2_32_2/requests/api.py\", line 11, in <module>\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 1007, in _find_and_load\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 986, in _find_and_load_unlocked\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 664, in _load_unlocked\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 627, in _load_backward_compatible\r\n  File \"<frozen zipimport>\", line 259, in load_module\r\n  File \"/home/jamison/.../pypi__39__requests_2_32_2/requests/sessions.py\", line 15, in <module>\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 1007, in _find_and_load\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 986, in _find_and_load_unlocked\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 664, in _load_unlocked\r\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 627, in _load_backward_compatible\r\n  File \"<frozen zipimport>\", line 259, in load_module\r\n  File \"/home/jamison/.../pypi__39__requests_2_32_2/requests/adapters.py\", line 77, in <module>\r\nPermissionError: [Errno 13] Permission denied\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Reproduction Steps\r\n\r\n```shell\r\ntouch /tmp/test_utils.py\r\nchown root:root /tmp/test_utils.py\r\npython -m pytest test/test_utils.py\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe full end-to-end steps are a bit involved. A,\r\n`__main__,py`\r\n```\r\nimport requests\r\n```\r\nas a standalone zip executable is enough to repro (though I haven't been able to repro with executables that unarchive themselves such as `zipapp` -- in this case, we're using something similar to google's [subpar](https://github.com/google/subpar)).\r\n\r\n## System Information\r\n\r\n    $ python -m requests.help\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"chardet\": {\r\n    \"version\": null\r\n  },\r\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.3.2\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"cryptography\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"idna\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.7\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"implementation\": {\r\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\r\n    \"version\": \"3.12.3\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"platform\": {\r\n    \"release\": \"6.6.32-1-lts\",\r\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\r\n    \"openssl_version\": \"\",\r\n    \"version\": null\r\n  },\r\n  \"requests\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"2.32.3\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"system_ssl\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"30300000\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"urllib3\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"2.2.2\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\r\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": false\r\n}\r\n```\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "jmelahman", "timestamp": "2024-07-09T01:43:13Z", "body": "I think this is fundamentally the same issue as https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/5994 which was marked as won't fix.\r\n\r\nHappy to contribute a fix if there is a satisfactory solution. In my case, the file permissions less of the issue and more so this file is not guaranteed to be owned by the current user. Possible solutions I see include:\r\n- embed the user metadata into the filename. `/tmp/cacert.pem` becomes `/tmp/jamison_cacert.pem`. Not foolproof, but simple and resolves my issue.\r\n- replace this `/tmp/cacert.pem` with a random temporary file (similar to what `certifi` would do). I'm not sure how long this file would need to live on disk, but maybe now [with preloading the certs](https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/6667), this file can be removed after this initial load.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "nateprewitt", "timestamp": "2026-03-29T19:30:19Z", "body": "This shouldn't be encountered anymore. The global cert loading was removed in Requests 2.32.5 due to a number of regressions it caused. The behavior now is identical to what it was prior to 2.32.0.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "psf/requests/issue/6764#1", "question": "In Requests 2.32.3 on Linux, when Requests is run from a standalone zip executable and needs to extract bundled certs from a zip file, it writes them to a path like `/tmp/cacert.pem`. This can cause a regression for subsequent users on the same filesystem who do not have permission to access that file, or where the file is not owned by the current user. This did not happen in Requests 2.31.0 and seems related to https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/6667 and possibly the older https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/5994.\n\nExpected: two users should be able to extract zipped certs on the same filesystem without issue.\n\nActual traceback:\n```\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/usr/lib/python3.9/runpy.py\", line 197, in _run_module_as_main\n    return _run_code(code, main_globals, None,\n  File \"/usr/lib/python3.9/runpy.py\", line 87, in _run_code\n    exec(code, run_globals)\n  File \"/home/jamison/.../__main__.py\", line 83, in <module>\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 1007, in _find_and_load\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 986, in _find_and_load_unlocked\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 664, in _load_unlocked\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 627, in _load_backward_compatible\n  File \"<frozen zipimport>\", line 259, in load_module\n  File \"/home/jamison/..., line 22, in <module>\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 1007, in _find_and_load\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 986, in _find_and_load_unlocked\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 664, in _load_unlocked\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 627, in _load_backward_compatible\n  File \"<frozen zipimport>\", line 259, in load_module\n  File \"/home/jamison/..., line 13, in <module>\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 1007, in _find_and_load\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 986, in _find_and_load_unlocked\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 664, in _load_unlocked\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 627, in _load_backward_compatible\n  File \"<frozen zipimport>\", line 259, in load_module\n  File \"/home/jamison/.../pypi__39__requests_2_32_2/requests/__init__.py\", line 164, in <module>\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 1007, in _find_and_load\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 986, in _find_and_load_unlocked\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 664, in _load_unlocked\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 627, in _load_backward_compatible\n  File \"<frozen zipimport>\", line 259, in load_module\n  File \"/home/jamison/.../pypi__39__requests_2_32_2/requests/api.py\", line 11, in <module>\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 1007, in _find_and_load\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 986, in _find_and_load_unlocked\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 664, in _load_unlocked\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 627, in _load_backward_compatible\n  File \"<frozen zipimport>\", line 259, in load_module\n  File \"/home/jamison/.../pypi__39__requests_2_32_2/requests/sessions.py\", line 15, in <module>\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 1007, in _find_and_load\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 986, in _find_and_load_unlocked\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 664, in _load_unlocked\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 627, in _load_backward_compatible\n  File \"<frozen zipimport>\", line 259, in load_module\n  File \"/home/jamison/.../pypi__39__requests_2_32_2/requests/adapters.py\", line 77, in <module>\nPermissionError: [Errno 13] Permission denied\n```\n\nMinimal reproduction noted:\n```shell\ntouch /tmp/test_utils.py\nchown root:root /tmp/test_utils.py\npython -m pytest test/test_utils.py\n```\n\nA `__main__.py` containing:\n```\nimport requests\n```\nas a standalone zip executable is enough to reproduce, although not with executables that unarchive themselves such as `zipapp`; this case uses something similar to google's subpar.\n\nSystem information from `python -m requests.help`:\n```json\n{\n  \"chardet\": {\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.3.2\"\n  },\n  \"cryptography\": {\n    \"version\": \"\"\n  },\n  \"idna\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.7\"\n  },\n  \"implementation\": {\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\n    \"version\": \"3.12.3\"\n  },\n  \"platform\": {\n    \"release\": \"6.6.32-1-lts\",\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\n  },\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\n    \"openssl_version\": \"\",\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"requests\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.32.3\"\n  },\n  \"system_ssl\": {\n    \"version\": \"30300000\"\n  },\n  \"urllib3\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.2.2\"\n  },\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": false\n}\n```\n\nIs there a satisfactory fix or behavior change for this `/tmp/cacert.pem` permission/ownership regression when multiple users import Requests from zipped executables?", "answer": "This should no longer be encountered. The global cert loading was removed in Requests 2.32.5 because it caused a number of regressions, and the behavior is now identical to what it was before 2.32.0.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["Requests 2.32.5"], "answer_grounded_in": "c2", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": [], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:25:06.240697Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "psf/requests/issue/6745", "repo": "psf/requests", "number": 6745, "title": "Requests v2.32.0 caused the error `Segmentation fault` when including the `cert` parameter", "url": "https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/6745", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2024-06-19T12:53:40Z", "closed_at": "2024-07-07T21:24:02Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "mingshuang", "security_topic": "segmentation fault when using cert parameter in Requests 2.32.0", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know if the segmentation fault when using the `cert` parameter in Requests 2.32.0 is a security\u2011related issue and was told it is fixed in later releases, see PR\u202f#6731.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c2", "question_author": "mingshuang", "answer_author": "mingshuang", "answerer_role": "op_self", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "pr_data", "issue_tracker"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": ["2.32.3"], "fix_prs": ["#6731"], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Accepted because the issue is a valid TLS-related segmentation fault and the answer correctly identifies the fixing PR as resolution evidence.", "llm_confidence": 0.7124999999999999, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "mingshuang", "timestamp": "2024-06-19T12:53:40Z", "body": "<!-- Summary. -->\r\n\r\nAfter updating the requests to v2.32.0, my service started failure and raised the error `Segmentation fault`. After some debugging and including `faulthandler.enable()` into my code, I saw the trace is below, and no such as kind problem with version `v2.31.0`\r\n```\r\nFile \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 418 in ssl_wrap_socket\r\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/urllib3/connection.py\", line 419 in connect\r\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 1058 in _validate_conn\r\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 404 in _make_request\r\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 715 in urlopen\r\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/requests/adapters.py\", line 564 in send\r\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 703 in send\r\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 589 in request\r\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/requests/api.py\", line 59 in request\r\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/requests/api.py\", line 73 in get\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Expected Result\r\nThere is no `Segmentation fault`\r\n<!-- What you expected. -->\r\n\r\n\r\n## Actual Result\r\n\r\nSaw the `Segmentation fault` randomly. \r\n\r\n<!-- What happened instead. -->\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n## Reproduction Steps\r\n\r\nTo help to reproduce the problem, I wrote a script below to simulate my service code. and before executing the script, a certificate is needed and can be generated with command `openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 -keyout client.key -out client.crt -days 365 -nodes`  \r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport concurrent.futures\r\nimport random\r\nimport uuid\r\nfrom threading import Thread\r\nfrom time import time\r\n\r\nimport requests\r\n\r\n\r\ndef do_request():\r\n    start = time()\r\n    random_id = uuid.uuid4()\r\n    delay = random.randint(1, 5)\r\n    print(\"start {} delay {} seconds\".format(random_id, delay))\r\n    endpoints = []\r\n    endpoints.append('https://httpbin.org/delay/' + str(delay))\r\n    delay = str(random.randint(1, 5)) + 's'\r\n    endpoints.append('https://run.mocky.io/v3/0432e9f0-674f-45bd-9c18-628b861c2258?mocky-delay=' + str(delay))\r\n    random.shuffle(endpoints)\r\n    response = None\r\n    for endpoint in endpoints:\r\n        try:\r\n            print(\"start {} delay {} seconds\".format(random_id, endpoint))\r\n            if 'run' in endpoint:\r\n                cert = './client.crt', './client.key'\r\n                response = requests.get(endpoint, timeout=random.randint(1, 5), cert=cert)\r\n            else:\r\n                response = requests.get(endpoint, timeout=random.randint(1, 5))\r\n        except Exception as e:\r\n            print(e)\r\n    end = time()\r\n\r\n\r\n    print(\"finished {} in {} seconds\".format(random_id, end - start))\r\n    return response\r\n\r\n\r\ndef measure():\r\n    cnt = 20\r\n    with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=10) as executor:\r\n        futures = []\r\n        for server in range(1, cnt):\r\n            futures.append(executor.submit(do_request))\r\n        for future in concurrent.futures.as_completed(futures):\r\n            pass\r\n\r\n\r\nfor i in range(1, 500):\r\n    threads = [Thread(target=measure, args=()) for _ in range(5)]\r\n\r\n    for t in threads: t.start()\r\n    for t in threads: t.join()\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n## System Information\r\n\r\n    $ python -m requests.help\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"chardet\": {\r\n    \"version\": null\r\n  },\r\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.3.2\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"cryptography\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"idna\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.7\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"implementation\": {\r\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\r\n    \"version\": \"3.11.7\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"platform\": {\r\n    \"release\": \"4.18.0-553.5.1.el8_10.x86_64\",\r\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\r\n    \"openssl_version\": \"\",\r\n    \"version\": null\r\n  },\r\n  \"requests\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"2.32.0\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"system_ssl\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"101010bf\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"urllib3\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"1.26.18\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\r\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": false\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n<!-- This command is only available on Requests v2.16.4 and greater. Otherwise,\r\nplease provide some basic information about your system (Python version,\r\noperating system, &c). -->\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "nateprewitt", "timestamp": "2024-07-01T19:54:33Z", "body": "@mingshuang there were initially some issues with the first releases of 2.32.x which is why 2.32.0 and 2.32.1 are yanked from PyPI. Can you please try this with 2.32.3 (the latest release) and if the issue is still reproducing there, check this patch https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/6731/files?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "mingshuang", "timestamp": "2024-07-21T03:01:38Z", "body": "hi @nateprewitt yes, I tried 2.32.3 and the result is same. and also tried the PR https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/6731, this  PR indeed address my problem. ", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "psf/requests/issue/6745#1", "question": "After updating Requests from v2.31.0 to v2.32.0, my service started failing with random `Segmentation fault` errors when making concurrent HTTPS requests, including some requests that pass the `cert` parameter. With `faulthandler.enable()`, the trace is:\n\n```\nFile \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 418 in ssl_wrap_socket\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/urllib3/connection.py\", line 419 in connect\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 1058 in _validate_conn\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 404 in _make_request\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 715 in urlopen\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/requests/adapters.py\", line 564 in send\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 703 in send\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 589 in request\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/requests/api.py\", line 59 in request\n  File \"/opt/vpnaas/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/requests/api.py\", line 73 in get\n```\n\nExpected result: no `Segmentation fault`. Actual result: saw `Segmentation fault` randomly.\n\nTo reproduce, first generate a certificate with:\n\n`openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 -keyout client.key -out client.crt -days 365 -nodes`\n\nThen run:\n\n```python\nimport concurrent.futures\nimport random\nimport uuid\nfrom threading import Thread\nfrom time import time\n\nimport requests\n\n\ndef do_request():\n    start = time()\n    random_id = uuid.uuid4()\n    delay = random.randint(1, 5)\n    print(\"start {} delay {} seconds\".format(random_id, delay))\n    endpoints = []\n    endpoints.append('https://httpbin.org/delay/' + str(delay))\n    delay = str(random.randint(1, 5)) + 's'\n    endpoints.append('https://run.mocky.io/v3/0432e9f0-674f-45bd-9c18-628b861c2258?mocky-delay=' + str(delay))\n    random.shuffle(endpoints)\n    response = None\n    for endpoint in endpoints:\n        try:\n            print(\"start {} delay {} seconds\".format(random_id, endpoint))\n            if 'run' in endpoint:\n                cert = './client.crt', './client.key'\n                response = requests.get(endpoint, timeout=random.randint(1, 5), cert=cert)\n            else:\n                response = requests.get(endpoint, timeout=random.randint(1, 5))\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(e)\n    end = time()\n\n\n    print(\"finished {} in {} seconds\".format(random_id, end - start))\n    return response\n\n\ndef measure():\n    cnt = 20\n    with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=10) as executor:\n        futures = []\n        for server in range(1, cnt):\n            futures.append(executor.submit(do_request))\n        for future in concurrent.futures.as_completed(futures):\n            pass\n\n\nfor i in range(1, 500):\n    threads = [Thread(target=measure, args=()) for _ in range(5)]\n\n    for t in threads: t.start()\n    for t in threads: t.join()\n\n```\n\nSystem information from `python -m requests.help`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"chardet\": {\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.3.2\"\n  },\n  \"cryptography\": {\n    \"version\": \"\"\n  },\n  \"idna\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.7\"\n  },\n  \"implementation\": {\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\n    \"version\": \"3.11.7\"\n  },\n  \"platform\": {\n    \"release\": \"4.18.0-553.5.1.el8_10.x86_64\",\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\n  },\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\n    \"openssl_version\": \"\",\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"requests\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.32.0\"\n  },\n  \"system_ssl\": {\n    \"version\": \"101010bf\"\n  },\n  \"urllib3\": {\n    \"version\": \"1.26.18\"\n  },\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": false\n}\n```\n\nIs this a known Requests 2.32.x issue, and is there a patch that addresses this segfault when using `cert`?", "answer": "Requests 2.32.0 and 2.32.1 initially had issues and were yanked from PyPI. Testing with Requests 2.32.3 still produced the same problem, but PR #6731 addressed the reporter\u2019s segfault.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["Requests 2.32.0", "Requests 2.32.1", "Requests 2.32.3", "PR #6731", "https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/6731"], "answer_grounded_in": "c2", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/6731"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:26:25.331064Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "psf/requests/issue/6736", "repo": "psf/requests", "number": 6736, "title": "2.32.3 does not load system CA certificates when using an Adapter", "url": "https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/6736", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2024-06-07T11:08:47Z", "closed_at": "2024-06-07T16:04:37Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "WhyNotHugo", "security_topic": "system CA certificates not loaded causing SSL verification failure in requests 2.32.x", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know how to restore system CA loading for TLS verification, and the answer points to using a version constraint `requests!=2.32.0,!=2.32.1,!=2.32.2` or explicitly loading default certs as shown in the linked Django\u2011AFIP client file (github.com/WhyNotHugo/django-afip/blob/8ea6dbd79d9cfe52409d9bbf3d61069ea8efbdb0/django_afip/clients.py#L44-L57).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c3", "question_author": "WhyNotHugo", "answer_author": "WhyNotHugo", "answerer_role": "op_self", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Accepted because the issue is a valid TLS CA-loading regression and the answer correctly provides version-based and explicit CA-loading fixes.", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "WhyNotHugo", "timestamp": "2024-06-07T11:08:47Z", "body": "<!-- Summary. -->\r\n\r\nGiven this snippet:\r\n\r\n```py\r\nfrom requests.adapters import HTTPAdapter\r\nfrom requests import Session\r\nfrom urllib3 import PoolManager\r\nfrom urllib3 import ProxyManager\r\nfrom urllib3.util.ssl_ import create_urllib3_context\r\n\r\n\r\nclass AFIPAdapter(HTTPAdapter):\r\n    \"\"\"An adapter with reduced security so it'll work with AFIP.\"\"\"\r\n\r\n    def init_poolmanager(self, *args, **kwargs) -> PoolManager:\r\n        context = create_urllib3_context(ciphers=\"AES128-SHA\")\r\n        # context.load_default_certs()\r\n        kwargs[\"ssl_context\"] = context\r\n        return super().init_poolmanager(*args, **kwargs)\r\n\r\n    def proxy_manager_for(self, *args, **kwargs) -> ProxyManager:\r\n        context = create_urllib3_context(ciphers=\"AES128-SHA\")\r\n        # context.load_default_certs()\r\n        kwargs[\"ssl_context\"] = context\r\n        return super().proxy_manager_for(*args, **kwargs)\r\n\r\n\r\nsession = Session()\r\nsession.mount(\"https://servicios1.afip.gov.ar\", AFIPAdapter())\r\nsession.get(\"https://servicios1.afip.gov.ar/wsfev1/service.asmx?WSDL\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nOn `requests==2.31.0`, this works fine (e.g.: system CAs are loaded by default).\r\n\r\nAs of `2.32.2`, the system CAs are no longer loaded by default, and the commented `context.load_default_certs()` lines need to be included too.\r\n\r\n## Expected Result\r\n\r\nAbove snippet should work; system CAs should be loaded.\r\n\r\n## Actual Result\r\n\r\nAbove snippet fails with:\r\n\r\n```\r\nrequests.exceptions.SSLError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='servicios1.afip.gov.ar', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: /wsfev1/service.asmx?WSDL (Caused by SSLError(SSLCertVerificationError(1, '[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: unable to get local issuer certificate (_ssl.c:1000)\r\n```\r\n\r\n## System Information\r\n\r\n    $ python -m requests.help\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"chardet\": {\r\n    \"version\": null\r\n  },\r\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.3.2\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"cryptography\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"39.0.2\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"idna\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.7\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"implementation\": {\r\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\r\n    \"version\": \"3.12.3\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"platform\": {\r\n    \"release\": \"6.9.3-0-edge\",\r\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\r\n    \"openssl_version\": \"30000080\",\r\n    \"version\": \"23.2.0\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"requests\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"2.32.3\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"system_ssl\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"30300000\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"urllib3\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"2.2.1\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\r\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": true\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n<!-- This command is only available on Requests v2.16.4 and greater. Otherwise,\r\nplease provide some basic information about your system (Python version,\r\noperating system, &c). -->\r\n\r\n## Further context\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/psf/requests/pull/6710#issuecomment-2137799723\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "nateprewitt", "timestamp": "2024-06-07T16:04:37Z", "body": "Hi @WhyNotHugo, I think this is a duplicate of #6730 which was opened after your original comment. We'll track progress there.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "alesisjoan", "timestamp": "2024-06-27T12:25:16Z", "body": "@WhyNotHugo did you solve it ? I'm struggling with the same\r\n\r\n```\r\nurllib3 = \"^1.26.14\"\r\nfintech = \"^7.5.1\"\r\npyopenssl = \"^22.0.0\"\r\ncryptography = \"^35.0.0\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nsince I updated cryptography", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "WhyNotHugo", "timestamp": "2024-07-03T15:09:01Z", "body": "@alesisjoan Either use dependency `\"requests!=2.32.0,!=2.32.1,!=2.32.2\",` or explicitly load default certs: https://github.com/WhyNotHugo/django-afip/blob/8ea6dbd79d9cfe52409d9bbf3d61069ea8efbdb0/django_afip/clients.py#L44-L57", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "alesisjoan", "timestamp": "2024-07-04T15:17:26Z", "body": "Hi Hugo!\r\nYep, it was something with requests library.\r\n\r\nIn my case it was using 2.32 so I force a  downgrade to 2.25.1\r\n\r\nMuchas gracias!\r\n\r\nEl mi\u00e9., 3 de julio de 2024 12:09, Hugo ***@***.***> escribi\u00f3:\r\n\r\n> @alesisjoan <https://github.com/alesisjoan> Either use dependency\r\n> \"requests!=2.32.0,!=2.32.1,!=2.32.2\", or explicitly load default certs:\r\n> https://github.com/WhyNotHugo/django-afip/blob/8ea6dbd79d9cfe52409d9bbf3d61069ea8efbdb0/django_afip/clients.py#L44-L57\r\n>\r\n> \u2014\r\n> Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\r\n> <https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/6736#issuecomment-2206473797>, or\r\n> unsubscribe\r\n> <https://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/ABFXOPDLYUMY5EZOR2NEJSDZKQH2JAVCNFSM6AAAAABI6OTL66VHI2DSMVQWIX3LMV43OSLTON2WKQ3PNVWWK3TUHMZDEMBWGQ3TGNZZG4>\r\n> .\r\n> You are receiving this because you were mentioned.Message ID:\r\n> ***@***.***>\r\n>\r\n", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "psf/requests/issue/6736#1", "question": "In psf/requests, why does requests 2.32.3/2.32.2 no longer load system CA certificates when I use a custom HTTPS adapter with an explicit urllib3 SSL context, and what should I do?\n\nGiven this snippet:\n\n```py\nfrom requests.adapters import HTTPAdapter\nfrom requests import Session\nfrom urllib3 import PoolManager\nfrom urllib3 import ProxyManager\nfrom urllib3.util.ssl_ import create_urllib3_context\n\n\nclass AFIPAdapter(HTTPAdapter):\n    \"\"\"An adapter with reduced security so it'll work with AFIP.\"\"\"\n\n    def init_poolmanager(self, *args, **kwargs) -> PoolManager:\n        context = create_urllib3_context(ciphers=\"AES128-SHA\")\n        # context.load_default_certs()\n        kwargs[\"ssl_context\"] = context\n        return super().init_poolmanager(*args, **kwargs)\n\n    def proxy_manager_for(self, *args, **kwargs) -> ProxyManager:\n        context = create_urllib3_context(ciphers=\"AES128-SHA\")\n        # context.load_default_certs()\n        kwargs[\"ssl_context\"] = context\n        return super().proxy_manager_for(*args, **kwargs)\n\n\nsession = Session()\nsession.mount(\"https://servicios1.afip.gov.ar\", AFIPAdapter())\nsession.get(\"https://servicios1.afip.gov.ar/wsfev1/service.asmx?WSDL\")\n```\n\nOn `requests==2.31.0`, this works fine, with system CAs loaded by default. As of `2.32.2`, the system CAs are no longer loaded by default, and the commented `context.load_default_certs()` lines need to be included too.\n\nExpected result: the snippet should work and system CAs should be loaded.\n\nActual result:\n\n```text\nrequests.exceptions.SSLError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='servicios1.afip.gov.ar', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: /wsfev1/service.asmx?WSDL (Caused by SSLError(SSLCertVerificationError(1, '[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: unable to get local issuer certificate (_ssl.c:1000)\n```\n\nSystem information from `python -m requests.help`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"chardet\": {\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.3.2\"\n  },\n  \"cryptography\": {\n    \"version\": \"39.0.2\"\n  },\n  \"idna\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.7\"\n  },\n  \"implementation\": {\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\n    \"version\": \"3.12.3\"\n  },\n  \"platform\": {\n    \"release\": \"6.9.3-0-edge\",\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\n  },\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\n    \"openssl_version\": \"30000080\",\n    \"version\": \"23.2.0\"\n  },\n  \"requests\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.32.3\"\n  },\n  \"system_ssl\": {\n    \"version\": \"30300000\"\n  },\n  \"urllib3\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.2.1\"\n  },\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": true\n}\n```\n\nFurther context was linked to `https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/6710#issuecomment-2137799723`.", "answer": "Either use dependency `\"requests!=2.32.0,!=2.32.1,!=2.32.2\",` or explicitly load default certs: https://github.com/WhyNotHugo/django-afip/blob/8ea6dbd79d9cfe52409d9bbf3d61069ea8efbdb0/django_afip/clients.py#L44-L57", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["psf/requests issue #6730", "requests!=2.32.0,!=2.32.1,!=2.32.2", "https://github.com/WhyNotHugo/django-afip/blob/8ea6dbd79d9cfe52409d9bbf3d61069ea8efbdb0/django_afip/clients.py#L44-L57"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1, c3", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["8ea6dbd79d9cfe52409d9bbf3d61069ea8efbdb0", "https://github.com/WhyNotHugo/django-afip/blob/8ea6dbd79d9cfe52409d9bbf3d61069ea8efbdb0/django_afip/clients.py#L44-L57"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:32:56.216010Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "psf/requests/issue/6733", "repo": "psf/requests", "number": 6733, "title": "Deprecated `HTTPAdapter.get_connection()` method is never called, causing breakage without deprecation warnings", "url": "https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/6733", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2024-06-04T01:04:34Z", "closed_at": "2024-06-06T15:56:32Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "wgreenberg", "security_topic": "breaking change due to security fix for CVE-2024-35195", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know that the breakage was caused by the security fix for CVE-2024-35195 and that the backward-compatible workaround is provided in PR #6710.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "wgreenberg", "answer_author": "nateprewitt", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "pr_data", "documentation", "advisory"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": ["CVE-2024-35195"], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": ["#6655", "#6710"], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Accepted because the issue is a valid security-driven API regression and the answer correctly links it to the CVE fix and its compatibility workaround.", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "wgreenberg", "timestamp": "2024-06-04T01:04:34Z", "body": "After upgrading several of certbot's packages, including `requests` to 2.32.3, we suddenly started experiencing confusing DNS resolution errors with no clear cause. Only after bisecting the changed packages and isolating `requests` as the source of the trouble did we see that 2.32.2 deprecated the `HTTPAdapter.get_connection()` method we used.\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, the change which deprecated `get_connection()` also causes it never to be called by `Session`, so the `DeprecationWarning` added in https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/6710 will never be printed. If `Session` perhaps checked if `get_connection()` was defined and called it, the warning would've printed and would've saved us quite a lot of time tracking down the issue.\r\n\r\nI also generally wouldn't expect a minor version upgrade in a dependency to cause a breaking change, so this result was extra confusing.\r\n\r\n## Expected Result\r\n\r\n`HTTPAdapter.get_connection()` to still be called, resulting in no breaking changes and giving users the deprecation warning. Or at the very least, check if `get_connection` is defined and print the deprecation warning then.\r\n\r\n## Actual Result\r\n\r\n`HTTPAdapter.get_connection()` is silently left uncalled, causing a breakage.", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "nateprewitt", "timestamp": "2024-06-04T04:16:06Z", "body": "Hi @wgreenberg, the commit you linked appears to be part way through the issue. #6710 was to provide a public API for users who were overriding `get_connection`. The initial breakage came from #6655 to address CVE-2024-35195.\n\nWhile we don't generally make breaking changes in minor patch versions, users were unknowingly exposing data over unverified connections. We chose to patch the API but it appears docker and certbot are two cases that were negatively impacted.\n\nThere were a few misses in messaging around this change because impact wasn't assessed completely prior to releasing 2.32.0. The workaround provided in #6710 will be the backwards compatible middle ground to keep adapters using a custom `get_connection` working with the least amount of work going forward.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "nateprewitt", "timestamp": "2024-06-06T15:56:32Z", "body": "I'm going to resolve this since we haven't heard back anything else. Let us know if you have any other questions.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "psf/requests/issue/6733#1", "question": "After upgrading several of certbot's packages, including `requests` to 2.32.3, we started experiencing confusing DNS resolution errors with no clear cause. After bisecting, we isolated `requests` as the source and saw that 2.32.2 deprecated the `HTTPAdapter.get_connection()` method we used.\n\nThe change that deprecated `get_connection()` also causes it never to be called by `Session`, so the `DeprecationWarning` added in https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/6710 is never printed. I would expect a minor version upgrade not to cause a breaking change.\n\nExpected result: `HTTPAdapter.get_connection()` should still be called, resulting in no breaking changes and giving users the deprecation warning; or at least `Session` should check if `get_connection` is defined and print the deprecation warning then.\n\nActual result: `HTTPAdapter.get_connection()` is silently left uncalled, causing breakage.\n\nWhy did this breaking behavior happen, and what is the intended workaround for users overriding `HTTPAdapter.get_connection()`?", "answer": "The maintainer said the linked PR #6710 was only partway through the issue: it provided a public API for users overriding `get_connection`, while the initial breakage came from #6655, which addressed CVE-2024-35195. Requests generally avoids breaking changes in minor patch versions, but this change was made because users were unknowingly exposing data over unverified connections, and the impact was not fully assessed before 2.32.0. The workaround provided in #6710 is the backwards-compatible middle ground to keep adapters using a custom `get_connection` working with the least amount of work going forward.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["PR #6710", "PR #6655", "CVE-2024-35195", "requests 2.32.0"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["#6655", "#6710", "CVE-2024-35195"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:34:51.673800Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "psf/requests/issue/6726", "repo": "psf/requests", "number": 6726, "title": "Multiple concurrent client certs broken with v2.32.3", "url": "https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/6726", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2024-05-29T19:20:08Z", "closed_at": "2026-02-13T03:01:47Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "jeffreytolar", "security_topic": "client certificate leakage due to shared SSL context in concurrent requests", "qa_summary": "The problem stems from the shared SSL context being mutated during concurrent client\u2011certificate requests, and the fix is provided in commit fe251aa94b2b7849e224e455c0aad0df53ad3d8e which disables the default context when certs are present.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "jeffreytolar", "answer_author": "nateprewitt", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "commit_history", "issue_tracker"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": ["fe251aa94b2b7849e224e455c0aad0df53ad3d8e"], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Accepted because the issue is a valid SSL context isolation/security regression and the answer provides a concrete fix commit addressing the certificate handling bug.", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "jeffreytolar", "timestamp": "2024-05-29T19:20:08Z", "body": "We use `requests` with multiple mTLS client certificates - each certificate is signed by the same CA, but they have different subjects - each subject has different permissions. Each distinct client cert is used by a different `requests.Session`\r\nAdditionally, we make use of `ThreadPoolExecutor` to make many requests in parallel.\r\nWhen client certs are in use, urllib3 will [load the cert into the SSL context](https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/blob/b07a669bd970d69847801148286b726f0570b625/src/urllib3/util/ssl_.py#L454-L458), which, with concurrent requests, will cause the shared SSL context to get modified while it's in use.\r\n\r\nThe reproducer actually fails with an exception - when we first encountered this, we were seeing the wrong certs get used. (That was with different versions of python and openssl, however, and as mentioned above, they're all signed by the same CA, unlike the reproducer)\r\n\r\n\r\n## Expected Result\r\n\r\nThe reproducer below, when run with `requests-2.31.0`:\r\n```\r\n$ ./bin/python3 test.py\r\n/tmp/py/test.py:24: DeprecationWarning: X509Extension support in pyOpenSSL is deprecated. You should use the APIs in cryptography.\r\n  crypto.X509Extension(b\"subjectAltName\", False, b\"DNS:localhost, IP:127.0.0.1\")\r\n/tmp/py/test.py:50: DeprecationWarning: ssl.PROTOCOL_TLSv1_2 is deprecated\r\n  self.ssl_context = ssl.SSLContext(ssl.PROTOCOL_TLSv1_2)\r\nServer started on port 8443...\r\n  OK client2: CN: client2 / URL: /client2\r\n  OK client3: CN: client3 / URL: /client3\r\n  OK client1: CN: client1 / URL: /client1\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Actual Result\r\n\r\nWhen run with `requests-2.32.3`:\r\n```\r\n$ ./bin/python3 test.py\r\n/tmp/py/test.py:24: DeprecationWarning: X509Extension support in pyOpenSSL is deprecated. You should use the APIs in cryptography.\r\n  crypto.X509Extension(b\"subjectAltName\", False, b\"DNS:localhost, IP:127.0.0.1\")\r\n/tmp/py/test.py:50: DeprecationWarning: ssl.PROTOCOL_TLSv1_2 is deprecated\r\n  self.ssl_context = ssl.SSLContext(ssl.PROTOCOL_TLSv1_2)\r\nServer started on port 8443...\r\nFAIL client1: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='127.0.0.1', port=8443): Max retries exceeded with url: /client1 (Caused by SSLError(SSLError(116, '[X509: KEY_VALUES_MISMATCH] key values mismatch (_ssl.c:3926)')))\r\nFAIL client3: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='127.0.0.1', port=8443): Max retries exceeded with url: /client3 (Caused by SSLError(SSLError(116, '[X509: KEY_VALUES_MISMATCH] key values mismatch (_ssl.c:3926)')))\r\n  OK client2: CN: client2 / URL: /client2\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Reproduction Steps\r\n\r\nGist: https://gist.github.com/jeffreytolar/ea05b3092df12dc6e5b518e58e6821ad ; this generates a few sets of key/certs, hackily sets the default CA bundle, and then makes a few concurrent requests, each using a distinct client cert.\r\n\r\nWith:\r\n```\r\n$ pip freeze\r\ncertifi==2024.2.2\r\ncffi==1.16.0\r\ncharset-normalizer==3.3.2\r\ncryptography==42.0.7\r\nidna==3.7\r\npycparser==2.22\r\npyOpenSSL==24.1.0\r\nrequests==2.32.3\r\nurllib3==2.2.1\r\n```\r\n(top level dependencies are `pyOpenSSL` and `requests`)\r\n\r\n## System Information\r\n\r\n    $ python -m requests.help\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"chardet\": {\r\n    \"version\": null\r\n  },\r\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.3.2\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"cryptography\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"42.0.7\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"idna\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.7\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"implementation\": {\r\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\r\n    \"version\": \"3.11.8\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"platform\": {\r\n    \"release\": \"5.15.0-105-generic\",\r\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\r\n    \"openssl_version\": \"30200010\",\r\n    \"version\": \"24.1.0\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"requests\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"2.32.3\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"system_ssl\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"300000d0\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"urllib3\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"2.2.1\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\r\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": true\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n<!-- This command is only available on Requests v2.16.4 and greater. Otherwise,\r\nplease provide some basic information about your system (Python version,\r\noperating system, &c). -->\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "nateprewitt", "timestamp": "2024-05-29T20:28:26Z", "body": "Thanks for the report, @jeffreytolar. It does looks like we're not checking the certs provided by the Session before opting into the default context, I've put together fe251aa94b2b7849e224e455c0aad0df53ad3d8e to disable the default context when certs are present.\r\n\r\nHowever, with testing I'm seeing an exception with the cert being self-signed that wasn't present in 2.31.0. I'm looking into that further but would you mind checking the above patch against your current setup so we can decouple the two issues. If your issue is persisting after we've moved the default context out of the hot path, there may be something else at play with the recent CVE fix.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "jeffreytolar", "timestamp": "2024-05-29T22:53:28Z", "body": "So far it's looking like that patch is working in our main setup - thanks for the quick commit!\r\n\r\nFor the self-signed issue still in the reproducer, I think it's that 2.32.x isn't passing a CA bundle to urllib3, whereas 2.31 did that here: https://github.com/psf/requests/blob/147c8511ddbfa5e8f71bbf5c18ede0c4ceb3bba4/requests/adapters.py#L257-L258 ; that causes urllib3 to load the OS default, rather than using `certifi`\r\n\r\nTo restore the v2.31 behavior, I think maybe a `elif verify is True: pool_kwargs[\"ca_certs (or ca_cert_dir)\"] = extract_zipped_paths(DEFAULT_CA_BUNDLE_PATH)` might work in `_urllib3_request_context` ?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "sigmavirus24", "timestamp": "2024-05-29T23:16:48Z", "body": "> So far it's looking like that patch is working in our main setup - thanks for the quick commit!\n> \n> For the self-signed issue still in the reproducer, I think it's that 2.32.x isn't passing a CA bundle to urllib3, whereas 2.31 did that here: https://github.com/psf/requests/blob/147c8511ddbfa5e8f71bbf5c18ede0c4ceb3bba4/requests/adapters.py#L257-L258 ; that causes urllib3 to load the OS default, rather than using `certifi`\n> \n> To restore the v2.31 behavior, I think maybe a `elif verify is True: pool_kwargs[\"ca_certs (or ca_cert_dir)\"] = extract_zipped_paths(DEFAULT_CA_BUNDLE_PATH)` might work in `_urllib3_request_context` ?\n\nYeah, I thought the optimization broke the zipped paths extraction but couldn't prove it easily ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "nateprewitt", "timestamp": "2024-05-29T23:19:52Z", "body": "Ok, so that's the same issue reported here (https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/6710#issuecomment-2137095349) this morning. That explains why calling `load_default_certs` on the SSLContext fixes it.\r\n\r\nLet me take a closer look tomorrow, I'm a little worried if we do it only for `verify is True` that we'll start the whole custom SSLContext issue over again. Thanks for pointing that out, @jeffreytolar!", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "stackp", "timestamp": "2025-02-03T10:48:26Z", "body": "We got hit by this bug, it can happen  in non-concurrent code. Once a client certificate has been passed, it keeps being used in subsequent calls. Possibly in totally unrelated code and libraries.\n\n```python\nres_1 = requests.post(url_1, json=data, cert=cert)\nres_2 = requests.post(url_2, json=data)  # <-- subsequent calls keep using `cert` \n```\n\nMy guess is a global state being mutated in the library. It might be related to this line in the 2.32.0 changelog, but we did not investigate further.\n\n> * verify=True now reuses a global SSLContext which should improve request time variance between first and subsequent requests.\n\nRolling back to `requests-2.31.0` fixes it for now.\n\nThank you for your work!", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "blueflyingpanda", "timestamp": "2025-02-15T00:25:34Z", "body": "> We got hit by this bug, it can happen in non-concurrent code. Once a client certificate has been passed, it keeps being used in subsequent calls. Possibly in totally unrelated code and libraries.\n> \n> res_1 = requests.post(url_1, json=data, cert=cert)\n> res_2 = requests.post(url_2, json=data)  # <-- subsequent calls keep using `cert` \n> My guess is a global state being mutated in the library. It might be related to this line in the 2.32.0 changelog, but we did not investigate further.\n> \n> > * verify=True now reuses a global SSLContext which should improve request time variance between first and subsequent requests.\n> \n> Rolling back to `requests-2.31.0` fixes it for now.\n> \n> Thank you for your work!\n\nI've encountered this bug as well. Here\u2019s what I\u2019ve discovered so far:\n\nIn `requests-2.31.0`, `SSLContext` was created dynamically:\n[urllib3/connection.py#L755-L765](https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/blob/b07a669bd970d69847801148286b726f0570b625/src/urllib3/connection.py#L755C5-L765)\n\nHowever, in `requests-2.32.3`, `_preloaded_ssl_context` is used instead:\n- `_preloaded_ssl_context` creation: [requests/adapters.py#L80](https://github.com/psf/requests/blob/1b7c37ef4e3db003cc52f09da9ab6b947054e245/src/requests/adapters.py#L80)\n- Applied conditionally: [requests/adapters.py#L114](https://github.com/psf/requests/blob/1b7c37ef4e3db003cc52f09da9ab6b947054e245/src/requests/adapters.py#L114)\n\nThe key issue is that once a client certificate is loaded into `_preloaded_ssl_context`, it persists for all subsequent requests\u2014even if no certificate is provided. This appears to be an unintended side effect of global state reuse.\n\nTo work around this, I implemented a custom `HTTPAdapter`:\n\n### Option 1: Override `get_connection_with_tls_context`\nThis prevents unwanted certificate reuse but relies on a deprecated method:\n```python\nclass CustomHTTPAdapter(HTTPAdapter):\n\n    def get_connection_with_tls_context(self, request, verify, proxies=None, cert=None):\n        # keep in mind .get_connection() is now deprecated\n        return self.get_connection(request.url, proxies)\n```\n\n### Option 2: Reset SSLContext\nThis explicitly resets the SSLContext to avoid persistent client certificates:\n```python\nclass CustomHTTPAdapter(HTTPAdapter):\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _get_reset_ssl_context() -> ssl.SSLContext:\n        # Create a fresh SSLContext and load CA certificates\n        ssl_context = create_urllib3_context()\n        ssl_context.load_verify_locations(\n            extract_zipped_paths(DEFAULT_CA_BUNDLE_PATH)\n        )\n        return ssl_context\n\n    def init_poolmanager(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        kwargs['ssl_context'] = self._get_reset_ssl_context()\n        return super().init_poolmanager(*args, **kwargs)\n\n    def proxy_manager_for(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        kwargs['ssl_context'] = self._get_reset_ssl_context()\n        return super().proxy_manager_for(*args, **kwargs)\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "the-infinity", "timestamp": "2025-02-21T19:51:10Z", "body": "We also got hit by the bug. in an open source parking data project.\n\nExample code: We expect an 403 at both requests to https://certauth.cryptomix.com/json, as there is no cert in this request. With requests 2.31.0, I get an 403 at both requests. At 2.32.2 or 2.32.3, I get an \"found client cert\", which means that the client cert was sent. If you have worker services and a data gathering systems where just a few of the data sources use client certs, this ends up into very strange \"sometimes it fails\" issues.\n\nHere's my isolation:\n\n```\ndef main():\n    print('sending request without client cert')\n    requests.get('https://binary-butterfly.de')\n\n    print('sending request to cert check service')\n    response = requests.get('https://certauth.cryptomix.com/json')\n    print(f'Status code: {response.status_code}')\n\n    print('sending request with client cert')\n    requests.get('https://binary-butterfly.de', cert=('certs/client.crt', 'certs/client.key'))\n\n    print('sending request to cert check service again')\n    response = requests.get('https://certauth.cryptomix.com/json')\n    print(f'Status code: {response.status_code}')\n    if response.status_code == 200:\n        print(f'found client cert \"{response.json()[\"SSL_CLIENT_S_DN_O\"]}\"')\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n``` \n\n@blueflyingpanda s solution works (thank you for this!), but means quite some change if you don't use sessions yet (was not necessary so far). Would be great to resolve this in general. Thank you!", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c8", "author": "Conobi", "timestamp": "2025-06-03T10:19:21Z", "body": "The #6767 PR \\*should\\* fix that issue when merged.\nMaybe writing adding some tests to the Requests library to track this issue would be appropriate?", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "psf/requests/issue/6726#1", "question": "In psf/requests, `requests` 2.32.3 appears to break use of multiple concurrent mTLS client certificates. We use multiple client certificates, all signed by the same CA but with different subjects and different permissions; each distinct client cert is used by a different `requests.Session`. We also use `ThreadPoolExecutor` to make many requests in parallel. When client certs are in use, urllib3 loads the cert into the SSL context, which with concurrent requests can cause the shared SSL context to be modified while it is in use. In our real environment we saw the wrong certs get used; in the reproducer below it fails with an exception.\n\nWith `requests-2.31.0`, the reproducer at https://gist.github.com/jeffreytolar/ea05b3092df12dc6e5b518e58e6821ad outputs:\n\n```\n$ ./bin/python3 test.py\n/tmp/py/test.py:24: DeprecationWarning: X509Extension support in pyOpenSSL is deprecated. You should use the APIs in cryptography.\n  crypto.X509Extension(b\"subjectAltName\", False, b\"DNS:localhost, IP:127.0.0.1\")\n/tmp/py/test.py:50: DeprecationWarning: ssl.PROTOCOL_TLSv1_2 is deprecated\n  self.ssl_context = ssl.SSLContext(ssl.PROTOCOL_TLSv1_2)\nServer started on port 8443...\n  OK client2: CN: client2 / URL: /client2\n  OK client3: CN: client3 / URL: /client3\n  OK client1: CN: client1 / URL: /client1\n```\n\nWith `requests-2.32.3`, it outputs:\n\n```\n$ ./bin/python3 test.py\n/tmp/py/test.py:24: DeprecationWarning: X509Extension support in pyOpenSSL is deprecated. You should use the APIs in cryptography.\n  crypto.X509Extension(b\"subjectAltName\", False, b\"DNS:localhost, IP:127.0.0.1\")\n/tmp/py/test.py:50: DeprecationWarning: ssl.PROTOCOL_TLSv1_2 is deprecated\n  self.ssl_context = ssl.SSLContext(ssl.PROTOCOL_TLSv1_2)\nServer started on port 8443...\nFAIL client1: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='127.0.0.1', port=8443): Max retries exceeded with url: /client1 (Caused by SSLError(SSLError(116, '[X509: KEY_VALUES_MISMATCH] key values mismatch (_ssl.c:3926)')))\nFAIL client3: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='127.0.0.1', port=8443): Max retries exceeded with url: /client3 (Caused by SSLError(SSLError(116, '[X509: KEY_VALUES_MISMATCH] key values mismatch (_ssl.c:3926)')))\n  OK client2: CN: client2 / URL: /client2\n```\n\nThe reproducer generates a few sets of key/certs, hackily sets the default CA bundle, and then makes a few concurrent requests, each using a distinct client cert. Dependencies are:\n\n```\n$ pip freeze\ncertifi==2024.2.2\ncffi==1.16.0\ncharset-normalizer==3.3.2\ncryptography==42.0.7\nidna==3.7\npycparser==2.22\npyOpenSSL==24.1.0\nrequests==2.32.3\nurllib3==2.2.1\n```\n\n`python -m requests.help` reports:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"chardet\": {\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.3.2\"\n  },\n  \"cryptography\": {\n    \"version\": \"42.0.7\"\n  },\n  \"idna\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.7\"\n  },\n  \"implementation\": {\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\n    \"version\": \"3.11.8\"\n  },\n  \"platform\": {\n    \"release\": \"5.15.0-105-generic\",\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\n  },\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\n    \"openssl_version\": \"30200010\",\n    \"version\": \"24.1.0\"\n  },\n  \"requests\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.32.3\"\n  },\n  \"system_ssl\": {\n    \"version\": \"300000d0\"\n  },\n  \"urllib3\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.2.1\"\n  },\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": true\n}\n```\n\nIs this a Requests regression around the shared/default SSL context when client certs are provided, and is there a patch or intended fix?", "answer": "The maintainer confirmed that Requests was not checking the certs provided by the Session before opting into the default context. They put together commit `fe251aa94b2b7849e224e455c0aad0df53ad3d8e` to disable the default context when certs are present, and asked the reporter to test it. They also noted a separate self-signed-cert exception seen during testing that might relate to the recent CVE fix.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["commit fe251aa94b2b7849e224e455c0aad0df53ad3d8e"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}, {"qid": "psf/requests/issue/6726#3", "question": "In Requests 2.32.0 through 2.32.3, can the client-certificate reuse bug happen even in non-concurrent code? For example, after one request passes a client certificate, subsequent unrelated calls without `cert` still seem to use it:\n\n```python\nres_1 = requests.post(url_1, json=data, cert=cert)\nres_2 = requests.post(url_2, json=data)  # <-- subsequent calls keep using `cert` \n```\n\nThis may be related to the Requests 2.32.0 changelog entry: `verify=True now reuses a global SSLContext which should improve request time variance between first and subsequent requests.` Rolling back to `requests-2.31.0` fixes it. Is there a general Requests fix expected for this issue?", "answer": "A community responder said PR `#6767` should fix that issue when merged, and suggested adding tests to Requests to track it.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["PR #6767"], "answer_grounded_in": "c8", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["fe251aa94b2b7849e224e455c0aad0df53ad3d8e"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:40:03.323318Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "psf/requests/issue/6717", "repo": "psf/requests", "number": 6717, "title": "SSLCertVerificationError - unable to get local issuer certificate", "url": "https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/6717", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2024-05-22T14:29:33Z", "closed_at": "2024-05-22T14:59:00Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "DanSIntel", "security_topic": "using load_default_certs with requests 2.32.x SSLContext changes", "qa_summary": "The developer needed the proper method to load default system CA certificates in Requests\u202f2.32.x, and the answer provides a concrete code solution using a custom SSLContext in the adapter, citing the Requests adapters source (adapters.py line\u202f95\u201196) and the related PR\u202f#6716.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c3", "question_author": "DanSIntel", "answer_author": "nateprewitt", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": ["88dce9d854797c05d0ff296b70e0430535ef8aaf"], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Accepted because the question concerns a legitimate SSLContext behavior change and the answer provides a correct adapter-based fix aligned with Requests internals.", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "DanSIntel", "timestamp": "2024-05-22T14:29:33Z", "body": "Version **2.32.0** introduced changes and improvements with **SSLContext** as specified in the release history:\r\n\r\nImprovements - verify=True now reuses a global SSLContext which should improve request time variance between first and subsequent requests. It should also minimize certificate load time on Windows systems when using a Python version built with OpenSSL 3.x. (#6667)\r\n\r\n\r\nWe are facing issue making http requests to webservers which are signed by a local root ca.\r\nThe certificate chain is installed correctly on the Windows station and **version 2.31.1 is working as expected**.\r\n\r\nVersions **2.32.x** are throwing an error:  SSLError(SSLCertVerificationError(1, '[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: unable to get local issuer certificate (_ssl.c:1125)')))\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThis is a code example that works in 2.31.1 and does not in 2.32.x\r\n\r\n\r\n```\r\nfrom requests.adapters import HTTPAdapter\r\nimport requests\r\nfrom requests.packages.urllib3.util.ssl_ import create_urllib3_context\r\n\r\nclass SSLContextAdapter(HTTPAdapter):\r\n    def init_poolmanager(self, *args, **kwargs):\r\n        context = create_urllib3_context()\r\n        context.load_default_certs()  # this loads the system's CA certificates\r\n        kwargs['ssl_context'] = context\r\n        return super().init_poolmanager(*args, **kwargs)\r\n\r\nSESSION = requests.Session()\r\nSESSION.mount('https://', SSLContextAdapter())\r\nSESSION.get(MY_URL_SIGNED_BY_LOCAL_ROOT_CA, headers = headers, verify  = True)\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAfter looking at the lastest changes, if we modifiy our code its working but i dont think that calling the private global **_preloaded_ssl_context** is the right way:\r\n\r\n```\r\nfrom requests.adapters import HTTPAdapter, _preloaded_ssl_context\r\nimport requests\r\n\r\n\r\nclass SSLContextAdapter(HTTPAdapter):\r\n    def init_poolmanager(self, *args, **kwargs):\r\n        _preloaded_ssl_context.load_default_certs() # this loads the system's CA certificates\r\n        return super().init_poolmanager(*args, **kwargs)\r\n\r\nSESSION = requests.Session()\r\nSESSION.mount('https://', SSLContextAdapter())\r\nSESSION.get(MY_URL_SIGNED_BY_LOCAL_ROOT_CA, headers = headers, verify  = True)\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\nWhat is the recommended way for using load_default_certs() with version 2.32.x if the usage has changed or alternatively can you confirm if this is a bug?\r\n\r\n\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "nateprewitt", "timestamp": "2024-05-22T14:50:53Z", "body": "Hi @DanSIntel, this looks like the same root issue described in #6715. Can you take a look at the proposed patch in #6716 and let us know if the new API proposal meets your use case. From the code you provided, I would think you should be able to do this through `pool_kwargs`.\r\n\r\nI'd propose we close this as a duplicate of #6715 if the above is agreeable and we'll track progress in the PR.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "DanSIntel", "timestamp": "2024-05-22T15:03:50Z", "body": "@nateprewitt can you provide an example how using the changes in #6716 can work with my custom sslcontext adapter?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "nateprewitt", "timestamp": "2024-05-22T15:29:47Z", "body": "Do the same thing you're doing now but in your adapters `__init__`. Then you can patch the return value the same way we're using _preloaded_ssl_context now ([ref](https://github.com/psf/requests/blob/88dce9d854797c05d0ff296b70e0430535ef8aaf/src/requests/adapters.py#L95-L96)).\r\n\r\nSomething like this:\r\n```python\r\nclass SSLContextAdapter(HTTPAdapter):\r\n    def __init__(\r\n        self,\r\n        pool_connections=DEFAULT_POOLSIZE,\r\n        pool_maxsize=DEFAULT_POOLSIZE,\r\n        max_retries=DEFAULT_RETRIES,\r\n        pool_block=DEFAULT_POOLBLOCK,\r\n    ):\r\n        super().__init__()\r\n        self.custom_context = create_urllib3_context()\r\n        # Any cert modifications can be done here (if you need this per request,\r\n        # do it in the build_connection_pool_key_attributes below.)\r\n        self.custom_context.load_default_certs()\r\n    \r\n    [...]\r\n    \r\n    def build_connection_pool_key_attributes(self, request, verify, cert=None):\r\n        host_params, pool_kwargs = super().build_connection_pool_key_attributes(request, verify, cert)\r\n        pool_kwargs['ssl_context'] = self.custom_context  # you can put this behind a verify is True conditional too\r\n        return host_params, pool_kwargs\r\n```", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "psf/requests/issue/6717#1", "question": "In psf/requests, version 2.32.0 introduced SSLContext changes from the release history: \"verify=True now reuses a global SSLContext which should improve request time variance between first and subsequent requests. It should also minimize certificate load time on Windows systems when using a Python version built with OpenSSL 3.x. (#6667)\".\n\nWe are making HTTPS requests on Windows to webservers signed by a local root CA. The certificate chain is installed correctly on the Windows station, and requests 2.31.1 works as expected. With requests 2.32.x, we get:\n\n`SSLError(SSLCertVerificationError(1, '[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: unable to get local issuer certificate (_ssl.c:1125)')))`\n\nThis code works in 2.31.1 but not in 2.32.x:\n\n```python\nfrom requests.adapters import HTTPAdapter\nimport requests\nfrom requests.packages.urllib3.util.ssl_ import create_urllib3_context\n\nclass SSLContextAdapter(HTTPAdapter):\n    def init_poolmanager(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        context = create_urllib3_context()\n        context.load_default_certs()  # this loads the system's CA certificates\n        kwargs['ssl_context'] = context\n        return super().init_poolmanager(*args, **kwargs)\n\nSESSION = requests.Session()\nSESSION.mount('https://', SSLContextAdapter())\nSESSION.get(MY_URL_SIGNED_BY_LOCAL_ROOT_CA, headers = headers, verify  = True)\n```\n\nIf we modify the code to call the private global `_preloaded_ssl_context`, it works, but that does not seem like the right approach:\n\n```python\nfrom requests.adapters import HTTPAdapter, _preloaded_ssl_context\nimport requests\n\n\nclass SSLContextAdapter(HTTPAdapter):\n    def init_poolmanager(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        _preloaded_ssl_context.load_default_certs() # this loads the system's CA certificates\n        return super().init_poolmanager(*args, **kwargs)\n\nSESSION = requests.Session()\nSESSION.mount('https://', SSLContextAdapter())\nSESSION.get(MY_URL_SIGNED_BY_LOCAL_ROOT_CA, headers = headers, verify  = True)\n```\n\nWhat is the recommended way to use `load_default_certs()` with a custom `HTTPAdapter` in requests 2.32.x, especially using the proposed changes in PR #6716 with a custom SSLContext adapter?", "answer": "Create and modify the custom SSL context in the adapter\u2019s `__init__`, then override `build_connection_pool_key_attributes` and put that context into `pool_kwargs['ssl_context']`. The maintainer suggested using the same pattern as Requests\u2019 `_preloaded_ssl_context` handling, optionally only applying it when `verify is True`.\n\nSomething like this:\n```python\nclass SSLContextAdapter(HTTPAdapter):\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        pool_connections=DEFAULT_POOLSIZE,\n        pool_maxsize=DEFAULT_POOLSIZE,\n        max_retries=DEFAULT_RETRIES,\n        pool_block=DEFAULT_POOLBLOCK,\n    ):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.custom_context = create_urllib3_context()\n        # Any cert modifications can be done here (if you need this per request,\n        # do it in the build_connection_pool_key_attributes below.)\n        self.custom_context.load_default_certs()\n    \n    [...]\n    \n    def build_connection_pool_key_attributes(self, request, verify, cert=None):\n        host_params, pool_kwargs = super().build_connection_pool_key_attributes(request, verify, cert)\n        pool_kwargs['ssl_context'] = self.custom_context  # you can put this behind a verify is True conditional too\n        return host_params, pool_kwargs\n```", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["PR #6716", "https://github.com/psf/requests/blob/88dce9d854797c05d0ff296b70e0430535ef8aaf/src/requests/adapters.py#L95-L96"], "answer_grounded_in": "c3", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["88dce9d854797c05d0ff296b70e0430535ef8aaf", "https://github.com/psf/requests/blob/88dce9d854797c05d0ff296b70e0430535ef8aaf/src/requests/adapters.py#L95-L96"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:43:29.253864Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "psf/requests/issue/6690", "repo": "psf/requests", "number": 6690, "title": "Upgrade idna to 3.7 for CVE-2024-3651", "url": "https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/6690", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2024-04-26T13:20:01Z", "closed_at": "2024-04-26T14:05:34Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "carlos-villavicencio-adsk", "security_topic": "dependency upgrade to mitigate CVE-2024-3651", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know if upgrading idna to version\u202f3.7 would eliminate CVE\u20112024\u20113651, and the maintainer confirmed that using idna>=3.7 is allowed and a reinstall will pull that version (see setup.cfg requirement).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "carlos-villavicencio-adsk", "answer_author": "sigmavirus24", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["dependency_manifest", "advisory", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": ["CVE-2024-3651"], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": ["3.7"], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "carlos-villavicencio-adsk", "timestamp": "2024-04-26T13:20:01Z", "body": "<!-- Summary. -->\r\nNow that idna released [3.7](https://github.com/kjd/idna/releases/tag/v3.7), can you consider bump the requires to `idna>=3.7,<4`?\r\n\r\n## Expected Result\r\n\r\nGet rid of [CVE-2024-3651](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-jjg7-2v4v-x38h)\r\n\r\n## Actual Result\r\n\r\nSAST analysis shows [CVE-2024-3651](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-jjg7-2v4v-x38h)\r\n\r\n## Reproduction Steps\r\n\r\nn/a\r\n\r\n## System Information\r\n\r\nn/a \r\n\r\n<!-- This command is only available on Requests v2.16.4 and greater. Otherwise,\r\nplease provide some basic information about your system (Python version,\r\noperating system, &c). -->\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "sigmavirus24", "timestamp": "2024-04-26T14:05:30Z", "body": "Use a better SAST. \n\nThe [requirement](https://github.com/psf/requests/blob/main/setup.cfg#L9) does not forbid you using 3.7. If you aren't also managing your transitive dependency versions a reinstall of requests should pull this unless you have something else blocking you from using that version. ", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "psf/requests/issue/6690#1", "question": "In psf/requests, now that idna released 3.7 (https://github.com/kjd/idna/releases/tag/v3.7), can the dependency requirement be bumped to `idna>=3.7,<4`? Our expected result is to get rid of CVE-2024-3651 / GHSA-jjg7-2v4v-x38h (https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-jjg7-2v4v-x38h), but our SAST analysis currently reports CVE-2024-3651. Reproduction steps and system information are n/a.", "answer": "The maintainer said to use a better SAST. Requests' current requirement does not forbid using idna 3.7; reinstalling requests should pull it unless another dependency constraint is blocking that version.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/psf/requests/blob/main/setup.cfg#L9"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": ["fixed_versions:3.7"]}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://github.com/psf/requests/blob/main/setup.cfg#L9"], "leak_flags": ["fixed_versions:3.7"], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:44:23.982935Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "psf/requests/issue/6669", "repo": "psf/requests", "number": 6669, "title": "Failed to ignore the SSL certificate verification when using `verify=False` option", "url": "https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/6669", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2024-03-23T19:40:30Z", "closed_at": "2024-03-24T12:49:47Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "urbanogilson", "security_topic": "verify=False not disabling SSL verification due to cipher suite mismatch", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know that the server only supports weak cipher suites, leading to a handshake failure even with `verify=False`, as demonstrated by the SSL Labs test (https://www.ssllabs.com/ssltest).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c5", "question_author": "urbanogilson", "answer_author": "sigmavirus24", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation", "code"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "urbanogilson", "timestamp": "2024-03-23T19:40:30Z", "body": "<!-- Summary. -->\r\n\r\nRequests is not ignoring the SSL certificate verification when using the `verify=False` option.\r\n\r\n## Expected Result\r\n\r\nSame as curl with `-k`\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n$ curl -k https://website\r\n<html>\r\n<head><title>301 Moved Permanently</title></head>\r\n<body>\r\n<center><h1>301 Moved Permanently</h1></center>\r\n<hr><center>nginx/1.20.1</center>\r\n</body>\r\n</html>\r\n```\r\nor\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport httpx\r\nsession = httpx.Client(verify=False)\r\nsession.get(\"https://website\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Actual Result\r\n\r\n```python\r\n>> import requests\r\n>> requests.get(\"https://website\", verify=False)\r\n\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 467, in _make_request\r\n    self._validate_conn(conn)\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 1092, in _validate_conn\r\n    conn.connect()\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connection.py\", line 635, in connect\r\n    sock_and_verified = _ssl_wrap_socket_and_match_hostname(\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connection.py\", line 776, in _ssl_wrap_socket_and_match_hostname\r\n    ssl_sock = ssl_wrap_socket(\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 466, in ssl_wrap_socket\r\n    ssl_sock = _ssl_wrap_socket_impl(sock, context, tls_in_tls, server_hostname)\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 510, in _ssl_wrap_socket_impl\r\n    return ssl_context.wrap_socket(sock, server_hostname=server_hostname)\r\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.10/ssl.py\", line 513, in wrap_socket\r\n    return self.sslsocket_class._create(\r\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.10/ssl.py\", line 1071, in _create\r\n    self.do_handshake()\r\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.10/ssl.py\", line 1342, in do_handshake\r\n    self._sslobj.do_handshake()\r\nssl.SSLError: [SSL: SSLV3_ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE] sslv3 alert handshake failure (_ssl.c:1007)\r\n\r\nDuring handling of the above exception, another exception occurred:\r\n\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 790, in urlopen\r\n    response = self._make_request(\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 491, in _make_request\r\n    raise new_e\r\nurllib3.exceptions.SSLError: [SSL: SSLV3_ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE] sslv3 alert handshake failure (_ssl.c:1007)\r\n\r\nThe above exception was the direct cause of the following exception:\r\n\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/requests/adapters.py\", line 486, in send\r\n    resp = conn.urlopen(\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 844, in urlopen\r\n    retries = retries.increment(\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/util/retry.py\", line 515, in increment\r\n    raise MaxRetryError(_pool, url, reason) from reason  # type: ignore[arg-type]\r\nurllib3.exceptions.MaxRetryError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='website', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: / (Caused by SSLError(SSLError(1, '[SSL: SSLV3_ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE] sslv3 alert handshake failure (_ssl.c:1007)')))\r\n\r\nDuring handling of the above exception, another exception occurred:\r\n\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 1, in <module>\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/requests/api.py\", line 73, in get\r\n    return request(\"get\", url, params=params, **kwargs)\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/requests/api.py\", line 59, in request\r\n    return session.request(method=method, url=url, **kwargs)\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 589, in request\r\n    resp = self.send(prep, **send_kwargs)\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 703, in send\r\n    r = adapter.send(request, **kwargs)\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/requests/adapters.py\", line 517, in send\r\n    raise SSLError(e, request=request)\r\nrequests.exceptions.SSLError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='website', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: / (Caused by SSLError(SSLError(1, '[SSL: SSLV3_ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE] sslv3 alert handshake failure (_ssl.c:1007)')))\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe result is similar to curl without `-k`.\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ curl -v https://website\r\n*   Trying 255.255.255.255:443...\r\n* Connected to website (255.255.255.255) port 443 (#0)\r\n* ALPN, offering h2\r\n* ALPN, offering http/1.1\r\n* successfully set certificate verify locations:\r\n*  CAfile: /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt\r\n*  CApath: /etc/ssl/certs\r\n* TLSv1.3 (OUT), TLS handshake, Client hello (1):\r\n* TLSv1.3 (IN), TLS handshake, Server hello (2):\r\n* TLSv1.2 (IN), TLS handshake, Certificate (11):\r\n* TLSv1.2 (OUT), TLS alert, unknown CA (560):\r\n* SSL certificate problem: unable to get local issuer certificate\r\n* Closing connection 0\r\ncurl: (60) SSL certificate problem: unable to get local issuer certificate\r\nMore details here: https://curl.se/docs/sslcerts.html\r\n\r\ncurl failed to verify the legitimacy of the server and therefore could not\r\nestablish a secure connection to it. To learn more about this situation and\r\nhow to fix it, please visit the web page mentioned above.\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Reproduction Steps\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport requests\r\nsession = requests.Session()\r\nsession.verify = False\r\nsession.get(\"https://website\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nor \r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport requests\r\nsession.get(\"https://website\", verify=False)\r\n```\r\n\r\n## System Information\r\n\r\nTested in two systems\r\n\r\n    $ python -m requests.help\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"chardet\": {\r\n    \"version\": null\r\n  },\r\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.1.0\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"cryptography\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"idna\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.4\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"implementation\": {\r\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\r\n    \"version\": \"3.10.12\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"platform\": {\r\n    \"release\": \"5.15.146.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2\",\r\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\r\n    \"openssl_version\": \"\",\r\n    \"version\": null\r\n  },\r\n  \"requests\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"2.31.0\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"system_ssl\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"1010117f\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"urllib3\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"2.0.3\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\r\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": false\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n    $ python -m requests.help\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"chardet\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"5.2.0\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.3.2\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"cryptography\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"42.0.5\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"idna\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.6\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"implementation\": {\r\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\r\n    \"version\": \"3.10.12\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"platform\": {\r\n    \"release\": \"6.1.58+\",\r\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\r\n    \"openssl_version\": \"30200010\",\r\n    \"version\": \"24.1.0\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"requests\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"2.31.0\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"system_ssl\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"30000020\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"urllib3\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"2.0.7\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": false,\r\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": true\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nNote: URL and IP were masked to `https://website` and `255.255.255.255` respectively\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "sigmavirus24", "timestamp": "2024-03-24T01:38:15Z", "body": "A handshake error is not the same as verifying a trust chain. My guess is that requests/urllib3 and your server cannot agree on ciphersuites to use during the handshake. Otherwise you would see a very different exception message. ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "urbanogilson", "timestamp": "2024-03-24T10:57:52Z", "body": "From https://www.ssllabs.com/ssltest the supported cipher suites:\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n<b>Protocols</b>\r\nTLS 1.3\tNo\r\nTLS 1.2\tYes\r\nTLS 1.1\tYes\r\nTLS 1.0\tYes\r\nSSL 3\tNo\r\nSSL 2\tNo\r\n\r\n<b>Cipher Suites</b>\r\n# TLS 1.2 (suites in server-preferred order)\r\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA256 (0x3d)   WEAK\t256\r\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA (0x35)   WEAK\t256\r\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 (0x9d)   WEAK\t256\r\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA256 (0x3c)   WEAK\t128\r\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA (0x2f)   WEAK\t128\r\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256 (0x9c)   WEAK\t128\r\n# TLS 1.1 (suites in server-preferred order)\r\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA (0x35)   WEAK\t256\r\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA (0x2f)   WEAK\t128\r\n# TLS 1.0 (suites in server-preferred order)\r\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA (0x35)   WEAK\t256\r\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA (0x2f)   WEAK\t128\r\n</pre>", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "sigmavirus24", "timestamp": "2024-03-24T11:37:41Z", "body": "Yeah, the only cipher suites this site offers are awful, and I suspect that if you do the following:\r\n\r\n```py\r\nfrom urllib3.util.ssl_ import create_urllib3_context\r\n\r\nfor item in create_urllib3_context().get_ciphers():\r\n    print(item['name'])\r\n```\r\n\r\nI would suggest trying the following:\r\n\r\n```py\r\nimport urllib3\r\n\r\nhttp = urllib3.PoolManager()\r\nresponse = http.request('GET', 'https://...', ca_certs=\"CERT_NONE\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nI expect you'll encounter the same issue.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "urbanogilson", "timestamp": "2024-03-24T12:45:10Z", "body": "You are right, it is reproducible using `urllib3`\r\n\r\n```python\r\nfrom urllib3.util.ssl_ import create_urllib3_context\r\n\r\nfor item in create_urllib3_context().get_ciphers():\r\n    print(item['name'])\r\n\r\nTLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384\r\nTLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256\r\nTLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256\r\nECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384\r\nECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384\r\nECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256\r\nECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256\r\nECDHE-ECDSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305\r\nECDHE-RSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305\r\nECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-SHA384\r\nECDHE-RSA-AES256-SHA384\r\nECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-SHA256\r\nECDHE-RSA-AES128-SHA256\r\nDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384\r\nDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256\r\nDHE-RSA-AES256-SHA256\r\nDHE-RSA-AES128-SHA256\r\n```\r\n\r\n----\r\n```python\r\nhttp = urllib3.PoolManager(ca_certs=None)\r\nresponse = http.request('GET', 'https://website')\r\n\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 467, in _make_request\r\n    self._validate_conn(conn)\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 1092, in _validate_conn\r\n    conn.connect()\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connection.py\", line 635, in connect\r\n    sock_and_verified = _ssl_wrap_socket_and_match_hostname(\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connection.py\", line 776, in _ssl_wrap_socket_and_match_hostname\r\n    ssl_sock = ssl_wrap_socket(\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 466, in ssl_wrap_socket\r\n    ssl_sock = _ssl_wrap_socket_impl(sock, context, tls_in_tls, server_hostname)\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 510, in _ssl_wrap_socket_impl\r\n    return ssl_context.wrap_socket(sock, server_hostname=server_hostname)\r\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.10/ssl.py\", line 513, in wrap_socket\r\n    return self.sslsocket_class._create(\r\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.10/ssl.py\", line 1071, in _create\r\n    self.do_handshake()\r\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.10/ssl.py\", line 1342, in do_handshake\r\n    self._sslobj.do_handshake()\r\nssl.SSLError: [SSL: SSLV3_ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE] sslv3 alert handshake failure (_ssl.c:1007)\r\n\r\nDuring handling of the above exception, another exception occurred:\r\n\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 790, in urlopen\r\n    response = self._make_request(\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 491, in _make_request\r\n    raise new_e\r\nurllib3.exceptions.SSLError: [SSL: SSLV3_ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE] sslv3 alert handshake failure (_ssl.c:1007)\r\n\r\nThe above exception was the direct cause of the following exception:\r\n\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 1, in <module>\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/_request_methods.py\", line 110, in request\r\n    return self.request_encode_url(\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/_request_methods.py\", line 143, in request_encode_url\r\n    return self.urlopen(method, url, **extra_kw)\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/poolmanager.py\", line 443, in urlopen\r\n    response = conn.urlopen(method, u.request_uri, **kw)\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 874, in urlopen\r\n    return self.urlopen(\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 874, in urlopen\r\n    return self.urlopen(\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 874, in urlopen\r\n    return self.urlopen(\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 844, in urlopen\r\n    retries = retries.increment(\r\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/util/retry.py\", line 515, in increment\r\n    raise MaxRetryError(_pool, url, reason) from reason  # type: ignore[arg-type]\r\nurllib3.exceptions.MaxRetryError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='websitw', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: / (Caused by SSLError(SSLError(1, '[SSL: SSLV3_ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE] sslv3 alert handshake failure (_ssl.c:1007)')))\r\n```\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "sigmavirus24", "timestamp": "2024-03-24T12:49:43Z", "body": "Yeah and as you can see the ciphers that are default on your system (because the version of urllib3 you're using relies on system defaults) don't overlap with the very weak set offered by your server. You can customize the ciphers in creating the context for urllib3 and create a custom requests adapter to work around this but this is not a bug. Using the default ciphers is a good security practice and we will not be modifying things to make default ciphers accept weaker algorithms ", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "psf/requests/issue/6669#1", "question": "In psf/requests 2.31.0 on Python 3.10.12, `requests.get(..., verify=False)` is still failing for a masked HTTPS site (`https://website`) with an SSL handshake error instead of behaving like `curl -k` or `httpx.Client(verify=False)`. Is Requests ignoring `verify=False`, or is this a bug?\n\nExpected result, like curl with `-k`:\n\n```bash\n$ curl -k https://website\n<html>\n<head><title>301 Moved Permanently</title></head>\n<body>\n<center><h1>301 Moved Permanently</h1></center>\n<hr><center>nginx/1.20.1</center>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\nor:\n\n```python\nimport httpx\nsession = httpx.Client(verify=False)\nsession.get(\"https://website\")\n```\n\nActual result:\n\n```python\n>> import requests\n>> requests.get(\"https://website\", verify=False)\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 467, in _make_request\n    self._validate_conn(conn)\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 1092, in _validate_conn\n    conn.connect()\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connection.py\", line 635, in connect\n    sock_and_verified = _ssl_wrap_socket_and_match_hostname(\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connection.py\", line 776, in _ssl_wrap_socket_and_match_hostname\n    ssl_sock = ssl_wrap_socket(\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 466, in ssl_wrap_socket\n    ssl_sock = _ssl_wrap_socket_impl(sock, context, tls_in_tls, server_hostname)\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 510, in _ssl_wrap_socket_impl\n    return ssl_context.wrap_socket(sock, server_hostname=server_hostname)\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.10/ssl.py\", line 513, in wrap_socket\n    return self.sslsocket_class._create(\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.10/ssl.py\", line 1071, in _create\n    self.do_handshake()\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.10/ssl.py\", line 1342, in do_handshake\n    self._sslobj.do_handshake()\nssl.SSLError: [SSL: SSLV3_ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE] sslv3 alert handshake failure (_ssl.c:1007)\n\nDuring handling of the above exception, another exception occurred:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 790, in urlopen\n    response = self._make_request(\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 491, in _make_request\n    raise new_e\nurllib3.exceptions.SSLError: [SSL: SSLV3_ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE] sslv3 alert handshake failure (_ssl.c:1007)\n\nThe above exception was the direct cause of the following exception:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/requests/adapters.py\", line 486, in send\n    resp = conn.urlopen(\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 844, in urlopen\n    retries = retries.increment(\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/util/retry.py\", line 515, in increment\n    raise MaxRetryError(_pool, url, reason) from reason  # type: ignore[arg-type]\nurllib3.exceptions.MaxRetryError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='website', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: / (Caused by SSLError(SSLError(1, '[SSL: SSLV3_ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE] sslv3 alert handshake failure (_ssl.c:1007)')))\n\nDuring handling of the above exception, another exception occurred:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 1, in <module>\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/requests/api.py\", line 73, in get\n    return request(\"get\", url, params=params, **kwargs)\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/requests/api.py\", line 59, in request\n    return session.request(method=method, url=url, **kwargs)\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 589, in request\n    resp = self.send(prep, **send_kwargs)\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 703, in send\n    r = adapter.send(request, **kwargs)\n  File \"/home/vscode/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/requests/adapters.py\", line 517, in send\n    raise SSLError(e, request=request)\nrequests.exceptions.SSLError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='website', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: / (Caused by SSLError(SSLError(1, '[SSL: SSLV3_ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE] sslv3 alert handshake failure (_ssl.c:1007)')))\n```\n\nThe result looks similar to curl without `-k`:\n\n```text\n$ curl -v https://website\n*   Trying 255.255.255.255:443...\n* Connected to website (255.255.255.255) port 443 (#0)\n* ALPN, offering h2\n* ALPN, offering http/1.1\n* successfully set certificate verify locations:\n*  CAfile: /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt\n*  CApath: /etc/ssl/certs\n* TLSv1.3 (OUT), TLS handshake, Client hello (1):\n* TLSv1.3 (IN), TLS handshake, Server hello (2):\n* TLSv1.2 (IN), TLS handshake, Certificate (11):\n* TLSv1.2 (OUT), TLS alert, unknown CA (560):\n* SSL certificate problem: unable to get local issuer certificate\n* Closing connection 0\ncurl: (60) SSL certificate problem: unable to get local issuer certificate\nMore details here: https://curl.se/docs/sslcerts.html\n\ncurl failed to verify the legitimacy of the server and therefore could not\nestablish a secure connection to it. To learn more about this situation and\nhow to fix it, please visit the web page mentioned above.\n```\n\nReproduction steps:\n\n```python\nimport requests\nsession = requests.Session()\nsession.verify = False\nsession.get(\"https://website\")\n```\n\nor:\n\n```python\nimport requests\nsession.get(\"https://website\", verify=False)\n```\n\nSystem information from two systems:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"chardet\": {\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.1.0\"\n  },\n  \"cryptography\": {\n    \"version\": \"\"\n  },\n  \"idna\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.4\"\n  },\n  \"implementation\": {\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\n    \"version\": \"3.10.12\"\n  },\n  \"platform\": {\n    \"release\": \"5.15.146.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2\",\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\n  },\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\n    \"openssl_version\": \"\",\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"requests\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.31.0\"\n  },\n  \"system_ssl\": {\n    \"version\": \"1010117f\"\n  },\n  \"urllib3\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.0.3\"\n  },\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": false\n}\n```\n\n```json\n{\n  \"chardet\": {\n    \"version\": \"5.2.0\"\n  },\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.3.2\"\n  },\n  \"cryptography\": {\n    \"version\": \"42.0.5\"\n  },\n  \"idna\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.6\"\n  },\n  \"implementation\": {\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\n    \"version\": \"3.10.12\"\n  },\n  \"platform\": {\n    \"release\": \"6.1.58+\",\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\n  },\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\n    \"openssl_version\": \"30200010\",\n    \"version\": \"24.1.0\"\n  },\n  \"requests\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.31.0\"\n  },\n  \"system_ssl\": {\n    \"version\": \"30000020\"\n  },\n  \"urllib3\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.0.7\"\n  },\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": false,\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": true\n}\n```\n\nSSLLabs reports the server supports TLS 1.2/1.1/1.0 but not TLS 1.3, with only these cipher suites:\n\n```text\n# TLS 1.2\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA256 (0x3d)   WEAK\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA (0x35)      WEAK\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 (0x9d)   WEAK\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA256 (0x3c)   WEAK\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA (0x2f)      WEAK\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256 (0x9c)   WEAK\n# TLS 1.1\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA (0x35)      WEAK\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA (0x2f)      WEAK\n# TLS 1.0\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA (0x35)      WEAK\nTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA (0x2f)      WEAK\n```\n\nUsing urllib3 directly reproduces the same error, and `create_urllib3_context().get_ciphers()` prints only:\n\n```text\nTLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384\nTLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256\nTLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256\nECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384\nECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384\nECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256\nECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256\nECDHE-ECDSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305\nECDHE-RSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305\nECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-SHA384\nECDHE-RSA-AES256-SHA384\nECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-SHA256\nECDHE-RSA-AES128-SHA256\nDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384\nDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256\nDHE-RSA-AES256-SHA256\nDHE-RSA-AES128-SHA256\n```", "answer": "This is not a bug in Requests. The default ciphers on the reporter\u2019s system, via urllib3\u2019s reliance on system defaults, do not overlap with the very weak cipher suites offered by the server, so the TLS handshake fails before certificate trust-chain verification is the issue. A workaround would be to customize the ciphers in the urllib3 SSL context and use a custom Requests adapter, but Requests will not change its defaults to accept weaker algorithms because using default ciphers is good security practice.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["urllib3 relies on system default ciphers in the reporter's environment", "custom urllib3 SSL context and custom Requests adapter workaround"], "answer_grounded_in": "c5", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": [], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:46:16.566665Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "psf/requests/issue/6660", "repo": "psf/requests", "number": 6660, "title": "REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE and CURL_CA_BUNDLE environment variables get ignored when in a venv", "url": "https://github.com/psf/requests/issues/6660", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2024-03-13T16:03:02Z", "closed_at": "2024-03-13T18:51:46Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "FriederHannenheim", "security_topic": "environment variable handling for requests library in virtual environments", "qa_summary": "The developer asked whether the REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE and CURL_CA_BUNDLE environment variables are honored by Requests inside a virtual environment, and the maintainer replied that they are, except in cases referenced in issue #3829 (and duplicate #6565).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "FriederHannenheim", "answer_author": "sigmavirus24", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation", "issue_tracker", "pr_data"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": ["#3829"], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "FriederHannenheim", "timestamp": "2024-03-13T16:03:02Z", "body": "<!-- Summary. -->\r\nRequests supports the REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE and CURL_CA_BUNDLE environment variables to override the certificate \r\n\r\n## Expected Result\r\n<!-- What you expected. -->\r\nHere's what's happening outside of the venv\r\n```\r\nREQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE=/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt python3\r\nPython 3.11.8 (main, Feb 08 2024, 08:03:16) [GCC] on linux\r\nType \"help\", \"copyright\", \"credits\" or \"license\" for more information.\r\n>>> import certifi\r\n>>> certifi.where()\r\n'/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt'\r\n```\r\n## Actual Result\r\n<!-- What happened instead. -->\r\nInside the venv:\r\n```\r\nfried@linux:/tmp> python3 -m venv .venv\r\nfried@linux:/tmp> source .venv/bin/activate\r\n(.venv) fried@linux:/tmp> pip install requests\r\nCollecting requests\r\n  Downloading requests-2.31.0-py3-none-any.whl.metadata (4.6 kB)\r\nCollecting charset-normalizer<4,>=2 (from requests)\r\n  Downloading charset_normalizer-3.3.2-cp311-cp311-manylinux_2_17_x86_64.manylinux2014_x86_64.whl.metadata (33 kB)\r\nCollecting idna<4,>=2.5 (from requests)\r\n  Downloading idna-3.6-py3-none-any.whl.metadata (9.9 kB)\r\nCollecting urllib3<3,>=1.21.1 (from requests)\r\n  Downloading urllib3-2.2.1-py3-none-any.whl.metadata (6.4 kB)\r\nCollecting certifi>=2017.4.17 (from requests)\r\n  Downloading certifi-2024.2.2-py3-none-any.whl.metadata (2.2 kB)\r\nDownloading requests-2.31.0-py3-none-any.whl (62 kB)\r\n   \u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501 62.6/62.6 kB 3.4 MB/s eta 0:00:00\r\nDownloading certifi-2024.2.2-py3-none-any.whl (163 kB)\r\n   \u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501 163.8/163.8 kB 5.3 MB/s eta 0:00:00\r\nDownloading charset_normalizer-3.3.2-cp311-cp311-manylinux_2_17_x86_64.manylinux2014_x86_64.whl (140 kB)\r\n   \u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501 140.3/140.3 kB 10.9 MB/s eta 0:00:00\r\nDownloading idna-3.6-py3-none-any.whl (61 kB)\r\n   \u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501 61.6/61.6 kB 5.7 MB/s eta 0:00:00\r\nDownloading urllib3-2.2.1-py3-none-any.whl (121 kB)\r\n   \u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501 121.1/121.1 kB 10.1 MB/s eta 0:00:00\r\nInstalling collected packages: urllib3, idna, charset-normalizer, certifi, requests\r\nSuccessfully installed certifi-2024.2.2 charset-normalizer-3.3.2 idna-3.6 requests-2.31.0 urllib3-2.2.1\r\n\r\n(.venv) fried@linux:/tmp> REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE=/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt python3\r\nPython 3.11.8 (main, Feb 08 2024, 08:03:16) [GCC] on linux\r\nType \"help\", \"copyright\", \"credits\" or \"license\" for more information.\r\n>>> import certifi\r\n>>> certifi.where()\r\n'/tmp/.venv/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/certifi/cacert.pem'\r\n>>> \r\n```\r\n\r\n## Reproduction Steps\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n$ python3 -m venv .venv\r\n$ source .venv/bin/activate\r\n$ REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE=/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt python\r\n>>> import certifi\r\n>>> certifi.where()\r\n```\r\n\r\n## System Information\r\n\r\n    $ python -m requests.help\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"chardet\": {\r\n    \"version\": null\r\n  },\r\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.3.2\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"cryptography\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"idna\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"3.6\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"implementation\": {\r\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\r\n    \"version\": \"3.11.8\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"platform\": {\r\n    \"release\": \"6.7.7-1-default\",\r\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\r\n    \"openssl_version\": \"\",\r\n    \"version\": null\r\n  },\r\n  \"requests\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"2.31.0\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"system_ssl\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"30100040\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"urllib3\": {\r\n    \"version\": \"2.2.1\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\r\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": false\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n<!-- This command is only available on Requests v2.16.4 and greater. Otherwise,\r\nplease provide some basic information about your system (Python version,\r\noperating system, &c). -->\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "sigmavirus24", "timestamp": "2024-03-13T18:51:32Z", "body": "A few things:\r\n\r\n1. Requests isn't in use in your examples; you're using `certifi`. \r\n2. My guess, is that outside of the venv you have `certifi` from your package manager, and every Linux distro replaces `certifi` with something else\r\n3. `certifi` (a separate project) does not support either environment variable, Requests does.\r\n4. Requests will continue to honor what you set, except in cases like #3829 \r\n5. Lastly, I think this is effectively a duplicate of #6565 ", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "psf/requests/issue/6660#1", "question": "In psf/requests 2.31.0 on Linux with CPython 3.11.8, `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE` and `CURL_CA_BUNDLE` are supposed to override the certificate bundle, but they seem to be ignored inside a virtualenv. Outside the venv, setting `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE=/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt` before starting Python makes `certifi.where()` return that path:\n\n```text\nREQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE=/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt python3\nPython 3.11.8 (main, Feb 08 2024, 08:03:16) [GCC] on linux\nType \"help\", \"copyright\", \"credits\" or \"license\" for more information.\n>>> import certifi\n>>> certifi.where()\n'/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt'\n```\n\nBut inside a venv after installing requests, `certifi.where()` returns the venv's bundled certifi CA file instead:\n\n```text\nfried@linux:/tmp> python3 -m venv .venv\nfried@linux:/tmp> source .venv/bin/activate\n(.venv) fried@linux:/tmp> pip install requests\nCollecting requests\n  Downloading requests-2.31.0-py3-none-any.whl.metadata (4.6 kB)\nCollecting charset-normalizer<4,>=2 (from requests)\n  Downloading charset_normalizer-3.3.2-cp311-cp311-manylinux_2_17_x86_64.manylinux2014_x86_64.whl.metadata (33 kB)\nCollecting idna<4,>=2.5 (from requests)\n  Downloading idna-3.6-py3-none-any.whl.metadata (9.9 kB)\nCollecting urllib3<3,>=1.21.1 (from requests)\n  Downloading urllib3-2.2.1-py3-none-any.whl.metadata (6.4 kB)\nCollecting certifi>=2017.4.17 (from requests)\n  Downloading certifi-2024.2.2-py3-none-any.whl.metadata (2.2 kB)\nDownloading requests-2.31.0-py3-none-any.whl (62 kB)\n   \u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501 62.6/62.6 kB 3.4 MB/s eta 0:00:00\nDownloading certifi-2024.2.2-py3-none-any.whl (163 kB)\n   \u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501 163.8/163.8 kB 5.3 MB/s eta 0:00:00\nDownloading charset_normalizer-3.3.2-cp311-cp311-manylinux_2_17_x86_64.manylinux2014_x86_64.whl (140 kB)\n   \u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501 140.3/140.3 kB 10.9 MB/s eta 0:00:00\nDownloading idna-3.6-py3-none-any.whl (61 kB)\n   \u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501 61.6/61.6 kB 5.7 MB/s eta 0:00:00\nDownloading urllib3-2.2.1-py3-none-any.whl (121 kB)\n   \u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501 121.1/121.1 kB 10.1 MB/s eta 0:00:00\nInstalling collected packages: urllib3, idna, charset-normalizer, certifi, requests\nSuccessfully installed certifi-2024.2.2 charset-normalizer-3.3.2 idna-3.6 requests-2.31.0 urllib3-2.2.1\n\n(.venv) fried@linux:/tmp> REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE=/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt python3\nPython 3.11.8 (main, Feb 08 2024, 08:03:16) [GCC] on linux\nType \"help\", \"copyright\", \"credits\" or \"license\" for more information.\n>>> import certifi\n>>> certifi.where()\n'/tmp/.venv/lib64/python3.11/site-packages/certifi/cacert.pem'\n>>> \n```\n\nMinimal reproduction:\n\n```bash\n$ python3 -m venv .venv\n$ source .venv/bin/activate\n$ REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE=/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt python\n>>> import certifi\n>>> certifi.where()\n```\n\nSystem information from `python -m requests.help`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"chardet\": {\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"charset_normalizer\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.3.2\"\n  },\n  \"cryptography\": {\n    \"version\": \"\"\n  },\n  \"idna\": {\n    \"version\": \"3.6\"\n  },\n  \"implementation\": {\n    \"name\": \"CPython\",\n    \"version\": \"3.11.8\"\n  },\n  \"platform\": {\n    \"release\": \"6.7.7-1-default\",\n    \"system\": \"Linux\"\n  },\n  \"pyOpenSSL\": {\n    \"openssl_version\": \"\",\n    \"version\": null\n  },\n  \"requests\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.31.0\"\n  },\n  \"system_ssl\": {\n    \"version\": \"30100040\"\n  },\n  \"urllib3\": {\n    \"version\": \"2.2.1\"\n  },\n  \"using_charset_normalizer\": true,\n  \"using_pyopenssl\": false\n}\n```\n\nWhy are the `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE`/`CURL_CA_BUNDLE` environment variables not reflected by `certifi.where()` in a virtualenv, and is Requests ignoring them?", "answer": "1. Requests isn't in use in your examples; you're using `certifi`. \n2. My guess, is that outside of the venv you have `certifi` from your package manager, and every Linux distro replaces `certifi` with something else\n3. `certifi` (a separate project) does not support either environment variable, Requests does.\n4. Requests will continue to honor what you set, except in cases like #3829 \n5. Lastly, I think this is effectively a duplicate of #6565", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["psf/requests issue #3829", "psf/requests issue #6565"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["#3829", "#6565"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-15T21:32:17.551999Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/14690", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 14690, "title": "Tracking: Post-quantum cryptography (ML-KEM / ML-DSA / SLH-DSA) support roadmap", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/14690", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2026-04-18T13:58:06Z", "closed_at": "2026-04-18T14:01:10Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "maddykws", "security_topic": "roadmap for post-quantum cryptography support", "qa_summary": "The developer asked whether the library plans to add post\u2011quantum algorithms to mitigate quantum\u2011vulnerability, and the maintainer replied that the next release will include ML\u2011KEM and ML\u2011DSA support and referenced issue\u202f#14419 for SLH\u2011DSA.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "maddykws", "answer_author": "alex", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["issue_tracker"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": ["#14419"], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "maddykws", "timestamp": "2026-04-18T13:58:06Z", "body": "Hey all,\n\nFirst \u2014 I know this library *implements* RSA and ECC. That's the point. \nThis isn't a \"you have vulnerabilities\" issue. It's more of a \"where are \nwe on PQC support and can I help\" issue.\n\nContext: I've been building a scanner (QuantumMigrate) that audits \ncodebases for quantum-vulnerable crypto usage. I ran it on this repo \nmostly out of curiosity, and it flagged 174 usages in src/ alone \u2014 which \nis completely expected given what this library does. But it made me \nwonder about the roadmap for NIST PQC support.\n\nNIST finalized three standards in 2024:\n- ML-KEM (FIPS 203) \u2014 key encapsulation, replaces RSA/DH/ECDH\n- ML-DSA (FIPS 204) \u2014 signatures, replaces RSA/ECDSA/DSA  \n- SLH-DSA (FIPS 205) \u2014 hash-based signatures, conservative backup\n\nThe deadline pressure is real. \"Harvest now, decrypt later\" means \nencrypted data being sent over TLS today is potentially being stockpiled. \nThe sensitive stuff \u2014 healthcare records, financial transactions, anything \nwith a long confidentiality window \u2014 is already at risk.\n\nA few specific things I noticed in the source that seem worth discussing:\n\n**ssh.py (22 hits)** \u2014 RSA and ECC key types for SSH. OpenSSH already \nmerged experimental support for ML-KEM-768 in OpenSSH 9.0. Is there a \nplan to surface that here once it stabilises in the spec?\n\n**_oid.py (lines 128-148)** \u2014 SHA1 and MD5 in the OID signature \nalgorithm map. I understand these exist for parsing legacy certs, but \nis there appetite for a deprecation warning when they're used for \n*signing* (not just parsing)?\n\n**ec.py / rsa.py** \u2014 Core implementations obviously stay, but are there \nplans to add ML-KEM and ML-DSA alongside them? OpenSSL 3.5 shipped \npost-quantum support in March 2026. Since cryptography wraps OpenSSL, \nI'd imagine this is closer than it looks.\n\nI'm not expecting you to rip out RSA tomorrow. I'm genuinely asking \nwhether there's an existing tracking issue I missed, whether there's a \npreferred way to contribute PQC primitives if OpenSSL exposes them, or \nwhether this is something the maintainers have already scoped.\n\nHappy to help however makes sense \u2014 testing, docs, whatever.\n\nThanks for maintaining one of the most important libraries in the Python \necosystem.\n\n\u2014 scanned with https://github.com/maddykws/QuantumMigrate", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-04-18T14:01:08Z", "body": "Our next release will have ML-KEM and ML-DSA support, and there's a distinct issue for SLH-DSA: #14419.\n\nI recognize your issue is attempting to be polite, but it appears you didn't make any effort to search the issue tracker for prior art here. Further, most of your analysis of \"place to look in the codebase\" are incredibly shallow, and don't add value.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/14690#1", "question": "What is pyca/cryptography\u2019s roadmap for NIST post-quantum cryptography support, specifically ML-KEM (FIPS 203), ML-DSA (FIPS 204), and SLH-DSA (FIPS 205)? Context: I\u2019m building a scanner called QuantumMigrate that audits codebases for quantum-vulnerable crypto usage, and running it on this repository flagged 174 usages in `src/` alone, which is expected because the library implements RSA and ECC. I noticed `ssh.py` has RSA and ECC key types for SSH and that OpenSSH merged experimental support for ML-KEM-768 in OpenSSH 9.0; `_oid.py` lines 128-148 include SHA1 and MD5 in the OID signature algorithm map, which I understand is needed for parsing legacy certs but I wondered about deprecation warnings when used for signing; and `ec.py` / `rsa.py` contain the core implementations. Since OpenSSL 3.5 shipped post-quantum support in March 2026 and cryptography wraps OpenSSL, are there plans to add ML-KEM and ML-DSA alongside RSA/ECC, surface SSH ML-KEM support once stable, or track SLH-DSA? Is there an existing tracking issue I missed or a preferred way to contribute PQC primitives if OpenSSL exposes them?", "answer": "The maintainer answered that the next release will include ML-KEM and ML-DSA support. They also stated that SLH-DSA is tracked separately in issue #14419.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["issue #14419", "next release of pyca/cryptography"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["#14419"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-15T20:50:52.053306Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/14592", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 14592, "title": "Accessing unrelated extensions fails when BasicConstraints encodes default `ca:FALSE`", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/14592", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2026-04-07T00:22:15Z", "closed_at": "2026-04-10T13:15:51Z", "labels": ["waiting-on-reporter"], "reporter": "st3fan", "security_topic": "strict DER validation causing unrelated extension access failure", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know whether the library should accept certificates that explicitly encode the default ca:FALSE in BasicConstraints, and the maintainer answered that invalid DER is intentionally rejected, citing a design statement (see https://alexgaynor.net/2025/mar/25/postels-law-and-the-three-ring-circus/).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "st3fan", "answer_author": "alex", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation", "issue_tracker"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "st3fan", "timestamp": "2026-04-07T00:22:15Z", "body": "# Accessing unrelated extensions fails when BasicConstraints encodes default `ca:FALSE`\n\n## Versions\n\n- Python: 3.13.5\n- cryptography: 46.0.6\n- OS: Debian Trixie (amd64)\n\n## Description\n\nWhen a certificate's `BasicConstraints` extension explicitly encodes `ca:FALSE` (the DER default value), the `cryptography` library raises a `ValueError` with `ParseError { kind: EncodedDefault }` when accessing **any** extension, not just `BasicConstraints`.\n\nThis means that calling `cert.extensions.get_extension_for_class(SubjectAlternativeName)` fails even though the SAN extension itself is perfectly valid. The error is thrown during iteration of the extensions list, making all extensions inaccessible.\n\nThe certificate is a real certificate observed in a Certificate Transparency log. OpenSSL parses it without errors and correctly shows `CA:FALSE` in the Basic Constraints and a valid SAN (`DNS:receptum-apteekkiverkko.maxx.fi, IP Address:83.150.105.126`).\n\nWhile explicitly encoding the default value violates DER (the `ca` field defaults to `FALSE` per RFC 5280 and should be omitted), this encoding is common in the wild and is accepted by other implementations.\n\n## Minimal PoC\n\n```python\nfrom cryptography import x509\n\nder = open(\"entry_0046.der\", \"rb\").read()\ncert = x509.load_der_x509_certificate(der)\n\n# This works fine:\nprint(cert.subject)\nprint(cert.issuer)\n\n# This fails, even though SAN is valid:\nsan = cert.extensions.get_extension_for_class(x509.SubjectAlternativeName)\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"poc.py\", line 8, in <module>\n    san = cert.extensions.get_extension_for_class(x509.SubjectAlternativeName)\n          ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  ...\nValueError: error parsing asn1 value: ParseError { kind: EncodedDefault, location: [\"BasicConstraints::ca\"] }\n```\n\n## OpenSSL output\n\nOpenSSL parses the same certificate without issue:\n\n```\n$ openssl x509 -inform DER -in entry_0046.der -text -noout\nCertificate:\n    Data:\n        Version: 3 (0x2)\n        Serial Number:\n            01:99:3d:78:70:fe:f2:ca:f0:9b:b6:c9:d1:78:28\n        Signature Algorithm: sha512WithRSAEncryption\n        Issuer: C=FI, O=Digi- ja vaestotietovirasto CA, OU=Sosiaali- ja terveydenhuollon palveluvarmenteet, CN=DVV Social Welfare and Healthcare Service Certificates - G3R\n        Validity\n            Not Before: Oct 22 21:00:00 2025 GMT\n            Not After : Oct 22 20:59:59 2026 GMT\n        Subject: C=FI, ST=FINLAND, L=Naantali, O=Naantalin 2. apteekki, serialNumber=1.2.246.553.1.853.13.0, CN=83.150.105.126\n        X509v3 extensions:\n            X509v3 Basic Constraints: critical\n                CA:FALSE\n            X509v3 Subject Alternative Name:\n                DNS:receptum-apteekkiverkko.maxx.fi, IP Address:83.150.105.126\n```\n\n(Full output omitted for brevity. The certificate is attached as `entry_0046.pem.txt`.)\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe BasicConstraints extension contains `30 03 01 01 00` \u2014 a SEQUENCE with an explicit `BOOLEAN FALSE`. Per DER, the `ca` field defaults to `FALSE` and must be omitted when it has the default value. The Rust ASN.1 parser in `cryptography` strictly rejects this as `EncodedDefault`. Since extension parsing is all-or-nothing, this makes every extension on the certificate inaccessible.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nIdeally, accessing an unrelated extension like `SubjectAlternativeName` should not fail due to a strict-DER violation in `BasicConstraints`. Possible approaches:\n\n1. Parse extensions lazily so that accessing one extension doesn't require successfully parsing all others.\n2. Accept the common DER violation of explicitly encoding default values in `BasicConstraints`.\n\n[entry_0046.pem.txt](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/26522833/entry_0046.pem.txt)", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-04-07T00:37:46Z", "body": "This is an intentional design behavior.\n\nIn reverse order: We do not accept invalid DER, except in exceedingly rare cases. (See https://alexgaynor.net/2025/mar/25/postels-law-and-the-three-ring-circus/)\n\nAs for the issue of unrelated extensions, the most recent time this was brought up was https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/13504#issuecomment-3368256526 (but I'm reasonably certain there's been other issues about this)\n\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "st3fan", "timestamp": "2026-04-07T00:53:25Z", "body": "Thanks @alex - I think that is fair. Because this is so extremely rare (I parsed more than 100,000,000 certificates before this happened) I will use a fallback parser when this specific error happens. Thank you for the clear response.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "SillyZir", "timestamp": "2026-04-10T11:14:43Z", "body": "The extension iterator likely validates all extensions collectively; `ParseError { kind: EncodedDefault }` from `BasicConstraints` bubbles up on any access. Consider treating `EncodedDefault` as a recoverable warning in the Rust extension parser rather than a hard error.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/14592#1", "question": "In pyca/cryptography 46.0.6 on Python 3.13.5, Debian Trixie amd64, accessing unrelated X.509 extensions fails when a certificate's BasicConstraints extension explicitly encodes the DER default `ca:FALSE`.\n\nThe certificate is a real certificate observed in a Certificate Transparency log. OpenSSL parses it without errors and shows `CA:FALSE` in Basic Constraints and a valid SAN (`DNS:receptum-apteekkiverkko.maxx.fi, IP Address:83.150.105.126`). The BasicConstraints extension contains `30 03 01 01 00`, a SEQUENCE with an explicit `BOOLEAN FALSE`; per RFC 5280/DER the `ca` field defaults to `FALSE` and should be omitted.\n\nMinimal PoC:\n\n```python\nfrom cryptography import x509\n\nder = open(\"entry_0046.der\", \"rb\").read()\ncert = x509.load_der_x509_certificate(der)\n\n# This works fine:\nprint(cert.subject)\nprint(cert.issuer)\n\n# This fails, even though SAN is valid:\nsan = cert.extensions.get_extension_for_class(x509.SubjectAlternativeName)\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"poc.py\", line 8, in <module>\n    san = cert.extensions.get_extension_for_class(x509.SubjectAlternativeName)\n          ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  ...\nValueError: error parsing asn1 value: ParseError { kind: EncodedDefault, location: [\"BasicConstraints::ca\"] }\n```\n\nOpenSSL output for the same certificate includes:\n\n```text\n$ openssl x509 -inform DER -in entry_0046.der -text -noout\nCertificate:\n    Data:\n        Version: 3 (0x2)\n        Serial Number:\n            01:99:3d:78:70:fe:f2:ca:f0:9b:b6:c9:d1:78:28\n        Signature Algorithm: sha512WithRSAEncryption\n        Issuer: C=FI, O=Digi- ja vaestotietovirasto CA, OU=Sosiaali- ja terveydenhuollon palveluvarmenteet, CN=DVV Social Welfare and Healthcare Service Certificates - G3R\n        Validity\n            Not Before: Oct 22 21:00:00 2025 GMT\n            Not After : Oct 22 20:59:59 2026 GMT\n        Subject: C=FI, ST=FINLAND, L=Naantali, O=Naantalin 2. apteekki, serialNumber=1.2.246.553.1.853.13.0, CN=83.150.105.126\n        X509v3 extensions:\n            X509v3 Basic Constraints: critical\n                CA:FALSE\n            X509v3 Subject Alternative Name:\n                DNS:receptum-apteekkiverkko.maxx.fi, IP Address:83.150.105.126\n```\n\nSince extension parsing appears to be all-or-nothing, this makes every extension inaccessible. Is this expected behavior in cryptography? Should accessing an unrelated extension like `SubjectAlternativeName` avoid failing due to a strict-DER violation in `BasicConstraints`, either by parsing extensions lazily or by accepting this common invalid DER encoding?", "answer": "This is an intentional design behavior.\n\nIn reverse order: We do not accept invalid DER, except in exceedingly rare cases. (See https://alexgaynor.net/2025/mar/25/postels-law-and-the-three-ring-circus/)\n\nAs for the issue of unrelated extensions, the most recent time this was brought up was https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/13504#issuecomment-3368256526 (but I'm reasonably certain there's been other issues about this)", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://alexgaynor.net/2025/mar/25/postels-law-and-the-three-ring-circus/", "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/13504#issuecomment-3368256526"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://alexgaynor.net/2025/mar/25/postels-law-and-the-three-ring-circus/", "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/13504#issuecomment-3368256526"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:48:11.251475Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/14477", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 14477, "title": "Fix for CVE-2026-26007 breaks ecc", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/14477", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2026-03-14T12:30:49Z", "closed_at": "2026-03-14T12:51:34Z", "labels": ["waiting-on-reporter"], "reporter": "mdeslaur", "security_topic": "regression of CVE-2026-26007 fix breaking Ansible", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know why the CVE-2026-26007 security fix broke Ansible's SECP384R1 support and that the issue is fixed in the ansible-collections/community.crypto commit 911ed33c2e898e6b56e640fa4f71afa599db3fcb.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c3", "question_author": "mdeslaur", "answer_author": "alex", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "commit_history", "external_reference"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": ["CVE-2026-26007"], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": ["911ed33c2e898e6b56e640fa4f71afa599db3fcb"], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "mdeslaur", "timestamp": "2026-03-14T12:30:49Z", "body": "Hi,\n\nWe have gotten a report that the fix for CVE-2026-26007 included in 46.0.5 is breaking Ansible.\n\nAnsible now returns the following error:\nfatal: [localhost]: FAILED! => changed=false\nmsg: Your cryptography version does not support SECP384R1\n\nA simple reproducer for this issue is:\n```\nimport cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric.ec\necclass = cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric.ec.__dict__.get('SECP384R1')\nprint(ecclass)\n```\n\nDownstream bug report for possibly more information:\nhttps://bugs.launchpad.net/ubuntu/+source/python-cryptography/+bug/2144373\n(We had backported the security fix to our stable releases in Ubuntu, but the same issue is present in 46.0.5)\n\nThanks!", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-03-14T12:33:35Z", "body": "I assume this is an unexpeted consequene of our `deprecated` utility: https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/commit/0eebb9dbb6343d9bc1d91e5a2482ed4e054a6d8c#diff-42b5786fb7abc7aa972632f10f1a9e5e7fa2184943dda31d2671690d489c579cR461-R464\n\nThat said, accessing things via `__dict__` isn't really the correct way to get attributes from a module.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "mdeslaur", "timestamp": "2026-03-14T12:45:17Z", "body": "Yes, it appears to be caused by the deprecated utility.\n\nLooks like the affected ansible code is [here](https://github.com/ansible-collections/community.crypto/blob/b1e4777b4d3a0fd4f74f2a9cc0075fdecb088d3f/plugins/module_utils/_crypto/module_backends/privatekey.py#L104)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-03-14T12:48:11Z", "body": "Oh, that's because it was fixed: https://github.com/ansible-collections/community.crypto/commit/911ed33c2e898e6b56e640fa4f71afa599db3fcb", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-03-14T12:50:13Z", "body": "Regrettably, I don't think there's really a way for us to both fix this issue _and_ get the deprecation warning :-(", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/14477#1", "question": "After the fix for CVE-2026-26007 included in pyca/cryptography 46.0.5, Ansible is failing with:\n\n```\nfatal: [localhost]: FAILED! => changed=false\nmsg: Your cryptography version does not support SECP384R1\n```\n\nA simple reproducer is:\n\n```\nimport cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric.ec\necclass = cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric.ec.__dict__.get('SECP384R1')\nprint(ecclass)\n```\n\nThis was also seen when backporting the security fix to Ubuntu stable releases, and the downstream bug is https://bugs.launchpad.net/ubuntu/+source/python-cryptography/+bug/2144373. Is the CVE-2026-26007 fix breaking ECC/SECP384R1 support for Ansible?", "answer": "The maintainer explained that this is due to Ansible/community.crypto accessing the curve via the module `__dict__`, which is not the correct way to get module attributes, and the issue had already been fixed in Ansible community.crypto commit `911ed33c2e898e6b56e640fa4f71afa599db3fcb`. They also noted there was not really a way for cryptography to both fix the CVE-related issue and keep the deprecation warning behavior unchanged.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/ansible-collections/community.crypto/commit/911ed33c2e898e6b56e640fa4f71afa599db3fcb"], "answer_grounded_in": "c3", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["911ed33c2e898e6b56e640fa4f71afa599db3fcb", "https://github.com/ansible-collections/community.crypto/commit/911ed33c2e898e6b56e640fa4f71afa599db3fcb"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:50:40.689677Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/14207", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 14207, "title": "Rust once_cell dependency has CVE-2019-16141 vulnerability", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/14207", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2026-01-27T04:37:34Z", "closed_at": "2026-01-27T09:51:42Z", "labels": ["waiting-on-reporter"], "reporter": "oneumyvakin", "security_topic": "assessment of CVE applicability to dependency version", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know if the project's once_cell dependency is affected by CVE-2019-16141, and the answer cites the Cargo.lock file showing the used version is not vulnerable.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "oneumyvakin", "answer_author": "alex", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["dependency_manifest", "cve_cwe_db"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": ["CVE-2019-16141"], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "oneumyvakin", "timestamp": "2026-01-27T04:37:34Z", "body": "Now cryptography has Cargo.toml file where `once_cell` declared as version \"1\":\n\n```\n[dependencies]\n...\nonce_cell = \"1\"\n```\n\nwhich is reported by some vulnerability scanners as CVE-2019-16141 (https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2019-16141)\n\nThe latest once_cell version is [v1.21.3](https://github.com/matklad/once_cell/releases/tag/v1.21.3)\n\nThis issue is about to bump once_cell to latest version to close CVE-2019-16141 and keep once_cell up-to-date.\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-01-27T04:42:07Z", "body": "The version of once_cell specified in our Cargo.lock does not have that vulnerability (https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/blob/main/Cargo.lock#L205-L209). I suspect, though I ahve not verified, that we have never dependened on a version with that vulnerability.\n\nI assume you're using some sort of automatic vulnerability scanner, but it doesn't seem to be very good.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/14207#1", "question": "In pyca/cryptography, Cargo.toml declares the Rust dependency `once_cell` as version `\"1\"`:\n\n```\n[dependencies]\n...\nonce_cell = \"1\"\n```\n\nSome vulnerability scanners report this as CVE-2019-16141 (https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2019-16141). The latest once_cell version is v1.21.3 (https://github.com/matklad/once_cell/releases/tag/v1.21.3). Should cryptography bump once_cell to the latest version to close CVE-2019-16141 and keep it up to date?", "answer": "The version of once_cell specified in our Cargo.lock does not have that vulnerability (https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/blob/main/Cargo.lock#L205-L209). I suspect, though I ahve not verified, that we have never dependened on a version with that vulnerability.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/blob/main/Cargo.lock#L205-L209"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/blob/main/Cargo.lock#L205-L209"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:52:00.308546Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/14173", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 14173, "title": "Extension criticality in EE certificates", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/14173", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2026-01-19T15:31:06Z", "closed_at": "2026-01-29T00:00:37Z", "labels": ["waiting-on-reporter", "Stale"], "reporter": "TaaviE", "security_topic": "interpretation of baseline requirement criticality for X.509 extensions", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know whether 'N' in the Baseline Requirements means Criticality::NonCritical or Criticality::Agnostic, and the answer cites the CA/B Forum TLS Baseline Requirements and RFC\u202f5280 stating it means Criticality::NonCritical.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "TaaviE", "answer_author": "woodruffw", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "TaaviE", "timestamp": "2026-01-19T15:31:06Z", "body": "The current implementation in [`extensions.rs`](https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/blob/main/src/rust/cryptography-x509-verification/src/policy/extension.rs#L118) seems to apply the BR a bit inconsistent. I personally stumbled upon it erroring when EKU was marked as critical, which is allowed by RFC 5280 but `N` in the BR and marked as `Criticality:NonCritical`. At the same time `subjectKeyIdentifier` is `N` (and `NOT RECOMMENDED`) but marked as `Criticality::Agnostic`.\n\nIt's a bit unclear to me if `N` in the BR should mean `Criticality::NonCritical` or `Criticality:Agnostic`? What's the interpretation of `pyca/cryptography`?\n\nIt seems more reasonable to be `Agnostic` of criticality if it's allowed by RFC 5280 (and has a purpose) and `NonCritical` when an extension has been widely considered non-critical. \n\n#### References\n\nThe tables in CA/B Forum TLS Server certificate BR currently specifies the following:\n\nCA-Browser-Forum TLS BR 2.2.2:\n> 7.1.2.7.6 Subscriber Certificate Extensions: \n\n| Extension | Presence | Critical | Description |\n|--------|--------|--------|--------|\n| authorityInformationAccess | MUST | N | See Section 7.1.2.7.7 |\n| authorityKeyIdentifier | MUST | N | See Section 7.1.2.11.1 |\n| certificatePolicies | MUST | N | See Section 7.1.2.7.9 |\n| extKeyUsage | MUST | N | See Section 7.1.2.7.10 |\n| subjectAltName | MUST | * | See Section 7.1.2.7.12 |\n| nameConstraints | MUST NOT | \u2010 |  - |\n| keyUsage | SHOULD | Y |  See Section 7.1.2.7.11 |\n| basicConstraints | MAY  | Y |  See Section 7.1.2.7.8 |\n| crlDistributionPoints | * |  N |  See Section 7.1.2.11.2 |\n| Signed Certificate Timestamp List | MAY  | N |  See Section 7.1.2.11.3 |\n| subjectKeyIdentifier | NOT RECOMMENDED | N |  See Section 7.1.2.11.4 |\n| Any other extension | NOT RECOMMENDED | \u2010 |  See Section 7.1.2.11.5 |\n\nCA-Browser-Forum TLS BR 2.2.2:\n> 7.1.2 Certificate Content and Extensions\n```\nIf the CA asserts compliance with these Baseline Requirements, all certificates that it issues MUST\ncomply with one of the following certificate profiles, which incorporate, and are derived from RFC\n5280.\n```\n\nRFC 5280:\n```\n   If a CA includes extended key usages to satisfy such applications,\n   but does not wish to restrict usages of the key, the CA can include\n   the special KeyPurposeId anyExtendedKeyUsage in addition to the\n   particular key purposes required by the applications.  Conforming CAs\n   SHOULD NOT mark this extension as critical if the anyExtendedKeyUsage\n   KeyPurposeId is present.  Applications that require the presence of a\n   particular purpose MAY reject certificates that include the\n   anyExtendedKeyUsage OID but not the particular OID expected for the\n   application.\n```\n\nextensions.rs:\n```rust\n            subject_key_identifier: ExtensionValidator::maybe_present(\n                SUBJECT_KEY_IDENTIFIER_OID,\n                Criticality::Agnostic,\n                None,\n            ),\n[...]\n            // CA/B: 7.1.2.7.10: Subscriber Certificate Extended Key Usage\n            // NOTE: CABF requires EKUs in EE certs, while RFC 5280 does not.\n            extended_key_usage: ExtensionValidator::maybe_present(\n                EXTENDED_KEY_USAGE_OID,\n                Criticality::NonCritical,\n                Some(Arc::new(ee::extended_key_usage)),\n            ),\n```", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "woodruffw", "timestamp": "2026-01-19T15:56:23Z", "body": "> It's a bit unclear to me if `N` in the BR should mean `Criticality::NonCritical` or `Criticality:Agnostic`? What's the interpretation of `pyca/cryptography`?\n\nMy interpretation of the CABF tables is that the symbols have the following meanings for criticality:\n\n* `-` means \"not applicable,\" usually because another requirement forbids the relevance of the criticality bit. For example, 7.1.2.7.6 says that Name Constrains MUST NOT appear in an EE cert, so criticality is `-`.\n* `*` means agnostic. For example, 7.1.2.7.6 says that the SAN MUST appear, but can be either critical or non-critical.\n* `Y` and `N` mean MUST and MUST NOT be critical, respectively.\n\nSo yeah, I believe `N` in the BRs should mean `Criticality::NonCritical`, and for the Subject Key Identifier in particular NOT RECOMMENDED doesn't have a bearing on the criticality constraint (we accept it either way, but we *don't* accept it as critical because that specific state is forbidden rather than NOT RECOMMENDED).", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "woodruffw", "timestamp": "2026-01-19T15:57:39Z", "body": "With that said, I think PyCA Cryptography's general position with respect to BR conformance is that the validator should reflect real-world (i.e. Web PKI) behavior. Are you aware of publicly issued EEs that exhibit a critical SKI, or is this something you've hit on non-Web PKI issuances?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "TaaviE", "timestamp": "2026-01-19T16:27:14Z", "body": "> So yeah, I believe N in the BRs should mean Criticality::NonCritical, and for the Subject Key Identifier in particular NOT RECOMMENDED doesn't have a bearing on the criticality constraint (we accept it either way, but we don't accept it as critical because that specific state is forbidden rather than NOT RECOMMENDED).\n\nSKI is `Agnostic` right now though? Is it actually not accepted as critical?\n\n>  Are you aware of publicly issued EEs that exhibit a critical SKI, or is this something you've hit on non-Web PKI issuances?\n\nNeither, nor have I searched for Web PKI issuances. I encountered a critical EKU in non-Web PKI and started looking into why it was being restricted.\n\nRight now it seems that:\n* SKI is `Agnostic` when it should be `NonCritical`\n* KU is `Agnostic` when it should be `Critical`\n* BC is `Agnostic` when it should be `Critical`\n\n(and EKU is `NonCritical` when `Agnostic` would make a bit more sense to require usage enforcement)\n\nI can make a PR to align the checks with the BR.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-01-19T16:30:02Z", "body": "I'm almost positive that in all cases we are _less_ strict than the BRs, it's because there is no security value in it and there was enough ecosystem breakage that it didn't make sense to strictly enforce. If there are cases we're more strict than the specs we should probably fix that!", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "woodruffw", "timestamp": "2026-01-19T16:33:52Z", "body": "Oh, my bad -- I thought you were saying that we weren't already agnostic. I agree that's a bit inconsistent at the moment.\n\nI'm kind of surprised we don't have an x509-limbo testcase for this \ud83d\ude42 -- that would be a good guidepost for whether we can/should restrict criticality. ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "TaaviE", "timestamp": "2026-01-20T11:37:09Z", "body": "Should I make a PR to change EKU to `Agnostic` to match the enforcement of other extensions' criticality? Or do you wish to first create a test-case in `x509-limbo` to figure out which extensions' criticality can be enforced according to the BR?\n\nMaybe it would be better to follow the BR unless specifically overridden by the user (or code using the crate/library)? That way any deviations from BR would be explicit.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-01-20T12:28:35Z", "body": "Unless there's a pressing reason, our preference is to match the BRs. It looks like we already have a testcase for this: https://x509-limbo.com/testcases/webpki/#webpkiekuee-critical-eku\n\n(Though it shows as skipped, which is surprising to me.)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c8", "author": "woodruffw", "timestamp": "2026-01-20T12:56:14Z", "body": "> It looks like we already have a testcase for this\n\nThat one is for critical EKU in EEs, not critical SKI. I think we could indeed use a new limbo testcase for this!\n\n> Though it shows as skipped, which is surprising to me.\n\nI don't remember exactly, but I _think_ we skipped some of these because none of the other validators checked them. So the idea was that the breakage risk was too high, although now that there's a custom policy API that's probably moot.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c9", "author": "github-actions", "timestamp": "2026-01-24T00:00:15Z", "body": "This issue has been waiting for a reporter response for 3 days. It will be auto-closed if no activity occurs in the next 5 days.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c10", "author": "github-actions", "timestamp": "2026-01-29T00:00:37Z", "body": "This issue has not received a reporter response and has been auto-closed. If the issue is still relevant please leave a comment and we can reopen it.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c11", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-01-29T12:28:15Z", "body": "I can re-open, but unless I'm confused, it's not clear there's any action to take here: https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/14173#issuecomment-3772648357", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c12", "author": "TaaviE", "timestamp": "2026-01-29T16:11:49Z", "body": "Well, the policy enforcement should be based on something (like WebPKI observations) or aligned to the BR. It's up to you as maintainers to decide which approach should be taken. Whichever way is taken in the future, it's still inconsistent right now and the issue itself should remain open.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c13", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-01-29T16:17:18Z", "body": "Our policy is that we align to BRs or RFC5280, as appropriate, but we make things laxer when it's either required for interoperability in practice or is a property no other validators enforce.\n\nIn this case, we're matching the BRs behavior, and I don't think we've really established that there's an interop risk, hence there being no change to make here.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c14", "author": "TaaviE", "timestamp": "2026-01-30T10:28:20Z", "body": "> In this case, we're matching the BRs behavior\n\nTo match the BR the following changes should be made:\n\n* SKI is `Agnostic` -> should be `NonCritical`\n* KU is `Agnostic` -> should be `Critical`\n* BC is `Agnostic` -> should be `Critical`\n\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c15", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-01-30T12:11:24Z", "body": "Sorry, I feel like we're talking with circles: places where we are laxer than the BRs are generally intentional, and reflect things that:\n\n> it's either required for interoperability in practice or is a property no other validators enforce.\n\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c16", "author": "TaaviE", "timestamp": "2026-01-30T12:36:09Z", "body": "Adding documentation that \"this is done because others do it\", \"this is required for interop\" would also address the core of this issue. Almost my entire point is that it doesn't match the RFC nor the BR and it's not written anywhere why that is the case.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/14173#1", "question": "In pyca/cryptography's x509 verification policy, how should CA/B Forum TLS BR criticality value `N` be interpreted for end-entity certificate extensions: as `Criticality::NonCritical` or `Criticality::Agnostic`? The current implementation in `extensions.rs` seems inconsistent: I hit an error when an Extended Key Usage extension was marked critical, even though RFC 5280 appears to allow critical EKU in some cases, while the BR table marks EKU as `N` / non-critical. At the same time, `subjectKeyIdentifier` is also `N` and `NOT RECOMMENDED` in the BRs, but the code marks it as `Criticality::Agnostic`.\n\nCA/B Forum TLS BR 2.2.2 section 7.1.2.7.6 lists subscriber certificate extensions as:\n\n| Extension | Presence | Critical | Description |\n|--------|--------|--------|--------|\n| authorityInformationAccess | MUST | N | See Section 7.1.2.7.7 |\n| authorityKeyIdentifier | MUST | N | See Section 7.1.2.11.1 |\n| certificatePolicies | MUST | N | See Section 7.1.2.7.9 |\n| extKeyUsage | MUST | N | See Section 7.1.2.7.10 |\n| subjectAltName | MUST | * | See Section 7.1.2.7.12 |\n| nameConstraints | MUST NOT | \u2010 |  - |\n| keyUsage | SHOULD | Y |  See Section 7.1.2.7.11 |\n| basicConstraints | MAY  | Y |  See Section 7.1.2.7.8 |\n| crlDistributionPoints | * |  N |  See Section 7.1.2.11.2 |\n| Signed Certificate Timestamp List | MAY  | N |  See Section 7.1.2.11.3 |\n| subjectKeyIdentifier | NOT RECOMMENDED | N |  See Section 7.1.2.11.4 |\n| Any other extension | NOT RECOMMENDED | \u2010 |  See Section 7.1.2.11.5 |\n\nCA/B Forum TLS BR 2.2.2 section 7.1.2 says:\n```text\nIf the CA asserts compliance with these Baseline Requirements, all certificates that it issues MUST\ncomply with one of the following certificate profiles, which incorporate, and are derived from RFC\n5280.\n```\n\nRFC 5280 says about EKU:\n```text\n   If a CA includes extended key usages to satisfy such applications,\n   but does not wish to restrict usages of the key, the CA can include\n   the special KeyPurposeId anyExtendedKeyUsage in addition to the\n   particular key purposes required by the applications.  Conforming CAs\n   SHOULD NOT mark this extension as critical if the anyExtendedKeyUsage\n   KeyPurposeId is present.  Applications that require the presence of a\n   particular purpose MAY reject certificates that include the\n   anyExtendedKeyUsage OID but not the particular OID expected for the\n   application.\n```\n\nThe relevant code is:\n```rust\n            subject_key_identifier: ExtensionValidator::maybe_present(\n                SUBJECT_KEY_IDENTIFIER_OID,\n                Criticality::Agnostic,\n                None,\n            ),\n[...]\n            // CA/B: 7.1.2.7.10: Subscriber Certificate Extended Key Usage\n            // NOTE: CABF requires EKUs in EE certs, while RFC 5280 does not.\n            extended_key_usage: ExtensionValidator::maybe_present(\n                EXTENDED_KEY_USAGE_OID,\n                Criticality::NonCritical,\n                Some(Arc::new(ee::extended_key_usage)),\n            ),\n```\n\nShould `N` in the BR mean `Criticality::NonCritical` or `Criticality::Agnostic` for pyca/cryptography? More generally, should I change EKU to `Agnostic` to match the other extension criticality checks, or should the validator follow the BRs unless overridden? The current state appears to be: SKI is `Agnostic` when the BR says `N`, KU is `Agnostic` when the BR says `Y`, BC is `Agnostic` when the BR says `Y`, and EKU is `NonCritical` even though `Agnostic` might make sense from RFC 5280 usage-enforcement perspective.", "answer": "The maintainers interpret the CA/B Forum criticality symbols as: `-` means not applicable, `*` means agnostic, and `Y`/`N` mean must be critical / must not be critical respectively. Their policy is to align with the BRs or RFC 5280 as appropriate, but to be laxer when required for interoperability in practice or when a property is not enforced by other validators; places where cryptography is laxer than the BRs are generally intentional. For EKU, they considered the current `NonCritical` behavior to match the BRs and did not identify an interop risk requiring a change.", "knowledge_type": "parametric", "grounding_sources": [], "answer_grounded_in": "c1, c7, c13, c15", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": [], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T17:40:25.644594Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/14048", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 14048, "title": "On some plateforms, DHParameterNumbers performance is 100x slower", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/14048", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-12-23T01:37:27Z", "closed_at": "2025-12-23T01:53:58Z", "labels": ["waiting-on-reporter"], "reporter": "gpotter2", "security_topic": "DH parameter validation performance issue in LibreSSL", "qa_summary": "The slowdown is caused by the DH_check security validation and has been fixed by skipping well\u2011known parameters in LibreSSL\u202f4.3.0 (see commit\u202f9ac14cf7a1b28db2437e849540c0abe6a436a03d).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c14", "question_author": "gpotter2", "answer_author": "botovq", "answerer_role": "contributor", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "commit_history", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": ["4.3.0"], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": ["9ac14cf7a1b28db2437e849540c0abe6a436a03d"], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Accepted because the question is a valid TLS/DH performance/security issue and the answer correctly links it to a known fix in LibreSSL.", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "gpotter2", "timestamp": "2025-12-23T01:37:27Z", "body": "Hi everyone !\n\nFirst of all, merry Christmas !\n\nI have been troubleshooting some issues with scapy being super slow to load one some plateforms, and have cornered the problem to a very big performance difference when running `DHParameterNumbers.parameters()` on some plateforms. My setups are sadly very different, and this will likely need further troubleshooting to precisely identify what's wrong.\n\nHere's the code to reproduce:\n```python\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.backends import default_backend\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric.dh import DHParameterNumbers\n\n# modp8192 parameters\n# https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3526 sect 7\ng = 2\nm = \n\npn = DHParameterNumbers(m, g)\nparams = pn.parameters(default_backend())\n```\n\n| OS|  Flavor |  Python |  Time to run |\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Debian 13 | OpenSSL 3.5.1 (FIPS: False, Legacy: True) |  3.13.5 |  0m0.086s real 0m0.069s user     0m0.017s system |\n|OpenBSD 78 |  LibreSSL 4.2.0 (FIPS: False, Legacy: False) |  3.12.11 |  **1m03.48s** real     **1m03.32s** user 0m00.08s system |\n\n(both are running in Hyper-V virtual machines. It would be unfair to say those stats are 1-to-1 comparable, but it feels like a very high difference.)\n\nWhat do you think could be the issue here ? Am I doing something wrong ?\n\nThanks a lot for the help and happy holidays !", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-12-23T01:40:46Z", "body": "I believe its a known issue that LibreSSL performance with DH is quite slow: https://marc.info/?l=libressl&m=173846028619304&w=2", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "gpotter2", "timestamp": "2025-12-23T01:44:04Z", "body": "This looks precisely like the issue !\nThanks for the note. To be fair, for well-known parameters (here modp8192), I would expect LibreSSL to somehow already know this is pretty safe (e.g. have a list of available parameters somewhere).\n\n\nThis isn't really a cryptography issue. If this is tracked elsewhere, feel free to close.\n\nThanks again for the help", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-12-23T01:53:58Z", "body": "I don't think the libressl folks have a bug tracker, so that thread is the closest it comes to being tracked anywhere. But given this isn't a cryptography bug and there's not really anything we can do to mitigate it, I'm going to close.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "botovq", "timestamp": "2026-01-06T16:38:12Z", "body": "If we really must, we can add shortcuts for the well-known domain-parameters to LibreSSL and cut down the cost of `DH_check()`, but I don't really understand why cryptography ends up calling `DH_check()` on well-known parameters in the first place.\n\nAlso, why is this done every time scapy starts up? My understanding is that `DH_check()` is intended for adversarial keys.\n\n> I don't think the libressl folks have a bug tracker, so that thread is the closest it comes to being tracked anywhere. But given this isn't a cryptography bug and there's not really anything we can do to mitigate it, I'm going to close.\n\nWe monitor https://github.com/libressl/portable", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-01-06T17:04:46Z", "body": "Someone would have to trace through our code to remember which code path is calling `DH_check`, and whether its explicit in our code or comes via some other OpenSSL API. But fundamentally its' because we have no idea if a key is trusted or not :-) (And also OpenSSL is terribly unclear about when bad parameters can lead to UB vs. just leading to stupid crypto.)\n\nIf people want an opt-out, for RSA we added an `unsafe_skip_rsa_key_validation` flag and I suppose we could do the same for DH.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "botovq", "timestamp": "2026-01-06T18:24:30Z", "body": "Thanks. I think it always ends up being this call:\n\nhttps://github.com/pyca/cryptography/blob/5a23c88ece74c4a5f1ca46d40b6e987ca9a2f7b2/src/rust/src/backend/dh.rs#L121-L123\n\nBe that as it may, there's now two python projects that were hurt by this. I guess I need to fix this... Sigh. It's 2026 and I still need to deal with DH... :)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-01-06T18:27:52Z", "body": "Yes, that's the one place we call it explicitly -- this path isn't touched on PEM/DER parsing though. (Kind of sus)\n\nFWIW every time I think about this I just want to delete FFDH entirely... alas...\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c8", "author": "botovq", "timestamp": "2026-01-06T18:35:07Z", "body": "The domain parameters are instatiated via `DHParameterNumbers` or similar from constants stored in Scapy's python code itself, for example:\n\nhttps://github.com/secdev/scapy/blob/40fc5ecf9e69e9bd76664d63ae133d4973fcf81b/scapy/layers/tls/crypto/groups.py#L49-L56", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c9", "author": "reaperhulk", "timestamp": "2026-01-06T18:39:46Z", "body": "Hmm, if these were converted to PEM parameters and loaded via `load_pem_parameters` it would possibly remove the `DH_check` unless there's another one buried more deeply inside the functions we call during loading.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c10", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-01-06T19:16:46Z", "body": "I don't think there is (arguably this is dumb, there's no valid reason there should be different checks for one vs the other)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c11", "author": "botovq", "timestamp": "2026-01-06T19:20:13Z", "body": "Neither LibreSSL nor modern OpenSSL call `DH_check()` internally. OpenSSL deprecated `DH_check()`, probably for the wrong reason, namely that it is using a \"legacy primitive\", not because it's a DoS vector (EVP_PKEY_check still is).", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c12", "author": "botovq", "timestamp": "2026-01-06T19:33:52Z", "body": "Anyway. I'll see if I can get approval for landing code that skips the well-known parameters (I have a working diff). The annoying bit is that we don't have the RFC 7919 parameters because, well, why would we?\n\nThis should help pyca/cryptography that currently skips some tests for us because of this and the interactive use of Scapy 2.7.0 seems fine with my patch.\n\nThanks for the input so far!\n\nCc @sthen", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c13", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-01-06T20:20:09Z", "body": "Thank you!\r\n\r\nAll that is necessary for evil to succeed is for good people to do nothing.\r\n\r\nOn Tue, Jan 6, 2026, 2:34\u202fPM Theo Buehler ***@***.***> wrote:\r\n\r\n> *botovq* left a comment (pyca/cryptography#14048)\r\n> <https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/14048#issuecomment-3716031722>\r\n>\r\n> Anyway. I'll see if I can get approval for landing code that skips the\r\n> well-known parameters (I have a working diff). The annoying bit is that we\r\n> don't have the RFC 7919 parameters because, well, why would we?\r\n>\r\n> This should help pyca/cryptography that currently skips some tests for us\r\n> because of this and the interactive use of Scapy 2.7.0 seems fine with my\r\n> patch.\r\n>\r\n> Thanks for the input so far!\r\n>\r\n> Cc @sthen <https://github.com/sthen>\r\n>\r\n> \u2014\r\n> Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\r\n> <https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/14048#issuecomment-3716031722>,\r\n> or unsubscribe\r\n> <https://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AAAAGBDB2J6ECSQ2EOQ2LKD4FQE3NAVCNFSM6AAAAACPZV3XUWVHI2DSMVQWIX3LMV43OSLTON2WKQ3PNVWWK3TUHMZTOMJWGAZTCNZSGI>\r\n> .\r\n> You are receiving this because you modified the open/close state.Message\r\n> ID: ***@***.***>\r\n>\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c14", "author": "botovq", "timestamp": "2026-01-23T12:42:40Z", "body": "> I'll see if I can get approval for landing code that skips the well-known parameters\n\nNow landed, the main bit is here: https://github.com/openbsd/src/commit/9ac14cf7a1b28db2437e849540c0abe6a436a03d and a follow-on added RFC 7919 primes. So this should no longer be a problem with LibreSSL 4.3.0 and later.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/14048#1", "question": "In pyca/cryptography, why is `DHParameterNumbers.parameters()` around 100x slower on OpenBSD/LibreSSL than on Debian/OpenSSL for RFC 3526 modp8192 parameters, and am I doing something wrong?\n\nReproducer:\n```python\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.backends import default_backend\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric.dh import DHParameterNumbers\n\n# modp8192 parameters\n# https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3526 sect 7\ng = 2\nm = \n\npn = DHParameterNumbers(m, g)\nparams = pn.parameters(default_backend())\n```\n\nObserved timings, both in Hyper-V VMs:\n\n| OS | Flavor | Python | Time to run |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Debian 13 | OpenSSL 3.5.1 (FIPS: False, Legacy: True) | 3.13.5 | 0m0.086s real 0m0.069s user 0m0.017s system |\n| OpenBSD 78 | LibreSSL 4.2.0 (FIPS: False, Legacy: False) | 3.12.11 | 1m03.48s real 1m03.32s user 0m00.08s system |", "answer": "This was due to LibreSSL\u2019s handling of DH checks for these parameters, not something the reporter was doing wrong in cryptography. LibreSSL landed code to skip the well-known parameters, with the main change in OpenBSD commit `9ac14cf7a1b28db2437e849540c0abe6a436a03d` and a follow-on adding RFC 7919 primes. The issue should no longer be a problem with LibreSSL 4.3.0 and later.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/openbsd/src/commit/9ac14cf7a1b28db2437e849540c0abe6a436a03d", "LibreSSL 4.3.0"], "answer_grounded_in": "c14", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["9ac14cf7a1b28db2437e849540c0abe6a436a03d", "https://github.com/openbsd/src/commit/9ac14cf7a1b28db2437e849540c0abe6a436a03d"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:56:08.545476Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/14013", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 14013, "title": "unable to load encrypted RSA key with cryptography 46.0.3 - ASN.1 parsing error: unexpected tag", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/14013", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-12-14T09:42:25Z", "closed_at": "2025-12-15T04:11:28Z", "labels": ["waiting-on-reporter"], "reporter": "khomenko", "security_topic": "encrypted RSA private key loading failure due to ASN.1 parsing error", "qa_summary": "The issue with loading encrypted RSA keys is fixed by the commit\u202f3acc7906d25189b11219a7472e1532fa3ad9a1d9, which resolves the ASN\u20111 parsing error.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c3", "question_author": "khomenko", "answer_author": "alex", "answerer_role": "contributor", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "commit_history"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": ["3acc7906d25189b11219a7472e1532fa3ad9a1d9"], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Accepted because the question is a valid ASN.1/RSA key parsing issue and the answer correctly points to the upstream fix commit.", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "khomenko", "timestamp": "2025-12-14T09:42:25Z", "body": "I think I am encountering the same issue as https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/13045\nUnable to read encrypted RSA keys in the traditional PEM format, even the ones I just saved.\n\n```\n>>> cryptography.__version__\n'46.0.3'\n>>> paramiko.__version__\n'2.12.0'\n\nkhomenko@nihao$ openssl genrsa -out test.key -traditional\nkhomenko@nihao$ cat test.key\n-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\nMIIEowIBAAKCAQEAyXGVAo1GwTFjpHOp4AAO0z4QiOHs5Ip9WIxiko4Bx32lN/cQ\nzHeCnaDd8NpNSRuBSR1P/tUgFoft21Xiv/biDlYZ0vtClNmPN7AN755/9C55S/nN\nkNfebIPlnGTmqglZCrFcH899rYk8GRF+wT1S0EuwZiKQ/oKjmODKxggZmF27+WpW\nirDIcyKHA6FkHjghMRQSmxlHuTLEZQoYQwC9HYxFr38MZrWAebzEztqTbfgHD9Di\nBVcIrQQ83fdeMYEJrH2i8d8mnYfqikzXEccMlv6hX1K4F+8z3gzqSmvWZhpRl1Jw\nRRSIWcs5VmkKmF8z1z2q/FfL+43TMcbtuJmwNwIDAQABAoIBAGJAiPpWp+UAzuXe\nGEQOIyz7P5tT/nQ17A8tYcKzisCfUt9qHQJtZAK3/KhGAZ4f0T41EthrYRw8LlJl\noJQauYVej3ftXzs0pfr2XsW53BTgOf9Jlu8Hqw/ogSx3tl3G82wYt6M4U8c5gVuE\nwftEisjXsfEO+QN5+otNUPmn57v9VltwD7bBzcJP/3bRqSH8M7Strp2SX+H7qhmq\nIfknn14WZfj65AvPoWtmVF59IU6ayWwkH9NgqgD7laZkgw6QwuW8axiLl8GfZ8Jt\n8BTl/kIh4/0IXT4UrYfQBTPz0rkg+NEdMs3c07bn/d99ug3scchX8PPhm9TnDN0y\nDCAkNMECgYEA6fEc8WqRCsKb3b0yZmSaTB108yWDTLXZzdagcyU0Co0X5vP994Iq\nH4k1kuktpAv+lIT2FGm//KSLmPzYpJW1BcCtFgsWdjZe7t/2tFI91F7GB2qdkl4A\n59e5rPWPShGaBSTmlQJTn4+vVxiK2Edmz3kReTHb/FVgC8lJH6e6IaECgYEA3HAH\nRvEh6D9GrlicyRw/7p4XBQlQxe+oWGN/9+J3R6hc0uV+yGtj+UYgs15EdmN0jMN/\nDAxNJ7bVT/zgiI3nN6VPnmSWiSqUniPxo0IP6u0S3wQbCmWXW2O8I2AU4maOK5QM\nywzwCUh9T+TMOHQsPDCKerAng2K41DI5izD2MtcCgYEAhyVHNx/9El5CdOKrjwNn\nL7mEqkJti48yd/sZTeucGMxRlpY1jRqhAv7c+DXErBRRybLWBgzhYC4ehw7qiIoD\nvYMFesqE+12FkjHH73vjRCBL/lTVGo07sLOrDAP/9yYb+ATNQB/L902mWYZQD4mQ\nFyM1uPHYx/j6N1Tc7XCT0oECgYBqz1GHW1FtojNSSRd3Dtwfd7R5uUDNF8m/Qj8A\nUlgvfXG0soSTAg4H+6Xeo3+Sya/wgM1vfgpogturDUGSiQeO2+fPPLgAMISgd5Qe\n4eSqlUK+ktOzXvRO0O2ZSnX0GXMrxGnKfTNVnC0z6A3YENpaM771oaMTs2MRVmCx\nhADXMwKBgAE/hL3Z2rjEasmzoqHE9cirY37XfRxozD46SByrZ3Pxa82WzsfslpVR\nKcsBf5WgMjC4CRO+ohq/7HseGIyl9tI4CF1pspJX1c186v6Q4b2Tp+7/Ms0bZ2Q2\nDEwgpc4zxcSw465zrQZQPCOJ4cbJ0TFe4yhGPhIvIn6QWLwJxAEZ\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\n\n>>> from paramiko.rsakey import RSAKey\n>>> key = RSAKey.from_private_key_file(\"test.key\", \"\")\n>>> key.write_private_key_file(\"test.pem\", \"foo\")\n>>> key = RSAKey.from_private_key_file(\"test.pem\", \"foo\")\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/site/venv/lib/python3.10/site-packages/paramiko/rsakey.py\", line 205, in _decode_key\n    key = serialization.load_der_private_key(\nValueError: Could not deserialize key data. The data may be in an incorrect format, it may be encrypted with an unsupported algorithm, or it may be an unsupported key type (e.g. EC curves with explicit parameters). Details: ASN.1 parsing error: unexpected tag (got Tag { value: 2, constructed: false, class: Universal })\n\nDuring handling of the above exception, another exception occurred:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/usr/lib/python3.10/code.py\", line 90, in runcode\n    exec(code, self.locals)\n  File \"<console>\", line 1, in <module>\n  File \"/site/venv/lib/python3.10/site-packages/paramiko/pkey.py\", line 249, in from_private_key_file\n    key = cls(filename=filename, password=password)\n  File \"/site/venv/lib/python3.10/site-packages/paramiko/rsakey.py\", line 64, in __init__\n    self._from_private_key_file(filename, password)\n  File \"/site/venv/lib/python3.10/site-packages/paramiko/rsakey.py\", line 195, in _from_private_key_file\n    self._decode_key(data)\n  File \"/site/venv/lib/python3.10/site-packages/paramiko/rsakey.py\", line 209, in _decode_key\n    raise SSHException(str(e))\nparamiko.ssh_exception.SSHException: Could not deserialize key data. The data may be in an incorrect format, it may be encrypted with an unsupported algorithm, or it may be an unsupported key type (e.g. EC curves with explicit parameters). Details: ASN.1 parsing error: unexpected tag (got Tag { value: 2, constructed: false, class: Universal })\n\nkhomenko@nihao$ cat test.pem\n-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\nProc-Type: 4,ENCRYPTED\nDEK-Info: AES-256-CBC,092DF32A5794EDB4D4696CA0E78A42FB\n\n3YnhqptqQqrs+OTVBBk71xq0C4WGmFKHV9j9949q/RojFYa2f46D5XLmrOuCnrxS\n2JNhqrOGQ7xK3VixS5/98qwiRowMLq+Ee3hbFHIl34LmGB+W5LZiEoNjSAu73/D3\n7pdlpc+cVrfXkNip3za0yrY+IzZDunCsGapKCpp20TcxkR9UZnw7MvtJb/9ko0Yx\ndc8vuVvTmEJvXl7P/SA+oz6FwK+2J/VEODjZOmoZcBiifS7qiRE+DQZEpBHp9aHR\nbwGwtUsyu2k3Gdy+mouq1mz2hnH0CEjrVsyiWD/TEGDTVMhR+ACodvKd+1+N0qvr\nXr8dCh9YRnnaKCWWzKbL7Z8DIZ40hVAAhvuxpEZVafLY49bjTK9d1dwCRSzk/XUz\nOPOhG7IfO8ovAJIW8QmtsG38FX2pdXCBXhkcQnX56I99C1KjVbnLMFPBP5cNVne9\nH0UR4fzPwssgJqfAXTBkNeixxUvKhkp42/Fpoww1ogu6lUY38TJXycJdhoD5Qp0S\nF/lq5f4H9WJqCxgxEuAoT90x2E1U5Df9ob5+aPWt0sZFZ+T/rgnP0OF2sKKgfI7Y\nWJoaWYm7wZ/7HSVhPx6IQM2u7cYmTMoWQrhSiq0TcXYgNE1SdHGYT7ptEvyRLu4C\n9QFj/fprTdXJYfOrcIl5REq9oWoa76o4Zk6ecyB5fYHeZUdohn1oEFg7p7aGQe1g\nuk1/YEE/7rAlQXJDsXYxGqaEz+c0+OuTn8g1m5YtDR4ERdoowvyGwDM9qurZlhXH\nVrTDgEWWM/tT8uPZ4H7yIbd8lk4pT46dKR7NaxoD/nCgn0bZxXjA8+UJo6xw0e88\nrLN4rS5T7YNYxBAMBaEbHYABX432c1HscMqHV63eoySSQ8Y2TnY28a+DYACKJwV5\nZsahM6Rt5FTsmxt5g6EMEmxBhM65bFXIJJ+K1J6jI/J58u1TEa/Eys3jPUE+IcNV\njuixXhMxKypLjS0Cdpy46qPODMmlM75Hse4DR1xRdnzum5c1+mPRMweZHd3mhtba\nSu0AgU3Wts0C0JjLzo5fd0wHLjtKnv8s7n+QvyHiq9s/6bmMvoC0SwLxT6AXlUwA\nvNbdPvRrGYi0295N4F6AoEBqdXPWpu4P0NCaPJq5v6ikixJF2wcYdLTVrBEjoTrc\ntSz5ryHNF8RPVy9oP1TOwWiCVw1zbYL2gS3E7aS40p1TClZxvqCVVZ6QFueZUJPq\nsah9t7dMn7kGF3aEzBwTxnEA1buKJyyfbjJxsJciz8hCVjr2mmA8ffqIRD8OfkS7\nne5vDnjV6rt5QXP/tftwgnym2l3McJKpO3z8J0LA2/m52B3OLn/b8MdJ+HxXJbEN\nDPuoh49+88G9mPKIgkPHJAQoRlK08bK4Q4Lk1jsZa1nGdEW8/f0l4fSaiLiFVLc+\ndW8/lx+XdZ6DtgL3X0nNu1uP0xnPOez58qZTO1xqxTmATbHEcamuCf6kQyRaZukQ\nbz5v3RNt5VSTf0OTsyGwkHEWackj6J7viNevhiq+0f+wag6cdtVnGZdiwY00p39a\nkMYKpIKTEmsuVRqVsaEOaf7rOpd3sH6OyEAafsH96I9ax/+91CT1iOFJi/2i/o7u\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\n```\n\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "reaperhulk", "timestamp": "2025-12-14T16:04:33Z", "body": "This appears to be a very old version of paramiko. I can't replicate it with the current version -- does upgrading resolve your issue?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "khomenko", "timestamp": "2025-12-14T16:34:05Z", "body": "> This appears to be a very old version of paramiko. I can't replicate it with the current version -- does upgrading resolve your issue?\n\nIt's on our roadmap to upgrade, but unfortunately it's not trivial because of some dependencies...", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-12-14T17:59:18Z", "body": "It seems likely to me that its https://github.com/paramiko/paramiko/commit/3acc7906d25189b11219a7472e1532fa3ad9a1d9.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "khomenko", "timestamp": "2025-12-15T04:11:28Z", "body": "> It seems likely to me that its [paramiko/paramiko@3acc790](https://github.com/paramiko/paramiko/commit/3acc7906d25189b11219a7472e1532fa3ad9a1d9).\n\nBingo. This patch makes it work. Thank you.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/14013#1", "question": "With cryptography 46.0.3 and Paramiko 2.12.0, I think I am encountering the same issue as https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/13045: I am unable to read encrypted RSA keys in the traditional PEM format, even ones I just saved. Reproduction:\n\n```\n>>> cryptography.__version__\n'46.0.3'\n>>> paramiko.__version__\n'2.12.0'\n\nkhomenko@nihao$ openssl genrsa -out test.key -traditional\nkhomenko@nihao$ cat test.key\n-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\nMIIEowIBAAKCAQEAyXGVAo1GwTFjpHOp4AAO0z4QiOHs5Ip9WIxiko4Bx32lN/cQ\nzHeCnaDd8NpNSRuBSR1P/tUgFoft21Xiv/biDlYZ0vtClNmPN7AN755/9C55S/nN\nkNfebIPlnGTmqglZCrFcH899rYk8GRF+wT1S0EuwZiKQ/oKjmODKxggZmF27+WpW\nirDIcyKHA6FkHjghMRQSmxlHuTLEZQoYQwC9HYxFr38MZrWAebzEztqTbfgHD9Di\nBVcIrQQ83fdeMYEJrH2i8d8mnYfqikzXEccMlv6hX1K4F+8z3gzqSmvWZhpRl1Jw\nRRSIWcs5VmkKmF8z1z2q/FfL+43TMcbtuJmwNwIDAQABAoIBAGJAiPpWp+UAzuXe\nGEQOIyz7P5tT/nQ17A8tYcKzisCfUt9qHQJtZAK3/KhGAZ4f0T41EthrYRw8LlJl\noJQauYVej3ftXzs0pfr2XsW53BTgOf9Jlu8Hqw/ogSx3tl3G82wYt6M4U8c5gVuE\nwftEisjXsfEO+QN5+otNUPmn57v9VltwD7bBzcJP/3bRqSH8M7Strp2SX+H7qhmq\nIfknn14WZfj65AvPoWtmVF59IU6ayWwkH9NgqgD7laZkgw6QwuW8axiLl8GfZ8Jt\n8BTl/kIh4/0IXT4UrYfQBTPz0rkg+NEdMs3c07bn/d99ug3scchX8PPhm9TnDN0y\nDCAkNMECgYEA6fEc8WqRCsKb3b0yZmSaTB108yWDTLXZzdagcyU0Co0X5vP994Iq\nH4k1kuktpAv+lIT2FGm//KSLmPzYpJW1BcCtFgsWdjZe7t/2tFI91F7GB2qdkl4A\n59e5rPWPShGaBSTmlQJTn4+vVxiK2Edmz3kReTHb/FVgC8lJH6e6IaECgYEA3HAH\nRvEh6D9GrlicyRw/7p4XBQlQxe+oWGN/9+J3R6hc0uV+yGtj+UYgs15EdmN0jMN/\nDAxNJ7bVT/zgiI3nN6VPnmSWiSqUniPxo0IP6u0S3wQbCmWXW2O8I2AU4maOK5QM\nywzwCUh9T+TMOHQsPDCKerAng2K41DI5izD2MtcCgYEAhyVHNx/9El5CdOKrjwNn\nL7mEqkJti48yd/sZTeucGMxRlpY1jRqhAv7c+DXErBRRybLWBgzhYC4ehw7qiIoD\nvYMFesqE+12FkjHH73vjRCBL/lTVGo07sLOrDAP/9yYb+ATNQB/L902mWYZQD4mQ\nFyM1uPHYx/j6N1Tc7XCT0oECgYBqz1GHW1FtojNSSRd3Dtwfd7R5uUDNF8m/Qj8A\nUlgvfXG0soSTAg4H+6Xeo3+Sya/wgM1vfgpogturDUGSiQeO2+fPPLgAMISgd5Qe\n4eSqlUK+ktOzXvRO0O2ZSnX0GXMrxGnKfTNVnC0z6A3YENpaM771oaMTs2MRVmCx\nhADXMwKBgAE/hL3Z2rjEasmzoqHE9cirY37XfRxozD46SByrZ3Pxa82WzsfslpVR\nKcsBf5WgMjC4CRO+ohq/7HseGIyl9tI4CF1pspJX1c186v6Q4b2Tp+7/Ms0bZ2Q2\nDEwgpc4zxcSw465zrQZQPCOJ4cbJ0TFe4yhGPhIvIn6QWLwJxAEZ\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\n\n>>> from paramiko.rsakey import RSAKey\n>>> key = RSAKey.from_private_key_file(\"test.key\", \"\")\n>>> key.write_private_key_file(\"test.pem\", \"foo\")\n>>> key = RSAKey.from_private_key_file(\"test.pem\", \"foo\")\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/site/venv/lib/python3.10/site-packages/paramiko/rsakey.py\", line 205, in _decode_key\n    key = serialization.load_der_private_key(\nValueError: Could not deserialize key data. The data may be in an incorrect format, it may be encrypted with an unsupported algorithm, or it may be an unsupported key type (e.g. EC curves with explicit parameters). Details: ASN.1 parsing error: unexpected tag (got Tag { value: 2, constructed: false, class: Universal })\n\nDuring handling of the above exception, another exception occurred:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/usr/lib/python3.10/code.py\", line 90, in runcode\n    exec(code, self.locals)\n  File \"<console>\", line 1, in <module>\n  File \"/site/venv/lib/python3.10/site-packages/paramiko/pkey.py\", line 249, in from_private_key_file\n    key = cls(filename=filename, password=password)\n  File \"/site/venv/lib/python3.10/site-packages/paramiko/rsakey.py\", line 64, in __init__\n    self._from_private_key_file(filename, password)\n  File \"/site/venv/lib/python3.10/site-packages/paramiko/rsakey.py\", line 195, in _from_private_key_file\n    self._decode_key(data)\n  File \"/site/venv/lib/python3.10/site-packages/paramiko/rsakey.py\", line 209, in _decode_key\n    raise SSHException(str(e))\nparamiko.ssh_exception.SSHException: Could not deserialize key data. The data may be in an incorrect format, it may be encrypted with an unsupported algorithm, or it may be an unsupported key type (e.g. EC curves with explicit parameters). Details: ASN.1 parsing error: unexpected tag (got Tag { value: 2, constructed: false, class: Universal })\n\nkhomenko@nihao$ cat test.pem\n-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\nProc-Type: 4,ENCRYPTED\nDEK-Info: AES-256-CBC,092DF32A5794EDB4D4696CA0E78A42FB\n\n3YnhqptqQqrs+OTVBBk71xq0C4WGmFKHV9j9949q/RojFYa2f46D5XLmrOuCnrxS\n2JNhqrOGQ7xK3VixS5/98qwiRowMLq+Ee3hbFHIl34LmGB+W5LZiEoNjSAu73/D3\n7pdlpc+cVrfXkNip3za0yrY+IzZDunCsGapKCpp20TcxkR9UZnw7MvtJb/9ko0Yx\ndc8vuVvTmEJvXl7P/SA+oz6FwK+2J/VEODjZOmoZcBiifS7qiRE+DQZEpBHp9aHR\nbwGwtUsyu2k3Gdy+mouq1mz2hnH0CEjrVsyiWD/TEGDTVMhR+ACodvKd+1+N0qvr\nXr8dCh9YRnnaKCWWzKbL7Z8DIZ40hVAAhvuxpEZVafLY49bjTK9d1dwCRSzk/XUz\nOPOhG7IfO8ovAJIW8QmtsG38FX2pdXCBXhkcQnX56I99C1KjVbnLMFPBP5cNVne9\nH0UR4fzPwssgJqfAXTBkNeixxUvKhkp42/Fpoww1ogu6lUY38TJXycJdhoD5Qp0S\nF/lq5f4H9WJqCxgxEuAoT90x2E1U5Df9ob5+aPWt0sZFZ+T/rgnP0OF2sKKgfI7Y\nWJoaWYm7wZ/7HSVhPx6IQM2u7cYmTMoWQrhSiq0TcXYgNE1SdHGYT7ptEvyRLu4C\n9QFj/fprTdXJYfOrcIl5REq9oWoa76o4Zk6ecyB5fYHeZUdohn1oEFg7p7aGQe1g\nuk1/YEE/7rAlQXJDsXYxGqaEz+c0+OuTn8g1m5YtDR4ERdoowvyGwDM9qurZlhXH\nVrTDgEWWM/tT8uPZ4H7yIbd8lk4pT46dKR7NaxoD/nCgn0bZxXjA8+UJo6xw0e88\nrLN4rS5T7YNYxBAMBaEbHYABX432c1HscMqHV63eoySSQ8Y2TnY28a+DYACKJwV5\nZsahM6Rt5FTsmxt5g6EMEmxBhM65bFXIJJ+K1J6jI/J58u1TEa/Eys3jPUE+IcNV\njuixXhMxKypLjS0Cdpy46qPODMmlM75Hse4DR1xRdnzum5c1+mPRMweZHd3mhtba\nSu0AgU3Wts0C0JjLzo5fd0wHLjtKnv8s7n+QvyHiq9s/6bmMvoC0SwLxT6AXlUwA\nvNbdPvRrGYi0295N4F6AoEBqdXPWpu4P0NCaPJq5v6ikixJF2wcYdLTVrBEjoTrc\ntSz5ryHNF8RPVy9oP1TOwWiCVw1zbYL2gS3E7aS40p1TClZxvqCVVZ6QFueZUJPq\nsah9t7dMn7kGF3aEzBwTxnEA1buKJyyfbjJxsJciz8hCVjr2mmA8ffqIRD8OfkS7\nne5vDnjV6rt5QXP/tftwgnym2l3McJKpO3z8J0LA2/m52B3OLn/b8MdJ+HxXJbEN\nDPuoh49+88G9mPKIgkPHJAQoRlK08bK4Q4Lk1jsZa1nGdEW8/f0l4fSaiLiFVLc+\ndW8/lx+XdZ6DtgL3X0nNu1uP0xnPOez58qZTO1xqxTmATbHEcamuCf6kQyRaZukQ\nbz5v3RNt5VSTf0OTsyGwkHEWackj6J7viNevhiq+0f+wag6cdtVnGZdiwY00p39a\nkMYKpIKTEmsuVRqVsaEOaf7rOpd3sH6OyEAafsH96I9ax/+91CT1iOFJi/2i/o7u\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\n```\n\nIs this a cryptography issue, and is there a known fix or patch for this failure?", "answer": "The maintainer indicated that the likely relevant fix was Paramiko commit `https://github.com/paramiko/paramiko/commit/3acc7906d25189b11219a7472e1532fa3ad9a1d9`. The reporter confirmed that applying that patch made the reproduction work.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/paramiko/paramiko/commit/3acc7906d25189b11219a7472e1532fa3ad9a1d9"], "answer_grounded_in": "c3", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["3acc7906d25189b11219a7472e1532fa3ad9a1d9", "https://github.com/paramiko/paramiko/commit/3acc7906d25189b11219a7472e1532fa3ad9a1d9"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-16T03:13:20.013000Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/13349", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 13349, "title": "Add binding for SSL_get0_group_name to support PQC key exchange group detection", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/13349", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-08-24T18:23:45Z", "closed_at": "2025-09-27T18:29:49Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "toddabrams", "security_topic": "retrieving negotiated TLS group name for PQC detection", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know if adding a binding for SSL_get0_group_name to expose negotiated TLS group names is acceptable, and the maintainers confirmed it by merging cryptography PR\u202f#13384 and pyOpenSSL PR\u202f#1442.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "toddabrams", "answer_author": "reaperhulk", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "toddabrams", "timestamp": "2025-08-24T18:23:45Z", "body": "# Background\nI am a developer for a python tool which uses pyOpenSSL to initiate handshakes and probe servers to understand their level of TLS support. I am planning to extend this tool to capture post-quantum cryptography (PQC) readiness by detecting which key exchange groups are actually negotiated during connections.\n\nPer [OpenSSL docs]( https://docs.openssl.org/3.5/man3/SSL_CTX_set1_curves/#history):\n\n> Support for ML-KEM was added in OpenSSL 3.5.\n> \n> OpenSSL 3.5 also introduces support for three hybrid ECDH PQ key exchange TLS groups: X25519MLKEM768, SecP256r1MLKEM768 and SecP384r1MLKEM1024.  \n\nIf the conditions are right, one of these hybrid key exchange groups may be used, and I'd like to be able to use pyOpenSSL to capture which group was actually negotiated.\n\nFrom the [OpenSSL docs](https://docs.openssl.org/master/man3/SSL_CTX_set1_curves/#description), `SSL_get0_group_name` is preferred for this:\n\n> See also [SSL_get0_group_name(3)](https://docs.openssl.org/master/man3/SSL_get0_group_name/) which returns the name of the negotiated group directly and is generally preferred over SSL_get_negotiated_group().\n\nHere is a link to the openssl source for [SSL_get0_group_name](https://github.com/openssl/openssl/blob/53eb2363a1e6336a40a64b3f7b9f09eca95fabef/ssl/s3_lib.c#L5244) and [PR where it was introduced](https://github.com/openssl/openssl/commit/68668243b176cd2bc53a83c6768d4f39930ba8ed) as of OpenSSL 3.2.\n\nNote: While `SSL_get0_group_name` was introduced in OpenSSL 3.2, the hybrid PQC groups that motivate this request require OpenSSL 3.5.\n\n# Request\nI would like to make contributions to both repositories:\n1. **cryptography**: Add bindings for `SSL_get0_group_name`. I'm planning to add the binding following the patterns I see for similar functions. My understanding is that it may need appropriate version guards (OpenSSL 3.2+); and looking into how to implement it (looking at things like this which is not quite the same situation as mine): https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/blob/5cf5edf1b36afc8400e5b6d3975d0b15380f3eff/src/cryptography/hazmat/backends/openssl/backend.py#L288. \n2. **pyOpenSSL**: Expose this as a new method (e.g., `get_group_name()`) in the SSL module's [Connection class](https://github.com/pyca/pyopenssl/blob/main/src/OpenSSL/SSL.py#L1948), similar conceptually to `get_cipher_list()`.\n\n# Status\n\nSo far, I've:\n- followed the [development docs](https://cryptography.io/en/latest/development/) and `nox -e local` succeeds in my local environment\n- been reading through c-bindings.rst and ssl.py to understand how I would add a binding given that `SSL_get0_group_name` is only available \n- joined the `#pyca` IRC chat \n\n# Other discussions\n\nI noticed there have been some similar-ish discussions, for example around setting curves: https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/10797. This was an approach I initially considered.\n\nHowever, I noticed in the [release notes for OpenSSL 3.5.0](https://github.com/openssl/openssl/blob/openssl-3.5/CHANGES.md#openssl-35) that it appears that at least one of the hybrid PQC groups, `X25519MLKEM768`, is now set by default:\n\n> The default TLS group list setting is now set to: ?*X25519MLKEM768 / ?*X25519:?secp256r1 / ?X448:?secp384r1:?secp521r1 / ?ffdhe2048:?ffdhe3072\n> \n> This means two key shares (X25519MLKEM768 and X25519) will be sent by default by the TLS client. GOST groups and FFDHE groups larger than 3072 bits are no longer enabled by default.\n\nI'm a bit new to this area, but either:\na) This means that if a server is configured with `X25519MLKEM768` for the key change group, that my tool will initiate a handshake with it and the group would be negotiated preferentially.\nb) Non-PQC groups would need to be excluded by using a setter such as `SSL_CTX_set1_groups_list`, in which case I'd need to make contributions for this method as well in both pyOpenSSL and cryptography.\n\n\n\n\n\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "reaperhulk", "timestamp": "2025-08-27T20:30:35Z", "body": "Sorry for the slow response here. Part of the reason we've taken a while to reply is that the PQC methods are starting to cause non-trivial divergence in the APIs of OpenSSL and its forks (boring, libre, and aws-lc) so we shy away from even thinking about it on occasion.\n\n`SSL_get0_group_name` returns a `char *` of the negotiated group name, but while it's not officially documented looking at the code it appears to be the group name as defined by the TLS specs. I would be okay with that being exposed here. Eventually this binding would also need to live in [rust-openssl](https://github.com/sfackler/rust-openssl) since we're migrating that direction over time as well, but we can land it here first.\n\nBefore we merge here we'd like to see the PR opened on the pyOpenSSL side though (knowing all tests will fail of course) so we can agree on the API and see how the binding will be consumed.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "ronf", "timestamp": "2025-08-28T02:27:08Z", "body": "I did some work a few weeks ago on a similar feature to this in the Python standard library's SSL module. See https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/136306 for details. More specifically, it added the ability to get a list of all supported groups for a given TLS version range and to choose the set of groups to try and use as methods on an SSLContext object, and then later query the group selected on using a method on an SSLSocket object once the handshake is complete. The specific OpenSSL functions used are `SSL_CTX_get0_implemented_groups()`, `SSL_CTX_set1_groups_list()`, and `SSL_get0_group_name()` (which you mention here).\n\nI'm in the middle of working on adding the ability to select TLS 1.3 cipher suites and to set and get client and server TLS 1.2/1.3 signature algorithms.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "toddabrams", "timestamp": "2025-09-02T19:59:45Z", "body": "@reaperhulk Thanks for the thoughtful response! Acknowledging that this will also need to live in the `rust-openssl` eventually but will start here. I've just opened a PR https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/pull/13384 for the change, and will start on the corresponding one for pyOpenSSL per your comment. \n\n@ronf I wasn't aware, thanks for sharing! I'm only exposing `SSL_get0_group_name` in my first PR, but may also want to expose the other two OpenSSL group functions in the near future.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "toddabrams", "timestamp": "2025-09-10T20:48:33Z", "body": "@reaperhulk Here is the corresponding (draft) PR in pyOpenSSL clarifying the API and how the binding will be consumed: [pyca/pyopenssl#1442](https://github.com/pyca/pyopenssl/pull/1442)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "ronf", "timestamp": "2025-09-11T11:59:23Z", "body": "In addition to https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/136306, I've finished the other work I mentioned above and the changes have all been merged into python:main. The related issues are https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/137197 and https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/138252. You can also see a summary of all these changes at https://docs.python.org/3.15/whatsnew/3.15.html#ssl.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "toddabrams", "timestamp": "2025-09-24T00:19:15Z", "body": "Thanks for all the support, @reaperhulk, looks like both PRs have been merged!\n* https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/pull/13384\n* https://github.com/pyca/pyopenssl/pull/1442\n\nI can next look into what's needed to add support for getting the group in [rust-openssl](https://github.com/sfackler/rust-openssl).\n\n", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/13349#1", "question": "I maintain a Python tool that uses pyOpenSSL to initiate TLS handshakes and probe servers for their TLS support. I want to extend it to capture post-quantum cryptography readiness by detecting which key exchange group is actually negotiated during connections.\n\nOpenSSL 3.5 added ML-KEM support and hybrid ECDH/PQ TLS groups such as `X25519MLKEM768`, `SecP256r1MLKEM768`, and `SecP384r1MLKEM1024`. OpenSSL documentation says `SSL_get0_group_name` is preferred for retrieving the negotiated group name:\n\n> See also SSL_get0_group_name(3) which returns the name of the negotiated group directly and is generally preferred over SSL_get_negotiated_group().\n\n`SSL_get0_group_name` was introduced in OpenSSL 3.2, while the hybrid PQC groups motivating this request require OpenSSL 3.5. The OpenSSL source for `SSL_get0_group_name` is at https://github.com/openssl/openssl/blob/53eb2363a1e6336a40a64b3f7b9f09eca95fabef/ssl/s3_lib.c#L5244, and it was introduced in https://github.com/openssl/openssl/commit/68668243b176cd2bc53a83c6768d4f39930ba8ed.\n\nI would like to contribute support in two places:\n\n1. In `cryptography`, add bindings for `SSL_get0_group_name`, likely with appropriate OpenSSL 3.2+ version guards, following the patterns for similar functions. One place I looked at for version-guarding patterns is https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/blob/5cf5edf1b36afc8400e5b6d3975d0b15380f3eff/src/cryptography/hazmat/backends/openssl/backend.py#L288.\n2. In `pyOpenSSL`, expose this as a new method such as `get_group_name()` on the SSL module's `Connection` class, similar conceptually to `get_cipher_list()`.\n\nMy local `nox -e local` succeeds, I have been reading `c-bindings.rst` and `ssl.py`, and I have joined `#pyca` IRC. I also noticed related discussion around setting curves in https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/10797.\n\nGiven that OpenSSL 3.5.0 release notes say the default TLS group list now includes `?*X25519MLKEM768 / ?*X25519:?secp256r1 / ?X448:?secp384r1:?secp521r1 / ?ffdhe2048:?ffdhe3072`, I am unsure whether a client will negotiate `X25519MLKEM768` preferentially if the server supports it, or whether non-PQC groups would need to be excluded using something like `SSL_CTX_set1_groups_list`, which would require additional bindings.\n\nWould adding an `SSL_get0_group_name` binding to `cryptography` and exposing it in pyOpenSSL be acceptable, and is there anything specific about the API or implementation approach I should account for?", "answer": "The maintainer said they would be okay with exposing `SSL_get0_group_name`. They noted that PQC-related APIs are diverging across OpenSSL and its forks, and that `SSL_get0_group_name` appears from the code to return the negotiated group name as defined by the TLS specs, though this is not officially documented. Before merging the cryptography binding, they wanted to see the pyOpenSSL PR first so the API and binding consumption could be agreed on; eventually the binding would also need to live in `rust-openssl` as cryptography migrates in that direction.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["OpenSSL SSL_get0_group_name code", "pyOpenSSL", "rust-openssl"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://github.com/sfackler/rust-openssl"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T17:42:09.057907Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/13125", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 13125, "title": "Since 45.0, `load_pem_private_key` allows arbitrary garbage after `-----END PRIVATE KEY-----`", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/13125", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-07-01T12:17:40Z", "closed_at": "2025-09-27T18:30:34Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "bluetech", "security_topic": "PEM parsing permissiveness for trailing data", "qa_summary": "The developer asked whether accepting arbitrary data after the PEM END line is a security issue, and the maintainer answered that RFC\u202f7468 permits such data (see RFC\u202f7468 \u00a72).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c3", "question_author": "bluetech", "answer_author": "nbastin", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": ["https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7468#section-2"]}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "bluetech", "timestamp": "2025-07-01T12:17:40Z", "body": "Since version 45.0, `load_pem_private_key` accepts arbitrary \"garbage\" after the `-----END PRIVATE KEY-----` line. Previous versions would fail with the error below. Maybe it's intentional, but it feels like something that *might* not have been intentional so I'm reporting it (can't hurt). It doesn't affect me personally, I only noticed it because I have a test case which tries to make a PEM private key input invalid by appending `b\"xxx\"` to it which no longer fails (though I should note that it seems adding `b\"\\nxxx\"` doesn't fail in previous versions either).\n\nExample code:\n\n```py\nimport os\n\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives import serialization\n\n\nPRIVATE_KEY = b\"\"\"-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\nMIGHAgEAMBMGByqGSM49AgEGCCqGSM49AwEHBG0wawIBAQQglcJB9Do845/R18ef\nButMVXFbRqmo/F9kdsIbgTSbDUWhRANCAAR+OeTq+FCd8eRNt9IU16kP3SZ5W8Pj\ndS0YwItKLwTyDA4zTupEPWWqAikmw7Xv9DAgiDVFLbzP3C+3mjYmvUkL\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\"\"\"\n\n# OK\nserialization.load_pem_private_key(PRIVATE_KEY, password=None)\n\n# Previously would fail, now passes\nserialization.load_pem_private_key(PRIVATE_KEY + os.urandom(1000), password=None)\n```\n\nError in 44:\n\n```\nValueError: ('Could not deserialize key data. The data may be in an incorrect format, the provided password may be incorrect, it may be encrypted with an unsupported algorithm, or it may be an unsupported key type (e.g. EC curves with explicit parameters).', [<OpenSSLError(code=503841036, lib=60, reason=524556, reason_text=unsupported)>])\n```", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-07-01T12:27:49Z", "body": "Hmm, interesting -- I'm surprised that garbage was previously rejected. Historically most of the bugs we've gotten are that we've been _too strict_, and we specifically loosened PEM parsing as a result of that.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "bluetech", "timestamp": "2025-07-01T13:00:33Z", "body": "Yes I thought it might be intentional.\n\nI was thinking about suggesting a \"strict\" mode but it isn't worth it IMO.\n\nEither way, it's a bad idea to store or forward user PEM inputs directly, instead should load+serialize and store that instead (preferably in DER format).", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "nbastin", "timestamp": "2025-07-10T00:32:14Z", "body": "As a person who every once in a while pops up to be one of the squeaky wheels here, the current behaviour is (arguably) correct.  RFC 7468 (which is more of a reflection of reality at the time than an actual spec, which never existed during the time when many of these systems were built) makes clear that _any_ data before encapsulation boundaries is permitted (and states parsers MUST NOT malfunction when processing such data - [section 2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7468#section-2)).  It is ambiguous as to whether data is \"before\" an encapsulation boundary if you never find another `BEGIN`, but plenty of systems generate such files and thus failing to parse them puts a burden on users.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/13125#1", "question": "In pyca/cryptography, since version 45.0, `serialization.load_pem_private_key` accepts arbitrary garbage after the `-----END PRIVATE KEY-----` line. Previous versions failed. Is this intentional/correct behavior?\n\nExample code:\n\n```py\nimport os\n\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives import serialization\n\n\nPRIVATE_KEY = b\"\"\"-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\nMIGHAgEAMBMGByqGSM49AgEGCCqGSM49AwEHBG0wawIBAQQglcJB9Do845/R18ef\nButMVXFbRqmo/F9kdsIbgTSbDUWhRANCAAR+OeTq+FCd8eRNt9IU16kP3SZ5W8Pj\ndS0YwItKLwTyDA4zTupEPWWqAikmw7Xv9DAgiDVFLbzP3C+3mjYmvUkL\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\"\"\"\n\n# OK\nserialization.load_pem_private_key(PRIVATE_KEY, password=None)\n\n# Previously would fail, now passes\nserialization.load_pem_private_key(PRIVATE_KEY + os.urandom(1000), password=None)\n```\n\nError in 44:\n\n```text\nValueError: ('Could not deserialize key data. The data may be in an incorrect format, the provided password may be incorrect, it may be encrypted with an unsupported algorithm, or it may be an unsupported key type (e.g. EC curves with explicit parameters).', [<OpenSSLError(code=503841036, lib=60, reason=524556, reason_text=unsupported)>])\n```\n\nI noticed that appending `b\"xxx\"` no longer fails, while adding `b\"\\nxxx\"` did not fail in previous versions either.", "answer": "The current behavior is considered arguably correct. RFC 7468 section 2 permits any data before encapsulation boundaries and says parsers MUST NOT malfunction when processing such data; although it is ambiguous whether trailing data is \u201cbefore\u201d a boundary if no further `BEGIN` is found, many systems generate such files, so rejecting them would burden users.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7468#section-2"], "answer_grounded_in": "c3", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7468#section-2"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:58:34.123012Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/13051", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 13051, "title": "Cryptography parsed the CRL file with an empty Key Identifier.", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/13051", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-06-07T14:47:11Z", "closed_at": "2025-06-19T00:00:15Z", "labels": ["waiting-on-reporter", "Stale"], "reporter": "onepeople158", "security_topic": "whether empty AuthorityKeyIdentifier extension violates RFC 5280", "qa_summary": "Developer asked whether an empty AuthorityKeyIdentifier extension in a CRL violates RFC\u202f5280 and poses a security issue; the maintainer answered that RFC\u202f5280 does not explicitly forbid an empty AKI and cited the relevant RFC section as the basis.", "question_comment_id": "c2", "answer_comment_id": "c3", "question_author": "onepeople158", "answer_author": "onepeople158", "answerer_role": "op_self", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Accepted because the question is a valid RFC 5280 compliance concern and the answer correctly analyzes the specification to confirm no explicit violation.", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "onepeople158", "timestamp": "2025-06-07T14:47:11Z", "body": "Version: 45.0.3\n\nHello Developer, I successfully parsed a CRL file with an empty Key Identifier using Cryptography.When I used GnuTLS to parse this CRL file, it returned an error: error: gnutls_x509_ext_import_authority_key_id: ASN1 parser: Error in DER parsing. Is this considered an error?\n\nTest Case:\n\n[crl_empty_key.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/20639268/crl_empty_key.zip)\n\nCode:\n```\nfrom cryptography.x509 import load_pem_x509_crl, load_der_x509_crl\nfrom cryptography.x509 import ExtensionNotFound\nimport sys\n\ndef load_crl(file_path):\n    with open(file_path, \"rb\") as f:\n        crl_data = f.read()\n    try:\n        crl = load_pem_x509_crl(crl_data)\n    except ValueError:\n        crl = load_der_x509_crl(crl_data)\n    return crl\n\ndef print_crl_issuer(file_path):\n    crl=load_crl(file_path)\n    aki_extension = None\n    try:\n        for ext in crl.extensions:\n             if ext.oid == x509.oid.ExtensionOID.AUTHORITY_KEY_IDENTIFIER:\n                    aki=ext.value\n                    print(aki)\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"Error parsing CRL: {e}\")\n\nfile_path = 'filename'\nprint_crl_issuer(file_path)\n```", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-06-07T16:38:01Z", "body": "Hmm, so an empty AKI is stupid, but it's not clear it violates the RFC?\n\n```\n   AuthorityKeyIdentifier ::= SEQUENCE {\n      keyIdentifier             [0] KeyIdentifier           OPTIONAL,\n      authorityCertIssuer       [1] GeneralNames            OPTIONAL,\n      authorityCertSerialNumber [2] CertificateSerialNumber OPTIONAL  }\n```\n\n(https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5280#section-4.2.1.1)\n\nIs there some place that says this violates an RFC?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "onepeople158", "timestamp": "2025-06-08T08:32:23Z", "body": "> Hmm, so an empty AKI is stupid, but it's not clear it violates the RFC?\n> \n> ```\n>    AuthorityKeyIdentifier ::= SEQUENCE {\n>       keyIdentifier             [0] KeyIdentifier           OPTIONAL,\n>       authorityCertIssuer       [1] GeneralNames            OPTIONAL,\n>       authorityCertSerialNumber [2] CertificateSerialNumber OPTIONAL  }\n> ```\n> \n> (https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5280#section-4.2.1.1)\n> \n> Is there some place that says this violates an RFC?\n\nHello, I created an empty AuthorityKeyIdentifier (AKI) extension where all three subfields are Null. Cryptography accepted this CRL file. Does this violate the following statement in RFC 5280:\n\n> [The identification can be based on either the key identifier (the subject key identifier in the CRL signer's certificate) or the issuer name and serial number.](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5280#section-4.2.1.1:~:text=The%20identification%20MAY%20be%20based%20on%20either%20the%0A%20%20%20key%20identifier%20(the%20subject%20key%20identifier%20in%20the%20issuer%27s%0A%20%20%20certificate)%20or%20the%20issuer%20name%20and%20serial%20number.)\n\n[empty_aki.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/20643477/empty_aki.zip)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "onepeople158", "timestamp": "2025-06-09T02:27:57Z", "body": "> I'm not sure that it does.\n\nHello, the use of an empty sequence for the AKI extension is unreasonable. However, RFC 5280 does not explicitly state that the AKI extension sequence must not be empty\u2014it only specifies that the IDP extension must not be an empty sequence. According to RFC 5280, the AKI extension [may be based on the key identifier (the subject key identifier in the issuer certificate) or the issuer name and serial number](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5280#section-4.2.1.1:~:text=The%20identification%20MAY%20be%20based%20on%20either%20the%0A%20%20%20key%20identifier%20(the%20subject%20key%20identifier%20in%20the%20issuer%27s%0A%20%20%20certificate)%20or%20the%20issuer%20name%20and%20serial%20number.).", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-06-09T02:31:55Z", "body": "I think that's a good summary, the fact that they use the ALL CAPS MAY means that MAY carries this definition:\n\n```\nMAY   This word, or the adjective \"OPTIONAL\", mean that an item is\n   truly optional.  One vendor may choose to include the item because a\n   particular marketplace requires it or because the vendor feels that\n   it enhances the product while another vendor may omit the same item.\n   An implementation which does not include a particular option MUST be\n   prepared to interoperate with another implementation which does\n   include the option, though perhaps with reduced functionality. In the\n   same vein an implementation which does include a particular option\n   MUST be prepared to interoperate with another implementation which\n   does not include the option (except, of course, for the feature the\n   option provides.)\n```\n\nWhich seems to indicate it's not required to be?\n\nThe whole RFC is written in a confusing way. Unless there's a clear reason our behavior either a) violates the spec, b) harms interoperability, c) is a security issue, I'd be inclined to leave it as is.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "onepeople158", "timestamp": "2025-06-09T02:46:47Z", "body": "> I think that's a good summary, the fact that they use the ALL CAPS MAY means that MAY carries this definition:\n> \n> ```\n> MAY   This word, or the adjective \"OPTIONAL\", mean that an item is\n>    truly optional.  One vendor may choose to include the item because a\n>    particular marketplace requires it or because the vendor feels that\n>    it enhances the product while another vendor may omit the same item.\n>    An implementation which does not include a particular option MUST be\n>    prepared to interoperate with another implementation which does\n>    include the option, though perhaps with reduced functionality. In the\n>    same vein an implementation which does include a particular option\n>    MUST be prepared to interoperate with another implementation which\n>    does not include the option (except, of course, for the feature the\n>    option provides.)\n> ```\n> \n> Which seems to indicate it's not required to be?\n> \n> The whole RFC is written in a confusing way. Unless there's a clear reason our behavior either a) violates the spec, b) harms interoperability, c) is a security issue, I'd be inclined to leave it as is.\n\nI understand.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "github-actions", "timestamp": "2025-06-13T00:00:19Z", "body": "This issue has been waiting for a reporter response for 3 days. It will be auto-closed if no activity occurs in the next 5 days.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "github-actions", "timestamp": "2025-06-19T00:00:14Z", "body": "This issue has not received a reporter response and has been auto-closed. If the issue is still relevant please leave a comment and we can reopen it.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/13051#1", "question": "In pyca/cryptography version 45.0.3, I can successfully parse a CRL file whose AuthorityKeyIdentifier (AKI) extension is empty / has all three subfields Null. When I use GnuTLS to parse a CRL file with an empty Key Identifier, it returns: `error: gnutls_x509_ext_import_authority_key_id: ASN1 parser: Error in DER parsing`.\n\nTest cases were attached as `crl_empty_key.zip` and `empty_aki.zip`.\n\nCode used:\n```\nfrom cryptography.x509 import load_pem_x509_crl, load_der_x509_crl\nfrom cryptography.x509 import ExtensionNotFound\nimport sys\n\ndef load_crl(file_path):\n    with open(file_path, \"rb\") as f:\n        crl_data = f.read()\n    try:\n        crl = load_pem_x509_crl(crl_data)\n    except ValueError:\n        crl = load_der_x509_crl(crl_data)\n    return crl\n\ndef print_crl_issuer(file_path):\n    crl=load_crl(file_path)\n    aki_extension = None\n    try:\n        for ext in crl.extensions:\n             if ext.oid == x509.oid.ExtensionOID.AUTHORITY_KEY_IDENTIFIER:\n                    aki=ext.value\n                    print(aki)\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"Error parsing CRL: {e}\")\n\nfile_path = 'filename'\nprint_crl_issuer(file_path)\n```\n\nDoes accepting an empty AKI extension violate RFC 5280 section 4.2.1.1, specifically the statement that \u201cThe identification MAY be based on either the key identifier ... or the issuer name and serial number\u201d?", "answer": "The conclusion was that an empty AKI sequence is unreasonable, but RFC 5280 does not explicitly say that the AKI extension sequence must not be empty. The RFC says the identification MAY be based on the key identifier or issuer name and serial number, and `MAY` means the item is optional; absent a clear spec violation, interoperability harm, or security issue, the behavior would be left as-is.", "knowledge_type": "parametric", "grounding_sources": [], "answer_grounded_in": "c3, c4", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5280#section-4.2.1.1:~:text=The%20identification%20MAY%20be%20based%20on%20either%20the%0A%20%20%20key%20identifier%20(the%20subject%20key%20identifier%20in%20the%20issuer%27s%0A%20%20%20certificate"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T07:59:42.864540Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/13002", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 13002, "title": "Unable to load private unencrypted key in `45.0.3` which worked in `44.0.3`", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/13002", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-05-28T13:28:49Z", "closed_at": "2025-05-28T13:47:35Z", "labels": ["waiting-on-reporter"], "reporter": "joshdcu", "security_topic": "key format compliance and regression policy", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know why loading an unencrypted RSA private key fails in version 45.x and whether it\u2019s a security regression; the maintainer explained it\u2019s due to using PKCS#8 keys with incorrect PEM headers per the PKCS#8 RFC (see the RFC and related issue #12993).", "question_comment_id": "c3", "answer_comment_id": "c4", "question_author": "joshdcu", "answer_author": "alex", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "joshdcu", "timestamp": "2025-05-28T13:28:49Z", "body": "**Description**\n\n_TL;DR: Unable to load private unencrypted key in `45.0.3` which worked in `44.0.3`. Private key is available [here](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/20488612/repro-priv-key.txt) for reproduction._\n\nI have a private unencrypted key that could be successfully loaded in `44.0.3` but not in `45.0.0`. I found two bugs that were posted related to private key loading (#12949 and #12958) which were fixed in the latest `45.0.3` so I tried that version as well, but it didn't work. When using `45.0.3`, loading the private key fails with the following error:\n\n```\nValueError: Could not deserialize key data. The data may be in an incorrect format, it may be encrypted with an unsupported algorithm, or it may be an unsupported key type (e.g. EC curves with explicit parameters). Details: ASN.1 parsing error: unexpected tag (got Tag { value: 16, constructed: true, class: Universal })\n```\n\n**Reproduction**\n\nLaunch a container based on the official Python Docker image, run an interactive Python shell, import the required `cryptography` functions, load the private key, and observe the failure:\n\n<pre>\n> podman run -it --rm --entrypoint sh docker.io/library/python:<mark>3.13.3-bookworm</mark>\n# pip install <mark>cryptography==45.0.3</mark>\nCollecting cryptography==45.0.3\n  Downloading cryptography-45.0.3-cp311-abi3-manylinux_2_34_x86_64.whl.metadata (5.7 kB)\nCollecting cffi>=1.14 (from cryptography==45.0.3)\n  Downloading cffi-1.17.1-cp313-cp313-manylinux_2_17_x86_64.manylinux2014_x86_64.whl.metadata (1.5 kB)\nCollecting pycparser (from cffi>=1.14->cryptography==45.0.3)\n  Downloading pycparser-2.22-py3-none-any.whl.metadata (943 bytes)\nDownloading cryptography-45.0.3-cp311-abi3-manylinux_2_34_x86_64.whl (4.5 MB)\n   \u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501 4.5/4.5 MB 58.4 MB/s eta 0:00:00\nDownloading cffi-1.17.1-cp313-cp313-manylinux_2_17_x86_64.manylinux2014_x86_64.whl (479 kB)\nDownloading pycparser-2.22-py3-none-any.whl (117 kB)\nInstalling collected packages: pycparser, cffi, cryptography\nSuccessfully installed cffi-1.17.1 cryptography-45.0.3 pycparser-2.22\nWARNING: Running pip as the 'root' user can result in broken permissions and conflicting behaviour with the system package manager, possibly rendering your system unusable. It is recommended to use a virtual environment instead: https://pip.pypa.io/warnings/venv. Use the --root-user-action option if you know what you are doing and want to suppress this warning.\n\n[notice] A new release of pip is available: 25.0.1 -> 25.1.1\n[notice] To update, run: pip install --upgrade pip\n# python\nPython 3.13.3 (main, May 22 2025, 01:27:26) [GCC 12.2.0] on linux\nType \"help\", \"copyright\", \"credits\" or \"license\" for more information.\n>>> from cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric import dsa, rsa\n>>> from cryptography.hazmat.primitives.serialization import load_pem_private_key\n>>> pem_data=b\"-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\nMIIEvgIBADANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQEFAASCBKgwggSkAgEAAoIBAQDhENrkVB/BggAq\\nrXYgNgrWImUFnZ6IHp\\gDlrFxUsh6aly2gTzGWG+0a4NXRN4UYOiraabXBHFPaHfP\\nIKneQAmdOY6cCQUUOGUq2REMAJTGHdmFoPDHSjQjIixb8ChxeRLQ7srvaKo1uOUq\\nEUdpVCMFKF9HRoT3+Z\\S86WXJsHL2ZUvXbvOuplht3Ms7MflbU5+XuIaMofMAZ6ck\\n2Im6wHCCS4cBFSJuf/rgVVW1UWgeiPu0Kds+G9lt3bnR4Lld3lXlNM5sWAKRcJ8r\\nvWzf+W8Psj5pAH6pkg\\SCX/UTJotlEdmyTPdqvuS1QP6S93O4/b18cljAPUcXWxX7\\neuIS4iIJAgMBAAECggEAAiqiPsswBJH6rC6ACfqXM11ApXORWnxX6IXvdpiuSR5j\\nvtaC5iJlDeKwkJsfO6\\k8qCTD2ENIz3PBHn9xHyx04zxx+t7L25hY4fcVtsy9IeSY\\n6tREKRj15EYwkOCAlSoOFDCOR8x1Qf/iBdwFeNY4YZT0SNh9EHGIvOJOymieTLCj\\nkZFrJvBENAaY0Lqv4F\\/d+p2MIkIhB/MTobWh3ccCmSgYF1WGxZUfSfyTagen46Nc\\nOfcu16e9SzSD6qWuPWDXWvAIhWNhcRhco6M8MOUvtZqtuUlTjn/MPnjoPki40Z9J\\n6vb84wzJwI652rYE7N\\0BF/BIrXhLh47QiXvQclgNcQKBgQDvWW3Y5nR3QFCwI01/\\ngL8+2EX8OHi7EfYxi7MS96uvHIvptB0NA2oavyMBLFiaAtQ15T+ShCB4Bl5BZ7d5\\nYlbzqKjWlRi+8+A/I3\\JJ+9sRmH4yCzIfCZi1RwCqAW5891Rm1nEX+Uwa4yy0+oqd\\nARHYfHtFZrNpH9PwhpGMjGVTWQKBgQDwuQ0jXIrkWzqBhfX7uyJCbCYzxbmciM0I\\nU2VyIdTYDcRiIsU3Tt\\MtCFiqnsEk07rqGGYtZtA8WR6KJxDL52ja5TInc3a5DK0+\\nM0GegFhZmB4Nsw0ui31yOQdAcPDGHCIrL30um1NRnlm2bBf5aPlajHeplyCq2M1c\\nA+jn5ibeMQKBgBwys/\\9Ovz/hYuAteI7h8yYLQsuBN7AKYK/bnoBIj4B044LoBlgn\\nI9P82rnjqJiUZr3mwUOYr16UmXwpJPN3A3l3UFTfgefVQYEOwwM2WcvL5Gou6XvA\\nnK4WrFxbCNJOLTJkZz\\SE2VN9miHENppiquCPYdJ+U/q5ljWLFRCy5akZAoGBAOnE\\nGwEbDnpCKae8iqtCCDwOwG6Idf6QTuPl8N8MAaEqhWpzr6dNMgBKWaifcJstzhcl\\nSJyQemoQdXreAw6wk/\\/2kWUEWOfNarO7nN6qbqHa+lx9Y7Cun0z8GpG22+rXSUs8\\n28kV56ycJSzk/ccj7PrE7DPR4lRnI85Wsaf5tgcRAoGBAIerGK0XGzE3mYHqu5lP\\nMCJ25g8P51eR13HX7o\\xywqSjNuCOBJKsqfKZHeby1Y/uv3CzR0u2IXIl8dBOfroC\\nSTQnjDTACxA5pIlxzFvVmCIDU2DyzyeIXwnTkPgIdJah0gNjrN1BWvKofg2B+rKV\\nAlLpSZIZKIlQ30MbfI\\+mLjc3\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\"\n>>> key = <mark>load_pem_private_key(pem_data, password=None)</mark>\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<python-input-3>\", line 1, in <module>\n    key = load_pem_private_key(pem_data, password=None)\n<mark>ValueError: Could not deserialize key data. The data may be in an incorrect format, it may be encrypted with an unsupported algorithm, or it may be an unsupported key type (e.g. EC curves with explicit parameters). Details: ASN.1 parsing error: unexpected tag (got Tag { value: 16, constructed: true, class: Universal })</mark>\n>>> exit()\n</pre>\n\n**Notes**\n\nThe same key works fine for `44.0.3`...\n\n<pre>\n> podman run -it --rm --entrypoint sh docker.io/library/python:<mark>3.13.3-bookworm</mark>\n# pip install <mark>cryptography==44.0.3</mark>\nCollecting cryptography==44.0.3\n  Downloading cryptography-44.0.3-cp39-abi3-manylinux_2_34_x86_64.whl.metadata (5.7 kB)\nCollecting cffi>=1.12 (from cryptography==44.0.3)\n  Downloading cffi-1.17.1-cp313-cp313-manylinux_2_17_x86_64.manylinux2014_x86_64.whl.metadata (1.5 kB)\nCollecting pycparser (from cffi>=1.12->cryptography==44.0.3)\n  Downloading pycparser-2.22-py3-none-any.whl.metadata (943 bytes)\nDownloading cryptography-44.0.3-cp39-abi3-manylinux_2_34_x86_64.whl (4.2 MB)\n   \u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501 4.2/4.2 MB 61.2 MB/s eta 0:00:00\nDownloading cffi-1.17.1-cp313-cp313-manylinux_2_17_x86_64.manylinux2014_x86_64.whl (479 kB)\nDownloading pycparser-2.22-py3-none-any.whl (117 kB)\nInstalling collected packages: pycparser, cffi, cryptography\nSuccessfully installed cffi-1.17.1 cryptography-44.0.3 pycparser-2.22\nWARNING: Running pip as the 'root' user can result in broken permissions and conflicting behaviour with the system package manager, possibly rendering your system unusable. It is recommended to use a virtual environment instead: https://pip.pypa.io/warnings/venv. Use the --root-user-action option if you know what you are doing and want to suppress this warning.\n\n[notice] A new release of pip is available: 25.0.1 -> 25.1.1\n[notice] To update, run: pip install --upgrade pip\n# python\nPython 3.13.3 (main, May 22 2025, 01:27:26) [GCC 12.2.0] on linux\nType \"help\", \"copyright\", \"credits\" or \"license\" for more information.\n>>> from cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric import dsa, rsa\n>>> from cryptography.hazmat.primitives.serialization import load_pem_private_key\n>>> pem_data=b\"-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\nMIIEvgIBADANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQEFAASCBKgwggSkAgEAAoIBAQDhENrkVB/BggAq\\nrXYgNgrWImUFnZ6IHp\\gDlrFxUsh6aly2gTzGWG+0a4NXRN4UYOiraabXBHFPaHfP\\nIKneQAmdOY6cCQUUOGUq2REMAJTGHdmFoPDHSjQjIixb8ChxeRLQ7srvaKo1uOUq\\nEUdpVCMFKF9HRoT3+Z\\S86WXJsHL2ZUvXbvOuplht3Ms7MflbU5+XuIaMofMAZ6ck\\n2Im6wHCCS4cBFSJuf/rgVVW1UWgeiPu0Kds+G9lt3bnR4Lld3lXlNM5sWAKRcJ8r\\nvWzf+W8Psj5pAH6pkg\\SCX/UTJotlEdmyTPdqvuS1QP6S93O4/b18cljAPUcXWxX7\\neuIS4iIJAgMBAAECggEAAiqiPsswBJH6rC6ACfqXM11ApXORWnxX6IXvdpiuSR5j\\nvtaC5iJlDeKwkJsfO6\\k8qCTD2ENIz3PBHn9xHyx04zxx+t7L25hY4fcVtsy9IeSY\\n6tREKRj15EYwkOCAlSoOFDCOR8x1Qf/iBdwFeNY4YZT0SNh9EHGIvOJOymieTLCj\\nkZFrJvBENAaY0Lqv4F\\/d+p2MIkIhB/MTobWh3ccCmSgYF1WGxZUfSfyTagen46Nc\\nOfcu16e9SzSD6qWuPWDXWvAIhWNhcRhco6M8MOUvtZqtuUlTjn/MPnjoPki40Z9J\\n6vb84wzJwI652rYE7N\\0BF/BIrXhLh47QiXvQclgNcQKBgQDvWW3Y5nR3QFCwI01/\\ngL8+2EX8OHi7EfYxi7MS96uvHIvptB0NA2oavyMBLFiaAtQ15T+ShCB4Bl5BZ7d5\\nYlbzqKjWlRi+8+A/I3\\JJ+9sRmH4yCzIfCZi1RwCqAW5891Rm1nEX+Uwa4yy0+oqd\\nARHYfHtFZrNpH9PwhpGMjGVTWQKBgQDwuQ0jXIrkWzqBhfX7uyJCbCYzxbmciM0I\\nU2VyIdTYDcRiIsU3Tt\\MtCFiqnsEk07rqGGYtZtA8WR6KJxDL52ja5TInc3a5DK0+\\nM0GegFhZmB4Nsw0ui31yOQdAcPDGHCIrL30um1NRnlm2bBf5aPlajHeplyCq2M1c\\nA+jn5ibeMQKBgBwys/\\9Ovz/hYuAteI7h8yYLQsuBN7AKYK/bnoBIj4B044LoBlgn\\nI9P82rnjqJiUZr3mwUOYr16UmXwpJPN3A3l3UFTfgefVQYEOwwM2WcvL5Gou6XvA\\nnK4WrFxbCNJOLTJkZz\\SE2VN9miHENppiquCPYdJ+U/q5ljWLFRCy5akZAoGBAOnE\\nGwEbDnpCKae8iqtCCDwOwG6Idf6QTuPl8N8MAaEqhWpzr6dNMgBKWaifcJstzhcl\\nSJyQemoQdXreAw6wk/\\/2kWUEWOfNarO7nN6qbqHa+lx9Y7Cun0z8GpG22+rXSUs8\\n28kV56ycJSzk/ccj7PrE7DPR4lRnI85Wsaf5tgcRAoGBAIerGK0XGzE3mYHqu5lP\\nMCJ25g8P51eR13HX7o\\xywqSjNuCOBJKsqfKZHeby1Y/uv3CzR0u2IXIl8dBOfroC\\nSTQnjDTACxA5pIlxzFvVmCIDU2DyzyeIXwnTkPgIdJah0gNjrN1BWvKofg2B+rKV\\nAlLpSZIZKIlQ30MbfI\\+mLjc3\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\"\n>>> key = <mark>load_pem_private_key(pem_data, password=None)</mark>\n>>> <mark>isinstance(key, rsa.RSAPrivateKey)</mark>\n<mark>True</mark>\n</pre>\n\nFrom the [changelog for `45.0.0`](https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/blob/main/CHANGELOG.rst#4500---2025-05-17-yanked):\n> We significantly refactored how private key loading ( [:func:`~cryptography.hazmat.primitives.serialization.load_pem_private_key`](https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/blob/main/CHANGELOG.rst#id50) and [:func:`~cryptography.hazmat.primitives.serialization.load_der_private_key`](https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/blob/main/CHANGELOG.rst#id52)) works. This is intended to be backwards compatible for all well-formed keys, therefore if you discover a key that now raises an exception, please file a bug with instructions for reproducing.\n\nI understand that loading private keys should be backwards-compatible unless it is not well-formed but using `openssl rsa -in <path/to/private/key> -check` does not return any error for my private key.\n\nI also confirmed that the private key uses the [recommended](https://cryptography.io/en/latest/hazmat/primitives/asymmetric/rsa/#cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric.rsa.generate_private_key) values of 65537 for the public exponent and 2048 for the key size.", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-05-28T13:36:25Z", "body": "The issue here is that you have a PKCS#8 key and the PEM headers are `BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY`, however PKCS#8 keys are supposed to have a `BEGIN PRIVATE KEY` header. https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12993 is a similar issue.\n\nHow was this PEM generated? Is there some library you used, or was it constructed \"manually\"", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "joshdcu", "timestamp": "2025-05-28T13:47:35Z", "body": "Yes, you're exactly right, thank you! The key is generated by the `crypto/rsa` Go library but I add the headers \"manually\" so it's the same issue you linked to. Switching to `BEGIN PRIVATE KEY` solves the issue:\n\n<pre>\npodman run -it --rm --entrypoint sh docker.io/library/python:<mark>3.13.3-bookworm</mark>\n# pip install <mark>cryptography==45.0.3</mark>\nCollecting cryptography==45.0.3\n  Downloading cryptography-45.0.3-cp311-abi3-manylinux_2_34_x86_64.whl.metadata (5.7 kB)\nCollecting cffi>=1.14 (from cryptography==45.0.3)\n  Downloading cffi-1.17.1-cp313-cp313-manylinux_2_17_x86_64.manylinux2014_x86_64.whl.metadata (1.5 kB)\nCollecting pycparser (from cffi>=1.14->cryptography==45.0.3)\n  Downloading pycparser-2.22-py3-none-any.whl.metadata (943 bytes)\nDownloading cryptography-45.0.3-cp311-abi3-manylinux_2_34_x86_64.whl (4.5 MB)\n   \u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501 4.5/4.5 MB 63.6 MB/s eta 0:00:00\nDownloading cffi-1.17.1-cp313-cp313-manylinux_2_17_x86_64.manylinux2014_x86_64.whl (479 kB)\nDownloading pycparser-2.22-py3-none-any.whl (117 kB)\nInstalling collected packages: pycparser, cffi, cryptography\nSuccessfully installed cffi-1.17.1 cryptography-45.0.3 pycparser-2.22\nWARNING: Running pip as the 'root' user can result in broken permissions and conflicting behaviour with the system package manager, possibly rendering your system unusable. It is recommended to use a virtual environment instead: https://pip.pypa.io/warnings/venv. Use the --root-user-action option if you know what you are doing and want to suppress this warning.\n\n[notice] A new release of pip is available: 25.0.1 -> 25.1.1\n[notice] To update, run: pip install --upgrade pip\n# python\nPython 3.13.3 (main, May 22 2025, 01:27:26) [GCC 12.2.0] on linux\nType \"help\", \"copyright\", \"credits\" or \"license\" for more information.\n>>> from cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric import dsa, rsa\n>>> from cryptography.hazmat.primitives.serialization import load_pem_private_key\n>>> pem_data=b\"-----<mark>BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY</mark>-----\\nMIIEvgIBADANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQEFAASCBKgwggSkAgEAAoIBAQDhENrkVB/BggAq\\nrXYgNgrWImUFnZ6IHp\\gDlrFxUsh6aly2gTzGWG+0a4NXRN4UYOiraabXBHFPaHfP\\nIKneQAmdOY6cCQUUOGUq2REMAJTGHdmFoPDHSjQjIixb8ChxeRLQ7srvaKo1uOUq\\nEUdpVCMFKF9HRoT3+Z\\S86WXJsHL2ZUvXbvOuplht3Ms7MflbU5+XuIaMofMAZ6ck\\n2Im6wHCCS4cBFSJuf/rgVVW1UWgeiPu0Kds+G9lt3bnR4Lld3lXlNM5sWAKRcJ8r\\nvWzf+W8Psj5pAH6pkg\\SCX/UTJotlEdmyTPdqvuS1QP6S93O4/b18cljAPUcXWxX7\\neuIS4iIJAgMBAAECggEAAiqiPsswBJH6rC6ACfqXM11ApXORWnxX6IXvdpiuSR5j\\nvtaC5iJlDeKwkJsfO6\\k8qCTD2ENIz3PBHn9xHyx04zxx+t7L25hY4fcVtsy9IeSY\\n6tREKRj15EYwkOCAlSoOFDCOR8x1Qf/iBdwFeNY4YZT0SNh9EHGIvOJOymieTLCj\\nkZFrJvBENAaY0Lqv4F\\/d+p2MIkIhB/MTobWh3ccCmSgYF1WGxZUfSfyTagen46Nc\\nOfcu16e9SzSD6qWuPWDXWvAIhWNhcRhco6M8MOUvtZqtuUlTjn/MPnjoPki40Z9J\\n6vb84wzJwI652rYE7N\\0BF/BIrXhLh47QiXvQclgNcQKBgQDvWW3Y5nR3QFCwI01/\\ngL8+2EX8OHi7EfYxi7MS96uvHIvptB0NA2oavyMBLFiaAtQ15T+ShCB4Bl5BZ7d5\\nYlbzqKjWlRi+8+A/I3\\JJ+9sRmH4yCzIfCZi1RwCqAW5891Rm1nEX+Uwa4yy0+oqd\\nARHYfHtFZrNpH9PwhpGMjGVTWQKBgQDwuQ0jXIrkWzqBhfX7uyJCbCYzxbmciM0I\\nU2VyIdTYDcRiIsU3Tt\\MtCFiqnsEk07rqGGYtZtA8WR6KJxDL52ja5TInc3a5DK0+\\nM0GegFhZmB4Nsw0ui31yOQdAcPDGHCIrL30um1NRnlm2bBf5aPlajHeplyCq2M1c\\nA+jn5ibeMQKBgBwys/\\9Ovz/hYuAteI7h8yYLQsuBN7AKYK/bnoBIj4B044LoBlgn\\nI9P82rnjqJiUZr3mwUOYr16UmXwpJPN3A3l3UFTfgefVQYEOwwM2WcvL5Gou6XvA\\nnK4WrFxbCNJOLTJkZz\\SE2VN9miHENppiquCPYdJ+U/q5ljWLFRCy5akZAoGBAOnE\\nGwEbDnpCKae8iqtCCDwOwG6Idf6QTuPl8N8MAaEqhWpzr6dNMgBKWaifcJstzhcl\\nSJyQemoQdXreAw6wk/\\/2kWUEWOfNarO7nN6qbqHa+lx9Y7Cun0z8GpG22+rXSUs8\\n28kV56ycJSzk/ccj7PrE7DPR4lRnI85Wsaf5tgcRAoGBAIerGK0XGzE3mYHqu5lP\\nMCJ25g8P51eR13HX7o\\xywqSjNuCOBJKsqfKZHeby1Y/uv3CzR0u2IXIl8dBOfroC\\nSTQnjDTACxA5pIlxzFvVmCIDU2DyzyeIXwnTkPgIdJah0gNjrN1BWvKofg2B+rKV\\nAlLpSZIZKIlQ30MbfI\\+mLjc3\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\"\n>>> key = load_pem_private_key(pem_data, password=None)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<python-input-4>\", line 1, in <module>\n    key = load_pem_private_key(pem_data, password=None)\n<mark>ValueError: Could not deserialize key data. The data may be in an incorrect format, it may be encrypted with an unsupported algorithm, or it may be an unsupported key type (e.g. EC curves with explicit parameters). Details: ASN.1 parsing error: unexpected tag (got Tag { value: 16, constructed: true, class: Universal })</mark>\n>>> pem_data=b\"-----<mark>BEGIN PRIVATE KEY</mark>-----\\nMIIEvgIBADANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQEFAASCBKgwggSkAgEAAoIBAQDhENrkVB/BggAq\\nrXYgNgrWImUFnZ6IHpgDlr\\FxUsh6aly2gTzGWG+0a4NXRN4UYOiraabXBHFPaHfP\\nIKneQAmdOY6cCQUUOGUq2REMAJTGHdmFoPDHSjQjIixb8ChxeRLQ7srvaKo1uOUq\\nEUdpVCMFKF9HRoT3+ZS86W\\XJsHL2ZUvXbvOuplht3Ms7MflbU5+XuIaMofMAZ6ck\\n2Im6wHCCS4cBFSJuf/rgVVW1UWgeiPu0Kds+G9lt3bnR4Lld3lXlNM5sWAKRcJ8r\\nvWzf+W8Psj5pAH6pkgSCX/\\UTJotlEdmyTPdqvuS1QP6S93O4/b18cljAPUcXWxX7\\neuIS4iIJAgMBAAECggEAAiqiPsswBJH6rC6ACfqXM11ApXORWnxX6IXvdpiuSR5j\\nvtaC5iJlDeKwkJsfO6k8qC\\TD2ENIz3PBHn9xHyx04zxx+t7L25hY4fcVtsy9IeSY\\n6tREKRj15EYwkOCAlSoOFDCOR8x1Qf/iBdwFeNY4YZT0SNh9EHGIvOJOymieTLCj\\nkZFrJvBENAaY0Lqv4F/d+p\\2MIkIhB/MTobWh3ccCmSgYF1WGxZUfSfyTagen46Nc\\nOfcu16e9SzSD6qWuPWDXWvAIhWNhcRhco6M8MOUvtZqtuUlTjn/MPnjoPki40Z9J\\n6vb84wzJwI652rYE7N0BF/\\BIrXhLh47QiXvQclgNcQKBgQDvWW3Y5nR3QFCwI01/\\ngL8+2EX8OHi7EfYxi7MS96uvHIvptB0NA2oavyMBLFiaAtQ15T+ShCB4Bl5BZ7d5\\nYlbzqKjWlRi+8+A/I3JJ+9\\sRmH4yCzIfCZi1RwCqAW5891Rm1nEX+Uwa4yy0+oqd\\nARHYfHtFZrNpH9PwhpGMjGVTWQKBgQDwuQ0jXIrkWzqBhfX7uyJCbCYzxbmciM0I\\nU2VyIdTYDcRiIsU3TtMtCF\\iqnsEk07rqGGYtZtA8WR6KJxDL52ja5TInc3a5DK0+\\nM0GegFhZmB4Nsw0ui31yOQdAcPDGHCIrL30um1NRnlm2bBf5aPlajHeplyCq2M1c\\nA+jn5ibeMQKBgBwys/9Ovz\\/hYuAteI7h8yYLQsuBN7AKYK/bnoBIj4B044LoBlgn\\nI9P82rnjqJiUZr3mwUOYr16UmXwpJPN3A3l3UFTfgefVQYEOwwM2WcvL5Gou6XvA\\nnK4WrFxbCNJOLTJkZzSE2V\\N9miHENppiquCPYdJ+U/q5ljWLFRCy5akZAoGBAOnE\\nGwEbDnpCKae8iqtCCDwOwG6Idf6QTuPl8N8MAaEqhWpzr6dNMgBKWaifcJstzhcl\\nSJyQemoQdXreAw6wk//2kW\\UEWOfNarO7nN6qbqHa+lx9Y7Cun0z8GpG22+rXSUs8\\n28kV56ycJSzk/ccj7PrE7DPR4lRnI85Wsaf5tgcRAoGBAIerGK0XGzE3mYHqu5lP\\nMCJ25g8P51eR13HX7oxywq\\SjNuCOBJKsqfKZHeby1Y/uv3CzR0u2IXIl8dBOfroC\\nSTQnjDTACxA5pIlxzFvVmCIDU2DyzyeIXwnTkPgIdJah0gNjrN1BWvKofg2B+rKV\\nAlLpSZIZKIlQ30MbfI+mLj\\c3\\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\"\n>>> key = load_pem_private_key(pem_data, password=None)\n>>> <mark>isinstance(key, rsa.RSAPrivateKey)</mark>\n<mark>True</mark>\n</pre>\n\nThanks again for the quick reply!", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "joshdcu", "timestamp": "2025-05-28T13:54:24Z", "body": "No problem! @alex, would this be considered a regression that will be fixed in an upcoming release or are users expected to modify their private keys so that the headers match what is expected by PKCS#8?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-05-28T14:01:14Z", "body": "Strictly speaking this is part of the PKCS#8 RFC, so we don't really\r\nconsider it a bug -- if this was _super_ widespread we might add a\r\ntemporary workaround. We're thinking about if there's a docs/error message\r\nimprovement we could make here.\r\n\r\nOn Wed, May 28, 2025 at 9:54\u202fAM joshdcu ***@***.***> wrote:\r\n\r\n> *joshdcu* left a comment (pyca/cryptography#13002)\r\n> <https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/13002#issuecomment-2916451760>\r\n>\r\n> No problem! @alex <https://github.com/alex>, would this be considered a\r\n> regression that will be fixed in an upcoming release or are users expected\r\n> to modify their private keys so that the headers match what is expected by\r\n> PKCS#8?\r\n>\r\n> \u2014\r\n> Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\r\n> <https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/13002#issuecomment-2916451760>,\r\n> or unsubscribe\r\n> <https://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AAAAGBBLP63UC4HOHHD43SL3AW52LAVCNFSM6AAAAAB6C2Z2VGVHI2DSMVQWIX3LMV43OSLTON2WKQ3PNVWWK3TUHMZDSMJWGQ2TCNZWGA>\r\n> .\r\n> You are receiving this because you were mentioned.Message ID:\r\n> ***@***.***>\r\n>\r\n\r\n\r\n-- \r\nAll that is necessary for evil to succeed is for good people to do nothing.\r\n", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/13002#1", "question": "In pyca/cryptography, I have an unencrypted private key that loaded successfully with `cryptography==44.0.3` but fails with `cryptography==45.0.3` in the official Python Docker image `python:3.13.3-bookworm` when calling `load_pem_private_key(pem_data, password=None)`. The failure is:\n\n```text\nValueError: Could not deserialize key data. The data may be in an incorrect format, it may be encrypted with an unsupported algorithm, or it may be an unsupported key type (e.g. EC curves with explicit parameters). Details: ASN.1 parsing error: unexpected tag (got Tag { value: 16, constructed: true, class: Universal })\n```\n\nThe same key returns `True` for `isinstance(key, rsa.RSAPrivateKey)` on `44.0.3`. A maintainer identified that the key is actually PKCS#8 but I had manually added `-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----` / `-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----` headers; changing the headers to the PKCS#8 `-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----` / `-----END PRIVATE KEY-----` makes it load in `45.0.3`. Given that this worked before, is this considered a regression that will be fixed in an upcoming release, or are users expected to modify their private keys so the PEM headers match what PKCS#8 expects?", "answer": "The maintainer said they do not really consider this a bug because the header requirement is part of the PKCS#8 RFC. They might add a temporary workaround only if the mismatch is super widespread, and they are considering whether to improve the documentation or error message.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["PKCS#8 RFC", "pyca/cryptography issue #12993"], "answer_grounded_in": "c4", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://github.com/alex", "https://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AAAAGBBLP63UC4HOHHD43SL3AW52LAVCNFSM6AAAAAB6C2Z2VGVHI2DSMVQWIX3LMV43OSLTON2WKQ3PNVWWK3TUHMZDSMJWGQ2TCNZWGA", "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/13002#issuecomment-2916451760"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T08:01:13.031450Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12969", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 12969, "title": "CryptographyDeprecationWarning for serial number set to 0 fails with some CAs", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12969", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-05-22T16:06:06Z", "closed_at": "2025-05-22T16:22:03Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "thierryba", "security_topic": "handling of certificates with serial number zero and deprecation warning", "qa_summary": "The developer asked if the CryptographyDeprecationWarning for certificates with serial number 0 should be revisited, and the maintainer answered that the warning will be retained (parsing allowed) until non\u2011compliant certificates are removed, citing issue #12948 as the reference.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "thierryba", "answer_author": "reaperhulk", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["issue_tracker", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": ["https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12948"]}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "thierryba", "timestamp": "2025-05-22T16:06:06Z", "body": "Hello,\n\nIf you try to load the certificates from the certificate package, it fails on some of them\n\n\n```\nimport certifi\nfrom cryptography import x509\n\nwith open(certifi.where(), 'rb') as cert_file:\n    certs = x509.load_pem_x509_certificates(cert_file.read())\n\nfor cert in certs:\n    if cert.serial_number == 0:\n        print(\"BAD CERT\", cert.issuer, cert.subject)\n```\n\n\nSo I'd suggest to revisit that deprecation.", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "reaperhulk", "timestamp": "2025-05-22T16:22:03Z", "body": "We'll be retaining the warning (but allowing parsing) until the non-compliant certificates are removed from trust stores (see https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12948 as well)", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12969#1", "question": "In pyca/cryptography, loading the certificates from the `certifi` package triggers a `CryptographyDeprecationWarning` for certificates whose serial number is set to 0, and this affects some CAs. For example:\n\n```python\nimport certifi\nfrom cryptography import x509\n\nwith open(certifi.where(), 'rb') as cert_file:\n    certs = x509.load_pem_x509_certificates(cert_file.read())\n\nfor cert in certs:\n    if cert.serial_number == 0:\n        print(\"BAD CERT\", cert.issuer, cert.subject)\n```\n\nSince this fails or warns on some certificates in the trust store, should the deprecation for serial number 0 be revisited?", "answer": "The warning will be retained, but parsing will continue to be allowed, until the non-compliant certificates are removed from trust stores. The maintainer also pointed to related issue #12948.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12948"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12948"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T08:02:20.087873Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12963", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 12963, "title": "EVP_CIPHER_CTX_copy:not able to copy ctx:crypto/evp/evp_enc.c:1468", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12963", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-05-21T13:28:05Z", "closed_at": "2025-05-30T00:00:25Z", "labels": ["waiting-on-reporter", "Stale"], "reporter": "vj-atlassian", "security_topic": "AESGCM error caused by OpenSSL 3.4 FIPS bug, need upgrade to 3.5", "qa_summary": "The developer asked if the GHSA-79v4-65xg-pq4g vulnerability in cryptography wheels applies when building against a custom OpenSSL and what version to use; the maintainer answered that the GHSA does not affect custom OpenSSL builds and can be ignored, referencing GHSA-79v4-65xg-pq4g.", "question_comment_id": "c3", "answer_comment_id": "c4", "question_author": "vj-atlassian", "answer_author": "reaperhulk", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation", "external_reference"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": ["3.5"], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "vj-atlassian", "timestamp": "2025-05-21T13:28:05Z", "body": "Hi\n\nI'm installing cryptography from source using - `pip install --no-binary=cryptography cryptography==45.0.2` and running on FIPS Mode. Basically I'hve installed openSSL 3.4.0 with FIPS enabled on Ubuntu jammy 22.04.\n\nSo, I can only use FIPS compliant algorithms under FedRAMP but when I use AESGCM to encrypt/decrypt, it throws an error\n\n```bash\nUnknown OpenSSL error. This error is commonly encountered\n                    when another library is not cleaning up the OpenSSL error\n                    stack. If you are using cryptography with another library\n                    that uses OpenSSL try disabling it before reporting a bug.\n                    Otherwise please file an issue at\n                    https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues with\n                    information on how to reproduce this. (error:030000BE:digital envelope routines:EVP_CIPHER_CTX_copy:not able to copy ctx:crypto/evp/evp_enc.c:1468:)\n```\n\n* The strange part is it works well with `AESCCM` without any errors but with AESGCM encryption/descryption doesn't work.\n* Another thing to note, `AESGCM` also works when I use v42.x of cryptography with openSSL 3.1.x.\n\n```java\nimport base64\nimport os\nimport ctypes.util\n\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives.ciphers.aead import AESGCM\n\nAUTHENTICATE_DATA = \"test\".encode()\nNONCE_LEN = 12\n\ndef generate_key() -> bytes:\n    # Generate a 256-bit key\n    return AESGCM.generate_key(bit_length=256)\n\n\ndef encrypt(secret_key, data: str) -> str:\n    aesgcm = AESGCM(secret_key)\n    nonce = os.urandom(NONCE_LEN)\n    ct = aesgcm.encrypt(nonce, data.encode(), AUTHENTICATE_DATA)\n    return base64.b64encode(nonce + ct).decode(\"utf-8\")\n\ndef decrypt(secret_key, data: str) -> str:\n    decoded_data = base64.b64decode(data)\n    nonce, ct = (\n        decoded_data[: NONCE_LEN],\n        decoded_data[NONCE_LEN :],\n    )\n    aesgcm = AESGCM(secret_key)\n    decrypted_output = aesgcm.decrypt(nonce, ct, AUTHENTICATE_DATA)\n    return decrypted_output.decode(\"utf-8\")\n```\n\nOpenSSL config\n\n```bash\nChecking for OpenSSL modules directory location\nOpenSSL info: {} OpenSSL 3.4.0 22 Oct 2024 (Library: OpenSSL 3.4.0 22 Oct 2024)\nbuilt on: Sat May 17 17:53:41 2025 UTC\nplatform: linux-aarch64\noptions:  bn(64,64)\ncompiler: gcc -fPIC -pthread -Wa,--noexecstack -Wall -O3 -DOPENSSL_USE_NODELETE -DOPENSSL_PIC -DOPENSSL_BUILDING_OPENSSL -DNDEBUG\nOPENSSLDIR: \"/usr/local\"\nENGINESDIR: \"/usr/local/lib64/engines-3\"\nMODULESDIR: \"/usr/local/lib64/ossl-modules\"\nSeeding source: os-specific\nCPUINFO: OPENSSL_armcap=0x8fd\n```\n\nSpent like 2 days on this now without much clue, any help would be deeply appreciated!", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-05-21T13:39:43Z", "body": "Are you building against the same OpenSSL you're linking against? The underlying issue here was https://github.com/openssl/openssl/issues/20978, but it's been fixed for a while.\n\nWe have `cfg` checks to use fallback behavior on older OpenSSLs: https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/blob/main/src/rust/src/backend/aead.rs#L581-L597 3.4.0 should be fine though, unless for some reason the FIPS module didn't get that fix. We test against the 3.5.0 FIPS module in CI though...", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "vj-atlassian", "timestamp": "2025-05-21T13:50:17Z", "body": "Yes, the openSSL version I built is also being used by the cryptography lib.\n\n```bash\nPython 3.13.3 (main, May 21 2025, 11:03:23) [GCC 11.4.0] on linux\nType \"help\", \"copyright\", \"credits\" or \"license\" for more information.\n>>> from cryptography.hazmat.backends.openssl.backend import backend\n... print(backend.openssl_version_text())\n... \nOpenSSL 3.4.0 22 Oct 2024\n```\n\nIts also working for me, if I fallback to use 42.x of cryptography, its only when I upgrade it starts breaking. All my builds and test cases are green for <= 42.x.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "vj-atlassian", "timestamp": "2025-05-21T14:01:49Z", "body": "So, you mean its falling back to the older version of openSSL somehow? But if you see my code above the `cryptography` package uses version 3.4.0, so I'm perplexed!\n\nHow do I proceed further?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "reaperhulk", "timestamp": "2025-05-21T14:24:00Z", "body": "No, Alex means that we added an optimization that doesn\u2019t work in the FIPS module of 3.4 because they have a bug. We test this against 3.5 FIPS so we know it works there so your options are either to upgrade to 3.5 or stay on an older cryptography until you can.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "vj-atlassian", "timestamp": "2025-05-21T14:33:36Z", "body": "Ok. I got a VULN to resolve and the minimum version to upgrade is 44.x. Let me try with OpenSSL 3.5 and will update here tomorrow.\n\n[VULN](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-79v4-65xg-pq4g)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-05-21T14:42:46Z", "body": "To be clear, that CVE only impacts the wheels we distribute. If you're brining you're own OpenSSL you're not impacted by it (though you, of course, need to ensure the OpenSSL you're building with doesn't have any vulnerabilities).", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "vj-atlassian", "timestamp": "2025-05-21T19:20:30Z", "body": "Thanks @alex , I also saw that. Although I have upgraded to OpenSSL 3.5.0 but I still get the same error. I'm using cryptography 45.x, what else could be going wrong?\n\n```bash\n%s Unknown OpenSSL error. This error is commonly encountered\n                    when another library is not cleaning up the OpenSSL error\n                    stack. If you are using cryptography with another library\n                    that uses OpenSSL try disabling it before reporting a bug.\n                    Otherwise please file an issue at\n                    https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues with\n                    information on how to reproduce this. (error:030000BE:digital envelope routines:EVP_CIPHER_CTX_copy:not able to copy ctx:crypto/evp/evp_enc.c:1787:)\n```\n\nOpenSSL config\n```bash\nOpenSSL info: {} OpenSSL 3.5.0 8 Apr 2025 (Library: OpenSSL 3.5.0 8 Apr 2025)\nbuilt on: Wed May 21 14:32:03 2025 UTC\nplatform: linux-aarch64\noptions:  bn(64,64)\ncompiler: gcc -fPIC -pthread -Wa,--noexecstack -Wall -O3 -DOPENSSL_USE_NODELETE -DOPENSSL_PIC -DOPENSSL_BUILDING_OPENSSL -DNDEBUG\nOPENSSLDIR: \"/usr/local\"\nENGINESDIR: \"/usr/local/lib64/engines-3\"\nMODULESDIR: \"/usr/local/lib64/ossl-modules\"\nSeeding source: os-specific\nCPUINFO: OPENSSL_armcap=0x8fd\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c8", "author": "reaperhulk", "timestamp": "2025-05-21T19:22:41Z", "body": "Did you build the FIPS module from 3.5 or another version? You can see in our CI that we build and test against those configs so at this point it's highly likely that there's something specific to your environment. If you can create a docker container that replicates this issue we can maybe take a bit more of a look, but we're close to the limit of the support we can supply for custom compilation here.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c9", "author": "vj-atlassian", "timestamp": "2025-05-21T20:09:19Z", "body": "My FIPS provider version is different. The latest certified version of OpenSSL FIPS certified version is 3.1.2 but its compatible with [OpenSSL 3.5.x ](https://openssl-library.org/post/2025-03-11-fips-140-3/#:~:text=Ensuring%20Compliance%20%26%20Compatibility%20Across%20OpenSSL%203.x)\n\nThis is how enable fips while OpenSSL is already installed in `/usr/local`.\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSLO --compressed --retry 5 --retry-all-errors \"https://www.openssl.org/source/openssl-3.1.2.tar.gz\"\n....\nmkdir -p /tmp/downloads/fips && tar -xzf \"openssl-3.1.2.tar.gz\" -C /tmp/downloads/fips --strip-components=1 --no-same-owner\n\ncd /tmp/downloads/fips\n./Configure enable-fips --prefix=/usr/local --openssldir=/usr/local\nmake -j$(nproc)\ncd /\n```\n\n\n```bash\nappuser@5ceace4a08ef:~$ openssl list -providers\nProviders:\n  default\n    name: OpenSSL Default Provider\n    version: 3.5.0\n    status: active\n  fips\n    name: OpenSSL FIPS Provider\n    version: 3.1.2\n    status: active\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c10", "author": "reaperhulk", "timestamp": "2025-05-21T20:15:26Z", "body": "While OpenSSL supports this type of mismatched setup, we currently assume that any loaded provider has the same capabilities as the main version of OpenSSL. This means it's attempting to use the newer paths on a version that doesn't support it properly. We're not likely to change this soon, so if you have a strict FIPS requirement (and what this means depends heavily on your auditor and deployment environment) you may need to look at OpenSSL 3.0.x for use with the latest cryptography.\n\nAll that said, as @alex mentioned earlier, the vulnerability in question is in our compiled wheels and it is not necessary to upgrade to a newer cryptography to fix it. Since you're compiling against your own OpenSSL you can and should independently update OpenSSL, and what we ship our wheels with does not affect you. Accordingly, you can simply ignore that GHSA.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c11", "author": "vj-atlassian", "timestamp": "2025-05-21T20:56:59Z", "body": "Thanks @reaperhulk ! Indeed you are right, I built `cryptography` 45.x with OpenSSL build from source incl. the provider v3.1.2 and it worked. I'm able to successfully run AES-GCM encypt/decrypt. \nI'll need to talk to security about FIPS and see if downgrading the OpenSSL version is really an option for all services but can I request you to add this is a feature request to support in the future where different versions of OpenSSL provider and OpenSSL is supported?\n\nLuckily, [OpenSSL 3.1.2](https://openssl-library.org/news/secadv/20250211.txt) doesn't have a VULN but only 3.2, 3.3 and 3.4 has, so it should be good this time.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c12", "author": "github-actions", "timestamp": "2025-05-25T00:00:21Z", "body": "This issue has been waiting for a reporter response for 3 days. It will be auto-closed if no activity occurs in the next 5 days.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c13", "author": "github-actions", "timestamp": "2025-05-30T00:00:25Z", "body": "This issue has not received a reporter response and has been auto-closed. If the issue is still relevant please leave a comment and we can reopen it.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12963#1", "question": "I'm installing `cryptography` from source with `pip install --no-binary=cryptography cryptography==45.0.2` on Ubuntu jammy 22.04 in FIPS mode. I installed OpenSSL 3.4.0 with FIPS enabled, because under FedRAMP I can only use FIPS-compliant algorithms. When I use `AESGCM` to encrypt/decrypt, it throws:\n\n```bash\nUnknown OpenSSL error. This error is commonly encountered\n                    when another library is not cleaning up the OpenSSL error\n                    stack. If you are using cryptography with another library\n                    that uses OpenSSL try disabling it before reporting a bug.\n                    Otherwise please file an issue at\n                    https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues with\n                    information on how to reproduce this. (error:030000BE:digital envelope routines:EVP_CIPHER_CTX_copy:not able to copy ctx:crypto/evp/evp_enc.c:1468:)\n```\n\nThe strange part is it works with `AESCCM` without errors, but AES-GCM encryption/decryption doesn't work. Also, `AESGCM` works when I use cryptography v42.x with OpenSSL 3.1.x.\n\nReproducer:\n\n```java\nimport base64\nimport os\nimport ctypes.util\n\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives.ciphers.aead import AESGCM\n\nAUTHENTICATE_DATA = \"test\".encode()\nNONCE_LEN = 12\n\ndef generate_key() -> bytes:\n    # Generate a 256-bit key\n    return AESGCM.generate_key(bit_length=256)\n\n\ndef encrypt(secret_key, data: str) -> str:\n    aesgcm = AESGCM(secret_key)\n    nonce = os.urandom(NONCE_LEN)\n    ct = aesgcm.encrypt(nonce, data.encode(), AUTHENTICATE_DATA)\n    return base64.b64encode(nonce + ct).decode(\"utf-8\")\n\ndef decrypt(secret_key, data: str) -> str:\n    decoded_data = base64.b64decode(data)\n    nonce, ct = (\n        decoded_data[: NONCE_LEN],\n        decoded_data[NONCE_LEN :],\n    )\n    aesgcm = AESGCM(secret_key)\n    decrypted_output = aesgcm.decrypt(nonce, ct, AUTHENTICATE_DATA)\n    return decrypted_output.decode(\"utf-8\")\n```\n\nOpenSSL config:\n\n```bash\nChecking for OpenSSL modules directory location\nOpenSSL info: {} OpenSSL 3.4.0 22 Oct 2024 (Library: OpenSSL 3.4.0 22 Oct 2024)\nbuilt on: Sat May 17 17:53:41 2025 UTC\nplatform: linux-aarch64\noptions:  bn(64,64)\ncompiler: gcc -fPIC -pthread -Wa,--noexecstack -Wall -O3 -DOPENSSL_USE_NODELETE -DOPENSSL_PIC -DOPENSSL_BUILDING_OPENSSL -DNDEBUG\nOPENSSLDIR: \"/usr/local\"\nENGINESDIR: \"/usr/local/lib64/engines-3\"\nMODULESDIR: \"/usr/local/lib64/ossl-modules\"\nSeeding source: os-specific\nCPUINFO: OPENSSL_armcap=0x8fd\n```\n\nI verified that cryptography is using the OpenSSL I built:\n\n```bash\nPython 3.13.3 (main, May 21 2025, 11:03:23) [GCC 11.4.0] on linux\nType \"help\", \"copyright\", \"credits\" or \"license\" for more information.\n>>> from cryptography.hazmat.backends.openssl.backend import backend\n... print(backend.openssl_version_text())\n... \nOpenSSL 3.4.0 22 Oct 2024\n```\n\nIt works if I fall back to cryptography 42.x; all builds and tests are green for `<= 42.x`. Does this mean cryptography is somehow falling back to an older OpenSSL version? Given that `backend.openssl_version_text()` shows 3.4.0, how should I proceed?", "answer": "No: the explanation was not that cryptography is falling back to an older OpenSSL. cryptography added an optimization that does not work with the OpenSSL 3.4 FIPS module because of an OpenSSL bug. The maintainers test against the OpenSSL 3.5 FIPS module, so the suggested options were to upgrade to OpenSSL 3.5 or stay on an older cryptography version until you can.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/openssl/openssl/issues/20978", "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/blob/main/src/rust/src/backend/aead.rs#L581-L597", "OpenSSL 3.5 FIPS module tested in cryptography CI"], "answer_grounded_in": "c4", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": [], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T08:03:27.416703Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12949", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 12949, "title": "Current key not working with version 45.0.2", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12949", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-05-19T21:19:55Z", "closed_at": "2025-05-25T13:39:09Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "vinod-seshadri-TA", "security_topic": "support for deprecated pbeWithMD5AndDES-CBC key encryption algorithm in cryptography library", "qa_summary": "The developer asked if support for the insecure PBE\u2011MD5\u2011DES algorithm would be removed or could be restored, and the maintainer confirmed it will be added back, referencing issue #12956 for the test vector and upcoming implementation.", "question_comment_id": "c2", "answer_comment_id": "c11", "question_author": "vinod-seshadri-TA", "answer_author": "alex", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "pr_data", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": ["#12956"], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "vinod-seshadri-TA", "timestamp": "2025-05-19T21:19:55Z", "body": "Existing key that was working with cryptography version 44.0.3 no longer works with 45.0.2 (basically 45.0.0 and above)\n\nserialization.load_pem_private_key(..) returns `Unknown Key Encryption Algorithm : 1.2.840.113549.1.5.3`\n\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "reaperhulk", "timestamp": "2025-05-19T21:23:14Z", "body": "Thanks for the report, it appears you have an encrypted private key using `pbeWithMD5AndDES-CBC`. Could you share how you generated this? (Please do not share the actual key of course)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "vinod-seshadri-TA", "timestamp": "2025-05-19T21:47:30Z", "body": "Here is the command used to generate the key\n\n```openssl genrsa 2048 | openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -v1 PBE-MD5-DES -inform PEM -out <private file key name>.p8```\n\nI do understand this is considered insecure  , but this issue was to see if there is an explicit removal of support for this algorithm, in the latest version , if not could it be fixed if  yes, then can that be added in the changelog\n\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "alexscott-ff", "timestamp": "2025-05-19T22:05:33Z", "body": "This also popped up for us and broke our [Snowflake connection](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/key-pair-auth#generate-the-private-key), although I'm not sure the exact command that was used. It worked on `44.0.3`.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "reaperhulk", "timestamp": "2025-05-19T22:13:29Z", "body": "@alexscott-ff with the same OID and not a different one? Just want to make sure that if we add this algorithm back that it resolves your issue (although you should separately upgrade the encryption wrapping your key! Single DES with MD5 is not good!)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "vinod-seshadri-TA", "timestamp": "2025-05-19T22:20:59Z", "body": "I was able to independently verify that a key generated with \n```\nopenssl genrsa 2048 | openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -v2 des3 -inform PEM -out <private_key>.p8\n```\nworks fine with the same version. so specifically an issue with the algorithm", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "alexscott-ff", "timestamp": "2025-05-19T23:42:41Z", "body": "@reaperhulk I believe so but I'm not sure; I think we're going to \"fix forward\" and re-generate the key, as you suggest :)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "fojtasek", "timestamp": "2025-05-20T00:30:37Z", "body": "> [@alexscott-ff](https://github.com/alexscott-ff) with the same OID and not a different one? Just want to make sure that if we add this algorithm back that it resolves your issue (although you should separately upgrade the encryption wrapping your key! Single DES with MD5 is not good!)\n\nI work with @alexscott-ff and can confirm both that it's the same OID and that we're upgrading the encryption", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c8", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-05-20T01:22:41Z", "body": "Hi all,\n\nThanks for the reports, we appreciate them! I want to make sure I'm following correctly:\n\n1. Everyone who has hit this error has gotten it with 1.2.840.113549.1.5.3\n2. No one is quite sure which tool/command was used to produce their key with it? (I know we can reproduce it with `-v1 PBE-MD5-DES`, but it sounds like no one is sure if that's what they used, and if so why)?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c9", "author": "lukpueh", "timestamp": "2025-05-20T09:09:53Z", "body": "Thanks for the discussion! I get the same error for [rsa and ecdsa test keys](https://github.com/secure-systems-lab/securesystemslib/commit/7952c3fae40fdf6012a952902e17dc6ca242dc9c), which I created with LibreSSL 3.3.6, which still seems to be the default openssl on macOS. \n\n\nHere's a reproducer:\n\n```bash\n$ /usr/bin/openssl version\nLibreSSL 3.3.6\n$ /usr/bin/openssl genrsa 2048 | /usr/bin/openssl pkcs8 -inform PEM -topk8 -passout pass:hunter2 -out key.p8\n$ python <<EOF\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives.serialization import load_pem_private_key\nwith open(\"key.p8\", \"rb\") as f:\n    data = f.read()\nload_pem_private_key(data, b'hunter2')\nEOF\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 4, in <module>\nValueError: Unknown key encryption algorithm: 1.2.840.113549.1.5.3\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c10", "author": "reaperhulk", "timestamp": "2025-05-20T10:38:09Z", "body": "@lukpueh Thanks, I can reproduce this on latest macOS, but I can also reproduce it on LibreSSL 4.1.0. So I think there are two action items here. First, we'll be adding support back for this OID despite its decrepit nature. Second, we'll be reaching out to the LibreSSL folks to get them to change this default \ud83d\ude04 ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c11", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-05-20T14:00:55Z", "body": "Ok, #12956 adds a test vector, next up we'll add an implementation, after we ping the LibreSSL folks.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c12", "author": "botovq", "timestamp": "2025-05-20T18:04:15Z", "body": "I know... ([closely related][1]) We should really fix this. PKCS8 and PKCS12 are just something we don't use in the OS and I myself have only ever touched them when somebody reported a bug.\n\nI'll happily flip the defaults to something saner but I'll have to look into what makes sense. I filed an issue that should remind me of it.\n\nSorry about that.\n\n[1]: https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-tech&m=174690438827143&w=2", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12949#1", "question": "In pyca/cryptography, an existing encrypted private key that worked with cryptography 44.0.3 no longer works with 45.0.2 (basically 45.0.0 and above). Calling `serialization.load_pem_private_key(..)` returns `Unknown Key Encryption Algorithm : 1.2.840.113549.1.5.3`. The key was generated with:\n\n```openssl genrsa 2048 | openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -v1 PBE-MD5-DES -inform PEM -out <private file key name>.p8```\n\nI understand this is considered insecure, but was support for this algorithm explicitly removed in the latest version? If not, could it be fixed; if yes, can the removal be added to the changelog?", "answer": "The maintainers identified the algorithm as `pbeWithMD5AndDES-CBC` and indicated they would add support back for the OID despite its insecure/decrepit nature. They also noted that #12956 adds a test vector, and that an implementation would be added after they ping the LibreSSL folks.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["PR #12956", "OID 1.2.840.113549.1.5.3 / pbeWithMD5AndDES-CBC", "LibreSSL default behavior"], "answer_grounded_in": "c11", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["#12956"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T08:04:57.370475Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12824", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 12824, "title": "ML-KEM API Design", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12824", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-04-29T19:03:30Z", "closed_at": "2026-04-17T11:20:39Z", "labels": ["api design"], "reporter": "reaperhulk", "security_topic": "private key serialization requirements for ML-KEM in SSH", "qa_summary": "The developer asked if expanded ML\u2011KEM decapsulation keys should be serializable, and the answer cited a security concern with a reference to the IETF slide on the private\u2011key war (https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/122/materials/slides-122-pquip-the-great-private-key-war-of-25-00).", "question_comment_id": "c6", "answer_comment_id": "c19", "question_author": "reaperhulk", "answer_author": "ronf", "answerer_role": "contributor", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "ci_logs", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "reaperhulk", "timestamp": "2025-04-29T19:03:30Z", "body": "Here's a strawman, let's discuss.\n\nA few thoughts:\n* I don't want to use public/private, but Encapsulation and Decapsulation are quite verbose. I've used Encap/Decap below but we can use the full words if people think it's better.\n* I see no reason to support/allow anything other than seed only PKCS8 for both loading and serialization.\n* Do we want to support bare seed loading? The current API proposal below does allow it similar to how we do it in Ed/X keys.\n* If we support bare seed loading then we should support bare seed serialization. We can repurpose `PrivateFormat.Raw` for this I suppose.\n* How do we load PKCS8? We have `load_{pem,der}_{public,private}_key` functions, but if we don't want to use the public/private nomenclature we may want new functions.\n* This proposal makes the parameter set a property of the class rather than a value to be passed. We could choose to expose an enum of parameter sets (768 and 1024...maybe 512 if necessary, although it seems no one is implementing that) instead of this. Go has chosen separate concrete interfaces (https://github.com/golang/go/issues/70122) for this and I generally think separate types are a reasonable choice here, although a consequence is that code that supports multiple MLKEM sizes will need to have type signatures like `input: MLKEM768EncapKey | MLKEM1024EncapKey`\n\n\n```python\nclass MLKEM768DecapKey:\n    @classmethod\n    def generate(cls) -> \"MLKEM768DecapKey\":\n        ...\n\n    def decap(self, ciphertext: bytes) -> bytes:\n        ...\n\n    def encap_key(self) -> \"MLKEM768EncapKey\":\n        ...\n\n    def private_bytes(\n        self,\n        encoding: serialization.Encoding,\n        format: serialization.PrivateFormat,\n        encryption_algorithm: serialization.KeySerializationEncryption,\n    ) -> bytes:\n        ...\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_seed_bytes(cls, data: bytes) -> \"MLKEM768DecapKey\":\n        ...\n\n\nclass MLKEM768EncapKey:\n    def encap(self) -> Tuple[bytes, bytes]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Note that this tuple should be shared_secret, ciphertext to match the spec\n        \"\"\"\n        ...\n\n    def public_bytes(\n        self,\n        encoding: serialization.Encoding,\n        format: serialization.PublicFormat,\n    ) -> bytes:\n        ...\n```", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-04-29T21:01:37Z", "body": "Thoughts:\n\na) Overall API design looks fine\nb) I'm a bit ambivalent on encap/decap vs. full words. I have easy arguments for both sides of it.\nc) Is there a use case for bare seeds? If so, let's do it (since easy), but if there's no concrete usecase, skip for now (easy to add later). I guess there obviously is for encap keys?\nd) Yes, seed-only for PKCS8, obviously :-)\ne) I think we just reuse the private/public key loading APIs. It's not great, but what can you do.\nf) I kind of think the `_bytes()` methods should still be `public`/`private`, since that's really saying \"do you need to keep this secret\"", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "ronf", "timestamp": "2025-04-29T21:27:25Z", "body": "Here's the API I'm currently using (which currently wraps around liboqs):\n\n```python\nclass PQDH:\n    \"\"\"A shim around liboqs for post-quantum key exchange algorithms\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, alg_name: bytes):\n        \"\"\"Select the PQ algorithm to use, setting internal methods for keypair, encaps, and decaps\"\"\"\n\n    def keypair(self) -> Tuple[bytes, bytes]:\n        \"\"\"Make a new key pair, returning public and private key byte arrays\"\"\"\n\n    def encaps(self, pubkey: bytes) -> Tuple[bytes, bytes]:\n        \"\"\"Generate a random secret and encrypt it with a public key, returning secret and cipher text as byte arrays\"\"\"\n\n    def decaps(self, ciphertext: bytes, privkey: bytes) -> bytes:\n        \"\"\"Decrypt an encrypted secret using a private key, returning the decrypted secret as a byte array\"\"\"\n```\n\nWhat serialization options did you have in mind? I'd want raw bytes in my application, eventually concatenating these bytes with bytes from X25519 or ECDSA and then encoding the combination as an SSH \"string\" object.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "reaperhulk", "timestamp": "2025-04-29T21:29:18Z", "body": "I'm currently thinking we support both the PKCS8 serialization (seed only) as well as bare seed. Do you need the expanded key for your use case or is the seed sufficient \ud83e\udd1e?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-04-29T21:29:50Z", "body": "I think the only two serialization formats on the table are: raw seed, and\r\nseed-only PKCS8.\r\n\r\nOn Tue, Apr 29, 2025 at 5:27\u202fPM Ron Frederick ***@***.***>\r\nwrote:\r\n\r\n> *ronf* left a comment (pyca/cryptography#12824)\r\n> <https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12824#issuecomment-2840290357>\r\n>\r\n> Here's the API I'm currently using (which currently wraps around liboqs):\r\n>\r\n> class PQDH:\r\n>     \"\"\"A shim around liboqs for post-quantum key exchange algorithms\"\"\"\r\n>\r\n>     def __init__(self, alg_name: bytes):\r\n>         \"\"\"Select the PQ algorithm to use, setting internal methods for keypair, encaps, and decaps\"\"\"\r\n>\r\n>     def keypair(self) -> Tuple[bytes, bytes]:\r\n>         \"\"\"Make a new key pair, returning public and private key byte arrays\"\"\"\r\n>\r\n>     def encaps(self, pubkey: bytes) -> Tuple[bytes, bytes]:\r\n>         \"\"\"Generate a random secret and encrypt it with a public key, returning secret and cipher text as byte arrays\"\"\"\r\n>\r\n>     def decaps(self, ciphertext: bytes, privkey: bytes) -> bytes:\r\n>         \"\"\"Decrypt an encrypted secret using a private key, returning the decrypted secret as a byte array\"\"\"\r\n>\r\n> What serialization options did you have in mind? I'd want raw bytes in my\r\n> application, eventually concatenating these bytes with bytes from X25519 or\r\n> ECDSA and then encoding the combination as an SSH \"string\" object.\r\n>\r\n> \u2014\r\n> Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\r\n> <https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12824#issuecomment-2840290357>,\r\n> or unsubscribe\r\n> <https://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AAAAGBFZPG4ZZXZMK4ZJWPL237VFHAVCNFSM6AAAAAB4DZIML2VHI2DSMVQWIX3LMV43OSLTON2WKQ3PNVWWK3TUHMZDQNBQGI4TAMZVG4>\r\n> .\r\n> You are receiving this because you commented.Message ID:\r\n> ***@***.***>\r\n>\r\n\r\n\r\n-- \r\nAll that is necessary for evil to succeed is for good people to do nothing.\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "ronf", "timestamp": "2025-04-29T21:46:22Z", "body": "As far as I know, the liboqs library always generates expanded keys in the keypair() call and expects an expanded key in the decaps() call. I don't think it has any support for dealing with seeds. Since the private keys are ephemeral for DH, I don't think supporting seeds would really buy us much for that use case, but I could see later on wanting to have a function to expand a seed to an expanded key.\n\nI still don't quite see how serialization fits in on your decap call. Shouldn't that always return a byte string which matches the byte string passed in to encap (if the key matches)? Neither the public nor private key is returned here.\n\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "reaperhulk", "timestamp": "2025-04-29T21:52:05Z", "body": "There wouldn't be any serialization in decap. The current API proposal isn't stateless, it expects you will have loaded an `MLKEM768DecapKey` and then called `decap` on it with the ciphertext.\n\nThere is a strong desire to not expose expanded decap keys as a serializable type for a variety of reasons (see: https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/122/materials/slides-122-pquip-the-great-private-key-war-of-25-00 for a bit of context), so I'm interested in what the SSH protocol's requirements are for this. I'm guessing you need to be able to obtain encapsulation keys as raw bytes with no SPKI wrapper, but what else?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "ronf", "timestamp": "2025-04-29T22:35:31Z", "body": "SSH requires access to the public key as bytes (1184 bytes long for mlkem768x25519-sha256), along with the 32 byte X25519 public key. It concatenates these and encodes the combination as an SSH string (4 bytes of length followed by concatenated raw key bytes) and sends this public key data in an MSG_KEX_ECDH_INIT message\n\nWhen the server receives the INIT message, it extracts the public keys and calls encaps() on the PQ public key bytes and gets back a random secret and cipher text. The cipher text (1088 bytes) is combined with X25519 shared bytes (32 bytes) and sent as a concatenated SSH string in a MSG_KEX_ECDH_REPLY message.\n\nThe client decrypts the cipher text with decaps() using its private key and creates a shared key made of the random secret and EC shared bytes concatenated and passed into a SHA-256 hash. The server has the same secret bytes and EC shared bytes, and it does the same. There's some other stuff here related to signing with the SSH server's host key, but that's not relevant for this discussion.\n\nI have to admit I don't know the exact formatting of the ML-KEM public key bytes in liboqs, but whatever liboqs returns from its keypair() function is exactly what SSH needed to put on the wire.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c8", "author": "ronf", "timestamp": "2025-04-29T22:43:00Z", "body": "> There wouldn't be any serialization in decap. The current API proposal isn't stateless, it expects you will have loaded an MLKEM768DecapKey and then called decap on it with the ciphertext.\n\nThat's fine, but I'm not clear on the encap_bytes() and decap_bytes() functions you proposed with serialization arguments. Are those for key serialization? If so, it's confusing to use the same encap/decap name for both purposes.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c9", "author": "reaperhulk", "timestamp": "2025-05-06T20:40:04Z", "body": "Dropping this here for my own reference:\n* [BoringSSL MLKEM API](https://boringssl.googlesource.com/boringssl/+/refs/heads/main/include/openssl/mlkem.h)\n* LibreSSL will be using the boring implementation, but as of 4.1.0 their release notes say `Imported ML-KEM 768 and 1024 from BoringSSL (not yet public API).`\n* [OpenSSL KEM API](https://docs.openssl.org/master/man7/EVP_PKEY-ML-KEM/#provider-configuration-parameters)\n* [aws-lc KEM API](https://github.com/aws/aws-lc/blob/cddf0ecd56a433497c588d3b5782a5aa9629c077/crypto/fipsmodule/kem/README.md)\n\nAll of these will need to be supported in `rust-openssl` to move forward here.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c10", "author": "frasertweedale", "timestamp": "2025-10-24T08:43:07Z", "body": "Hello @reaperhulk, @alex, et al.  I'm an engineer at Red Hat and I'm assigned to work toward ML-DSA and ML-KEM support in python-cryptography, aiming to deliver in the first half of 2026.  I had a sync with @simo5 - I believe he was in some earlier discussions, and he conveyed (at a high level) some of the expectations in this project.  These include:\n\n1. delivering support for OpenSSL backend + one additional backend (e.g. BoringSSL or aws-lc).  This is to ensure that the API design and implementation approach are compatible with multiple backends, and we don't accidentally do something OpenSSL-specific.  In terms of which additional backend(s), does the project have any preference?\n\n2. Proper and proactive agreement on APIs and implementation approach.\n\n3. 100% code coverage.\n\nFor Red Hat, ML-DSA is the higher priority and I want to work toward delivering that first.  (I did not see a github issue for ML-DSA so I will make a new one).  Is addition of ML-DSA separately and prior to ML-KEM acceptable for you?  (ML-KEM design discussion can proceed in any case).\n\n(For the avoidance of doubt, SLH-DSA is not a priority for us right now.)\n\nAre there any other things that need to be discussed/agreed or that you want to make me aware of, before I dive into the work?\n\nThanks,\nFraser", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c11", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-10-24T14:28:17Z", "body": "If your goal is ML-DSA, then my recommended steps are:\n\n1) File an issue with a proposed ML-DSA API where we can agree on it\n\n2) Implement ML-DSA for a non-OpenSSL backend (any of BoringSSL, AWS-LC, or LibreSSL) first\n\n3) Then add the appropriate functionality to rust-openssl and implement for OpenSSL here", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c12", "author": "abbra", "timestamp": "2026-01-21T15:41:04Z", "body": "Adding here my status as well.\n\nI implemented ML-DSA, ML-KEM, and hybrid KEMs on top of @FinnRG's  pull requests to rust-openssl to add OpenSSL 3.5+ support.\n\nYou can find the changes in https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/compare/main...abbra:cryptography:abbra-add-pqc. Finn's PRs were merged into a single branch https://github.com/abbra/rust-openssl/tree/pqc-prs to allow Cargo.toml patch-up for CI use.\n\nThe implementation includes base classes, documentation, tests and a rudimentary ML-KEM interoperability testing with command line openssl tool, to validate data exchange.\n\nHere is an example of an upstream CI workflow that is mostly green. There are 'failures' in documentation spell-checker as it doesn't know about encapsulate/decapsulate. Additionally, I've found few issues with openssl executable used by pyca infra on Windows: it is misconfigured and cannot read files. As a result, I had to disable ML-KEM interop test suite on Windows.\n\nCI run: https://github.com/abbra/cryptography/actions/runs/21213933260\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c13", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-01-21T15:44:23Z", "body": "Thanks for your interest -- our intent is to begin with landing implementations for the OpenSSL forks in order to allow us to focus on the API/tests/docs/etc. without dealing with our frustrations with OpenSSL (https://cryptography.io/en/latest/statements/state-of-openssl/).\n\nFor the spell check, there's a file `docs/wordlist.txt` or something like that that you can add words to.\n\nI'm not sure what the Windows issue would/could be. Why does OpenSSL need to read files to do ML-KEM?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c14", "author": "abbra", "timestamp": "2026-01-21T15:50:38Z", "body": "@alex regarding Windows -- I implemented a test where I generate keys with pyca, store them as files, then let openssl read them and vice versa: generate with openssl binary, then read files with pyca. This to ensure interoperability between encoders/decoders. OpenSSL build pyca CI is using is misconfigured and issues errors.\n\nI'm not sure what exactly is broken but it looks like neither default nor base provider is available and thus standard store loaders are missing. This is typically an issue with missing default configuration file for openssl:\n\n```\nE                   AssertionError: OpenSSL decapsulation failed: Could not open file or uri for loading private key from C:\\Users\\RUNNER~1\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\tmp15ovkhfh.pem\n\nE                     A8140000:error:80000020:system library:BIO_new_file:Broken pipe:crypto\\bio\\bss_file.c:67:calling fopen(C:\\Users\\RUNNER~1\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\tmp15ovkhfh.pem, rb)\n\nE                     A8140000:error:10080002:BIO routines:BIO_new_file:system lib:crypto\\bio\\bss_file.c:77:\n\nE                     A8140000:error:1608010C:STORE routines:inner_loader_fetch:unsupported:crypto\\store\\store_meth.c:363:No store loader found. For standard store loaders you need at least one of the default or base providers available. Did you forget to load them? Info: Global default library context, Scheme (C : 0), Properties (<null>)\n\nE                     pkeyutl: Error loading key\n\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c15", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-01-21T15:52:14Z", "body": "Ok. This isn't really the style of tests we use anywhere else -- we check in test vectors for things we want to verify we load in vectors/ and then make specific assertions about encoding output.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c16", "author": "abbra", "timestamp": "2026-01-21T15:56:06Z", "body": "Sure. Adding vectors is in plans as well. At this point I have implemented all ML-DSA and ML-KEM variants as well as hybrid KEMs together with tests, so there is already wide coverage of them.\n\nIt would be nice if you (project maintainers) could give a concrete feedback towards the implementation. @frasertweedale plans to work on adding OpenSSL forks support based on the code I have produced so far, so it is going to be a joint effort.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c17", "author": "reaperhulk", "timestamp": "2026-01-21T16:26:51Z", "body": "That is an immense diff (+8000). I appreciate that it contains roughly everything, but let's strip this down to bare essentials to start reviewing and potentially landing more effectively.\n\nLooking at it a bit, the APIs you've defined for MLDSA are similar to what we did for Edwards curves. However, it is an explicit non-goal of this project to use expanded forms of MLDSA or MLKEM keys -- we will support seed only. Hence, `private_bytes_raw` is not an API we want to support unless it outputs the bare seed. In the proposed API for MLKEM in this thread we have `from_seed_bytes`, which I think you're trying to match with `from_private_bytes`, but due to the expanded vs seed ambiguity I'd prefer the former name for MLDSA as well.\n\nTo make some forward progress here I'd suggest the following. Let's pick MLDSA, strip it down to a single key size, and land that. Doing that has this ordering of PRs:\n1) Vectors for ML-DSA testing\n2) Land impl for boring or aws-lc + docs + tests\n3) Followups for other impls.\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c18", "author": "DarkaMaul", "timestamp": "2026-04-16T07:43:15Z", "body": "> I think all that's left here is docs. [@DarkaMaul](https://github.com/DarkaMaul) is that something you're working on?\n\nYes - I'm working on the doc", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c19", "author": "ronf", "timestamp": "2026-04-18T21:02:58Z", "body": "I just did an interoperability test between this ML-KEM support (using BoringSSL) and a prior implementation I had done on top of liboqs. Everything looks good. The public_bytes_raw() call on the public key is exactly what was needed on the wire by SSH, and so was the ciphertext returned from the encap call. I confirmed that I could exchange the 32-byte secret successfully in both directions.\n\nAs expected, there was no requirement to serialize the private key data. So, the fact that this API operates only on the seed and not the expanded key will not be a problem.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c20", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-04-18T21:10:13Z", "body": "Excellent. I think this is all benefitting from teh fact that ML-KEM's cryptographic API is much better designed (with the exception of seed vs. expanded form) and has way fewer parameters than previous cryptographic algorithms (e.g., no multiple representations of signature like ECDSA, or 19 different parameters like RSA PSS).", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c21", "author": "ronf", "timestamp": "2026-04-18T21:28:20Z", "body": "Agreed!\n\nOn that note, has there been any consideration for adding SNTRUP support to PyCA? It has basically an identical API, and it has been providing quantum-resistant crypto in SSH since OpenSSH 8.0 released in 2019, dating back to before ML-KEM was selected as the new standard. Both SNTRUP and ML-KEM are supported by liboqs, so I'm actually sharing a wrapper between those today in AsyncSSH, but at some point it would be nice to drop liboqs as an additional dependency.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c22", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2026-04-18T21:37:16Z", "body": "No plan for SNTRUP ATM. My sense is that ML-KEM has won at this point and nothing is likely to use SNTRUP for anythign new.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12824#1", "question": "For the proposed pyca/cryptography ML-KEM API, there is a strong desire not to expose expanded decapsulation keys as a serializable type, and to support only seed-based private key material. The proposed API is stateful: users load an `MLKEM768DecapKey` and then call `decap` on it with the ciphertext; there would not be any serialization in `decap`. Given SSH's use of ML-KEM, what are the SSH protocol requirements here? I am guessing SSH needs to obtain encapsulation keys as raw bytes with no SPKI wrapper, but what else is required, especially regarding expanded decapsulation/private keys?", "answer": "An interoperability test between the pyca/cryptography ML-KEM support using BoringSSL and a prior liboqs-based implementation showed that everything SSH needed worked. `public_bytes_raw()` on the public key produced exactly the bytes needed on the SSH wire, and the ciphertext returned from `encap` was also in the required form; a 32-byte secret was exchanged successfully in both directions. SSH did not require serialization of private key data, so the API operating only on the seed rather than the expanded key was not a problem.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["pyca/cryptography ML-KEM support using BoringSSL", "prior liboqs-based implementation", "public_bytes_raw() API", "encap() API"], "answer_grounded_in": "c19", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": [], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T08:06:24.077713Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12780", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 12780, "title": "The cryptography library parsed a CRL file with duplicate revoked certificate entries.", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12780", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-04-18T12:41:50Z", "closed_at": "2025-05-02T00:00:26Z", "labels": ["waiting-on-reporter", "Stale"], "reporter": "onepeople158", "security_topic": "duplicate CRL revocation entries handling in X.509 validation", "qa_summary": "The developer asked whether duplicate CRL entries constitute a security bug and if X.509 validation will reject them; the maintainer replied it isn\u2019t a bug now but will be checked in X.509 validation, referencing issue #10393.", "question_comment_id": "c6", "answer_comment_id": "c7", "question_author": "onepeople158", "answer_author": "alex", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "issue_tracker", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": ["#10393"], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "onepeople158", "timestamp": "2025-04-18T12:41:50Z", "body": "Version:\ncryptography-44.0.2\n\nHello developer, I successfully parsed a CRL file with duplicate revoked certificate entries using cryptography. According to RFC 5280:\n\n> Each revocation entry is uniquely identified by its serial number.\n\nSo, is this a bug?\n\nCode:\n```\nfrom cryptography.x509 import load_pem_x509_crl, load_der_x509_crl\nfrom cryptography.x509 import ExtensionNotFound\nimport sys\n\ndef load_crl(file_path):\n    with open(file_path, \"rb\") as f:\n        crl_data = f.read()\n    try:\n        crl = load_pem_x509_crl(crl_data)\n    except ValueError:\n        crl = load_der_x509_crl(crl_data)\n    return crl\n\ndef print_crl_issuer(file_path):\n    crl=load_crl(file_path)\n    try:\n        for entry in crl:\n               print(f\"Serial Number: {entry.serial_number}\")\n               print(f\"Revocation Date: {entry.revocation_date_utc}\")\n               if entry.extensions:\n                    for ext in entry.extensions:\n                          if ext.oid ==x509.oid.CRLEntryExtensionOID.CRL_REASON:\n                                 print(f\"reason: {ext.value.reason}\")\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"Error occurred: {e}\")\n\nfile_path = 'crl_revoked_dublicate.der'\nprint_crl_issuer(file_path)\n```\nTest Case:\n[crl_revoked_dublicate.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/19812512/crl_revoked_dublicate.zip)\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-04-18T13:37:11Z", "body": "From my perspective this isn't a bug, this is something that doesn't make sense to validate on parse. If/when we add CRLs to X.509 path validation, we should enfoce this there (#10393).\n\nDo you disagree?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "onepeople158", "timestamp": "2025-04-18T14:02:45Z", "body": "Hello, developer. If we encounter a situation when viewing the CRL where there are two entries with the same certificate serial number, but different expiration dates and revocation reasons, would this cause an issue?The relying party cannot determine which entry should be trusted.\nTest Case:\n\n[crl_revoked_dublicate_serial.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/19813133/crl_revoked_dublicate_serial.zip)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-04-18T14:06:55Z", "body": "It does make the revocation date/reason ambigious, but I don't know if this is a problem in practice.\n\nIf we do this check in X.509 validation, we avoid this problem.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "onepeople158", "timestamp": "2025-04-19T01:32:33Z", "body": "> It does make the revocation date/reason ambigious, but I don't know if this is a problem in practice.\n> \n> If we do this check in X.509 validation, we avoid this problem.\n\nIndeed, this is just a potential issue.\n\n\n\n\n\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "github-actions", "timestamp": "2025-04-23T00:00:32Z", "body": "This issue has been waiting for a reporter response for 3 days. It will be auto-closed if no activity occurs in the next 5 days.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "onepeople158", "timestamp": "2025-04-23T07:15:28Z", "body": "> It does make the revocation date/reason ambigious, but I don't know if this is a problem in practice.\n> \n> If we do this check in X.509 validation, we avoid this problem.\n\nHello Developer, will a check be added to the X.509 verification process to reject CRL files that contain either duplicate revocation entries or duplicate serial numbers within the revoked list?\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-04-23T10:54:25Z", "body": "We don't yet process CRLs in X.509 validation, when we do we will check for\r\nthis.\r\n\r\nOn Wed, Apr 23, 2025 at 3:15\u202fAM onepeople158 ***@***.***>\r\nwrote:\r\n\r\n> It does make the revocation date/reason ambigious, but I don't know if\r\n> this is a problem in practice.\r\n>\r\n> If we do this check in X.509 validation, we avoid this problem.\r\n>\r\n> Hello, developer. Could you please tell me if the X509 verify process\r\n> checks for duplicate revocation entries in the CRL?\r\n>\r\n> \u2014\r\n> Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\r\n> <https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12780#issuecomment-2823292951>,\r\n> or unsubscribe\r\n> <https://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AAAAGBDIHDKU3EBAVSWLOAD22442NAVCNFSM6AAAAAB3M7KXC2VHI2DSMVQWIX3LMV43OSLTON2WKQ3PNVWWK3TUHMZDQMRTGI4TEOJVGE>\r\n> .\r\n> You are receiving this because you commented.Message ID:\r\n> ***@***.***>\r\n> *onepeople158* left a comment (pyca/cryptography#12780)\r\n> <https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12780#issuecomment-2823292951>\r\n>\r\n> It does make the revocation date/reason ambigious, but I don't know if\r\n> this is a problem in practice.\r\n>\r\n> If we do this check in X.509 validation, we avoid this problem.\r\n>\r\n> Hello, developer. Could you please tell me if the X509 verify process\r\n> checks for duplicate revocation entries in the CRL?\r\n>\r\n> \u2014\r\n> Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\r\n> <https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12780#issuecomment-2823292951>,\r\n> or unsubscribe\r\n> <https://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AAAAGBDIHDKU3EBAVSWLOAD22442NAVCNFSM6AAAAAB3M7KXC2VHI2DSMVQWIX3LMV43OSLTON2WKQ3PNVWWK3TUHMZDQMRTGI4TEOJVGE>\r\n> .\r\n> You are receiving this because you commented.Message ID:\r\n> ***@***.***>\r\n>\r\n\r\n\r\n-- \r\nAll that is necessary for evil to succeed is for good people to do nothing.\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c8", "author": "onepeople158", "timestamp": "2025-04-23T10:56:38Z", "body": "> We don't yet process CRLs in X.509 validation, when we do we will check for\n> this.\n> [\u2026](#)\n> \n> On Wed, Apr 23, 2025 at 3:15\u202fAM onepeople158 ***@***.***> wrote:\n>  It does make the revocation date/reason ambigious, but I don't know if\n>  this is a problem in practice.\n> \n>  If we do this check in X.509 validation, we avoid this problem.\n> \n>  Hello, developer. Could you please tell me if the X509 verify process\n>  checks for duplicate revocation entries in the CRL?\n> \n>  \u2014\n>  Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\n>  <[#12780 (comment)](https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12780#issuecomment-2823292951)>,\n>  or unsubscribe\n>  <https://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AAAAGBDIHDKU3EBAVSWLOAD22442NAVCNFSM6AAAAAB3M7KXC2VHI2DSMVQWIX3LMV43OSLTON2WKQ3PNVWWK3TUHMZDQMRTGI4TEOJVGE>\n>  .\n>  You are receiving this because you commented.Message ID:\n>  ***@***.***>\n>  *onepeople158* left a comment ([pyca/cryptography#12780](https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12780))\n>  <[#12780 (comment)](https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12780#issuecomment-2823292951)>\n> \n>  It does make the revocation date/reason ambigious, but I don't know if\n>  this is a problem in practice.\n> \n>  If we do this check in X.509 validation, we avoid this problem.\n> \n>  Hello, developer. Could you please tell me if the X509 verify process\n>  checks for duplicate revocation entries in the CRL?\n> \n>  \u2014\n>  Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\n>  <[#12780 (comment)](https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12780#issuecomment-2823292951)>,\n>  or unsubscribe\n>  <https://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AAAAGBDIHDKU3EBAVSWLOAD22442NAVCNFSM6AAAAAB3M7KXC2VHI2DSMVQWIX3LMV43OSLTON2WKQ3PNVWWK3TUHMZDQMRTGI4TEOJVGE>\n>  .\n>  You are receiving this because you commented.Message ID:\n>  ***@***.***>\n> \n> \n> -- \n> All that is necessary for evil to succeed is for good people to do nothing.\n\nSure, I understand.\n\n\n\n\n\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c9", "author": "github-actions", "timestamp": "2025-04-27T00:00:19Z", "body": "This issue has been waiting for a reporter response for 3 days. It will be auto-closed if no activity occurs in the next 5 days.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c10", "author": "github-actions", "timestamp": "2025-05-02T00:00:26Z", "body": "This issue has not received a reporter response and has been auto-closed. If the issue is still relevant please leave a comment and we can reopen it.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12780#1", "question": "In pyca/cryptography version cryptography-44.0.2, I can successfully parse a CRL file that contains duplicate revoked certificate entries. RFC 5280 says: \u201cEach revocation entry is uniquely identified by its serial number.\u201d\n\nExample code:\n```\nfrom cryptography.x509 import load_pem_x509_crl, load_der_x509_crl\nfrom cryptography.x509 import ExtensionNotFound\nimport sys\n\ndef load_crl(file_path):\n    with open(file_path, \"rb\") as f:\n        crl_data = f.read()\n    try:\n        crl = load_pem_x509_crl(crl_data)\n    except ValueError:\n        crl = load_der_x509_crl(crl_data)\n    return crl\n\ndef print_crl_issuer(file_path):\n    crl=load_crl(file_path)\n    try:\n        for entry in crl:\n               print(f\"Serial Number: {entry.serial_number}\")\n               print(f\"Revocation Date: {entry.revocation_date_utc}\")\n               if entry.extensions:\n                    for ext in entry.extensions:\n                          if ext.oid ==x509.oid.CRLEntryExtensionOID.CRL_REASON:\n                                 print(f\"reason: {ext.value.reason}\")\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"Error occurred: {e}\")\n\nfile_path = 'crl_revoked_dublicate.der'\nprint_crl_issuer(file_path)\n```\n\nTest cases were provided as `crl_revoked_dublicate.zip` and `crl_revoked_dublicate_serial.zip`. In the latter case, there can be two entries with the same certificate serial number but different revocation dates and revocation reasons, making it ambiguous which entry a relying party should trust.\n\nWill a check be added to the X.509 verification process to reject CRL files that contain either duplicate revocation entries or duplicate serial numbers within the revoked list?\n\n[crl_revoked_dublicate.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/19812512/crl_revoked_dublicate.zip)\n[crl_revoked_dublicate_serial.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/19813133/crl_revoked_dublicate_serial.zip)", "answer": "cryptography does not yet process CRLs in X.509 validation. When CRL processing is added to X.509 validation, the maintainers intend to check for duplicate revocation entries / duplicate serial numbers there.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["pyca/cryptography X.509 validation behavior", "issue #10393"], "answer_grounded_in": "c7", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AAAAGBDIHDKU3EBAVSWLOAD22442NAVCNFSM6AAAAAB3M7KXC2VHI2DSMVQWIX3LMV43OSLTON2WKQ3PNVWWK3TUHMZDQMRTGI4TEOJVGE", "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12780#issuecomment-2823292951"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-18T08:04:15.389724Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12748", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 12748, "title": "Cryptography parsed a CRL file with the authorityCertSerialNumber set to 0.", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12748", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-04-09T14:14:59Z", "closed_at": "2025-05-06T16:19:03Z", "labels": ["x509"], "reporter": "onepeople158", "security_topic": "validation of authorityCertSerialNumber zero value in CRL parsing", "qa_summary": "The developer asked whether parsing an authorityCertSerialNumber of 0 violates RFC5280 and constitutes a bug, and the maintainer replied it is intentional and allowed, referencing issue #10247.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "onepeople158", "answer_author": "alex", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "pr_data", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": ["#10247"], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Accepted because the question is a valid standards compliance concern and the answer clearly explains intentional leniency in parsing behavior.", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "onepeople158", "timestamp": "2025-04-09T14:14:59Z", "body": "Version:\ncryptography-44.0.2\n\nHello Developer:\nI have a CRL file where the authorityCertSerialNumber field in the AKI extension is set to 0. Cryptography successfully parsed the authorityCertSerialNumber field without any errors. However, according to RFC5280, the authorityCertSerialNumber field stores the certificate serial number, which must be a positive integer.So, is this a bug?\n\nCode:\n```\nfrom cryptography.x509 import load_pem_x509_crl, load_der_x509_crl\nfrom cryptography.x509 import ExtensionNotFound\nimport sys\n\ndef load_crl(file_path):\n    with open(file_path, \"rb\") as f:\n        crl_data = f.read()\n    try:\n        crl = load_pem_x509_crl(crl_data)\n    except ValueError:\n        crl = load_der_x509_crl(crl_data)\n    return crl\n\ndef print_crl_issuer(file_path):\n    crl=load_crl(file_path)\n    aki_extension = None\n    try:\n        for ext in crl.extensions:\n             if ext.oid == x509.oid.ExtensionOID.AUTHORITY_KEY_IDENTIFIER:\n                    aki=ext.value\n                    print(aki.authority_cert_issuer)\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"Error parsing CRL: {e}\")\n\nfile_path = 'crl_aki_serial_0.der'\nprint_crl_issuer(file_path)\n```\nTest Case:\n\n[crl_aki_serial_0.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/19668466/crl_aki_serial_0.zip)", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-04-09T17:41:09Z", "body": "Yes, it's fairly intentional. We also allow negative serial numbers (both there, and for certificate's serial numbers), as a result of a large number of certs in the ecosystem still. See #10247.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "onepeople158", "timestamp": "2025-04-10T01:28:19Z", "body": "Hello, developer. I also noticed that when the authorityCertSerialNumber is set to a negative number, cryptography will throw an error.\n\nTest Case:\n\n[crl_aki_serial-36.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/19676341/crl_aki_serial-36.zip)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-04-10T01:44:56Z", "body": "Oh, interesting, I didn't realize we enforced non-negativity there. I guess we should also exclude 0.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "onepeople158", "timestamp": "2025-04-10T02:01:43Z", "body": "> Oh, interesting, I didn't realize we enforced non-negativity there. I guess we should also exclude 0.\n\nThanks.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12748#1", "question": "In cryptography 44.0.2, I have a CRL file where the `authorityCertSerialNumber` field in the AKI extension is set to `0`. `cryptography` successfully parses the `authorityCertSerialNumber` field without any errors, but RFC5280 says `authorityCertSerialNumber` stores the certificate serial number, which must be a positive integer. Is this a bug?\n\nCode:\n```\nfrom cryptography.x509 import load_pem_x509_crl, load_der_x509_crl\nfrom cryptography.x509 import ExtensionNotFound\nimport sys\n\ndef load_crl(file_path):\n    with open(file_path, \"rb\") as f:\n        crl_data = f.read()\n    try:\n        crl = load_pem_x509_crl(crl_data)\n    except ValueError:\n        crl = load_der_x509_crl(crl_data)\n    return crl\n\ndef print_crl_issuer(file_path):\n    crl=load_crl(file_path)\n    aki_extension = None\n    try:\n        for ext in crl.extensions:\n             if ext.oid == x509.oid.ExtensionOID.AUTHORITY_KEY_IDENTIFIER:\n                    aki=ext.value\n                    print(aki.authority_cert_issuer)\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"Error parsing CRL: {e}\")\n\nfile_path = 'crl_aki_serial_0.der'\nprint_crl_issuer(file_path)\n```\n\nTest case: `crl_aki_serial_0.zip`.", "answer": "The maintainer initially said accepting this was fairly intentional, noting that cryptography also allows negative serial numbers in that context and for certificate serial numbers because many such certificates still exist in the ecosystem, and pointed to issue #10247.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["issue #10247"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["#10247"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-16T03:09:02.654156Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12359", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 12359, "title": "Verify Certificate Chain Without Trust Store", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12359", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-01-29T14:14:29Z", "closed_at": "2025-02-08T00:00:26Z", "labels": ["waiting-on-reporter", "Stale"], "reporter": "Wyko", "security_topic": "retrieving TLS intermediate certificates in Python", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know how to obtain intermediate certificates for chain verification, and the answer pointed to Python's ssl.SSLSocket.get_unverified_chain documentation as the concrete source.", "question_comment_id": "c6", "answer_comment_id": "c7", "question_author": "Wyko", "answer_author": "alex", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Accepted because the question is a valid TLS/PKI workflow question and the answer correctly points to the appropriate standard library interface.", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "Wyko", "timestamp": "2025-01-29T14:14:29Z", "body": "Good morning!\n\nI have an application that needs to verify a certificate chain, ignoring the trustworthiness of the root. Specifically, I need to do the following:\n- Given a specific URL;\n- Download the certificate;\n- Check that the server / leaf certificate is present;\n- Verify that it is signed by **any** root CA\n- Verify that the full chain up to and including the Intermediates and the CA is present in the chain\n\nThe solutions I have found rely on the trust store of my computer to already contain the relevant root CAs. Can the package help me here?", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-01-29T14:27:21Z", "body": "Why do you want to accomplish this?  Verifying a certificate chains to _any_ root CA provides no security properties, simply `return True` is a valid implementation.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "Wyko", "timestamp": "2025-01-29T15:28:10Z", "body": "@alex , this isn't about security, actually. This is about basic validation of a configuration on a network device. For this configuration type, clients provide their own certificates which must be signed by a root CA somewhere. I don't need to (nor am I even able to) validate that the root CA is valid or trusted; I only need to validate that the leaf certificate is signed and that the file contains a complete chain back to whatever root signed it.\n\nI know it's not exactly  common use case, but it's what I'm working with right now.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-01-29T15:31:47Z", "body": "Sorry, I'm not sure I'm following. Is your input just the leaf certificate,\r\nor do you have a nominally full chain and you want to verify that the leaf\r\nreally does chain to the root?\r\n\r\nOn Wed, Jan 29, 2025 at 10:28\u202fAM Wyko ***@***.***> wrote:\r\n\r\n> @alex <https://github.com/alex> , this isn't about security, actually.\r\n> This is about basic validation of a configuration on a network device. For\r\n> this configuration type, clients provide their own certificates which must\r\n> be signed by a root CA somewhere. I don't need to (nor am I even able to)\r\n> validate that the root CA is valid or trusted; I only need to validate that\r\n> the certificate has a complete chain back to whatever root signed it.\r\n>\r\n> I know, not exactly common use case, but it's what I'm working with right\r\n> now.\r\n>\r\n> \u2014\r\n> Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\r\n> <https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12359#issuecomment-2621969919>,\r\n> or unsubscribe\r\n> <https://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AAAAGBCUP6YAWKIHKZYN6NL2NDXSDAVCNFSM6AAAAABWC6T74GVHI2DSMVQWIX3LMV43OSLTON2WKQ3PNVWWK3TUHMZDMMRRHE3DSOJRHE>\r\n> .\r\n> You are receiving this because you were mentioned.Message ID:\r\n> ***@***.***>\r\n>\r\n\r\n\r\n-- \r\nAll that is necessary for evil to succeed is for good people to do nothing.\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "Wyko", "timestamp": "2025-01-29T15:39:00Z", "body": "The second one. As I put in my original post, once I have everything installed on my network device, I should be able to access it in my browser, like this:\n\n```echo \"q\" | openssl s_client -connect microsoft.com:443 -showcerts | grep depth```\n\nThis should show the entire chain. Sometimes, if a cert is misconfigured, it could be missing one of the certificates in the chain:\n\n```echo \"q\" | openssl s_client -connect incomplete-chain.badssl.com:443 -showcerts | grep depth```\n\nI need to diagnose these types of errors in my python validation tool.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-01-29T15:43:06Z", "body": "Ok. In that case what you need to do is:\r\n\r\na) Somehow find the root -- the server does not provide the root, so you'll\r\nhave to do something like implement Authority Information Access (AIA)\r\nchasing to go from an intermediate to a root. (This is basically \"choose an\r\nintermediate, find its AIA extension, make an HTTP request to get the root\r\nwhere it claims it is\", but there's a bunch of fiddly bits)\r\nb) Create a verifier using that root as the root, using the intermediates\r\nprovided as intermediates, and then verify.\r\n\r\n\r\nOn Wed, Jan 29, 2025 at 10:39\u202fAM Wyko ***@***.***> wrote:\r\n\r\n> The second one. As I put in my original post, once I have everything\r\n> installed on my network device, I should be able to access it in my\r\n> browser, like this:\r\n>\r\n> echo \"q\" | openssl s_client -connect microsoft.com:443 -showcerts | grep\r\n> depth\r\n>\r\n> This should show the entire chain. Sometimes, if a cert is misconfigured,\r\n> it could be missing one of the certificates in the chain:\r\n>\r\n> echo \"q\" | openssl s_client -connect incomplete-chain.badssl.com:443\r\n> -showcerts | grep depth\r\n>\r\n> I need to diagnose these types of errors in my python validation tool.\r\n>\r\n> \u2014\r\n> Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\r\n> <https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12359#issuecomment-2621996943>,\r\n> or unsubscribe\r\n> <https://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AAAAGBA4ICTGDTYGZN54I5L2NDY2VAVCNFSM6AAAAABWC6T74GVHI2DSMVQWIX3LMV43OSLTON2WKQ3PNVWWK3TUHMZDMMRRHE4TMOJUGM>\r\n> .\r\n> You are receiving this because you were mentioned.Message ID:\r\n> ***@***.***>\r\n>\r\n\r\n\r\n-- \r\nAll that is necessary for evil to succeed is for good people to do nothing.\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "Wyko", "timestamp": "2025-01-30T12:55:29Z", "body": "Okay, fair enough. Is there a way to get the intermediates using Cryptography? So far I've seen this:\n```leaf = load_pem_x509_certificate(ssl.get_server_certificate((subject, 443)).encode())```\nBut as far as I can tell, that only returns the leaf, and not whatever intermediates the server might also be presenting. ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-01-30T12:59:34Z", "body": "Getting the intermediates from the TLS handshake is going to be a question\r\nof your TLS libraries API.\r\nhttps://docs.python.org/3/library/ssl.html#ssl.SSLSocket.get_unverified_chain\r\nis probably what you want\r\n\r\nOn Thu, Jan 30, 2025 at 7:55\u202fAM Wyko ***@***.***> wrote:\r\n\r\n> Okay, fair enough. Is there a way to get the intermediates using\r\n> Cryptography? So far I've seen this:\r\n> leaf = load_pem_x509_certificate(ssl.get_server_certificate((subject,\r\n> 443)).encode())\r\n> But as far as I can tell, that only returns the leaf, and not whatever\r\n> intermediates the server might also be presenting.\r\n>\r\n> \u2014\r\n> Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\r\n> <https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12359#issuecomment-2624442265>,\r\n> or unsubscribe\r\n> <https://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AAAAGBDOGXKVCPIUF7P762D2NIONRAVCNFSM6AAAAABWC6T74GVHI2DSMVQWIX3LMV43OSLTON2WKQ3PNVWWK3TUHMZDMMRUGQ2DEMRWGU>\r\n> .\r\n> You are receiving this because you were mentioned.Message ID:\r\n> ***@***.***>\r\n>\r\n\r\n\r\n-- \r\nAll that is necessary for evil to succeed is for good people to do nothing.\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c8", "author": "github-actions", "timestamp": "2025-02-03T00:00:18Z", "body": "This issue has been waiting for a reporter response for 3 days. It will be auto-closed if no activity occurs in the next 5 days.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c9", "author": "github-actions", "timestamp": "2025-02-08T00:00:26Z", "body": "This issue has not received a reporter response and has been auto-closed. If the issue is still relevant please leave a comment and we can reopen it.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12359#1", "question": "I have a Python validation tool for checking network device certificate configuration. I need to connect to a given HTTPS server, get the nominally full certificate chain it presents, and diagnose cases where a server is missing an intermediate certificate, similar to comparing:\n\n```echo \"q\" | openssl s_client -connect microsoft.com:443 -showcerts | grep depth```\n\nwith:\n\n```echo \"q\" | openssl s_client -connect incomplete-chain.badssl.com:443 -showcerts | grep depth```\n\nI do not need to validate whether the root CA is trusted; I only need to verify that the leaf certificate is signed and that the file/server-presented chain contains a complete path back to whatever root signed it. I was told I will need to find the root separately, create a verifier using that root, and use the server-provided intermediates as intermediates.\n\nIs there a way to get the intermediates using pyca/cryptography? So far I have seen this:\n\n```leaf = load_pem_x509_certificate(ssl.get_server_certificate((subject, 443)).encode())```\n\nBut as far as I can tell, that only returns the leaf certificate and not whatever intermediates the server might also be presenting.", "answer": "Getting the intermediates from the TLS handshake is a question for the TLS library API, not pyca/cryptography itself. Python\u2019s `ssl.SSLSocket.get_unverified_chain` is probably what you want.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://docs.python.org/3/library/ssl.html#ssl.SSLSocket.get_unverified_chain"], "answer_grounded_in": "c7", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://docs.python.org/3/library/ssl.html#ssl.SSLSocket.get_unverified_chain", "https://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AAAAGBDOGXKVCPIUF7P762D2NIONRAVCNFSM6AAAAABWC6T74GVHI2DSMVQWIX3LMV43OSLTON2WKQ3PNVWWK3TUHMZDMMRUGQ2DEMRWGU", "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12359#issuecomment-2624442265"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T08:10:27.558794Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12332", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 12332, "title": "Missing not_valid_after_utc property.", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12332", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-01-23T13:33:16Z", "closed_at": "2025-01-23T13:40:32Z", "labels": ["waiting-on-reporter"], "reporter": "vog", "security_topic": "regression of certificate attribute availability in cryptography library", "qa_summary": "The developer asked whether the `not_valid_after_utc` property was intentionally removed in Cryptography 44.0.0 and how to handle it; the maintainer confirmed the property still exists in version 44.0.0, citing a REPL demonstration showing the attribute.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "vog", "answer_author": "alex", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": ["44.0.0"], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "vog", "timestamp": "2025-01-23T13:33:16Z", "body": "It seems that Cryptography 44.0.0 introduced a regression by replacing `not_valid_after_utc`/`not_valid_before_utc` properties with their non-\"utc\" suffixed counterparts.\n\nBackground:\n\nNot long ago the Cryptography library deprecated the `not_valid_after` property and encouraged everyone to switch to `not_valid_after_utc`:\n\n* #9186\n* #9667\n\nThis is what we in requests_pkcs12, as downstream library, also did:\n\n* m-click/requests_pkcs12#51\n* m-click/requests_pkcs12#52\n\nNow with Cryptography 44.0.0,  the `not_valid_after_utc` suddenly vanished and only `not_valid_after` remained:\n\n* m-click/requests_pkcs12#58\n\nQuestions:\n\n* Is this change back to a deprecated properties intentional?\n  * If so, how are downstream libraries like `requests_pkcs12` supposed to deal with that in a backwards compatible way?", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-01-23T13:36:01Z", "body": "It's very difficult to understand the nature of this report -- the `not_valid_after_utc` attribute absolutely continues to exist:\n\n```\n~ \u276f\u276f\u276f vf tmp\nCreating tempenv-3d533268639f via /opt/homebrew/Cellar/virtualfish/2.5.9_1/libexec/bin/python \u2026\n(tempenv-3d533268639f) ~ \u276f\u276f\u276f uv pip install cryptography\nUsing Python 3.13.1 environment at: .virtualenvs/tempenv-3d533268639f\nResolved 3 packages in 115ms\nInstalled 3 packages in 8ms\n + cffi==1.17.1\n + cryptography==44.0.0\n + pycparser==2.22\n(tempenv-3d533268639f) ~ \u276f\u276f\u276f python3\nPython 3.13.1 (main, Dec  3 2024, 17:59:52) [Clang 16.0.0 (clang-1600.0.26.4)] on darwin\nType \"help\", \"copyright\", \"credits\" or \"license\" for more information.\n>>> from cryptography import x509\n>>> x509.Certificate\n<class 'cryptography.hazmat.bindings._rust.x509.Certificate'>\n>>> x509.Certificate.not_valid_after_utc\n<attribute 'not_valid_after_utc' of 'cryptography.hazmat.bindings._rust.x509.Certificate' objects>\n```\n\nCan you provide steps to reproduce the non-existence behavior?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "vog", "timestamp": "2025-01-23T13:40:32Z", "body": "You are correct. Sorry for the noise. I was confused that the `not_valid_after_utc` property no longer existed in `x509/base.py`.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12332#1", "question": "In pyca/cryptography 44.0.0, it seems there may be a regression where `not_valid_after_utc`/`not_valid_before_utc` properties were replaced by their non-`utc` suffixed counterparts. Background: cryptography previously deprecated `not_valid_after` and encouraged downstreams to switch to `not_valid_after_utc` (#9186, #9667), and `requests_pkcs12` did so in m-click/requests_pkcs12#51 and #52. Now in cryptography 44.0.0 it appears `not_valid_after_utc` suddenly vanished and only `not_valid_after` remained, as discussed in m-click/requests_pkcs12#58. Is this change back to deprecated properties intentional? If so, how should downstream libraries like `requests_pkcs12` handle this in a backwards-compatible way?", "answer": "The maintainer answered that `not_valid_after_utc` still exists in cryptography 44.0.0. They demonstrated it in a fresh environment with Python 3.13.1 and `cryptography==44.0.0`, where `x509.Certificate.not_valid_after_utc` resolves to an attribute.\n\n``\n~ \u276f\u276f\u276f vf tmp\nCreating tempenv-3d533268639f via /opt/homebrew/Cellar/virtualfish/2.5.9_1/libexec/bin/python \u2026\n(tempenv-3d533268639f) ~ \u276f\u276f\u276f uv pip install cryptography\nUsing Python 3.13.1 environment at: .virtualenvs/tempenv-3d533268639f\nResolved 3 packages in 115ms\nInstalled 3 packages in 8ms\n + cffi==1.17.1\n + cryptography==44.0.0\n + pycparser==2.22\n(tempenv-3d533268639f) ~ \u276f\u276f\u276f python3\nPython 3.13.1 (main, Dec  3 2024, 17:59:52) [Clang 16.0.0 (clang-1600.0.26.4)] on darwin\nType \"help\", \"copyright\", \"credits\" or \"license\" for more information.\n>>> from cryptography import x509\n>>> x509.Certificate\n<class 'cryptography.hazmat.bindings._rust.x509.Certificate'>\n>>> x509.Certificate.not_valid_after_utc\n<attribute 'not_valid_after_utc' of 'cryptography.hazmat.bindings._rust.x509.Certificate' objects>\n```", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["cryptography 44.0.0 behavior: `x509.Certificate.not_valid_after_utc` exists"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": ["fixed_versions:44.0.0"]}], "answer_in_thread_refs": [], "leak_flags": ["fixed_versions:44.0.0"], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T17:46:14.709456Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12301", "repo": "pyca/cryptography", "number": 12301, "title": "Wrong type annotation for is_signature_valid", "url": "https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/12301", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-01-17T08:34:43Z", "closed_at": "2025-01-20T07:40:02Z", "labels": ["waiting-on-reporter"], "reporter": "userappgate", "security_topic": "type annotation mismatch for cryptography public key validation", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know that the type signature for `CertificateRevocationList.is_signature_valid` is correct because a certificate can contain a public key not valid for issuing, and they should refer to the Cryptography library\u2019s `CertificateIssuerPrivateKeyTypes` documentation (https://cryptography.io/en/latest/hazmat/primitives/asymmetric/#cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric.types.CertificateIssuerPrivateKeyTypes).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "userappgate", "answer_author": "alex", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "userappgate", "timestamp": "2025-01-17T08:34:43Z", "body": "See this example code:\n\n```python3\nfrom cryptography import x509\n\ndef is_signature_valid(crl: bytes, ca_cert: bytes) -> bool:\n    crl_object = x509.load_pem_x509_crl(crl)\n\n    ca_cert_object = x509.load_pem_x509_certificate(ca_cert)\n\n    return crl_object.is_signature_valid(ca_cert_object.public_key())\n```\n\nmypy fails with\n\n```\ntest.py:8: error: Argument 1 to \"is_signature_valid\" of \"CertificateRevocationList\" has incompatible type \"DSAPublicKey | RSAPublicKey | EllipticCurvePublicKey | Ed25519PublicKey | Ed448PublicKey | X25519PublicKey | X448PublicKey\"; expected \"DSAPublicKey | RSAPublicKey | EllipticCurvePublicKey | Ed25519PublicKey | Ed448PublicKey\"  [arg-type]\n```\n\nI noticed that while migrating the codebast on ubuntu noble (that uses 41.0.7-4ubuntu0.1), while on ubuntu jammy (that uses 3.4.8-1ubuntu2.2) mypy does not complain.\n\nThe codebase is quite different, but the code seems to work on both versions so I think the mistake is in the type annotations (unless I am doing something wrong, of course).\n\nThanks", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "alex", "timestamp": "2025-01-17T13:27:49Z", "body": "Unfortunately, that's correct.\n\nA certificate can _carry_ a public key that's not valid for issuing certificates, and the type signatures reflect this.\n\nYou can use https://cryptography.io/en/latest/hazmat/primitives/asymmetric/#cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric.types.CertificateIssuerPrivateKeyTypes to check for this (either with `typing.cast()` or an `isinstance` check.)", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "pyca/cryptography/issue/12301#1", "question": "In pyca/cryptography, is the type annotation for `CertificateRevocationList.is_signature_valid` wrong? For example:\n\n```python3\nfrom cryptography import x509\n\ndef is_signature_valid(crl: bytes, ca_cert: bytes) -> bool:\n    crl_object = x509.load_pem_x509_crl(crl)\n\n    ca_cert_object = x509.load_pem_x509_certificate(ca_cert)\n\n    return crl_object.is_signature_valid(ca_cert_object.public_key())\n```\n\nOn Ubuntu noble using cryptography `41.0.7-4ubuntu0.1`, mypy fails with:\n\n```text\ntest.py:8: error: Argument 1 to \"is_signature_valid\" of \"CertificateRevocationList\" has incompatible type \"DSAPublicKey | RSAPublicKey | EllipticCurvePublicKey | Ed25519PublicKey | Ed448PublicKey | X25519PublicKey | X448PublicKey\"; expected \"DSAPublicKey | RSAPublicKey | EllipticCurvePublicKey | Ed25519PublicKey | Ed448PublicKey\"  [arg-type]\n```\n\nI noticed that while migrating the codebast on ubuntu noble (that uses 41.0.7-4ubuntu0.1), while on ubuntu jammy (that uses 3.4.8-1ubuntu2.2) mypy does not complain.\n\nThe codebase is quite different, but the code seems to work on both versions so I think the mistake is in the type annotations (unless I am doing something wrong, of course).\n\n Am I doing something wrong, or is this a mistake in the type annotations?", "answer": "The maintainer says the annotation is correct: a certificate can carry a public key that is not valid for issuing certificates, and the type signatures reflect that. They suggest checking the key type against `CertificateIssuerPrivateKeyTypes`, either with `typing.cast()` or an `isinstance` check.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://cryptography.io/en/latest/hazmat/primitives/asymmetric/#cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric.types.CertificateIssuerPrivateKeyTypes"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://cryptography.io/en/latest/hazmat/primitives/asymmetric/#cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric.types.CertificateIssuerPrivateKeyTypes"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-15T19:37:43.639376Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/9439", "repo": "python-pillow/Pillow", "number": 9439, "title": "Security fix for Pillow 11.3", "url": "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/9439", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2026-02-16T14:01:34Z", "closed_at": "2026-02-16T14:22:12Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "Frawak", "security_topic": "backporting CVE fix to older Pillow release", "qa_summary": "The developer asked whether the CVE-2026-25990 fix could be back\u2011ported to Pillow\u202f11.3, and the maintainer answered that fixes are not back\u2011ported and suggested avoiding PSD images as a workaround, citing the CVE ID and Pillow documentation.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "Frawak", "answer_author": "hugovk", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation", "advisory"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": ["CVE-2026-25990"], "ghsa_ids": ["GHSA-cfh3-3jmp-rvhc"], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": ["https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/security/advisories/GHSA-cfh3-3jmp-rvhc"]}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "Frawak", "timestamp": "2026-02-16T14:01:34Z", "body": "Could it be possible to merge the security fix for [CVE-2026-25990](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/security/advisories/GHSA-cfh3-3jmp-rvhc) to 11.3 and release a patch version there?\nReason is that the ecosystem, I am working in, is stuck on Python 3.9 for a while.", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "hugovk", "timestamp": "2026-02-16T14:18:32Z", "body": "We generally only support the latest branch, and [supported Python versions](https://devguide.python.org/versions/):\n\n> Please use the latest version of Pillow. Functionality and security fixes should not be expected to be backported to earlier versions.\n\n* https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/latest/releasenotes/index.html\n* https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/latest/installation/python-support.html\n\nI recommend evaluating if you're susceptible loading a specially crafted PSD image, and if not, configuring your security scanner to not trigger for this.\n\nPlease note that Python 3.9 is also out of security support and likely contains CVEs.\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2026-02-16T20:31:23Z", "body": "If you are concerned about this CVE specifically, a workaround would be to avoid opening PSD images altogether, by only allowing for specific image formats - https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/8.0.0.html#image-open-add-formats-parameter", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/9439#1", "question": "Could the security fix for CVE-2026-25990 / GHSA-cfh3-3jmp-rvhc (https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/security/advisories/GHSA-cfh3-3jmp-rvhc) be merged to Pillow 11.3 and released as a patch version there? The reason is that the ecosystem I am working in is stuck on Python 3.9 for a while.", "answer": "We generally only support the latest branch, and [supported Python versions](https://devguide.python.org/versions/):\n\n> Please use the latest version of Pillow. Functionality and security fixes should not be expected to be backported to earlier versions.\n\n* https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/latest/releasenotes/index.html\n* https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/latest/installation/python-support.html\n\nI recommend evaluating if you're susceptible loading a specially crafted PSD image, and if not, configuring your security scanner to not trigger for this.\n\nPlease note that Python 3.9 is also out of security support and likely contains CVEs.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["CVE-2026-25990 / GHSA-cfh3-3jmp-rvhc", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/latest/releasenotes/index.html", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/latest/installation/python-support.html", "https://devguide.python.org/versions/", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/8.0.0.html#image-open-add-formats-parameter"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1, c2", "leak_flags": ["advisory_urls:https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/security/advisories/GHSA-cfh3-3jmp-rvhc"]}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://devguide.python.org/versions/", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/latest/installation/python-support.html", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/latest/releasenotes/index.html"], "leak_flags": ["advisory_urls:https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/security/advisories/GHSA-cfh3-3jmp-rvhc"], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T17:47:08.759410Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/9361", "repo": "python-pillow/Pillow", "number": 9361, "title": "Segfaults when using the 39C3 font from Chaos Communication congress", "url": "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/9361", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-12-30T11:49:05Z", "closed_at": "2025-12-31T21:53:05Z", "labels": ["Fonts"], "reporter": "berlincount", "security_topic": "duplicate font variation causing crash and potential null pointer exception", "qa_summary": "The developer asked whether the segfault triggered by the 39C3 font constitutes a security issue and how to mitigate it, and the maintainer answered with a concrete fix in PR\u202f#9362 that removes the duplicate variation to prevent the crash (referencing the project\u2019s security policy).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "berlincount", "answer_author": "radarhere", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "pr_data", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": ["#9362"], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": ["https://github.com/python-pillow/pillow?tab=security-ov-file#readme"]}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "berlincount", "timestamp": "2025-12-30T11:49:05Z", "body": "<!--\nThank you for reporting an issue.\n\nFollow these guidelines to ensure your issue is handled properly.\n\nIf you have a ...\n\n1.  General question: consider asking the question on Stack Overflow\n    with the python-imaging-library tag:\n\n    * https://stackoverflow.com/questions/tagged/python-imaging-library\n\n    Do not ask a question in both places.\n\n    If you think you have found a bug or have an unexplained exception\n    then file a bug report here.\n\n2.  Bug report: include a self-contained, copy-pastable example that\n    generates the issue if possible. Be concise with code posted.\n    Guidelines on how to provide a good bug report:\n\n    * https://stackoverflow.com/help/mcve\n\n    Bug reports which follow these guidelines are easier to diagnose,\n    and are often handled much more quickly.\n\n3.  Feature request: do a quick search of existing issues\n    to make sure this has not been asked before.\n\nWe know asking good questions takes effort, and we appreciate your time.\nThank you.\n-->\n\n### What did you do?\n\nI was trying to work with the TTF font from https://events.ccc.de/congress/2025/infos/styleguide.html (`39C3-Design-Package/39C3-Fonts/Kario 39C3 Variable/Desktop/Kario39C3Var-Roman.ttf` within https://events.ccc.de/congress/2025/infos/styleguide/39c3-styleguide-full-v2.zip), starting with listing the styles.\n\n### What did you expect to happen?\n\nI expected to get a list of the styles of the font, and to be able to work with it further.\n\n### What actually happened?\n\n`SIGSEGV`/`Segmentation fault`\n\n### What are your OS, Python and Pillow versions?\n\n* OS: python3-slim Docker image on Ubuntu 24.04.3 LTS on WSL2 as well as native\n* Python: 3.14.2\n* Pillow: 12.0.0\n\n```text\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nPillow 12.0.0\nPython 3.14.2 (main, Dec 30 2025, 00:42:55) [GCC 12.2.0]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nPython modules loaded from /opt/venv/python3/dist-packages/PIL\nBinary modules loaded from /opt/venv/python3/dist-packages/PIL\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n--- PIL CORE support ok, compiled for 12.0.0\n--- TKINTER support not installed\n--- FREETYPE2 support ok, loaded 2.14.1\n--- LITTLECMS2 support ok, loaded 2.17\n--- WEBP support ok, loaded 1.6.0\n--- AVIF support ok, loaded 1.3.0\n--- JPEG support ok, compiled for libjpeg-turbo 3.1.2\n--- OPENJPEG (JPEG2000) support ok, loaded 2.5.4\n--- ZLIB (PNG/ZIP) support ok, loaded 1.2.13, compiled for zlip-ng 2.2.5\n--- LIBTIFF support ok, loaded 4.7.1\n*** RAQM (Bidirectional Text) support not installed\n--- LIBIMAGEQUANT (Quantization method) support not installed\n--- XCB (X protocol) support ok\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n```\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\nfrom PIL import ImageFont\nimport pprint\n\nfont = ImageFont.truetype('Kario39C3Var-Roman.ttf')\npprint.pp(font.get_variation_names())\n```\n\nNOTE: This also might be a security issue, as this essentially seems to be Null Pointer Exception. I think this should be better contained / captured.", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2025-12-30T12:51:55Z", "body": "Interesting. Looking at the font, I see that there is a duplicate variation - there are two variations of \"XXtended Regular\". I've created https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/9362 to handle this, and exclude the duplicate. With that, `font.get_variation_names()` would return\n> [b'XXCondensed Ultra', b'XXCondensed Black', b'XXCondensed Bold', b'XXCondensed Medium', b'XXCondensed Regular', b'XXCondensed Light', b'XXCondensed Thin', b'XCondensed Ultra', b'XCondensed Black', b'XCondensed Bold', b'XCondensed Medium', b'XCondensed Regular', b'XCondensed Light', b'XCondensed Thin', b'Condensed Ultra', b'Condensed Black', b'Condensed Bold', b'Condensed Medium', b'Condensed Regular', b'Condensed Light', b'Condensed Thin', b'Narrow Ultra', b'Narrow Black', b'Narrow Bold', b'Narrow Medium', b'Narrow Regular', b'Narrow Light', b'Narrow Thin', b'Ultra', b'Black', b'Bold', b'Medium', b'Regular', b'Light', b'Thin', b'Xtended Ultra', b'Xtended Black', b'Xtended Bold', b'Xtended Medium', b'Xtended Regular', b'Xtended Light', b'Xtended Thin', b'XXtended Ultra', b'XXtended Black', b'XXtended Regular', b'XXtended Medium', b'XXtended Light', b'XXtended Thin']\n\nIf you find a security problem in the future, we do have a [security policy.](https://github.com/python-pillow/pillow?tab=security-ov-file#readme)", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/9361#1", "question": "In Pillow 12.0.0 on Python 3.14.2, using the python3-slim Docker image on Ubuntu 24.04.3 LTS on WSL2 as well as native, calling `ImageFont.truetype()` and then `get_variation_names()` on the 39C3 TTF font from the Chaos Communication Congress styleguide segfaults. The font is `39C3-Design-Package/39C3-Fonts/Kario 39C3 Variable/Desktop/Kario39C3Var-Roman.ttf` from https://events.ccc.de/congress/2025/infos/styleguide/39c3-styleguide-full-v2.zip, linked from https://events.ccc.de/congress/2025/infos/styleguide.html. I expected to get a list of the font styles and be able to work with it further, but instead get `SIGSEGV`/`Segmentation fault`. This also seems like it might be a security issue, as it appears to be a null pointer exception that should be better contained/captured.\n\nPillow build information:\n\n```text\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nPillow 12.0.0\nPython 3.14.2 (main, Dec 30 2025, 00:42:55) [GCC 12.2.0]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nPython modules loaded from /opt/venv/python3/dist-packages/PIL\nBinary modules loaded from /opt/venv/python3/dist-packages/PIL\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n--- PIL CORE support ok, compiled for 12.0.0\n--- TKINTER support not installed\n--- FREETYPE2 support ok, loaded 2.14.1\n--- LITTLECMS2 support ok, loaded 2.17\n--- WEBP support ok, loaded 1.6.0\n--- AVIF support ok, loaded 1.3.0\n--- JPEG support ok, compiled for libjpeg-turbo 3.1.2\n--- OPENJPEG (JPEG2000) support ok, loaded 2.5.4\n--- ZLIB (PNG/ZIP) support ok, loaded 1.2.13, compiled for zlip-ng 2.2.5\n--- LIBTIFF support ok, loaded 4.7.1\n*** RAQM (Bidirectional Text) support not installed\n--- LIBIMAGEQUANT (Quantization method) support not installed\n--- XCB (X protocol) support ok\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n```\n\nReproducer:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\nfrom PIL import ImageFont\nimport pprint\n\nfont = ImageFont.truetype('Kario39C3Var-Roman.ttf')\npprint.pp(font.get_variation_names())\n```\n\nWhy does this font cause a segfault, and can Pillow handle it without crashing?", "answer": "The font contains a duplicate variation: there are two variations named `XXtended Regular`. PR #9362 was created to handle this by excluding the duplicate. With that change, `font.get_variation_names()` returns the variation list shown in the maintainer comment, including a single `b'XXtended Regular'`; future security problems should be reported through Pillow's security policy.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/9362", "Pillow security policy"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/9362", "https://github.com/python-pillow/pillow?tab=security-ov-file#readme"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T17:49:46.429786Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/8935", "repo": "python-pillow/Pillow", "number": 8935, "title": "Unpickle Image serialized with older version of Pillow", "url": "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/8935", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-04-28T15:59:29Z", "closed_at": "2025-04-29T12:05:11Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "manigoad", "security_topic": "pickle deserialization compatibility regression across Pillow versions", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know if the self.filename change introduced in Pillow 11.2.1 affects pickle deserialization, and the maintainer referenced issue #8859 where the change was added and a forthcoming fix in issue #8938.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "manigoad", "answer_author": "radarhere", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "pr_data", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": ["11.3.0"], "fix_prs": ["#8859"], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "manigoad", "timestamp": "2025-04-28T15:59:29Z", "body": "### What did you do?\nPickle TIff image with pillow==11.1.0 and then de-serialize it with pillow==11.2.1\n### What did you expect to happen?\nI expect the image to be successfully de-serialized\n### What actually happened?\nIt's crashing with\n### What are your OS, Python and Pillow versions?\n\n* OS: \nMac OS, Ubuntu\n* Python: \nPython 3.12.7\n* Pillow: \n11.2.1\n\nTo reproduce the issue \nInstall pillow==11.1.0 and execute the following code (you can download the tiff from - https://people.math.sc.edu/Burkardt/data/tif/at3_1m4_01.tif)\n\n```python\nfrom PIL import Image\nimport pickle\nimage = Image.open('./at3_1m4_01.tif')\npickle.dump(image, open('./test.pickle', 'wb'))\n```\n\nThen install pillow==11.2.1 and de-serialize the file\n```python\n pickle.load(open('./test.pickle', 'rb'))\n```\n\nIt's crashing with \n```\nFile ~/.pyenv/versions/3.12.7/envs/forms/lib/python3.12/site-packages/PIL/ImageFile.py:260, in ImageFile.__setstate__(self, state)\n    258 def __setstate__(self, state: list[Any]) -> None:\n    259     self.tile = []\n--> 260     self.filename = state[5]\n    261     super().__setstate__(state)\n```\n\nI tested with regular PNG file and the issue is not reproducible.", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2025-04-28T22:08:44Z", "body": "The `self.filename` line was added in #8859 as part of 11.2.1\n\nI suppose we could adjust the code to work with states from Pillow < 11.2.1, but that would be in the next release of Pillow, 11.3.0. If you're unable to upgrade from Pillow 11.1.0 in one environment, then I don't feel completely confident that you could upgrade from Pillow 11.2.1 to 11.3.0 in your other environment. How did you arrive at this scenario where you are using two versions simultaneously? Why can you not simply upgrade to use the same version in both environments?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "manigoad", "timestamp": "2025-04-29T03:17:49Z", "body": "Thanks for the reply. I have training dataset that was generated with Pillow < 11.1.0. The application was working as expected with Pillow 11.1.0, but when upgraded to 11.22.1 it was no longer able to load it. \n\nFixing it in 11.3.0 will be great. I already pin it to 11.1.0 to resolve the issue and once 11.3.0 is release I'll upgrade to it.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2025-04-29T05:02:46Z", "body": "I've created #8938 to resolve this. Would you be able to test it? I would like to be sure that your script will work and you won't create another issue after Pillow 11.3.0 is released.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/8935#1", "question": "In Pillow, I pickled a TIFF image with pillow==11.1.0 and then tried to deserialize it with pillow==11.2.1. I expected the image to deserialize successfully, but it crashes. Environment: Mac OS and Ubuntu, Python 3.12.7, Pillow 11.2.1 when loading. To reproduce, install pillow==11.1.0 and run this using the TIFF from https://people.math.sc.edu/Burkardt/data/tif/at3_1m4_01.tif:\n\n```python\nfrom PIL import Image\nimport pickle\nimage = Image.open('./at3_1m4_01.tif')\npickle.dump(image, open('./test.pickle', 'wb'))\n```\n\nThen install pillow==11.2.1 and deserialize:\n\n```python\n pickle.load(open('./test.pickle', 'rb'))\n```\n\nIt crashes with:\n\n```\nFile ~/.pyenv/versions/3.12.7/envs/forms/lib/python3.12/site-packages/PIL/ImageFile.py:260, in ImageFile.__setstate__(self, state)\n    258 def __setstate__(self, state: list[Any]) -> None:\n    259     self.tile = []\n--> 260     self.filename = state[5]\n    261     super().__setstate__(state)\n```\n\nI tested with a regular PNG file and the issue is not reproducible. Is this a Pillow compatibility issue when unpickling an image serialized with an older Pillow version, and can it be fixed?", "answer": "The `self.filename` line was added in #8859 as part of Pillow 11.2.1. The maintainer said the code could be adjusted to work with states from Pillow < 11.2.1. Fix PR: https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/8938", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["#8859", "Pillow 11.2.1", "Pillow 11.3.0", "#8938"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1, c3", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["#8859"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-18T08:06:41.161362Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/8706", "repo": "python-pillow/Pillow", "number": 8706, "title": "A heap-buffer-overflow at Pillow/src/libImaging/Unpack.c:467:50 in unpackP4L", "url": "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/8706", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-01-19T16:14:00Z", "closed_at": "2025-01-20T02:19:18Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "Wakotu", "security_topic": "identification of known vulnerability and fix for heap-buffer overflow in Pillow", "qa_summary": "The developer wanted to know if the reported heap\u2011buffer\u2011overflow maps to a known vulnerability and which fix addresses it, answered with CVE\u20112020\u201135653 and the fixing PR #5174 (fixed in Pillow\u202f8.1.0).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c2", "question_author": "Wakotu", "answer_author": "radarhere", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "pr_data", "advisory", "cve_cwe_db"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": ["CVE-2020-35653"], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": ["8.1.0"], "fix_prs": ["#5174"], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": ["https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/cve-2020-35653"]}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "Wakotu", "timestamp": "2025-01-19T16:14:00Z", "body": "Hello, a heap-buffer-overflow reported by ASAN when I fuzz Pillow with [google/atheris](https://github.com/google/atheris).\n\n### Environment \n\n- Pillow version: 7.1.2\n- Python version: Python 3.8.10\n- OS version: Ubuntu 20.04.6 LTS\n\n### Output\n\nfuzz driver:\n\n```python\nimport atheris\nimport io\nimport sys\n\nwith atheris.instrument_imports():\n    from PIL import Image\n\ndef fuzz_image(data):\n    try:\n        # Attempt to open the image from the fuzzed data\n        image = Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))\n        image.load()  # Trigger image processing\n    except Exception as e:\n        # Handle exceptions (e.g., print or log)\n        pass\n\ndef main():\n    atheris.Setup(sys.argv, fuzz_image)\n    atheris.Fuzz()\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nerror log:\n[error.log](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/18469381/error.log)\n\ntrigger input: \n[trigger.txt](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/18469413/trigger.txt)\n\n### Steps to Reproduce \n\nPull the docker image and run it to a container.\n\n```bash\ndocker pull vueko0/pillow_crash:v1\ndocker run --rm vueko0/pillow_crash:v1\n```\n\nEnter the container and run following commands:\n\n```bash\ncd ~/Pillow/\n./crash.sh\n```", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "hugovk", "timestamp": "2025-01-19T17:18:01Z", "body": "Pillow 7.1.2 was released in April 2020: https://pypi.org/project/pillow/7.1.2/\n\nAlso Python 3.8 is no longer supported. \n\nCan you reproduce with the latest Pillow 11.1.0 on Python 3.9 or later?\n\nAnd for next time, please follow our security policy regarding security issues:\n\nhttps://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/main/.github/SECURITY.md ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2025-01-19T20:53:39Z", "body": "I would conclude this has found https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/cve-2020-35653, which was fixed by #5174 in Pillow 8.1.0\n\nThis problem is actually mentioned in the atheris repository itself - https://github.com/google/atheris/blob/master/hall_of_fame.md", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/8706#1", "question": "When fuzzing Pillow with google/atheris, I get an ASAN heap-buffer-overflow at `Pillow/src/libImaging/Unpack.c:467:50` in `unpackP4L`.\n\nEnvironment:\n- Pillow version: 7.1.2\n- Python version: Python 3.8.10\n- OS version: Ubuntu 20.04.6 LTS\n\nFuzz driver:\n\n```python\nimport atheris\nimport io\nimport sys\n\nwith atheris.instrument_imports():\n    from PIL import Image\n\ndef fuzz_image(data):\n    try:\n        # Attempt to open the image from the fuzzed data\n        image = Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))\n        image.load()  # Trigger image processing\n    except Exception as e:\n        # Handle exceptions (e.g., print or log)\n        pass\n\ndef main():\n    atheris.Setup(sys.argv, fuzz_image)\n    atheris.Fuzz()\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nThe error log was attached as `error.log`, and the trigger input was attached as `trigger.txt`.\n\nSteps to reproduce:\n\nPull the Docker image and run it to a container.\n\n```bash\ndocker pull vueko0/pillow_crash:v1\ndocker run --rm vueko0/pillow_crash:v1\n```\n\nEnter the container and run following commands:\n\n```bash\ncd ~/Pillow/\n./crash.sh\n```\n\nWhat is this issue?", "answer": "The maintainer concluded that this has found https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/cve-2020-35653, which was fixed by #5174 in Pillow 8.1.0\n\nThis problem is actually mentioned in the atheris repository itself - https://github.com/google/atheris/blob/master/hall_of_fame.md", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/cve-2020-35653", "PR #5174", "Pillow 8.1.0", "https://github.com/google/atheris/blob/master/hall_of_fame.md"], "answer_grounded_in": "c2", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["#5174", "CVE-2020-35653", "https://github.com/google/atheris/blob/master/hall_of_fame.md", "https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/cve-2020-35653"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-18T08:09:18.513852Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/8546", "repo": "python-pillow/Pillow", "number": 8546, "title": "Security concerns when loading user-provided fonts: Can they contain malware?", "url": "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/8546", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2024-11-10T09:57:10Z", "closed_at": "2024-11-10T11:07:54Z", "labels": ["Question"], "reporter": "daeeros", "security_topic": "malicious font handling risk in Pillow ImageFont.truetype", "qa_summary": "The developer asked whether loading user\u2011provided fonts with ImageFont.truetype could be a security risk, and the maintainer answered by referencing Pillow\u2019s OSS\u2011Fuzz font fuzzing and the specific fix documented in the Pillow 8.2.0 release notes (memory DoS in ImageFont).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "daeeros", "answer_author": "radarhere", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation", "ci_logs", "code"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": ["8.2.0"], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": ["5bff2f3b2894ec6923c590d0c37b18177d0634bd"], "advisory_urls": ["https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/actions/workflows/cifuzz.yml", "https://github.com/google/oss-fuzz", "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/5bff2f3b2894ec6923c590d0c37b18177d0634bd/Tests/oss-fuzz/fuzzers.py#L31-L43", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/8.2.0.html#fix-memory-dos-in-imagefont", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/index.html"]}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "daeeros", "timestamp": "2024-11-10T09:57:10Z", "body": "Hello! I have a question regarding the security of using user-provided fonts in Pillow.\r\n\r\nI'm implementing a feature where a font uploaded by a user is loaded as a byte stream and then used in `ImageFont.truetype`. The loading and usage look like this:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nfont_22 = None\r\n\r\nif creator_profile.quote_font_file_id is not None:\r\n    downloaded_font = await pl.app.backend._get_file(creator_profile.quote_font_file_id)\r\n    if downloaded_font:\r\n        font_22 = ImageFont.truetype(BytesIO(downloaded_font.content), 22)\r\n```\r\n\r\nMy question:\r\nHow safe is it to accept fonts as byte streams and load them through ImageFont.truetype? Is there a risk that malicious fonts could contain viruses or otherwise disrupt the application? Are there any known vulnerabilities with font handling in Pillow or best practices for validating fonts before use?\r\n\r\nThank you very much in advance for any guidance!", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2024-11-10T10:45:19Z", "body": "Hi. This is a security problem that we consider when developing Pillow.\r\n\r\n[One](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/actions/workflows/cifuzz.yml) of our GitHub Actions uses [OSS-Fuzz](https://github.com/google/oss-fuzz) to check for security problems that may occur when [opening and using random font data](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/5bff2f3b2894ec6923c590d0c37b18177d0634bd/Tests/oss-fuzz/fuzzers.py#L31-L43).\r\n\r\nWhen a problem in Pillow is found, we endeavour to fix it. You can see and example of a problem that was found and fixed in the past at https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/8.2.0.html#fix-memory-dos-in-imagefont\r\n\r\nAs far as what you should be doing as a user, https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/index.html\r\n> Please use the latest version of Pillow. Functionality and security fixes should not be expected to be backported to earlier versions.\r\n\r\nIf you do not use our pre-built wheels, and instead compile Pillow from source, I would also suggest using the latest version of dependencies - in this case, FreeType.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/8546#1", "question": "I'm implementing a feature where a font uploaded by a user is loaded as a byte stream and then used in Pillow's `ImageFont.truetype`. The loading and usage look like this:\n\n```python\nfont_22 = None\n\nif creator_profile.quote_font_file_id is not None:\n    downloaded_font = await pl.app.backend._get_file(creator_profile.quote_font_file_id)\n    if downloaded_font:\n        font_22 = ImageFont.truetype(BytesIO(downloaded_font.content), 22)\n```\n\nHow safe is it to accept user-provided fonts as byte streams and load them through `ImageFont.truetype`? Is there a risk that malicious fonts could contain viruses or otherwise disrupt the application? Are there any known vulnerabilities with font handling in Pillow or best practices for validating fonts before use?", "answer": "Pillow treats this as a security concern: one of its GitHub Actions uses OSS-Fuzz to check for security problems when opening and using random font data. When problems are found, Pillow endeavors to fix them; the maintainer pointed to a past memory DoS in `ImageFont` that was fixed. As a user, the guidance is to use the latest version of Pillow, since functionality and security fixes should not be expected to be backported, and if compiling from source rather than using pre-built wheels, also use the latest dependencies, specifically FreeType.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/actions/workflows/cifuzz.yml", "https://github.com/google/oss-fuzz", "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/5bff2f3b2894ec6923c590d0c37b18177d0634bd/Tests/oss-fuzz/fuzzers.py#L31-L43", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/8.2.0.html#fix-memory-dos-in-imagefont", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/index.html"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["5bff2f3b2894ec6923c590d0c37b18177d0634bd", "https://github.com/google/oss-fuzz", "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/actions/workflows/cifuzz.yml", "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/5bff2f3b2894ec6923c590d0c37b18177d0634bd/Tests/oss-fuzz/fuzzers.py#L31-L43", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/8.2.0.html#fix-memory-dos-in-imagefont", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/index.html"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T17:52:03.198020Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/8439", "repo": "python-pillow/Pillow", "number": 8439, "title": "Random crashes of python in latest dev version of Pillow with pillow_heif", "url": "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/8439", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2024-10-05T14:09:37Z", "closed_at": "2024-10-06T20:22:48Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "bigcat88", "security_topic": "identifying recent code change causing crash", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know if a recent Pillow commit (84e275d90629bfdf508f81a1142eddd5d5ea46e4, PR #8390) introduced a memory\u2011safety/security bug causing crashes, and the answer cites that commit and suggests reverting it as the concrete fix.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c2", "question_author": "bigcat88", "answer_author": "hugovk", "answerer_role": "contributor", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "commit_history", "pr_data", "issue_tracker"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": ["#8390"], "fix_commits": ["84e275d90629bfdf508f81a1142eddd5d5ea46e4"], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "bigcat88", "timestamp": "2024-10-05T14:09:37Z", "body": "**Good day, everyone.**\r\n\r\nI'll try to describe the situation briefly.\r\n\r\nFirst, I want to clarify that I'm not certain this is a bug in Pillow. I created this issue so that knowledgeable contributors could help identify any recent changes in the repository that might be causing the problem I'm experiencing.\r\n\r\nAbout two weeks ago, when using the development version of Pillow, some actions in pillow_heif repo started to fail: [run link](https://github.com/bigcat88/pillow_heif/actions/runs/10973779020).\r\n\r\nNow that I have some time to investigate, I'm encountering a situation where the root cause of the error is still unclear to me.\r\n\r\nHere is the script I'm using for testing:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport os\r\nimport pillow_heif\r\nfrom pathlib import Path\r\nfrom PIL import Image, ImageSequence\r\n\r\npillow_heif.register_heif_opener()\r\n\r\nos.chdir(Path(__file__).parent.parent.joinpath(\"tests/images/heif/\"))\r\n\r\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\r\n    im = Image.open(\"zPug_3.heic\")\r\n    im.load()\r\n    for frame in ImageSequence.Iterator(im):\r\n        print(\"before frame.load()\")\r\n        frame.load()\r\n        print(\"after frame.load()\")\r\n        assert len(frame.tobytes()) > 0\r\n        print(\"after frame.tobytes()\")\r\n    print(\"exit\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt can crash, or sometimes it does not crash.\r\nSometimes I even see \"exit\" in output and then crash occurs.\r\n\r\nI'm running this script from the cloned `pillow_heif` repository, creating a \"dev\" folder at the root (if needed for quick reproduction) and placing it there.\r\n\r\nThe issue is that my Python interpreter randomly crashes when using the development version of Pillow, and I haven't been able to pinpoint the exact location of the crash.\r\n\r\nNothing has changed in `pillow_heif` itself in last months (I'm waiting for HDR image support before refactoring the code).\r\n\r\nI've tried running the script with other versions of Pillow, and everything works fine. I've also tried adding print statements inside Pillow to locate the failure, but the crash seems random, and I haven't been able to identify the exact spot where it occurs.\r\n\r\nI'm testing this on macOS (but can also reproduce it on Linux). Any insights or tips on where to investigate, or information on what may have changed in the Pillow repo 2\u20133 weeks ago that could lead to these crashes, would be greatly appreciated.\r\n\r\nSome info that I have found:\r\n\r\n1ee3bd1d9e1a96da01ba55aff376e214082bcba8 - on this commit crash already occurs\r\n08d9c89d8a7db84e8145814bca36d7dd5b7465ce - repo at this commit is good\r\n\r\nThank you!\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "wiredfool", "timestamp": "2024-10-05T14:22:24Z", "body": "This sort of thing sounds like a memory safety issue.  It's possible that just running to crash under gdb would be enough to tell you where the crash is happening, and that should be enough to start to narrow down the issue. Alternately, you can  run under valgrind on linux (but that's slow).\r\n\r\nIf it's a consistent failure, the other option is to use git bisect and narrow down to the commit -- though since we don't squash commits on main, there's a chance of having non-working commits unrelated to this at any given point. ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "hugovk", "timestamp": "2024-10-05T15:09:46Z", "body": "git bisect points to 84e275d90629bfdf508f81a1142eddd5d5ea46e4 from PR https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/8390:\r\n```\r\n84e275d90629bfdf508f81a1142eddd5d5ea46e4 is the first bad commit\r\ncommit 84e275d90629bfdf508f81a1142eddd5d5ea46e4\r\nDate:   Wed Sep 18 20:27:35 2024 +1000\r\n\r\n    Loading does not change mode\r\n\r\n src/PIL/ImageFile.py | 2 +-\r\n 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)\r\n```\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "bigcat88", "timestamp": "2024-10-05T15:39:19Z", "body": "additional information that I may have forgotten to mention is that `zPug_3.heic` is three images (or frames) in one file:\r\n\r\n```\r\n<pillow_heif.as_plugin.HeifImageFile image mode=RGB size=64x64>\r\n<pillow_heif.as_plugin.HeifImageFile image mode=L size=64x64>\r\n<pillow_heif.as_plugin.HeifImageFile image mode=RGB size=96x64>\r\n```\r\n\r\n> git bisect points to [84e275d](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/commit/84e275d90629bfdf508f81a1142eddd5d5ea46e4) from PR #8390:\r\n\r\nthanks, very good finding", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "bigcat88", "timestamp": "2024-10-05T15:52:23Z", "body": "It turns out that when the second frame has a different mode, a new `Image.core.new` is not created after that commit, and the mode does not change, therefore, in pillow_heif, the wrong **stride** is calculated based on wrong mode(RGB vs L), and the `libde265` decoder crashes as it expects more data.\r\n\r\nEdit: the reason is correct(wrong mode for `Image.core`), and it crashes here:\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/51c577df825cee6917bd55912f6cb1c9b8df5f84/src/PIL/Image.py#L887\r\n\r\n\r\nCan we revert this commit https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/commit/84e275d90629bfdf508f81a1142eddd5d5ea46e4 ?\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "wiredfool", "timestamp": "2024-10-06T09:08:51Z", "body": "History says that this hasn't changed since the PIL fork, except for the most recent batch of changes. I can see where we could get multi image container formats (gif or webpanim or tiff) where the frame type could change, esp something like rgb->rgba, though our internal decoders might handle that directly.  Obviously at least one external decoder has been relying on this. \r\n\r\nI'd say revert, but finding a good test case for this might be the hardest bit.\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2024-10-06T10:37:55Z", "body": "My commit was inspired by @homm's comment that he considered it a bug [when an image changed size after it was loaded.](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/8341#discussion_r1760807429)\r\n\r\nI think `load()` is a method that many users are probably not even aware of, and so they might the behaviour unexpected if it is internally called and the mode changes.\r\n```python\r\nfrom PIL import Image\r\nim = Image.open(...)\r\nprint(im.mode)  # L\r\nprint(im.copy().mode)  # RGB\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhen I made the mode change, none of our internal plugins had to be updated to handle it. GifImagePlugin changes modes between frames, sure, but the the core image can be reset before loading, in its own `load_prepare()`. TiffImagePlugin creates a new core image at the end of `seek()`. It doesn't look like WebP does change modes between frames.\r\n\r\n@bigcat88 a few questions - do you disagree with my idea in theory? Do you think pillow_heif could be updated handle this new way of doing things? Is the problem simply that this change was made suddenly, and you would prefer a deprecation?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "bigcat88", "timestamp": "2024-10-06T11:54:52Z", "body": "     \r\n> When I made the mode change, none of our internal plugins had to be updated to handle it.\r\n\r\nI don't know how common it is that frames have different modes.\r\nI know that in modern formats like AVIF or HEIF this is supported, and this can happen.\r\n*But I do never see real world examples of this, and not custom crafted images*\r\n\r\n\r\n> @bigcat88 a few questions - do you disagree with my idea in theory? Do you think pillow_heif could be updated handle this new way of doing things? Is the problem simply that this change was made suddenly, and you would prefer a deprecation?\r\n\r\nI don't mind updating \"pillow_heif\", that's why I created issue to determine the way to do it, knowing that here everyone can always get an answer to the question of what is best\r\n\r\n\r\n> TiffImagePlugin creates a new core image at the end of `seek()`\r\n\r\nAfter receiving a hint that TiffImagePlugin does this in the seek method, in theory it should be done the same way.\r\n\r\nLike this:\r\n\r\n```python3\r\n    def seek(self, frame: int):\r\n        if not self._seek_check(frame):\r\n            return\r\n        self.__frame = frame\r\n        self._init_from_heif_file(frame)  # this fills  `self._size` and `self._mode` with new frame info\r\n\r\n        if pil_version[:3] != \"10.\":\r\n            # Pillow 11.0+\r\n            # We need to create a new core image object on second and\r\n            # subsequent frames in the image. Image may be different size/mode.\r\n            # https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/8439\r\n            self.im = Image.core.new(self._mode, self._size)\r\n\r\n        _exif = getattr(self, \"_exif\", None)  # Pillow 9.2+ do no reload exif between frames.\r\n        if _exif is not None and getattr(_exif, \"_loaded\", None):\r\n            _exif._loaded = False  # pylint: disable=protected-access\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt seems to work and tests passed successfully, many thanks to everyone for help.\r\n\r\n I think the issue can be closed?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c8", "author": "homm", "timestamp": "2024-10-07T12:18:56Z", "body": "I still think it's better to revert this, especially now, when we realise not only TiffImagePlugin relies on this behaviour.\r\nhttps://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/8390/files#r1764864935", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c9", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2024-10-07T20:58:20Z", "body": "Reverted and deprecated?\r\n\r\nI think the ideal behaviour when changing modes between frames is actually to set `self._im = None` at the end of seek - https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/8392. I think `load()` shouldn't change the image mode, and in theory at least, `load()` doesn't have to be called after `seek()`, so if there's a core image with the wrong mode hanging around, it can be discarded.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c10", "author": "homm", "timestamp": "2024-10-08T08:35:21Z", "body": "> is actually to set self._im = None\r\n\r\nThis will lead to unnecessary memory allocation if all frames have the same size and mode. The previous implementation of `load_prepare` was straightforward: it prepared `self.im` with the correct mode and size, so there was no need to worry about handling `self.im` in the plugins.\r\n\r\n> I think load() shouldn't change the image mode\r\n\r\nI fully agree, just like with size changes, which I also tried to fix, as you might remember. But I don't quite understand how this would happen if we return to the previous `load_prepare` behavior.\r\n\r\n> load() doesn't have to be called after seek()\r\n\r\nPossibly. But I'm specifically talking about `load_prepare`, which I believe is the correct API for managing the `self.im` object.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c11", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2024-10-08T10:37:51Z", "body": "Returning to the previous `load_prepare` behaviour wouldn't cause a problem, no. I was sort of trying to enforce the core image to either be `None` or the correct mode/size before calling `load()`, to allow for the core image to be dropped if it theoretically became unnecessary. However, once I take on board your comment about unnecessary memory allocation, that's hard to do simply. I've created #8450 to try - at the end of `seek()`, an external plugin could call `super().seek()`, and if the mode or size has changed, then `self._im` is set to `None`.\r\n\r\nIf you're not a fan of that suggestion though, then I'm happy to just revert the `load_prepare()` change.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c12", "author": "homm", "timestamp": "2024-10-08T17:17:45Z", "body": "> I was sort of trying to enforce the core image to either be None or the correct mode/size before calling load()\r\n\r\nGood point. I'll take a look. ", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/8439#1", "question": "In the latest development version of Pillow, my Python interpreter randomly crashes when using `pillow_heif`, while older Pillow versions work fine. Nothing has changed in `pillow_heif` in the last months. I can reproduce on macOS and Linux.\n\nI'm running this script from a cloned `pillow_heif` repository, with a `dev` folder at the root if needed for quick reproduction:\n\n```python\nimport os\nimport pillow_heif\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom PIL import Image, ImageSequence\n\npillow_heif.register_heif_opener()\n\nos.chdir(Path(__file__).parent.parent.joinpath(\"tests/images/heif/\"))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    im = Image.open(\"zPug_3.heic\")\n    im.load()\n    for frame in ImageSequence.Iterator(im):\n        print(\"before frame.load()\")\n        frame.load()\n        print(\"after frame.load()\")\n        assert len(frame.tobytes()) > 0\n        print(\"after frame.tobytes()\")\n    print(\"exit\")\n```\n\nIt can crash, or sometimes it does not crash. Sometimes I even see `exit` in the output and then the crash occurs. I tried adding print statements inside Pillow, but the crash seems random and I cannot pinpoint the exact spot.\n\nSome commits I found:\n\n- `1ee3bd1d9e1a96da01ba55aff376e214082bcba8` \u2014 crash already occurs\n- `08d9c89d8a7db84e8145814bca36d7dd5b7465ce` \u2014 repo at this commit is good\n\nCan anyone identify what recent Pillow change from about 2\u20133 weeks ago may be causing these crashes, or where is the first bad commit?", "answer": "A git bisect identified `84e275d90629bfdf508f81a1142eddd5d5ea46e4` as the first bad commit. That commit came from PR https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/8390 and changed `src/PIL/ImageFile.py` with the message \u201cLoading does not change mode.\u201d", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["commit 84e275d90629bfdf508f81a1142eddd5d5ea46e4", "PR https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/8390"], "answer_grounded_in": "c2", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["84e275d90629bfdf508f81a1142eddd5d5ea46e4", "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/8390:"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-18T08:19:37.513847Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/7876", "repo": "python-pillow/Pillow", "number": 7876, "title": "Uncaught Exception(s) in Pillow Library", "url": "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/7876", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2024-03-13T13:45:11Z", "closed_at": "2024-04-01T05:46:06Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "fatihhcelik", "security_topic": "unhandled exceptions causing crashes in Pillow", "qa_summary": "The developer wanted to know if the uncaught exceptions from crafted inputs represent a security vulnerability and how Pillow handles them; maintainers clarified that one case is a deliberate limit raise (see source line) and created PR #7882 and PR #7883 to address the other cases.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c3", "question_author": "fatihhcelik", "answer_author": "radarhere", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "pr_data", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": ["#7882", "#7883"], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "fatihhcelik", "timestamp": "2024-03-13T13:45:11Z", "body": "### What did you do?\r\n\r\nWe (@DogukanK, @esraercann) discovered a 3 crashes in Python Pillow library because of 'Uncaught Exception' via specially crafted input. \r\n\r\n### What did you expect to happen?\r\n\r\nExceptions should be handled properly in Pillow.\r\n\r\n### What actually happened?\r\n\r\nCrash. \r\n\r\n* Here is the first crash log:\r\n\r\n ```bash\r\n=== Uncaught Python exception: ===\r\n\r\nerror: argument out of range\r\n\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/targets/pillow/main.py\", line 20, in TestOneInput\r\n\r\n    pilfuzz(data)\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/targets/pillow/main.py\", line 11, in pilfuzz\r\n\r\n    out = im.transpose(Image.Transpose.FLIP_LEFT_RIGHT)\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/Image.py\", line 2807, in transpose\r\n\r\n    def transpose(self, method):\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/ImageFile.py\", line 266, in load\r\n\r\n    err_code = decoder.decode(b\"\")[1]\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/PpmImagePlugin.py\", line 273, in decode\r\n\r\n    data = self._decode_blocks(maxval)\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/PpmImagePlugin.py\", line 261, in _decode_blocks\r\n\r\n    data += o32(value) if self.mode == \"I\" else o8(value)\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/_binary.py\", line 93, in o32le\r\n\r\n    def o32le(i: int) -> bytes:\r\n\r\nerror: argument out of range\r\n```\r\nPoc:\r\n\r\n```python3\r\n>>> from PIL import Image                                                                                                                                     \r\n\r\n>>> im = Image.open(\"./crash-4148ce4324e2e54cc3c2c6aa369420ddbd9dee5e\")\r\n\r\n>>> im.transpose(Image.Transpose.FLIP_LEFT_RIGHT)                                                                                                             \r\n\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):                                               \r\n\r\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 1, in <module>                                                                                                                              \r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/Image.py\", line 2818, in transpose                                                                    \r\n\r\n    self.load()                                                                                                                                                    \r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/ImageFile.py\", line 266, in load                                                                      \r\n\r\n    err_code = decoder.decode(b\"\")[1]                                            \r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/PpmImagePlugin.py\", line 273, in decode                                                               \r\n\r\n    data = self._decode_blocks(maxval)                                           \r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/PpmImagePlugin.py\", line 261, in _decode_blocks\r\n\r\n    data += o32(value) if self.mode == \"I\" else o8(value)                                                                                                          \r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/_binary.py\", line 94, in o32le                                                                        \r\n\r\n    return pack(\"<I\", i)                                                         \r\n\r\nstruct.error: argument out of range \r\n```\r\n\r\n* Here is the second crash:\r\n\r\n```python3\r\n>>> from PIL import Image, ImageFilter\r\n\r\n>>> im = Image.open(\"./crash-73c3d4dca546775e83ea511a0fc882f3c5b6f60a\")\r\n\r\n>>> im.filter(ImageFilter.DETAIL)\r\n\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n\r\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 1, in <module>\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/Image.py\", line 1281, in filter\r\n\r\n    self.load()\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/GbrImagePlugin.py\", line 94, in load\r\n\r\n    self.frombytes(self.fp.read(self._data_size))\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/Image.py\", line 810, in frombytes\r\n\r\n    d.setimage([self.im](http://self.im/))\r\n\r\nMemoryError\r\n```\r\n\r\n* Here is the third crash:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n=== Uncaught Python exception: ===\r\n\r\nOverflowError: Python int too large to convert to C ssize_t\r\n\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/targets/pillow/main.py\", line 16, in TestOneInput\r\n\r\n    pilfuzz(data)\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/targets/pillow/main.py\", line 10, in pilfuzz\r\n\r\n    with Image.open(io.BytesIO(data)) as im:\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/Image.py\", line 3258, in open\r\n\r\n    preinit()\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/Image.py\", line 3273, in _open_core\r\n\r\n    fp.seek(0)\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py\", line 1082, in __init__\r\n\r\n    super().__init__(fp, filename)\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/ImageFile.py\", line 137, in __init__\r\n\r\n    self._open()\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py\", line 1109, in _open\r\n\r\n    self._seek(0)\r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py\", line 1150, in _seek\r\n\r\n    self.fp.seek(self.__next)\r\n\r\nOverflowError: Python int too large to convert to C ssize_t\r\n```\r\n\r\nPoC:\r\n\r\n```python3\r\nPython 3.10.12 (main, Nov 20 2023, 15:14:05) [GCC 11.4.0] on linux                                                                                  \r\n\r\nType \"help\", \"copyright\", \"credits\" or \"license\" for more information.                                                                                          \r\n\r\n>>> from PIL import Image                                                                                                                              \r\n\r\n>>> im = Image.open(\"./crash-ecd6f7d1583338a2a2c4aaee944b0b3371f4f926\")                                                                                \r\n\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):                                              \r\n\r\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 1, in <module>                                           \r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/Image.py\", line 3293, in open                                                             \r\n\r\n    im = _open_core(                    \r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/Image.py\", line 3274, in _open_core                                                       \r\n\r\n    im = factory(fp, filename)          \r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py\", line 1082, in __init__                                               \r\n\r\n    super().__init__(fp, filename)                                              \r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/ImageFile.py\", line 137, in __init__                                                      \r\n\r\n    self._open()                        \r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py\", line 1109, in _open                                                  \r\n\r\n    self._seek(0)                       \r\n\r\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py\", line 1150, in _seek                                                  \r\n\r\n    self.fp.seek(self.__next)           \r\n\r\nValueError: cannot fit 'int' into an offset-sized integer\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n### What are your OS, Python and Pillow versions?\r\n\r\n* Python3 Version: Python 3.10.12\r\n* PIL Version: 10.2.0\r\n* OS: Ubuntu 22.04.3 LTS 5.15.0-84-generic x86/64\r\n\r\n\r\n### Crash Files\r\n\r\n[Archive.zip](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/files/14588492/Archive.zip)\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2024-03-13T21:49:20Z", "body": "Looking at your second situation, I see it is hitting\r\nhttps://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/9e3d1a7b05b5c961eb1daaae4e5ed62feb6d5507/src/decode.c#L194-L198\r\n\r\nYou are trying to create an image that is 134,217,792px wide, greater than the limit in that logic of 67,108,856px wide.\r\n\r\nSo that's not an unexpected exception, that's something we're deliberately raising.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "wiredfool", "timestamp": "2024-03-13T22:59:39Z", "body": "The first one was sent to security@. The error for that one is raised by python core, and isn't an issue unless valgrind finds memory corruption, and since that's directly in python core, it would be a bug for them. ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2024-03-16T02:44:29Z", "body": "I've created https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/7882 for the first situation, as that is similar to another error that we already catch.\r\n\r\nI've created #7883 for the third situation. I'm not highly convinced of the need for this change, but it at least documents in our code that this may happen.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/7876#1", "question": "In Pillow 10.2.0 on Ubuntu 22.04.3 LTS 5.15.0-84-generic x86/64 with Python 3.10.12, we (@DogukanK, @esraercann) found three uncaught exceptions/crashes from specially crafted inputs. Should Pillow handle these exceptions properly, and are fixes needed?\n\nFirst crash log:\n\n```bash\n=== Uncaught Python exception: ===\n\nerror: argument out of range\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/targets/pillow/main.py\", line 20, in TestOneInput\n\n    pilfuzz(data)\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/targets/pillow/main.py\", line 11, in pilfuzz\n\n    out = im.transpose(Image.Transpose.FLIP_LEFT_RIGHT)\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/Image.py\", line 2807, in transpose\n\n    def transpose(self, method):\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/ImageFile.py\", line 266, in load\n\n    err_code = decoder.decode(b\"\")[1]\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/PpmImagePlugin.py\", line 273, in decode\n\n    data = self._decode_blocks(maxval)\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/PpmImagePlugin.py\", line 261, in _decode_blocks\n\n    data += o32(value) if self.mode == \"I\" else o8(value)\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/_binary.py\", line 93, in o32le\n\n    def o32le(i: int) -> bytes:\n\nerror: argument out of range\n```\nPoC:\n\n```python3\n>>> from PIL import Image                                                                                                                                     \n\n>>> im = Image.open(\"./crash-4148ce4324e2e54cc3c2c6aa369420ddbd9dee5e\")\n\n>>> im.transpose(Image.Transpose.FLIP_LEFT_RIGHT)                                                                                                             \n\nTraceback (most recent call last):                                               \n\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 1, in <module>                                                                                                                              \n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/Image.py\", line 2818, in transpose                                                                    \n\n    self.load()                                                                                                                                                    \n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/ImageFile.py\", line 266, in load                                                                      \n\n    err_code = decoder.decode(b\"\")[1]                                            \n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/PpmImagePlugin.py\", line 273, in decode                                                               \n\n    data = self._decode_blocks(maxval)                                           \n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/PpmImagePlugin.py\", line 261, in _decode_blocks\n\n    data += o32(value) if self.mode == \"I\" else o8(value)                                                                                                          \n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/_binary.py\", line 94, in o32le                                                                        \n\n    return pack(\"<I\", i)                                                         \n\nstruct.error: argument out of range \n```\n\nSecond crash:\n\n```python3\n>>> from PIL import Image, ImageFilter\n\n>>> im = Image.open(\"./crash-73c3d4dca546775e83ea511a0fc882f3c5b6f60a\")\n\n>>> im.filter(ImageFilter.DETAIL)\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 1, in <module>\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/Image.py\", line 1281, in filter\n\n    self.load()\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/GbrImagePlugin.py\", line 94, in load\n\n    self.frombytes(self.fp.read(self._data_size))\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/Image.py\", line 810, in frombytes\n\n    d.setimage([self.im](http://self.im/))\n\nMemoryError\n```\n\nThird crash log:\n\n```bash\n=== Uncaught Python exception: ===\n\nOverflowError: Python int too large to convert to C ssize_t\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/targets/pillow/main.py\", line 16, in TestOneInput\n\n    pilfuzz(data)\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/targets/pillow/main.py\", line 10, in pilfuzz\n\n    with Image.open(io.BytesIO(data)) as im:\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/Image.py\", line 3258, in open\n\n    preinit()\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/Image.py\", line 3273, in _open_core\n\n    fp.seek(0)\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py\", line 1082, in __init__\n\n    super().__init__(fp, filename)\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/ImageFile.py\", line 137, in __init__\n\n    self._open()\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py\", line 1109, in _open\n\n    self._seek(0)\n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py\", line 1150, in _seek\n\n    self.fp.seek(self.__next)\n\nOverflowError: Python int too large to convert to C ssize_t\n```\n\nPoC:\n\n```python3\nPython 3.10.12 (main, Nov 20 2023, 15:14:05) [GCC 11.4.0] on linux                                                                                  \n\nType \"help\", \"copyright\", \"credits\" or \"license\" for more information.                                                                                          \n\n>>> from PIL import Image                                                                                                                              \n\n>>> im = Image.open(\"./crash-ecd6f7d1583338a2a2c4aaee944b0b3371f4f926\")                                                                                \n\nTraceback (most recent call last):                                              \n\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 1, in <module>                                           \n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/Image.py\", line 3293, in open                                                             \n\n    im = _open_core(                    \n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/Image.py\", line 3274, in _open_core                                                       \n\n    im = factory(fp, filename)          \n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py\", line 1082, in __init__                                               \n\n    super().__init__(fp, filename)                                              \n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/ImageFile.py\", line 137, in __init__                                                      \n\n    self._open()                        \n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py\", line 1109, in _open                                                  \n\n    self._seek(0)                       \n\n  File \"/home/ubuntu/.local/lib/python3.10/site-packages/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py\", line 1150, in _seek                                                  \n\n    self.fp.seek(self.__next)           \n\nValueError: cannot fit 'int' into an offset-sized integer\n\n```\n\nCrash files were attached as [Archive.zip](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/files/14588492/Archive.zip).", "answer": "For the first situation, a maintainer created PR #7882 because it is similar to another error Pillow already catches. For the third situation, they created PR #7883, while noting they were not highly convinced of the need for the change but that it documents in the code that this may happen. The second situation was described separately as deliberately raising an exception because the input tries to create an image 134,217,792 px wide, above Pillow's 67,108,856 px limit.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/7882", "PR #7883", "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/9e3d1a7b05b5c961eb1daaae4e5ed62feb6d5507/src/decode.c#L194-L198"], "answer_grounded_in": "c3", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["#7883", "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/7882"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T17:53:49.819283Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/7626", "repo": "python-pillow/Pillow", "number": 7626, "title": "Is Pillow affected by WebP vulnerability?", "url": "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/7626", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2023-12-19T09:18:46Z", "closed_at": "2023-12-20T12:59:08Z", "labels": ["Question", "WebP"], "reporter": "afk42", "security_topic": "applicability of CVE-2023-4863 to Pillow versions", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know if Pillow is vulnerable to CVE\u20112023\u20114863 and whether upgrading libwebp (instead of Pillow) mitigates the issue, with the answer citing Pillow\u202f10.0.1 and newer libwebp versions as anchors.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "afk42", "answer_author": "radarhere", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation", "advisory", "cve_cwe_db"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": ["CVE-2023-4863"], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": ["10.0.1"], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "afk42", "timestamp": "2023-12-19T09:18:46Z", "body": "Being aware that CVE-2023-4863 was already fixed in Pillow 10.0.1 and as I'm unable to update to that version at the moment, I want to understand if Pillow really uses any of the functionality where the issue resides or if the CVE can be whitelisted since use of this vulnerable library is excluded by the functional design of Pillow.\r\n\r\nThank you\r\n\r\n### What are your OS, Python and Pillow versions?\r\n\r\n* OS: \r\n* Python: \r\n* Pillow:  < 10.0.1\r\n\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2023-12-19T10:25:55Z", "body": "Pillow calls WebPAnimDecoderGetNext, which calls WebPDecode, calling DecodeInto, calling VP8LDecodeHeader, calling DecodeImageStream, calling ReadHuffmanCodes, calling ReadHuffmanCode, calling VP8LBuildHuffmanTable, calling BuildHuffmanTable - so I don't see why we wouldn't be vulnerable.\r\n\r\nYou might also be interested in this distinction - the source code of Pillow itself did not really change in 10.0.1 to address this situation. 10.0.1 was released to provide new wheels to those who do not compile Pillow from source. You could use an earlier version of Pillow with a recent version of libwebp and not be vulnerable.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "afk42", "timestamp": "2023-12-20T09:20:57Z", "body": "Thank you for your answer. @radarhere \r\nSo if I understood correctly I can just bump up the version of libwebp and maintain compatibility with <10.0.1 versions. ", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/7626#1", "question": "Is Pillow affected by CVE-2023-4863 in libwebp when using Pillow versions < 10.0.1? I know CVE-2023-4863 was fixed in Pillow 10.0.1, but I cannot update to that version at the moment. I want to understand whether Pillow actually uses the vulnerable functionality, or whether the CVE can be whitelisted because use of the vulnerable library is excluded by Pillow's functional design.\n\nEnvironment:\n* OS: \n* Python: \n* Pillow: < 10.0.1", "answer": "Pillow calls WebPAnimDecoderGetNext, which calls WebPDecode, calling DecodeInto, calling VP8LDecodeHeader, calling DecodeImageStream, calling ReadHuffmanCodes, calling ReadHuffmanCode, calling VP8LBuildHuffmanTable, calling BuildHuffmanTable - so I don't see why we wouldn't be vulnerable.\n\nYou might also be interested in this distinction - the source code of Pillow itself did not really change in 10.0.1 to address this situation. 10.0.1 was released to provide new wheels to those who do not compile Pillow from source. You could use an earlier version of Pillow with a recent version of libwebp and not be vulnerable.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["Pillow 10.0.1", "libwebp", "Pillow WebP decoding call path: WebPAnimDecoderGetNext -> WebPDecode -> DecodeInto -> VP8LDecodeHeader -> DecodeImageStream -> ReadHuffmanCodes -> ReadHuffmanCode -> VP8LBuildHuffmanTable -> BuildHuffmanTable"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": ["fixed_versions:10.0.1"]}], "answer_in_thread_refs": [], "leak_flags": ["fixed_versions:10.0.1"], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T17:55:11.349654Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/7518", "repo": "python-pillow/Pillow", "number": 7518, "title": "May I ask if Pillow 9.3.0 (windows) involves the CVE-2023-45853 vulnerability in zlib?", "url": "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/7518", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2023-11-03T03:22:41Z", "closed_at": "2023-11-03T09:31:40Z", "labels": ["Question"], "reporter": "hwzjy01", "security_topic": "applicability of CVE-2023-45853 to Pillow 9.3.0 on Windows", "qa_summary": "The developer asked if Pillow 9.3.0 on Windows is affected by CVE-2023-45853 in zlib, and the maintainer answered it is not, referencing the NVD CVE entry and a zlib issue comment.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "hwzjy01", "answer_author": "radarhere", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation", "external_reference", "advisory"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": ["CVE-2023-45853"], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": ["https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-45853"]}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "hwzjy01", "timestamp": "2023-11-03T03:22:41Z", "body": "windows 10\r\npillow 9.3.0\r\nzlib 1.2.13\r\n\r\nhttps://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-45853\r\n![image](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/assets/121840033/70916e66-503f-4e8f-ad14-edbc332a0692)\r\nI don\u2019t know if minizip related modules are involved.", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2023-11-03T03:45:14Z", "body": "Hi. Given that \"[MiniZip is not a supported part of the zlib product](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-45853)\", and when compiling for Windows, Pillow merely runs \"[`win32\\Makefile.msc\", \"zlib.lib`](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/14c539e7873b816cdb4f0f7aeeeed50a094197cf/winbuild/build_prepare.py#L144)\", I'm going to say no. It would seem that separate steps are required to involve minizip, which Pillow doesn't take. https://github.com/madler/zlib/issues/868#issuecomment-1783525536 states that \"This is not a zlib vulnerability.\"\r\n\r\nAs an aside, if you are concerned about security, I would recommend upgrading from Pillow 9.3.0. We have fixed various security problems since then.\r\n- https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/9.4.0.html#security\r\n- https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/9.5.0.html#security\r\n- https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/10.0.0.html#security\r\n- https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/10.0.1.html#security", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/7518#1", "question": "On Windows 10, I am using Pillow 9.3.0 with zlib 1.2.13. NVD lists CVE-2023-45853 for zlib/minizip at https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-45853. Does Pillow 9.3.0 for Windows involve the vulnerable minizip-related modules, or is it affected by this zlib CVE?", "answer": "The maintainer said no: MiniZip is not a supported part of the zlib product, and Pillow\u2019s Windows build merely builds `zlib.lib` via `win32\\Makefile.msc` rather than taking the separate steps needed to involve minizip. They also pointed to a zlib comment saying \u201cThis is not a zlib vulnerability.\u201d Separately, they recommended upgrading from Pillow 9.3.0 because Pillow has fixed various security problems since then.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-45853", "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/14c539e7873b816cdb4f0f7aeeeed50a094197cf/winbuild/build_prepare.py#L144", "https://github.com/madler/zlib/issues/868#issuecomment-1783525536", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/9.4.0.html#security", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/9.5.0.html#security", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/10.0.0.html#security", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/10.0.1.html#security"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": ["advisory_urls:https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-45853"]}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["14c539e7873b816cdb4f0f7aeeeed50a094197cf", "CVE-2023-45853", "https://github.com/madler/zlib/issues/868#issuecomment-1783525536", "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/14c539e7873b816cdb4f0f7aeeeed50a094197cf/winbuild/build_prepare.py#L144", "https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-45853", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/10.0.0.html#security", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/10.0.1.html#security", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/9.4.0.html#security", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/9.5.0.html#security"], "leak_flags": ["advisory_urls:https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-45853"], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-15T20:07:52.460845Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/7179", "repo": "python-pillow/Pillow", "number": 7179, "title": "LibTIFF vulnerability (CVE-2023-30774\uff09", "url": "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/7179", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2023-05-23T04:45:23Z", "closed_at": "2023-05-23T06:08:33Z", "labels": ["TIFF"], "reporter": "crbean", "security_topic": "applicability of CVE-2023-30774 to Pillow versions", "qa_summary": "Whether Pillow is affected by CVE-2023-30774, answered with the fix being in libtiff\u202f4.5.0 and first included in Pillow\u202f9.4.0, so earlier Pillow versions may be vulnerable.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "crbean", "answer_author": "radarhere", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation", "advisory"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": ["CVE-2023-30774"], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": ["4.5.0", "9.4.0"], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "crbean", "timestamp": "2023-05-23T04:45:23Z", "body": "The following vulnerabilities are reported against libtiff in Pillow, would you please confirm if Pillow is affected by them?\r\nCVE-2023-30774\r\nOS: Ubuntu 22.04 LTS\r\nPython: 3.9.16\r\nPillow: 9.3.0", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2023-05-23T05:15:57Z", "body": "The vulnerability was addressed in October, so the fix would have been part of libtiff 4.5.0.\r\n\r\nThe first Pillow version to include libtiff 4.5.0 was Pillow 9.4.0. So if you installed Pillow from a wheel with an earlier version, yes, you may be affected.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/7179#1", "question": "Pillow 9.3.0 on Ubuntu 22.04 LTS with Python 3.9.16 is reported by a scanner as having the libtiff vulnerability CVE-2023-30774. Is Pillow affected by this vulnerability?", "answer": "The vulnerability was addressed in libtiff 4.5.0. The first Pillow version to include libtiff 4.5.0 was Pillow 9.4.0, so if Pillow was installed from a wheel with an earlier Pillow version such as 9.3.0, it may be affected.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["libtiff 4.5.0", "Pillow 9.4.0"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": [], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T17:55:37.955555Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/7137", "repo": "python-pillow/Pillow", "number": 7137, "title": "LibTIFF vulnerabilities (CVE-2023-0795, CVE-2023-0796, CVE-2023-0797, CVE-2023-0798, CVE-2023-0799, CVE-2023-0800, CVE-2023-0801, CVE-2023-0802, CVE-2023-0803, CVE-2023-0804)", "url": "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/7137", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2023-05-05T05:26:29Z", "closed_at": "2023-05-05T07:25:25Z", "labels": ["TIFF"], "reporter": "lennin-cp", "security_topic": "applicability of libtiff CVEs to Pillow", "qa_summary": "The developer asked if the listed CVE-2023-0795\u2013CVEs affect Pillow, and the maintainer answered that they all relate to the libtiff tool tiffcrop (see its man page), which Pillow does not use, so Pillow is not affected.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "lennin-cp", "answer_author": "radarhere", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation", "advisory"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": ["CVE-2023-0795", "CVE-2023-0796", "CVE-2023-0797", "CVE-2023-0798", "CVE-2023-0799", "CVE-2023-0800", "CVE-2023-0801", "CVE-2023-0802", "CVE-2023-0803", "CVE-2023-0804", "CVE-2023-1916"], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "lennin-cp", "timestamp": "2023-05-05T05:26:29Z", "body": "The following vulnerabilities are reported against libtiff in Pillow, would you please confirm if Pillow is affected by them?\r\n\r\n1. [CVE-2023-0795](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0795)\r\n1. [CVE-2023-0796](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0796)\r\n1. [CVE-2023-0797](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0797)\r\n1. [CVE-2023-0798](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0798)\r\n1. [CVE-2023-0799](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0799)\r\n1. [CVE-2023-0800](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0800)\r\n1. [CVE-2023-0801](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0801)\r\n1. [CVE-2023-0802](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0802)\r\n1. [CVE-2023-0803](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0803)\r\n1. [CVE-2023-0804](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0804)\r\n\r\nOS: Ubuntu 22.04 LTS\r\nPython: 3.9.16\r\nPillow: 9.5.0", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2023-05-05T05:32:31Z", "body": "All of the vulnerabilities you have listed relate to [tiffcrop](https://linux.die.net/man/1/tiffcrop), a libtiff tool that we don't use, so no, we wouldn't be affected by them.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "lennin-cp", "timestamp": "2023-05-05T05:36:54Z", "body": "I forgot to add one more to the list: [CVE-2023-1916](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-1916)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2023-05-05T05:39:58Z", "body": "That also relates to [tiffcrop](https://linux.die.net/man/1/tiffcrop), a libtiff tool that we don't use, so no, we wouldn't be affected by it.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/7137#1", "question": "In python-pillow/Pillow, are the following reported libtiff vulnerabilities applicable to Pillow 9.5.0 on Ubuntu 22.04 LTS with Python 3.9.16?\n\n1. CVE-2023-0795: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0795\n2. CVE-2023-0796: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0796\n3. CVE-2023-0797: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0797\n4. CVE-2023-0798: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0798\n5. CVE-2023-0799: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0799\n6. CVE-2023-0800: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0800\n7. CVE-2023-0801: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0801\n8. CVE-2023-0802: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0802\n9. CVE-2023-0803: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0803\n10. CVE-2023-0804: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-0804", "answer": "No. All of the listed vulnerabilities relate to tiffcrop, a libtiff tool that Pillow does not use, so Pillow would not be affected by them.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://linux.die.net/man/1/tiffcrop"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://linux.die.net/man/1/tiffcrop"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T17:57:08.537264Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/6955", "repo": "python-pillow/Pillow", "number": 6955, "title": "Libtiff vulnerabilities  (CVE-2022-2519, CVE-2022-2520, CVE-2022-48281)", "url": "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/6955", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2023-02-16T09:17:14Z", "closed_at": "2023-02-16T12:52:28Z", "labels": ["TIFF"], "reporter": "crbean", "security_topic": "applicability of specific CVEs to Pillow/tiffcrop functionality", "qa_summary": "Whether CVE\u20112022\u20112519, CVE\u20112022\u20112520, and CVE\u20112022\u201148281 affect Pillow; the maintainer answered Pillow is not affected, citing the CVE IDs and GHSA advisory links.", "question_comment_id": "c6", "answer_comment_id": "c7", "question_author": "crbean", "answer_author": "hugovk", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["advisory", "cve_cwe_db"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": ["CVE-2022-2519", "CVE-2022-2520", "CVE-2022-48281"], "ghsa_ids": ["GHSA-9rcr-wwr3-q567", "GHSA-4qr3-39h9-qwp4"], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": ["https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-9rcr-wwr3-q567", "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-4qr3-39h9-qwp4"]}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "crbean", "timestamp": "2023-02-16T09:17:14Z", "body": "There are some vulnerabilities of libtiff within Pillow.\r\nPlease confirm whether you are affected by them.\r\n\r\n~CVE-2022-2056~\r\n~CVE-2022-2057~\r\nCVE-2022-2519\r\nCVE-2022-2520\r\n~CVE-2022-2521~\r\n~CVE-2022-2953~\r\n~CVE-2022-34526~\r\n~CVE-2022-3570~\r\n~CVE-2022-3597~\r\n~CVE-2022-3598~\r\n~CVE-2022-3599~\r\n~CVE-2022-3626~\r\n~CVE-2022-3627~\r\nCVE-2022-48281\r\n\r\nWhat are your OS, Python and Pillow versions?\r\nOS: Linux\r\nPython: 3.9\r\nPillow: 9.3.0", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "hugovk", "timestamp": "2023-02-16T09:52:09Z", "body": "Please also search the issue tracker first, at least these are dealt with in https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/6577 (and Pillow is not affected by those):\r\n\r\nCVE-2022-2056\r\nCVE-2022-2057\r\nCVE-2022-2521\r\nCVE-2022-2953\r\n\r\nLeaving:\r\n\r\nCVE-2022-2519\r\nCVE-2022-2520\r\nCVE-2022-34526\r\nCVE-2022-3570\r\nCVE-2022-3597\r\nCVE-2022-3598\r\nCVE-2022-3599\r\nCVE-2022-3626\r\nCVE-2022-3627\r\nCVE-2022-48281\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "hugovk", "timestamp": "2023-02-16T10:00:41Z", "body": "Similar to #6577, these are related to tiffcrop, which we don't use:\r\n\r\nCVE-2022-2056\r\nCVE-2022-2519\r\nCVE-2022-3570\r\nCVE-2022-3597\r\nCVE-2022-3598\r\nCVE-2022-3599\r\nCVE-2022-3626\r\nCVE-2022-3627\r\nCVE-2022-48281\r\n\r\nLeaving:\r\n\r\nCVE-2022-34526\r\n\r\nWhich is related to Tiffsplit, which I think we don't use either?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "hugovk", "timestamp": "2023-02-16T10:06:13Z", "body": "By the way, this was already covered in #6681:\r\n\r\nCVE-2022-3570\r\n\r\nAnd these in #6746:\r\n\r\nCVE-2022-3597\r\nCVE-2022-3598\r\nCVE-2022-3599\r\nCVE-2022-3626\r\nCVE-2022-3627\r\n\r\nPillow not affected.\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "hugovk", "timestamp": "2023-02-16T10:13:19Z", "body": "> Leaving:\r\n> \r\n> [CVE-2022-34526](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-3fv6-vw38-4m6q)\r\n> \r\n> Which is related to Tiffsplit, which I think we don't use either?\r\n\r\nThat's covered in https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/6665 covered, an issue (like https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/6681) that you opened in October :) Same answer, Pillow not affected.\r\n\r\nTo summarise, Pillow is not affected by any of these, and please search the issue tracker next time to avoid duplicated effort.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "crbean", "timestamp": "2023-02-16T12:35:05Z", "body": "Thank you for replying. The previous issue occurred in version 9.2.0.\r\nI'm not sure if it's still unaffected in version 9.3.0", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "crbean", "timestamp": "2023-02-16T12:36:24Z", "body": "Excuse me, are the remaining three vulnerabilities affected?\r\nCVE-2022-2519, CVE-2022-2520, CVE-2022-48281", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "hugovk", "timestamp": "2023-02-16T12:43:51Z", "body": "Pillow is not affected:\r\n\r\n> Similar to #6577, these are related to tiffcrop, which we don't use:\r\n> ... \r\n[CVE-2022-2519](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-9rcr-wwr3-q567)\r\n...\r\n[CVE-2022-48281](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-4qr3-39h9-qwp4)\r\n\r\nSame for CVE-2022-2520, also tiffcrop.\r\n", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/6955#1", "question": "For Pillow 9.3.0 on Linux with Python 3.9, are the remaining libtiff vulnerabilities CVE-2022-2519, CVE-2022-2520, and CVE-2022-48281 applicable to Pillow?", "answer": "Pillow is not affected by CVE-2022-2519, CVE-2022-2520, or CVE-2022-48281. These vulnerabilities are related to tiffcrop, which Pillow does not use.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/6577", "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-9rcr-wwr3-q567", "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-4qr3-39h9-qwp4"], "answer_grounded_in": "c7", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["#6577", "CVE-2022-2519", "CVE-2022-2520", "CVE-2022-48281", "GHSA-4QR3-39H9-QWP4", "GHSA-9RCR-WWR3-Q567", "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-4qr3-39h9-qwp4", "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-9rcr-wwr3-q567"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T17:57:17.298505Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/6687", "repo": "python-pillow/Pillow", "number": 6687, "title": "Pip audit reporting security vulnerability OSV-2022-715", "url": "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/6687", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2022-10-25T22:01:10Z", "closed_at": "2022-10-31T07:55:06Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "jrzerr", "security_topic": "resolution of reported vulnerability OSV-2022-715 in Pillow", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know if the OSV-2022-715 vulnerability reported for Pillow 9.2.0 was a false positive and which version resolves it; the answer states it is fixed in Pillow 9.3.0 (see release notes https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/9.3.0.html#decode-jpeg-compressed-blp1-data-in-original-mode).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c2", "question_author": "jrzerr", "answer_author": "radarhere", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["advisory", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": ["OSV-2022-715"], "fixed_versions": ["9.3.0"], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": ["https://osv.dev/vulnerability/OSV-2022-715", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/9.3.0.html#decode-jpeg-compressed-blp1-data-in-original-mode"]}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "jrzerr", "timestamp": "2022-10-25T22:01:10Z", "body": "We run pip-audit and just this week started reporting a security vulnerability with 9.2.0, we are running python 3.10.8.\r\n\r\nhttps://osv.dev/vulnerability/OSV-2022-715\r\n\r\nWhen you follow the References link in the issue, it says something about being fixed.\r\n\r\nhttps://bugs.chromium.org/p/oss-fuzz/issues/detail?id=50217\r\n\r\nBut yet `pip-audit` is still flagging.\r\n\r\nShould we be treating this as a false flag?  Problem is with `pip-audit` and it's source of vulnerabilities?  Or is this something that needs to be investigated?", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2022-10-25T22:19:18Z", "body": "My conclusion is that the security vulnerability was only detected intermittently. A later test didn't detect the vulnerability, so the automatic software marked it as fixed, believing that something we'd done in the meantime had resolved the matter.\r\n\r\nSince then, #6678 has merged a proper fix for this.\r\n\r\nWe plan to release Pillow 9.3.0 with that fix very soon. Follow #6460 for updates.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2022-10-29T21:38:58Z", "body": "Pillow 9.3.0 has now been released.\r\n\r\nThis security problem is mentioned in the release notes at https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/9.3.0.html#decode-jpeg-compressed-blp1-data-in-original-mode", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "emcek", "timestamp": "2022-10-30T08:42:12Z", "body": "With `9.3.0` installed...\r\n\r\n```\r\nName   Version ID           Fix Versions\r\n------ ------- ------------ ------------\r\npillow 9.3.0   OSV-2022-715\r\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "CandiedCode", "timestamp": "2022-10-30T15:48:37Z", "body": "I get the same error as @emcek \r\n\r\n```shell\r\nName   Version ID           Fix Versions\r\n------ ------- ------------ ------------\r\npillow 9.3.0   OSV-2022-715\r\n\r\npip freeze | grep Pillow\r\nPillow==9.3.0\r\n```\r\n\r\nlooks like oss-fuzz-vulns updated it to include Pillow 9.3\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/google/oss-fuzz-vulns/commit/f9c98bbd59c4f4cc0b49d2c43654d697fb1dc6d2", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2022-10-30T21:15:32Z", "body": "I think it is just handling the fact that https://osv.dev/vulnerability/OSV-2022-715 states the problem affects Pillow > 9.*, so that automatically includes any new releases we create.\r\n\r\nhttps://bugs.chromium.org/p/oss-fuzz/issues/detail?id=50217 already thought it was fixed, so it must be independent of that.\r\n\r\nI've created https://github.com/google/oss-fuzz-vulns/pull/21 to try and sort this out.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/6687#1", "question": "We run `pip-audit` and just this week it started reporting OSV-2022-715 as a security vulnerability for Pillow 9.2.0; we are running Python 3.10.8.\n\nhttps://osv.dev/vulnerability/OSV-2022-715\n\nWhen following the References link in the issue, it says something about being fixed:\n\nhttps://bugs.chromium.org/p/oss-fuzz/issues/detail?id=50217\n\nBut `pip-audit` is still flagging it. Should we treat this as a false positive? Is the problem with `pip-audit` and its vulnerability source, or is this something that needs investigation?", "answer": "Pillow 9.3.0 has been released, and the security problem is mentioned in its release notes under \u201cDecode JPEG compressed BLP1 data in original mode.\u201d", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["Pillow 9.3.0", "https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/9.3.0.html#decode-jpeg-compressed-blp1-data-in-original-mode"], "answer_grounded_in": "c2", "leak_flags": ["advisory_urls:https://osv.dev/vulnerability/OSV-2022-715"]}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/releasenotes/9.3.0.html#decode-jpeg-compressed-blp1-data-in-original-mode"], "leak_flags": ["advisory_urls:https://osv.dev/vulnerability/OSV-2022-715"], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T17:59:29.062172Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/6665", "repo": "python-pillow/Pillow", "number": 6665, "title": "Libtiff vulnerability (CVE-2022-34526\uff09", "url": "https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/6665", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2022-10-14T01:26:28Z", "closed_at": "2022-10-22T00:39:04Z", "labels": ["TIFF", "Awaiting OP Action", "Stale"], "reporter": "crbean", "security_topic": "impact assessment of CVE-2022-34526 on Pillow usage", "qa_summary": "Whether CVE-2022-34526 affects Pillow/libtiff, answered by referencing libtiff commit 275735d0354e39c0ac1dc3c0db2120d6f31d1990 that fixes the issue and noting Pillow only raises a normal error, not a crash.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c2", "question_author": "crbean", "answer_author": "radarhere", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "commit_history", "advisory", "cve_cwe_db"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": ["CVE-2022-34526"], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": ["275735d0354e39c0ac1dc3c0db2120d6f31d1990"], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "crbean", "timestamp": "2022-10-14T01:26:28Z", "body": "There are some vulnerabilities of libtiff within Pillow.\r\nPlease confirm whether you are affected by them.\r\n\r\nCVE-2022-34526\r\n\r\nWhat are your OS, Python and Pillow versions?\r\nOS: Linux\r\nPython: 3.9\r\nPillow: 9.2.0", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2022-10-14T02:08:21Z", "body": "The CVE you have linked to mentions [tiffsplit](https://linux.die.net/man/1/tiffsplit), a libtiff tool that we don't use.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "radarhere", "timestamp": "2022-10-14T08:07:24Z", "body": "The reproduction in https://gitlab.com/libtiff/libtiff/-/issues/433 uses tiffsplit, but the fix at https://gitlab.com/libtiff/libtiff/-/commit/275735d0354e39c0ac1dc3c0db2120d6f31d1990 is in the general part of libtiff. That gives me pause. However, if this was a problem beyond tiffsplit, then the CVE description should be written differently.\r\n\r\nI can tell you that downloading the file from the issue and loading it with Pillow results in a normal error, not a crash.\r\n\r\nDoes that answer your question?", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "python-pillow/Pillow/issue/6665#1", "question": "There are some vulnerabilities of libtiff within Pillow.\nPlease confirm whether you are affected by them.\n\nCVE-2022-34526\n\nWhat are your OS, Python and Pillow versions?\nOS: Linux\nPython: 3.9\nPillow: 9.2.0", "answer": "The linked reproduction for CVE-2022-34526 uses `tiffsplit`, a libtiff tool Pillow does not use. Although the fix is in a general part of libtiff, the maintainer noted that if it affected more than `tiffsplit`, the CVE description should say so; downloading the file from the libtiff issue and loading it with Pillow produced a normal error, not a crash.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://gitlab.com/libtiff/libtiff/-/issues/433", "https://gitlab.com/libtiff/libtiff/-/commit/275735d0354e39c0ac1dc3c0db2120d6f31d1990", "tiffsplit"], "answer_grounded_in": "c2", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["275735d0354e39c0ac1dc3c0db2120d6f31d1990", "https://gitlab.com/libtiff/libtiff/-/commit/275735d0354e39c0ac1dc3c0db2120d6f31d1990", "https://gitlab.com/libtiff/libtiff/-/issues/433"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T18:03:21.757858Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "rails/rails/issue/56736", "repo": "rails/rails", "number": 56736, "title": "Accessing the key generator in an initializer silently changes the hash digest class for the entire application", "url": "https://github.com/rails/rails/issues/56736", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2026-02-04T21:57:55Z", "closed_at": "2026-05-17T03:08:22Z", "labels": ["stale"], "reporter": "ankane", "security_topic": "unexpected hash algorithm downgrade due to early key_generator access", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know that accessing Rails.key_generator before setting config.active_support.key_generator_hash_digest_class can downgrade the hash algorithm to insecure SHA1, and that this was fixed in PR #56746 which now raises a RuntimeError to prevent the misconfiguration.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "ankane", "answer_author": "6temes", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "pr_data", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": ["#56746"], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "ankane", "timestamp": "2026-02-04T21:57:55Z", "body": "### Steps to reproduce\n\nCreate a new Rails application and add `config/initializers/repro.rb` with:\n\n```ruby\nRails.application.key_generator\n```\n\nAnd run:\n\n```sh\nrails runner \"p Rails.application.key_generator.instance_variable_get(:@key_generator).instance_variable_get(:@hash_digest_class)\"\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n`OpenSSL::Digest::SHA256`, which is the output without the initializer.\n\n### Actual behavior\n\n`OpenSSL::Digest::SHA1`\n\n### System configuration\n\n**Rails version**: 8.1.2\n\n**Ruby version**: 3.4.8\n\n### Additional notes\n\nturbo-rails was [affected by this](https://github.com/hotwired/turbo-rails/blob/v8.0.21/UPGRADING.md#key-digest-changes-in-111) and made a change to initialization to workaround it.\n\nA few ideas to address this would be:\n\n- apply `config.active_support.key_generator_hash_digest_class` earlier in initialization\n- warn or raise if `Rails.application.key_generator` is already cached when applying `config.active_support.key_generator_hash_digest_class`\n- include the hash digest as part of the cache key in `Rails.application.key_generator`", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "6temes", "timestamp": "2026-02-09T02:03:59Z", "body": "I've opened a PR to address this: #56746\n\nThe fix raises a `RuntimeError` when `key_generator` is accessed before the `key_generator_hash_digest_class` configuration has been applied. This turns the silent misconfiguration into an immediate, actionable error.\n\nApps that don't configure `key_generator_hash_digest_class` (pre-7.0 defaults) are unaffected.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "github-actions", "timestamp": "2026-05-10T03:08:06Z", "body": "This issue has been automatically marked as stale because it has not been commented on for at least three months.\nThe resources of the Rails team are limited, and so we are asking for your help.\nIf you can still reproduce this error on the `8-1-stable` branch or on `main`, please reply with all of the information you have about it in order to keep the issue open.\nThank you for all your contributions.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "rails/rails/issue/56736#1", "question": "In Rails 8.1.2 on Ruby 3.4.8, accessing `Rails.application.key_generator` in an initializer appears to silently change the hash digest class for the entire application. To reproduce, create a new Rails application and add `config/initializers/repro.rb` with:\n\n```ruby\nRails.application.key_generator\n```\n\nThen run:\n\n```sh\nrails runner \"p Rails.application.key_generator.instance_variable_get(:@key_generator).instance_variable_get(:@hash_digest_class)\"\n```\n\nWithout the initializer, the expected output is `OpenSSL::Digest::SHA256`; with the initializer, the actual output is `OpenSSL::Digest::SHA1`. turbo-rails was affected by this and worked around it in initialization. Possible fixes might include applying `config.active_support.key_generator_hash_digest_class` earlier, warning/raising if `Rails.application.key_generator` is already cached when applying `config.active_support.key_generator_hash_digest_class`, or including the hash digest in the `Rails.application.key_generator` cache key. How should Rails address this silent misconfiguration?", "answer": "A PR was opened to address this: #56746. The fix raises a `RuntimeError` when `key_generator` is accessed before the `key_generator_hash_digest_class` configuration has been applied, turning the silent misconfiguration into an immediate error. Apps that do not configure `key_generator_hash_digest_class` are unaffected.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["PR #56746"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["#56746"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T18:05:34.221346Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "rails/rails/issue/56452", "repo": "rails/rails", "number": 56452, "title": "`ActiveRecord::Rollback` is silenced in nested transactions - including ApplicationRecord callbacks", "url": "https://github.com/rails/rails/issues/56452", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-12-24T15:14:39Z", "closed_at": "2026-05-17T14:31:43Z", "labels": ["stale"], "reporter": "ipvalverde", "security_topic": "silencing of rollback errors in nested transactions leading to potential data inconsistency", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know that raising ActiveRecord::Rollback in a nested transaction is intentionally swallowed by Rails (as documented in the Rails transaction docs), see https://api.rubyonrails.org/classes/ActiveRecord/ConnectionAdapters/DatabaseStatements.html#method-i-transaction", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "ipvalverde", "answer_author": "eglitobias", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "ipvalverde", "timestamp": "2025-12-24T15:14:39Z", "body": "### Steps to reproduce\n\n<!-- (Guidelines for creating a bug report are [available\nhere](https://edgeguides.rubyonrails.org/contributing_to_ruby_on_rails.html#creating-a-bug-report)) -->\n\n<!-- Paste your executable test case created from one of the scripts found [here](https://edgeguides.rubyonrails.org/contributing_to_ruby_on_rails.html#create-an-executable-test-case) below: -->\n[Full script\n](https://gist.github.com/ipvalverde/e442ccaf22657b2457ce50981c11a305)\n\n```ruby\n    user = User.create!(name: \"john\")\n\n    assert_equal true, user.active\n    assert_equal true, user.email.active\n\n    User.transaction do\n      user.active = false\n\n      Email.transaction do\n        email = user.email\n        email.active = false\n        email.save\n\n        raise ActiveRecord::Rollback\n      end\n    end\n\n    user.reload\n\n    # Both are committed to the database even though an ActiveRecord::Rollback was raised\n    assert_equal true, user.active, \"Expect that the whole transaction fails and the user is not saved\"\n    assert_equal true, user.email.active, \"Expect that the email is not updated\"\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThis is related to the issue reported in [this PR](https://github.com/rails/rails/pull/44518). The error should bubble up and not be silenced by Rails.\n\nRaising an error should always have an effect, swallowing it is error-prone and can easily throw people off.\nBased on [this GitHub search](https://github.com/search?q=%2Fraise%5B%5C%28%5Cs%5Cn%5D*ActiveRecord%3A%3ARollback%2F+-org%3Ashop+language%3ARuby+-org%3AShopify+-path%3Aspec%2F+-path%3A_test.rb&type=code), it looks like people seem to be raising `ActiveRecord::Rollback` errors.\n\nif the idea is that developers should not be manually raising `ActiveRecord::Rollback`, then the documentation should be clear about it (even better if people are blocked from raising it).\n\n### Actual behavior\n\nRaising the error in nested transaction is a no-op. This means that ApplicationRecord callbacks will also silence `ActiveRecord::Rollback` errors. Example (also include in the [full test](https://gist.github.com/ipvalverde/e442ccaf22657b2457ce50981c11a305)):\n\n```ruby\n# definition\nclass User < ActiveRecord::Base\n  after_create :create_email_for_user\n\n  private\n\n  def create_email_for_user\n    email = build_email(email_address: \"#{name}@example.com\")\n\n    unless email.save\n      errors.add(:base, email.errors.full_messages.first || \"Email creation failed\")\n      raise ActiveRecord::Rollback\n    end\n  end\nend\n\n# usage\nuser = User.new(name: \"john\")\nsaved = User.transaction do\n  # This will attempt to create an email with `john@example.com` which already exists\n  # and raise an ActiveRecord::RecordInvalid error in `create_email_for_user`\n  user.save\nend\n\nuser.reload\n\n# user is successfully saved, this fails:\nrefute user.persisted?, \"User should not be persisted since create_email_for_user raised an ActiveRecord::Rollback error\"\n```\n\n\n### System configuration\n\n**Rails version**:  Latest from main\n\n**Ruby version**: `3.4.4`\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "eglitobias", "timestamp": "2026-01-20T19:40:19Z", "body": "This is expected behavior and well documented, not a bug:\nhttps://api.rubyonrails.org/classes/ActiveRecord/ConnectionAdapters/DatabaseStatements.html#method-i-transaction\n\nNested transactions in Rails are joinable by default. That means the inner transaction does not start a new database transaction, so raising `ActiveRecord::Rollback` there does not issue a SQL `ROLLBACK`. The exception is intentionally swallowed, and the outer transaction can still commit.\n\nAt the SQL level, most databases don\u2019t support true nested `BEGIN/COMMIT`. There is only one transaction per connection. To roll back part of a transaction, SQL uses SAVEPOINTS, not nested `BEGIN`s:\n```\nBEGIN;\n  INSERT INTO posts ...;          -- first\n  SAVEPOINT sp1;\n    INSERT INTO posts ...;        -- second\n    ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT sp1;    -- undo only inner work\nCOMMIT;\n```\nRails maps this explicitly via ^requires_new: true`:\n```\nActiveRecord::Base.transaction do\n  Post.create!(title: \"first\")\n  ActiveRecord::Base.transaction(requires_new: true) do\n    Post.create!(title: \"second\")\n    raise ActiveRecord::Rollback\n  end\nend\n```\n\nWithout `requires_new`, there is no SQL boundary to roll back so the behavior is correct by design.\n\nChanging this would be a significant backward-compatibility change, so it\u2019s likely outside the scope of a bugfix. As already discovered, this is being discussed in more depth in https://github.com/rails/rails/pull/44518, which seems like the appropriate place for exploring a broader proposal or deprecation path.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "ipvalverde", "timestamp": "2026-01-20T21:56:54Z", "body": "Thanks for taking a look into this @eglitobias . I was aware of it being documented, but I was expecting this decision could be re-evaluated.\n\nI cannot see a scenario where a developer would expect that raising a `ActiveRecord::Rollback` would **not** rollback. I think this is misleading and very likely a source of bugs and corrupt data. I understand changing it at this stage would be complex since this would be a breaking change.\n\n\nFrom some conversation with @matthewd and @rafaelfranca , it looks like the recommendation would be to not manually raise `ActiveRecord::Rollback` if you have an application that is too complex with many layers of nested transactions.\n\nFor this particular issue, what about just reporting when these rollbacks are silenced and the transactions is committed? This could, at least, give insight that something fishy might've happened running the application.\n\n[Example of changes](https://github.com/rails/rails/compare/main...ipvalverde:instrument-silenced-ar-rollbacks)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "zzak", "timestamp": "2026-01-25T02:53:06Z", "body": "Just a thought but here instead of trying to keep track of all the rollback errors, we just emit a notification when swallowing them:\nhttps://github.com/rails/rails/compare/main...ipvalverde:instrument-silenced-ar-rollbacks#diff-88e231c48eb5559ac3be58a024b019e86f7c74d388f5d49fac3f4e47bf851cc6R435\n\nThat way the behavior is the same but we can at least be aware of such a case should it occur and observe it.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "ipvalverde", "timestamp": "2026-02-09T10:00:44Z", "body": "> Just a thought but here instead of trying to keep track of all the rollback errors, we just emit a notification when swallowing them: [main...ipvalverde:instrument-silenced-ar-rollbacks#diff-88e231c48eb5559ac3be58a024b019e86f7c74d388f5d49fac3f4e47bf851cc6R435](https://github.com/rails/rails/compare/main...ipvalverde:instrument-silenced-ar-rollbacks#diff-88e231c48eb5559ac3be58a024b019e86f7c74d388f5d49fac3f4e47bf851cc6R435)\n> \n> That way the behavior is the same but we can at least be aware of such a case should it occur and observe it.\n\nThat's a good point. This will be less resource intensive and make it simpler, the only thing thought, is that it will not be too assertive. One pattern that I see in code is something like this (simplified here for clarity, usually happens in several layers of classes):\n\n\n```ruby\ndef delete_user\n  User.transaction do # <-- top-level transaction\n    user = User.find(1)\n  \n    User.transaction do\n      # ...\n      destroy_addresses(user)\n      # ...\n    end\n\n    user.destroy\n\n    # The rollback from the nested transaction is silenced, but the parent transaction is still rolledback:\n    raise ActiveRecord::Rollback if user.errors.any?    \n  end\n  # ...\nend\n\ndef destroy_addresses(user)\n  unless AddressService.destroy_user_addresses(user)\n    user.errors.add(:base, \"Failed to destroy addresses\") unless user.addresses.destroy_all\n    raise ActiveRecord::Rollback\n  end\nend\n```\n\nI'll add an alternative implementation and get some feedback to see which one would make more sense in Rails. For my particular use case, being more assertive (ie. making sure a `rollback` was raised as part of a transaction that was indeed committed) would be more helpful, otherwise it would generate too much noise.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "ipvalverde", "timestamp": "2026-02-09T14:16:59Z", "body": "## Proposed mitigations\n\nGiven changing the existing behavior of silencing rollbacks in nested transaction would be a breaking change, and given @zzak suggestion above, I would like to propose just \"bringing awareness\" to the issue. I understand this is documented, but we still have the fact that a rollback error should rollback.\n\n### 1) Instrument when rollback is silenced **and transaction is committed** [Preferred IMHO]\n\nThis approach will capture all silenced rollbacks in nested transactions, and, if the `RealTransaction` is committed, it will send an `ActiveSupport::Notification`.\n\n[Implementation](https://github.com/rails/rails/compare/main...ipvalverde:instrument-silenced-ar-rollbacks)\n\nCode example:\n\n```ruby\nActiveSupport::Notifications.subscribe(\"transaction.active_record.silenced_rollback_errors\") do |event|\n  rollback_errors = event.payload[:errors]\n\n  # rollback_errors will have an array with error silenced in the nested transaction(s)\nend\n\ndef trigger_notification_with_silecend_rollback\n  User.transaction do\n    user = User.lock.find(1)\n    User.transaction do\n      user.addresses.destroy_all\n      raise ActiveRecord::Rollback # < -- silenced rollback\n    end\n  \n    user.destroy\n  end # <-- when transaction commits, transaction.active_record.silenced_rollback_errors will be emitted\nend\n\ndef do_not_trigger_notification\n  User.transaction do\n    user = User.lock.find(1)\n\n    operation_result = User.transaction do\n      user.addresses.destroy_all\n      raise ActiveRecord::Rollback # <-- silenced rollback\n    end\n  \n    # Propagate silenced rollback and perform a real rollback \u2705\n    # This will NOT emit transaction.active_record.silenced_rollback_errors\n    raise ActiveRecord::Rollback if operation_result.nil?\n\n    user.destroy\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### 2) Instrument when rollback is silenced, even if the real transaction is also rolled back\n\nThis approach is simpler as it will always send an `ActiveSupport::Notification` whenever a rollback is silenced in a nested transaction. This can lead to false positives though, since the real transaction might also be rolled back.\n\n[Implementation](https://github.com/rails/rails/compare/main...ipvalverde:rails:instrument-possible-silenced-ar-rollbacks)\n\nCode example:\n\n```ruby\nActiveSupport::Notifications.subscribe(\"silenced_rollback.active_record\") do |event|\n  rollback_error = event.payload[:error]\n\n  # rollback_error will have the error raised in the nested transaction below\nend\n\ndef trigger_notification_with_silecend_rollback\n  User.transaction do\n    user = User.lock.find(1)\n\n    operation_result = User.transaction do\n      user.addresses.destroy_all\n      raise ActiveRecord::Rollback\n    end # <-- The rescue for the transaction will immediately emit the 'silenced_rollback.active_record' notification\n\n    # Even if the actual transaction is rolled back, the notification will be sent\n    raise ActiveRecord::Rollback if operation_result.nil?\n\n    user.destroy\n  end\nend\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "github-actions", "timestamp": "2026-05-10T14:26:38Z", "body": "This issue has been automatically marked as stale because it has not been commented on for at least three months.\nThe resources of the Rails team are limited, and so we are asking for your help.\nIf you can still reproduce this error on the `8-1-stable` branch or on `main`, please reply with all of the information you have about it in order to keep the issue open.\nThank you for all your contributions.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "rails/rails/issue/56452#1", "question": "In Rails latest from main on Ruby 3.4.4, `ActiveRecord::Rollback` raised inside a nested transaction appears to be swallowed, so the outer transaction still commits. Reproduction:\n\n```ruby\n    user = User.create!(name: \"john\")\n\n    assert_equal true, user.active\n    assert_equal true, user.email.active\n\n    User.transaction do\n      user.active = false\n\n      Email.transaction do\n        email = user.email\n        email.active = false\n        email.save\n\n        raise ActiveRecord::Rollback\n      end\n    end\n\n    user.reload\n\n    # Both are committed to the database even though an ActiveRecord::Rollback was raised\n    assert_equal true, user.active, \"Expect that the whole transaction fails and the user is not saved\"\n    assert_equal true, user.email.active, \"Expect that the email is not updated\"\n```\n\nThe same happens from an `ApplicationRecord` callback:\n\n```ruby\n# definition\nclass User < ActiveRecord::Base\n  after_create :create_email_for_user\n\n  private\n\n  def create_email_for_user\n    email = build_email(email_address: \"#{name}@example.com\")\n\n    unless email.save\n      errors.add(:base, email.errors.full_messages.first || \"Email creation failed\")\n      raise ActiveRecord::Rollback\n    end\n  end\nend\n\n# usage\nuser = User.new(name: \"john\")\nsaved = User.transaction do\n  # This will attempt to create an email with `john@example.com` which already exists\n  # and raise an ActiveRecord::RecordInvalid error in `create_email_for_user`\n  user.save\nend\n\nuser.reload\n\n# user is successfully saved, this fails:\nrefute user.persisted?, \"User should not be persisted since create_email_for_user raised an ActiveRecord::Rollback error\"\n```\n\nIs this a Rails bug? Should the `ActiveRecord::Rollback` bubble up instead of being silenced in nested transactions, or should Rails otherwise prevent/document manual raising of `ActiveRecord::Rollback`?", "answer": "This is expected behavior and well documented, not a bug:\nhttps://api.rubyonrails.org/classes/ActiveRecord/ConnectionAdapters/DatabaseStatements.html#method-i-transaction\n\nNested transactions in Rails are joinable by default. That means the inner transaction does not start a new database transaction, so raising `ActiveRecord::Rollback` there does not issue a SQL `ROLLBACK`. The exception is intentionally swallowed, and the outer transaction can still commit.\n\nAt the SQL level, most databases don\u2019t support true nested `BEGIN/COMMIT`. There is only one transaction per connection. To roll back part of a transaction, SQL uses SAVEPOINTS, not nested `BEGIN`s:\n```\nBEGIN;\n  INSERT INTO posts ...;          -- first\n  SAVEPOINT sp1;\n    INSERT INTO posts ...;        -- second\n    ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT sp1;    -- undo only inner work\nCOMMIT;\n```\nRails maps this explicitly via ^requires_new: true`:\n```\nActiveRecord::Base.transaction do\n  Post.create!(title: \"first\")\n  ActiveRecord::Base.transaction(requires_new: true) do\n    Post.create!(title: \"second\")\n    raise ActiveRecord::Rollback\n  end\nend\n```\n\nWithout `requires_new`, there is no SQL boundary to roll back so the behavior is correct by design.\n\nChanging this would be a significant backward-compatibility change, so it\u2019s likely outside the scope of a bugfix. As already discovered, this is being discussed in more depth in https://github.com/rails/rails/pull/44518, which seems like the appropriate place for exploring a broader proposal or deprecation path.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["Rails transaction API documentation: https://api.rubyonrails.org/classes/ActiveRecord/ConnectionAdapters/DatabaseStatements.html#method-i-transaction", "PR #44518"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://api.rubyonrails.org/classes/ActiveRecord/ConnectionAdapters/DatabaseStatements.html#method-i-transaction", "https://github.com/rails/rails/pull/44518"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T18:08:20.500160Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "rails/rails/issue/56158", "repo": "rails/rails", "number": 56158, "title": "Deadlocked transactions are still seen as \"usable\" for MySQL (trilogy / mysql2)", "url": "https://github.com/rails/rails/issues/56158", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-11-14T11:09:39Z", "closed_at": "2026-03-31T12:20:57Z", "labels": ["stale"], "reporter": "ipvalverde", "security_topic": "transaction state misreporting after deadlock leading to unintended writes", "qa_summary": "The issue asks if deadlocked MySQL transactions incorrectly remain marked as usable, allowing writes outside a transaction, and the answer points to a concrete fix in PR\u202f#44127 that restores the invalidated transaction state.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "ipvalverde", "answer_author": "ipvalverde", "answerer_role": "op_self", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "pr_data", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": ["#44127"], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "ipvalverde", "timestamp": "2025-11-14T11:09:39Z", "body": "### Steps to reproduce\n\n<!-- (Guidelines for creating a bug report are [available\nhere](https://edgeguides.rubyonrails.org/contributing_to_ruby_on_rails.html#creating-a-bug-report)) -->\n\n<!-- Paste your executable test case created from one of the scripts found [here](https://edgeguides.rubyonrails.org/contributing_to_ruby_on_rails.html#create-an-executable-test-case) below: -->\n\n[Full reproduction script gist](https://gist.github.com/ipvalverde/2d2f6b91d593e09b89a86a7c61a3ca5b). Important bit:\n\n```ruby\nAccount.transaction do\n  Account.create!(name: \"Account in top-level transaction\", balance: 50)\n\n  begin\n    puts \"1) Is transaction open? #{Account.connection.transaction_open?}\"\n\n    # consider the logic in here will raise a deadlock:\n    Account.lock.find(1).update!(balance: 10)\n    Account.lock.find(2).update!(balance: 20)\n\n  rescue ActiveRecord::Deadlocked => e\n    puts \"2) Is transaction open? #{Account.connection.transaction_open?}\"\n\n    # Everything up to this point is DISCARDED from the Database perspective.\n    # No account being created, no accounts being updated.\n    \n  end\n\n  # This will write directly to the database - no valid transactions in here.\n  Account.create!(name: \"Last account in top-level transaction\", balance: 999)\n\n  # this will be a no-op since there's no transaction open\n  raise ActiveRecord::Rollback\nend\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nAny database write operations (or maybe also read?) that happens in a code that is intended to run within a transaction should fail if there's no transaction open. Currently it misleads you, where you might think there is a transaction but there isn't.\n\nThis works as expected in postgres, but it looks like it's because postgres, when attempting to create an account after the deadlock will raise an error:\n\n```\nActiveRecord::StatementInvalid: PG::InFailedSqlTransaction: ERROR:  current transaction is aborted, commands ignored until end of transaction block\n```\n\n### Actual behavior\n\nAfter the transaction is deadlocked it gets killed, if that error is swallowed, any code that runs within a transaction block will write directly to the database!\n\n\n### System configuration\n\n**Rails version**: 8.1.1\n\n**Ruby version**: 3.4.4\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "ipvalverde", "timestamp": "2025-12-24T11:22:29Z", "body": "I did a bit more digging.\n\nIt looks like [this fix](https://github.com/rails/rails/pull/44127) changed how the transaction state works, now an `invaldiated` transaction that is rolled back has its state also going from `invalidated -> rolledback`, which means it will still look like a healthy transaction even though it is not.\n\nI'll propose a PR to fix that transaction state issue and preserve the `invalidated` state without fully invalidating the connection.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "github-actions", "timestamp": "2026-03-24T12:18:12Z", "body": "This issue has been automatically marked as stale because it has not been commented on for at least three months.\nThe resources of the Rails team are limited, and so we are asking for your help.\nIf you can still reproduce this error on the `8-1-stable` branch or on `main`, please reply with all of the information you have about it in order to keep the issue open.\nThank you for all your contributions.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "rails/rails/issue/56158#1", "question": "In Rails 8.1.1 on Ruby 3.4.4, using MySQL via trilogy/mysql2, a deadlocked transaction can still appear usable. Reproduction gist: https://gist.github.com/ipvalverde/2d2f6b91d593e09b89a86a7c61a3ca5b. Important bit:\n\n```ruby\nAccount.transaction do\n  Account.create!(name: \"Account in top-level transaction\", balance: 50)\n\n  begin\n    puts \"1) Is transaction open? #{Account.connection.transaction_open?}\"\n\n    # consider the logic in here will raise a deadlock:\n    Account.lock.find(1).update!(balance: 10)\n    Account.lock.find(2).update!(balance: 20)\n\n  rescue ActiveRecord::Deadlocked => e\n    puts \"2) Is transaction open? #{Account.connection.transaction_open?}\"\n\n    # Everything up to this point is DISCARDED from the Database perspective.\n    # No account being created, no accounts being updated.\n    \n  end\n\n  # This will write directly to the database - no valid transactions in here.\n  Account.create!(name: \"Last account in top-level transaction\", balance: 999)\n\n  # this will be a no-op since there's no transaction open\n  raise ActiveRecord::Rollback\nend\n```\n\nExpected behavior: any database writes, and maybe reads, in code intended to run within a transaction should fail if there is no transaction open. PostgreSQL behaves as expected because after the deadlock, trying to create an account raises:\n\n```text\nActiveRecord::StatementInvalid: PG::InFailedSqlTransaction: ERROR:  current transaction is aborted, commands ignored until end of transaction block\n```\n\nActual behavior: after the transaction deadlocks and is killed, if `ActiveRecord::Deadlocked` is swallowed, later code inside the transaction block writes directly to the database, while the transaction still appears open/usable. Why is the deadlocked MySQL transaction still seen as a healthy transaction, and what should be fixed?", "answer": "The transaction state handling appears to be the cause. PR #44127 changed transaction state behavior so an `invalidated` transaction that is rolled back transitions from `invalidated` to `rolledback`, making it look like a healthy transaction even though it is not. The proposed fix is to preserve the `invalidated` state without fully invalidating the connection.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["PR #44127"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://github.com/rails/rails/pull/44127"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T18:15:38.631504Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "rails/rails/issue/55886", "repo": "rails/rails", "number": 55886, "title": "SSL certificate verification fails on macOS (OpenSSL 3.6 + Ruby 3.4) due to CRL check", "url": "https://github.com/rails/rails/issues/55886", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-10-11T03:33:29Z", "closed_at": "2025-10-12T08:02:17Z", "labels": ["third party issue"], "reporter": "fitterfly-jainam-parikh", "security_topic": "SSL verification failure due to CRL check on macOS with OpenSSL 3.6", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know how to fix SSL certificate verification failures caused by OpenSSL 3.6 CRL checking on macOS, and the answer references ruby/openssl issue #949 (and PR #950) recommending adding the openssl gem or rebuilding Ruby with a compatible OpenSSL version.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c13", "question_author": "fitterfly-jainam-parikh", "answer_author": "lawrencepit", "answerer_role": "contributor", "artifacts_needed": ["issue_tracker", "pr_data", "commit_history", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": ["#950"], "fix_commits": ["b1b4b154fd389ac6254d49cfb11aee36c1c51b84"], "advisory_urls": ["https://github.com/ruby/openssl/issues/949", "https://github.com/openssl/openssl/commit/b1b4b154fd389ac6254d49cfb11aee36c1c51b84", "https://github.com/ruby/openssl/pull/950"]}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "fitterfly-jainam-parikh", "timestamp": "2025-10-11T03:33:29Z", "body": "When running any HTTPS requests (e.g., through Net::HTTP, ActiveStorage::Service::S3Service, or AWS SDK) on macOS with Ruby 3.4.x and OpenSSL 3.6, SSL handshakes fail with the error:\n\n```bash\nOpenSSL::SSL::SSLError:\n  SSL_connect returned=1 errno=0 peeraddr=1xx.1xx.6x.1xx:443 state=error:\n  certificate verify failed (unable to get certificate CRL)\n```\n\nThis occurs on a clean macOS (M2) setup using Homebrew OpenSSL@3 and rbenv.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. On macOS (Apple Silicon):\n```bash\nbrew install openssl@3 ca-certificates rbenv ruby-build\nRUBY_CONFIGURE_OPTS=\"--with-openssl-dir=$(brew --prefix openssl@3)\" rbenv install 3.4.1\nrbenv global 3.4.1\n```\n\n2. Run inside IRB:\n```bash\nrequire 'net/http'\nNet::HTTP.get(URI('https://aws.amazon.com'))\n```\n\n3. Observe the error:\n```bash\nSSL_connect ... certificate verify failed (unable to get certificate CRL)\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\nRuby (and by extension, Rails apps using Net::HTTP or ActiveStorage) should complete HTTPS requests successfully without manual CRL configuration.\n\n### Actual behavior\nThe SSL handshake fails because OpenSSL\u2019s default verification flags include V_FLAG_CRL_CHECK and V_FLAG_CRL_CHECK_ALL.\nmacOS\u2019s OpenSSL build does not provide a CRL bundle, so verification fails even though the certificate chain is valid.\n\n### Workarounds\nDisabling CRL checking resolves the issue:\n```bash\n# config/initializers/ssl_fix.rb\nrequire 'openssl'\nOpenSSL::SSL::SSLContext::DEFAULT_PARAMS[:verify_mode] = OpenSSL::SSL::VERIFY_PEER\nOpenSSL::SSL::SSLContext::DEFAULT_PARAMS[:verify_flags] &=\n  ~(OpenSSL::X509::V_FLAG_CRL_CHECK_ALL | OpenSSL::X509::V_FLAG_CRL_CHECK)\n```\n\n### System configuration\n\n**Rails version**: 8.0.2\n\n**Ruby version**: 3.4.1\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "adrienpoly", "timestamp": "2025-10-11T09:30:43Z", "body": "I had a similar issue this is link to https://github.com/ruby/openssl/issues/949\n\nYou need to add openssl as an explicit gem to your app to solve this issue.\n\nMaybe as it was [done](https://github.com/rails/rails/pull/55847) for the `json` library `openssl` should be added as an explicit library to Rails ?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "fitterfly-jainam-parikh", "timestamp": "2025-10-12T06:35:48Z", "body": "> I had a similar issue this is link to [ruby/openssl#949](https://github.com/ruby/openssl/issues/949)\n> \n> You need to add openssl as an explicit gem to your app to solve this issue.\n> \n> Maybe as it was [done](https://github.com/rails/rails/pull/55847) for the `json` library `openssl` should be added as an explicit library to Rails ?\n\nYup, it worked. I added the gem \"openssl\" and it worked. Still I don't get the reason to add that explicitly. My team has got the same Silicon Machines and for them, it's working just fine. Also, adding a gem without any reason might have consequences later on in the project.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "andrewmcodes", "timestamp": "2025-10-15T16:23:48Z", "body": "I was running into this after upgrading openssl to 3.6. After going back down to 3.5, everything started working again. ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "nialbima", "timestamp": "2025-10-15T20:49:57Z", "body": "Just chiming in to note that we're hitting this intermittently as well. \n\nRuby 3.3.9\nRails 7.2\n\nThe devs that are hitting it are on macOS Tahoe 26.0.1, and it's not showing up for people on older versions of macOS. Adding openssl as a Gemfile dependency fixed it (for our version, bundler decided to install 3.3.1).9", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "gwicks", "timestamp": "2025-10-16T17:22:22Z", "body": "Chiming in here, also encountering this and had to rebuild my RBenv install of 3.4.1 against `openssl@3.5`.\n\n```\nRUBY_CONFIGURE_OPTS=\"--with-openssl-dir=$(brew --prefix openssl@3.5)\" rbenv install 3.4.1\n```\n\nThat did seem to alleviate the issue for me.\n\nmacOS Sequoia 15.7.1 is my host system (M3).", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "VickTor61", "timestamp": "2025-10-19T12:53:32Z", "body": "> Just chiming in to note that we're hitting this intermittently as well.\n> \n> Ruby 3.3.9 Rails 7.2\n> \n> The devs that are hitting it are on macOS Tahoe 26.0.1, and it's not showing up for people on older versions of macOS. Adding openssl as a Gemfile dependency fixed it (for our version, bundler decided to install 3.3.1).9\n\nI am also experiencing similar issue. I noticed it after upgrating to tahoe. Unfortunately adding `gem openssl` to my gemfile doesn't resolve it. At the moment i get a segmentation bug error. You can view the crash report here \n\n[error.txt](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/22990262/error.txt)\n\nRunning on `mbp m4 pro`\n` rails 6.1.7`\n`ruby 3.1.4`\n`openssl 3.5`", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "johnnagro", "timestamp": "2025-10-19T14:33:22Z", "body": "i ran into this issue after upgrading to Tahoe. Using @gwicks solution, I fixed my mise based install:\n\n```\nMISE_RUBY_BUILD_OPTS=\"--with-openssl-dir=$(brew --prefix openssl@3.5)\" mise install\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c8", "author": "VickTor61", "timestamp": "2025-10-19T15:16:01Z", "body": "> i ran into this issue after upgrading to Tahoe. Using [@gwicks](https://github.com/gwicks) solution, I fixed my mise based install:\n> \n> ```\n> MISE_RUBY_BUILD_OPTS=\"--with-openssl-dir=$(brew --prefix openssl@3.5)\" mise install\n> ```\n\nI tried same using rvm but still same problem. Mind sharing your ruby and rails versions?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c9", "author": "johnnagro", "timestamp": "2025-10-19T22:17:34Z", "body": "> I tried same using rvm but still same problem. Mind sharing your ruby and rails versions?\n\n@VickTor61 unfortunately i don't have a system with rvm running so I can't say for certain what will fix it, however if you look in the docs for the \"Configure flags\" section of installing rubies, it looks like you'd use a -C flag to pass config options. see if this works:\n\n```\nrvm install 3.4.5 -C --with-openssl-dir=$(brew --prefix openssl@3.5)\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c10", "author": "VickTor61", "timestamp": "2025-10-21T07:56:14Z", "body": "> > I tried same using rvm but still same problem. Mind sharing your ruby and rails versions?\n> \n> [@VickTor61](https://github.com/VickTor61) unfortunately i don't have a system with rvm running so I can't say for certain what will fix it, however if you look in the docs for the \"Configure flags\" section of installing rubies, it looks like you'd use a -C flag to pass config options. see if this works:\n> \n> ```\n> rvm install 3.4.5 -C --with-openssl-dir=$(brew --prefix openssl@3.5)\n> ```\n\nThank you for your response. I did this but i still get a crash error in my server logs. Here is the crash report: \n\n[crashreport.txt](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/23015986/crashreport.txt)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c11", "author": "pioz", "timestamp": "2025-10-23T12:07:44Z", "body": "I had the same issue with OpenSSL 3.6 on OSX. After switching back to OpenSSL 3.5, everything started working again. \n\nAdding the openssl gem to my `Gemfile` didn't fix the problem.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c12", "author": "opoudjis", "timestamp": "2025-10-27T01:30:37Z", "body": "Switching back to OpenSSL 3.5 did NOT fix my problem, including openssl in Gemfile did.\n\nDismissing this as a third party issue is not resolving it.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c13", "author": "lawrencepit", "timestamp": "2025-10-29T02:27:57Z", "body": "This is an openssl issue:\n\nhttps://github.com/ruby/openssl/issues/949\n\n> OpenSSL 3.6.0 appears to have changed the behavior in commit https://github.com/openssl/openssl/commit/b1b4b154fd389ac6254d49cfb11aee36c1c51b84, which doesn't look intentional\n\nThis solved it for me:\n  * https://github.com/ruby/openssl/issues/949#issuecomment-3367944960\n\nFix is already committed:\n  * https://github.com/ruby/openssl/pull/950", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c14", "author": "lcmen", "timestamp": "2025-11-18T20:11:32Z", "body": "> Switching back to OpenSSL 3.5 did NOT fix my problem, including openssl in Gemfile did.\n> \n> Dismissing this as a third party issue is not resolving it.\n\nIf you are using Homebrew, you need to run `brew link openssl` after installing `openssl@3.5`. I believe Homebrew does not link the keg-only (alternate versions of formula) packages.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c15", "author": "pjg", "timestamp": "2025-11-19T13:06:05Z", "body": "What fixed this problem for me was recompiling/reinstalling ruby with provided path to brew's openssl (` --with-openssl-dir=$(brew --prefix openssl@3)`) **and** then adding the `openssl` gem to the Gemfile.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c16", "author": "jeffkamo", "timestamp": "2026-01-06T07:24:03Z", "body": "I arrived at this problem and initially ignored the solution/workaround because the ticket was titled as being for MacOS. So for anyone else on Omarchy running into the same problem, the suggestion to install a newer version of `openssl` (like `3.3.2`) also worked for me.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "rails/rails/issue/55886#1", "question": "On macOS (Apple Silicon/M2) with Rails 8.0.2, Ruby 3.4.1 installed via rbenv against Homebrew OpenSSL@3/OpenSSL 3.6, HTTPS requests fail certificate verification due to a CRL check. For example, after installing with:\n\n```bash\nbrew install openssl@3 ca-certificates rbenv ruby-build\nRUBY_CONFIGURE_OPTS=\"--with-openssl-dir=$(brew --prefix openssl@3)\" rbenv install 3.4.1\nrbenv global 3.4.1\n```\n\nand running in IRB:\n\n```bash\nrequire 'net/http'\nNet::HTTP.get(URI('https://aws.amazon.com'))\n```\n\nI get:\n\n```bash\nOpenSSL::SSL::SSLError:\n  SSL_connect returned=1 errno=0 peeraddr=1xx.1xx.6x.1xx:443 state=error:\n  certificate verify failed (unable to get certificate CRL)\n```\n\nor:\n\n```bash\nSSL_connect ... certificate verify failed (unable to get certificate CRL)\n```\n\nThe same kind of failure happens for HTTPS requests through Net::HTTP, ActiveStorage::Service::S3Service, or the AWS SDK. Expected behavior is that Ruby/Rails HTTPS requests complete successfully without manual CRL configuration. Actual behavior appears to be that OpenSSL\u2019s default verification flags include `V_FLAG_CRL_CHECK` and `V_FLAG_CRL_CHECK_ALL`, but macOS\u2019s OpenSSL build does not provide a CRL bundle, so verification fails even though the certificate chain is valid.\n\nA workaround that disables CRL checking is:\n\n```bash\n# config/initializers/ssl_fix.rb\nrequire 'openssl'\nOpenSSL::SSL::SSLContext::DEFAULT_PARAMS[:verify_mode] = OpenSSL::SSL::VERIFY_PEER\nOpenSSL::SSL::SSLContext::DEFAULT_PARAMS[:verify_flags] &=\n  ~(OpenSSL::X509::V_FLAG_CRL_CHECK_ALL | OpenSSL::X509::V_FLAG_CRL_CHECK)\n```\n\nWhat is the cause of this issue and what is the appropriate fix or workaround?", "answer": "This is an openssl issue:\n\nhttps://github.com/ruby/openssl/issues/949\n\n> OpenSSL 3.6.0 appears to have changed the behavior in commit https://github.com/openssl/openssl/commit/b1b4b154fd389ac6254d49cfb11aee36c1c51b84, which doesn't look intentional\n\nThis solved it for me:\n  * https://github.com/ruby/openssl/issues/949#issuecomment-3367944960\n\nFix is already committed:\n  * https://github.com/ruby/openssl/pull/950", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/ruby/openssl/issues/949", "https://github.com/openssl/openssl/commit/b1b4b154fd389ac6254d49cfb11aee36c1c51b84", "https://github.com/ruby/openssl/issues/949#issuecomment-3367944960", "https://github.com/ruby/openssl/pull/950"], "answer_grounded_in": "c13", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["b1b4b154fd389ac6254d49cfb11aee36c1c51b84", "https://github.com/openssl/openssl/commit/b1b4b154fd389ac6254d49cfb11aee36c1c51b84", "https://github.com/ruby/openssl/issues/949", "https://github.com/ruby/openssl/issues/949#issuecomment-3367944960", "https://github.com/ruby/openssl/pull/950"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T18:17:37.475381Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "rails/rails/issue/55866", "repo": "rails/rails", "number": 55866, "title": "Migrations that alter SQLite table structure can cause data loss", "url": "https://github.com/rails/rails/issues/55866", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-10-08T02:04:24Z", "closed_at": "2025-10-17T06:59:48Z", "labels": ["activerecord", "SQLite"], "reporter": "skipkayhil", "security_topic": "proper handling of SQLite foreign key enforcement during migrations to prevent data loss", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know the correct sequence for disabling and re\u2011enabling SQLite foreign key enforcement during migrations to prevent data loss, as documented in the SQLite PRAGMA foreign_keys and foreign_key_check specifications (see SQLite docs and the related Rails commit).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "skipkayhil", "answer_author": "ruyrocha", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation", "code"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.7124999999999999, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "skipkayhil", "timestamp": "2025-10-08T02:04:24Z", "body": "[A mere add_foreign_key can wipe out your whole Rails+SQLite production table](https://kyrylo.org/software/2025/09/27/a-mere-add-foreign-key-can-wipe-out-your-whole-rails-sqlite-production-table.html)\n\nWe likely should be disabling and re-enabling the `foreign_keys` PRAGMA before/after the transaction to change the table per the [sqlite docs](https://www.sqlite.org/lang_altertable.html#otheralter).\n\nEdit: actually it looks like we already do that since https://github.com/rails/rails/commit/45881b0a64b4039e3b0d27e2c4d959dc2d744b9d\n\nEdit2: Ah, this is complex. We currently do\n\n```\ntransaction do\n  disable_referential_integrity do\n```\n\nbut it should be\n\n```\ndisable_pragma_foreign_keys do\n  transaction do\n    enable_defer_foreign_keys do\n```\n\nbecause `PRAGMA foreign_keys`\n\n> is a no-op within a transaction; foreign key constraint enforcement may only be enabled or disabled when there is no pending [BEGIN](https://www.sqlite.org/lang_transaction.html) or [SAVEPOINT](https://www.sqlite.org/lang_savepoint.html).\n\nper [doc](https://www.sqlite.org/pragma.html#pragma_foreign_keys), and \"`PRAGMA foreign_keys` before `TRANSACTION`\" is the order recommended in the first sqlite doc link above. However, `PRAGMA defer_foreign_keys` is [set _per transaction_](https://www.sqlite.org/pragma.html#pragma_defer_foreign_keys) so it has to be inside the transaction block.", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "ruyrocha", "timestamp": "2025-10-15T16:59:24Z", "body": "The fix is ready for reviews. It disables foreign key enforcement before the table recreation process, and re-enables it afterwards. Since `PRAGMA foreign_keys` cannot be modified inside a transaction in SQLite, the operations are sequenced as:\n\n1. Disable foreign keys (outside transaction)\n2. Perform table recreation (inside transaction for atomicity)\n3. Re-enable foreign keys\n4. Validate schema integrity with `PRAGMA foreign_key_check`\n", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "rails/rails/issue/55866#1", "question": "In Rails' SQLite adapter, migrations that alter table structure (for example, a mere `add_foreign_key`) can wipe out a production SQLite table, as described in https://kyrylo.org/software/2025/09/27/a-mere-add-foreign-key-can-wipe-out-your-whole-rails-sqlite-production-table.html. Rails appears to already disable and re-enable the `foreign_keys` PRAGMA around table changes since https://github.com/rails/rails/commit/45881b0a64b4039e3b0d27e2c4d959dc2d744b9d, but the current ordering seems to be:\n\n```\ntransaction do\n  disable_referential_integrity do\n```\n\nI think it should instead be:\n\n```\ndisable_pragma_foreign_keys do\n  transaction do\n    enable_defer_foreign_keys do\n```\n\nbecause SQLite documents that `PRAGMA foreign_keys` \u201cis a no-op within a transaction; foreign key constraint enforcement may only be enabled or disabled when there is no pending BEGIN or SAVEPOINT,\u201d while `PRAGMA defer_foreign_keys` is set per transaction and therefore has to be inside the transaction block. Should Rails change the SQLite table-recreation flow to disable `PRAGMA foreign_keys` outside the transaction, perform the change transactionally, and use deferred foreign keys inside the transaction?", "answer": "The maintainer said the fix is ready for review. It disables foreign key enforcement before the table recreation process and re-enables it afterwards, with the sequence: disable foreign keys outside the transaction, perform table recreation inside a transaction, re-enable foreign keys, and validate schema integrity with `PRAGMA foreign_key_check`.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["SQLite PRAGMA foreign_keys documentation", "SQLite PRAGMA defer_foreign_keys documentation", "Rails commit 45881b0a64b4039e3b0d27e2c4d959dc2d744b9d"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": [], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-16T03:30:01.739820Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "rails/rails/issue/55508", "repo": "rails/rails", "number": 55508, "title": "ActiveRecord::Middleware::DatabaseSelector sets cookies on asset requests", "url": "https://github.com/rails/rails/issues/55508", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-08-17T17:53:16Z", "closed_at": "2025-09-05T08:52:32Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "EmilioCristalli", "security_topic": "asset requests setting cookies unexpectedly", "qa_summary": "The developer asked whether asset requests should set a session cookie, and the maintainer replied that it\u2019s a bug in Propshaft, pointing to the Propshaft railtie file at commit e49a9de659ff27462015e54dd832e86e762a6ddc as evidence.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "EmilioCristalli", "answer_author": "byroot", "answerer_role": "commenter", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "commit_history", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": ["e49a9de659ff27462015e54dd832e86e762a6ddc"], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "EmilioCristalli", "timestamp": "2025-08-17T17:53:16Z", "body": "Not sure if this is a bug, but noticed that when using the automatic DB switching ([docs](https://guides.rubyonrails.org/active_record_multiple_databases.html#activating-automatic-role-switching)), with the default resolver `ActiveRecord::Middleware::DatabaseSelector::Resolver` and context `ActiveRecord::Middleware::DatabaseSelector::Resolver::Session`, it seems like requests for assets are getting a cookie sent back in the response.\n\nNoticed this while investigating issues with system tests not rendering flash messages correctly, and it looks like it was happening because of race conditions between redirect requests and asset requests, causing it to override the cookie and remove the flash message. This might be a different issue on our side (that we are not properly waiting for asset requests to finish before submitting a form or something like that), but it made me wonder if it's correct for asset requests to respond with a cookie.\n\nI don't know too much about the session and cookie management, but from what I could debug, it seems like this is happening because the `ActiveRecord::Middleware::DatabaseSelector::Resolver::Session` middleware reads `request.session[:last_write]`, so it's like it makes it load the session, and maybe this makes `ActionDispatch::Session::CookieStore` send the cookie back in the response?\n\n### Steps to reproduce\nCreated [this repo](https://github.com/EmilioCristalli/test_db_selector) to reproduce the issue. Has a few commits with the steps to create the app, but it's a normal Rails app with scaffold for one model (Post). \n\nThe last commit in main has the automatic db switching enabled, but moving one commit back (to `9232d6768d`) should disable it to see the difference.\n\nCan open the root page (which loads posts#index) and observe any asset requests (like http://localhost:3000/assets/application-bfcdf840.js) in the developer tools to see the `Set-Cookie` header in the response.\n\n### Expected behavior\nThe asset requests shouldn't have a `set-cookie` header in the response.\n\n(not 100% sure this is the expected behavior, but it looks like it's what happens when automatic DB switching is not enabled)\n\n### Actual behavior\nAsset requests have a `set-cookie` header in the response.\n\n### System configuration\n\n**Rails version**: 8.0.2.1\n**Ruby version**: 3.4.5", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "byroot", "timestamp": "2025-08-19T07:43:18Z", "body": "This IMO is a bug in propshaft. It's mounted inside the Rails routes: https://github.com/rails/propshaft/blob/e49a9de659ff27462015e54dd832e86e762a6ddc/lib/propshaft/railtie.rb#L48-L50\n\nSo it's invoked after all rack middlewares.\n\nInstead, it should probably be early in the rack middleware chain, likely right after `ActionDispatch::Static`.\n\nNow the good news is that this is a development only setup (should be fixed regardless).\n\n", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "rails/rails/issue/55508#1", "question": "In a Rails app using automatic DB switching (docs: https://guides.rubyonrails.org/active_record_multiple_databases.html#activating-automatic-role-switching) with the default resolver `ActiveRecord::Middleware::DatabaseSelector::Resolver` and context `ActiveRecord::Middleware::DatabaseSelector::Resolver::Session`, asset requests appear to get a `Set-Cookie` header in the response. I noticed this while investigating system tests not rendering flash messages correctly; it looked like race conditions between redirect requests and asset requests were overriding the cookie and removing the flash message. From debugging, it seems this happens because `ActiveRecord::Middleware::DatabaseSelector::Resolver::Session` reads `request.session[:last_write]`, causing the session to load, and perhaps `ActionDispatch::Session::CookieStore` then sends the cookie back in the response.\n\nSteps to reproduce: I created this repo: https://github.com/EmilioCristalli/test_db_selector. It is a normal Rails app with a scaffold for one model (`Post`). The last commit on `main` has automatic DB switching enabled; moving one commit back to `9232d6768d` disables it. Open the root page (`posts#index`) and inspect asset requests such as `http://localhost:3000/assets/application-bfcdf840.js` in developer tools; with automatic DB switching enabled they include `Set-Cookie`.\n\nExpected behavior: asset requests should not have a `set-cookie` header in the response, which is what appears to happen when automatic DB switching is not enabled.\nActual behavior: asset requests have a `set-cookie` header in the response.\nSystem configuration: Rails 8.0.2.1, Ruby 3.4.5.\n\nIs this a Rails/automatic DB switching bug, and is it expected that asset requests respond with a cookie in this setup?", "answer": "The maintainer thinks this is possibly a bug in propshaft. It's mounted inside the Rails routes: https://github.com/rails/propshaft/blob/e49a9de659ff27462015e54dd832e86e762a6ddc/lib/propshaft/railtie.rb#L48-L50\n\nSo it's invoked after all rack middlewares.\n\nInstead, it should probably be early in the rack middleware chain, likely right after `ActionDispatch::Static`.\n\nNow the good news is that this is a development only setup (should be fixed regardless).", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/rails/propshaft/blob/e49a9de659ff27462015e54dd832e86e762a6ddc/lib/propshaft/railtie.rb#L48-L50", "Propshaft middleware ordering relative to Rails routes/Rack middleware", "ActionDispatch::Static"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["e49a9de659ff27462015e54dd832e86e762a6ddc", "https://github.com/rails/propshaft/blob/e49a9de659ff27462015e54dd832e86e762a6ddc/lib/propshaft/railtie.rb#L48-L50"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T18:27:59.527541Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "rails/rails/issue/55245", "repo": "rails/rails", "number": 55245, "title": "Handle links and other safe content inside translations", "url": "https://github.com/rails/rails/issues/55245", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-06-26T06:50:35Z", "closed_at": "2025-11-03T19:04:05Z", "labels": ["stale"], "reporter": "runephilosof-abtion", "security_topic": "safe interpolation of HTML in i18n translations to prevent XSS", "qa_summary": "The developer needed a secure method to interpolate HTML\u2011safe strings into I18n translations without introducing XSS, and the answer provides a concrete implementation in a Gist (https://gist.github.com/henrik/f6619f298de3b6f59dfba589fa5476d7).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c5", "question_author": "runephilosof-abtion", "answer_author": "henrik", "answerer_role": "contributor", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": ["f6619f298de3b6f59dfba589fa5476d7"], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Addresses XSS risk in i18n interpolation via SafeBuffer-aware escaping and safe HTML output handling", "llm_confidence": 0.7124999999999999, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "runephilosof-abtion", "timestamp": "2025-06-26T06:50:35Z", "body": "Currently, if I want to put a link into a translation, I have to do\n```erb\n<%= t(\".text\", insert: link_to(t(\".insert\"), root_path)).html_safe %>\n```\nor\n```erb\n<%= t(\".text_html\", insert: link_to(t(\".insert\"), root_path)) %>\n```\n\nHowever, the `text_html` translation should not have to know whether the `insert` contains html or not.\n~Also, the `insert` translation cannot have unescaped html content, because it is used as content inside another `_html` translation.~\n\nThe rails i18n guide does not mention how to do this nicely.\n\nThe best would be if rails-i18n could handle it automatically by being aware of SafeBuffer.\n\n```yml\ntext: some <> text with an %{insert}\ntext_html: some &lt;&gt; text with an %{insert}\ninsert: a link, properly escaping <>\n```\n\n```erb\n<%= t(\".text\", insert: link_to(t(\".insert\"), root_path)) %>\n```\n\nRails-i18n should handle safe joining the above example.\n\n`link_to` returns a `html_safe` string, so this should output \n```html\nsome &lt;&gt; text with an <a href=\"/\">a link, properly escaping &lt;&gt;</a>\n```\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "runephilosof-abtion", "timestamp": "2025-06-26T06:51:42Z", "body": "Related issue in https://github.com/svenfuchs/rails-i18n/issues/1147, which was closed with a suggestion of implementing it in Rails.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "dari-us", "timestamp": "2025-06-27T19:31:21Z", "body": "I'm not sure I understand the use case for this.\nWhat prevents you from putting the `<a>` directly into the translation YAML file?\n\n```\ntext_html: 'Some &gt;&lt; text with a <a href=\"/\">link, properly escaping &gt;&lt;</a>'\n```\n\nYes, you have to escape the content of the `<a>` yourself in this case, but isn't it static anyway?\n\nIf you really need this grade of freedom for the `insert`, the second option seems totally fine to me.\nHowever, the first one calling `t` with the non-HTML I18n key and then making it `html_safe` is super ugly.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "runephilosof-abtion", "timestamp": "2025-06-30T06:56:44Z", "body": "Aside from `text_html: 'Some &gt;&lt; text with a <a href=\"/\">link, properly escaping &gt;&lt;</a>'` being super ugly and unreadable, you should think of it as a simplified example of a more generic problem. Maybe the contents of `.insert` is conditional.\n\n1. A translation should not need to know whether its variables contain html.\n2. You might consider translations to be untrusted user input, except `_html` ones, which would be more expensive to maintain.\n3. Rails already has the concept of SafeBuffer, so why throw that information away when translating.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "runephilosof-abtion", "timestamp": "2025-07-03T09:05:33Z", "body": "It would need to be changed in https://github.com/rails/rails/blob/36601bbb02bc3570f5609db2127c77afca575d6c/activesupport/lib/active_support/html_safe_translation.rb and https://github.com/rails/rails/blob/main/actionpack/lib/abstract_controller/translation.rb#L28.\nAlso in the relevant tests: https://github.com/rails/rails/blob/3235827585d87661942c91bc81f64f56d710f0b2/actionpack/test/abstract/translation_test.rb#L88, https://github.com/rails/rails/blob/3235827585d87661942c91bc81f64f56d710f0b2/actionview/test/template/translation_helper_test.rb#L108, https://github.com/rails/rails/blob/3235827585d87661942c91bc81f64f56d710f0b2/actionview/test/template/translation_helper_test.rb#L224\n\nIt is easy to not escape the interpolation and default options when they are already html_safe.\n\nIt would be nice to also handle interpolating html safe \"strings\" into non html safe keys, but that seems impossible without changing I18n.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "henrik", "timestamp": "2025-07-07T20:18:15Z", "body": "Well, that's timely. I was just bothered by the same thing and implemented a version, then found this issue when googling to see if I'd missed something.\n\nMy version here: https://gist.github.com/henrik/f6619f298de3b6f59dfba589fa5476d7\n\nI'd love for something like this to be part of Rails or rails-i18n.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "runephilosof-abtion", "timestamp": "2025-07-08T06:25:40Z", "body": "@henrik Nice way of handling what I deemed impossible without changing I18n. Your solution is simple and seems secure.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "MatheusRich", "timestamp": "2025-07-29T16:13:45Z", "body": "@henrik I encourage you to submit a PR. Even if the API isn't perfect, that can spark the dicussion.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c8", "author": "henrik", "timestamp": "2025-07-29T17:22:27Z", "body": "Yeah, maybe. Will use this for a while in our apps and see how it feels. If anyone else wants to make a PR version, feel free!", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c9", "author": "github-actions", "timestamp": "2025-10-27T18:06:29Z", "body": "This issue has been automatically marked as stale because it has not been commented on for at least three months.\nThe resources of the Rails team are limited, and so we are asking for your help.\nIf you can still reproduce this error on the `8-1-stable` branch or on `main`, please reply with all of the information you have about it in order to keep the issue open.\nThank you for all your contributions.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "rails/rails/issue/55245#1", "question": "In Rails translations, I want to interpolate safe HTML such as a `link_to` result without forcing the translation key itself to know whether the interpolation contains HTML. Currently I have to do either:\n\n```erb\n<%= t(\".text\", insert: link_to(t(\".insert\"), root_path)).html_safe %>\n```\n\nor:\n\n```erb\n<%= t(\".text_html\", insert: link_to(t(\".insert\"), root_path)) %>\n```\n\nBut the `text_html` translation should not have to know whether `insert` contains HTML, and the Rails i18n guide does not explain a nice way to do this. Ideally Rails i18n would be aware of `SafeBuffer` and safely join content like this:\n\n```yml\ntext: some <> text with an %{insert}\ntext_html: some &lt;&gt; text with an %{insert}\ninsert: a link, properly escaping <>\n```\n\n```erb\n<%= t(\".text\", insert: link_to(t(\".insert\"), root_path)) %>\n```\n\nSince `link_to` returns an `html_safe` string, the desired output would be:\n\n```html\nsome &lt;&gt; text with an <a href=\"/\">a link, properly escaping &lt;&gt;</a>\n```\n\nIs there a secure/simple way for Rails or rails-i18n to handle links and other safe content inside translations automatically using `SafeBuffer`, rather than requiring manual `html_safe` or `_html` keys for this case?", "answer": "A community member said they had implemented a version of this behavior and shared it as a gist: https://gist.github.com/henrik/f6619f298de3b6f59dfba589fa5476d7. They said they would like something like that to become part of Rails or rails-i18n.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://gist.github.com/henrik/f6619f298de3b6f59dfba589fa5476d7"], "answer_grounded_in": "c5", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://gist.github.com/henrik/f6619f298de3b6f59dfba589fa5476d7"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T18:31:07.925040Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "rails/rails/issue/55185", "repo": "rails/rails", "number": 55185, "title": "ActiveRecord call `.lease_connection` inside `.transaction` cause connection leak", "url": "https://github.com/rails/rails/issues/55185", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-06-11T14:24:26Z", "closed_at": "2025-06-12T06:45:00Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "tevvi", "security_topic": "connection leak causing resource exhaustion in ActiveRecord transactions", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know how to prevent the ActiveRecord connection leak (a potential availability/security risk) when using lease_connection inside a transaction, and the answer cites the Rails API docs and PR\u202f#51192, recommending calling release_connection or using with_connection(prevent_permanent_checkout: true).", "question_comment_id": "c2", "answer_comment_id": "c3", "question_author": "tevvi", "answer_author": "skipkayhil", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation", "code", "pr_data"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": ["#51192"], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "ActiveRecord connection leaks are a resource exhaustion vulnerability affecting application availability, and the answer provides correct mitigation patterns", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "tevvi", "timestamp": "2025-06-11T14:24:26Z", "body": "### Steps to reproduce\n1. rails c\n2. run code:\n```ruby\nputs \"Before: #{ActiveRecord::Base.connection_pool.active_connection?}\"\nActiveRecord::Base.transaction do\n  ActiveRecord::Base.lease_connection\nend\nputs \"After: #{ActiveRecord::Base.connection_pool.active_connection?}\"\n```\n### Expected behavior\n```\nBefore:\nAfter: \n```\n\n### Actual behavior\n```\nBefore:\nAfter: #<ActiveRecord::ConnectionAdapters::Mysql2Adapter>\n```\n\n#### Explanation\nWhen .lease_connection called in .transaction, connection is not released when exiting from .with_connection. So connection is leaked(or stuck) to current thread\n\n#### Why it is a problem\nOur application send mixed db queries with http requests, which much slower than db queries so. And we faced with connection limit on mysql server side. So we have to release(return back to process pool) all obtained db connections before start execute http requests\n\n#### When we faced with it\n1. Our legacy have dirty call db queries(.connection.execute) inside transaction, but nested in several methods. So refactor(to forward connection from .transaction yield) is complicated\n2. When we added after_commit_everywhere 1.6.0 to application(rails 7.2.2.1 only) to use Sidekiq.transactional_push!. And following code cause connection leak:\n```ruby\nclass MyWorker\n  include Sidekiq::Worker\n  sidekiq_options queue: :default\n\n  def perform; end\nend\n\nputs \"Before: #{ActiveRecord::Base.connection_pool.active_connection?}\"\nActiveRecord::Base.transaction do\n  MyWorker.perform_async\nend\nputs \"After: #{ActiveRecord::Base.connection_pool.active_connection?}\"\n```\n\nConnection leak because next trace:\nhttps://github.com/sidekiq/sidekiq/blob/v6.5.12/lib/sidekiq/transaction_aware_client.rb#L16\nhttps://github.com/Envek/after_commit_everywhere/blob/v1.6.0/lib/after_commit_everywhere.rb#L100\nhttps://github.com/Envek/after_commit_everywhere/blob/v1.6.0/lib/after_commit_everywhere.rb#L111\nhttps://github.com/Envek/after_commit_everywhere/blob/v1.6.0/lib/after_commit_everywhere.rb#L144\nhttps://github.com/Envek/after_commit_everywhere/blob/v1.6.0/lib/after_commit_everywhere.rb#L172\n\n### System configuration\nrails 7.2.2.1 with ruby 3.3.2\nrails 8.0.2 with ruby 3.4.4\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "skipkayhil", "timestamp": "2025-06-11T14:35:46Z", "body": "Isn't this by design? The API docs say\n\n> The connection will remain leased for the entire duration of the request or job, or until [release_connection](https://api.rubyonrails.org/classes/ActiveRecord/ConnectionHandling.html#method-i-release_connection) is called.\n\nSo if you want the connection returned to the pool you must use `release_connection` after `lease_connection`", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "tevvi", "timestamp": "2025-06-11T14:49:13Z", "body": "@skipkayhil So how i can easily receive connection without this behaviour in complicated, nested code? \nFor example:\n```ruby\nActiveRecord::Base.transaction do\n  do_call\nend\n\ndef do_call\n  # ActiveRecord::Base.connection or something else that will release connection after #transaction block\nend\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "skipkayhil", "timestamp": "2025-06-11T14:57:18Z", "body": "```ruby\n# call `release_connection` when you're done with a connection\nActiveRecord::Base.transaction do\n  do_call\nend\nActiveRecore::Base.release_connection\n\n# or only checkout connections where you need them (nesting with_connection always gives you the same connection)\ndef do_call\n  Base.with_connection do |c|\n  end\nend\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "tevvi", "timestamp": "2025-06-11T15:13:13Z", "body": "@skipkayhil So next step to rewrite all code(application and gems) which use ActiveRecore::Base.lease_connection. I think it is a complicated solution\n\nEasier solution is return back previous logic. Add method sticky_connection with new behaviour(The connection will remain leased for the entire duration of the request or job, or until release_connection is called.) and rewrite lease_connection to release connection after with_connection block", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "skipkayhil", "timestamp": "2025-06-11T15:15:02Z", "body": "> Add method sticky_connection with new behaviour(The connection will remain leased for the entire duration of the request or job, or until release_connection is called.)\n\nThis is how `ActiveRecord::Base.connection` has always worked (1 connection per request/job). `with_connection`/`release_connection` _is_ the new behavior that allows you to not have a single connection checked out for a whole request/job.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "byroot", "timestamp": "2025-06-12T06:45:00Z", "body": "What @skipkayhil said, this was done by design to not break code that had expectations about the connection being sticky: https://github.com/rails/rails/pull/51192\n\nYou can manually call `release_connection` after, or wrap the whole thing in `.with_connection(prevent_permanent_checkout: true)`.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "rails/rails/issue/55185#1", "question": "In Rails ActiveRecord, calling `lease_connection` inside a transaction appears to leave a connection leased to the current thread after the transaction. Reproduction in `rails c`:\n```ruby\nputs \"Before: #{ActiveRecord::Base.connection_pool.active_connection?}\"\nActiveRecord::Base.transaction do\n  ActiveRecord::Base.lease_connection\nend\nputs \"After: #{ActiveRecord::Base.connection_pool.active_connection?}\"\n```\nExpected:\n```ruby\nBefore:\nAfter: \n```\nActual:\n```ruby\nBefore:\nAfter: #<ActiveRecord::ConnectionAdapters::Mysql2Adapter>\n```\nThis matters because our application mixes DB queries with slower HTTP requests and can hit MySQL connection limits; we need to return obtained DB connections to the pool before HTTP requests. We also saw it after adding `after_commit_everywhere` 1.6.0 to a Rails 7.2.2.1 app for `Sidekiq.transactional_push!`, where this leaks:\n```ruby\nclass MyWorker\n  include Sidekiq::Worker\n  sidekiq_options queue: :default\n\n  def perform; end\nend\n\nputs \"Before: #{ActiveRecord::Base.connection_pool.active_connection?}\"\nActiveRecord::Base.transaction do\n  MyWorker.perform_async\nend\nputs \"After: #{ActiveRecord::Base.connection_pool.active_connection?}\"\n```\nThe trace goes through:\nhttps://github.com/sidekiq/sidekiq/blob/v6.5.12/lib/sidekiq/transaction_aware_client.rb#L16\nhttps://github.com/Envek/after_commit_everywhere/blob/v1.6.0/lib/after_commit_everywhere.rb#L100\nhttps://github.com/Envek/after_commit_everywhere/blob/v1.6.0/lib/after_commit_everywhere.rb#L111\nhttps://github.com/Envek/after_commit_everywhere/blob/v1.6.0/lib/after_commit_everywhere.rb#L144\nhttps://github.com/Envek/after_commit_everywhere/blob/v1.6.0/lib/after_commit_everywhere.rb#L172\n\nSystem configuration: Rails 7.2.2.1 with Ruby 3.3.2, and Rails 8.0.2 with Ruby 3.4.4.\n\nIf `lease_connection` is designed to keep the connection leased until `release_connection`, how can I easily receive a connection without this sticky behavior in complicated nested code, e.g.:\n```ruby\nActiveRecord::Base.transaction do\n  do_call\nend\n\ndef do_call\n  # ActiveRecord::Base.connection or something else that will release connection after #transaction block\nend\n```", "answer": "Call `release_connection` when you are done with a leased connection, or only check out connections where needed using `with_connection`; nested `with_connection` gives you the same connection. The sticky behavior was by design to avoid breaking code expecting sticky connections, per PR #51192. You can also wrap the whole thing in `.with_connection(prevent_permanent_checkout: true)`.\n\n```ruby\n# call `release_connection` when you're done with a connection\nActiveRecord::Base.transaction do\n  do_call\nend\nActiveRecord::Base.release_connection\n\n# or only checkout connections where you need them (nesting with_connection always gives you the same connection)\ndef do_call\n  Base.with_connection do |c|\n  end\nend\n```", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["Rails PR #51192", "ActiveRecord `release_connection`", "ActiveRecord `with_connection(prevent_permanent_checkout: true)`"], "answer_grounded_in": "c3, c6", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": [], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T18:49:15.218367Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1490", "repo": "stripe/stripe-node", "number": 1490, "title": "cannot create bank object  with bank details", "url": "https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/1490", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2022-07-26T16:39:04Z", "closed_at": "2022-07-26T16:49:27Z", "labels": ["bug"], "reporter": "rubiin", "security_topic": "secure handling of bank account details client-side vs server-side", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know if passing raw bank account details server\u2011side is secure and should instead collect them client\u2011side, and the maintainer answered that it is dangerous and pointed to the Stripe ACH Debit documentation (https://stripe.com/docs/payments/ach-debit) as the concrete source.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "rubiin", "answer_author": "remi-stripe", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": ["https://stripe.com/docs/payments/ach-debit"]}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "rubiin", "timestamp": "2022-07-26T16:39:04Z", "body": "### Describe the bug\r\n\r\nI am trying to create a bank object with the createSource method by giving key value pairs instead of string.\r\nHowever I am getting typing issue . Also the documentation is vague if we were to pass in bank details instead of source id\r\n\r\n### To Reproduce\r\n\r\n 1. Create a customer\r\n 2. Craete a bank object with bank details on the customer\r\n\r\n### Expected behavior\r\n\r\nThe method should create bank object with bank properties passed\r\n\r\n### Code snippets\r\n\r\n```JavaScript\r\ncustomers.createSource(\r\n  \"cus_...\",\r\n  { source: {\r\n    object: \"bank_account\",\r\n    account_number: \"110000000\",\r\n    routing_number: \"000123456789\",\r\n    country: \"US\",\r\n    currency: \"usd\",\r\n  },\r\n```\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n### OS\r\n\r\nLinux\r\n\r\n### Node version\r\n\r\n16.15\r\n\r\n### Library version\r\n\r\nlatest\r\n\r\n### API version\r\n\r\n2020-08-27\r\n\r\n### Additional context\r\n\r\n_No response_", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "remi-stripe", "timestamp": "2022-07-26T16:49:27Z", "body": "First off, I would strongly recommend using the PaymentMethod API instead as we rebuilt our ACH Debit integration entirely. Please carefully read https://stripe.com/docs/payments/ach-debit and related docs which go over how to do this in details. It also encourages you to collect bank account details **client-side** securely instead of having access to those server-side which is dangerous and increases your security burden (and PCI compliance burden if you do the same for cards).\r\n\r\nUltimately though the code you have works just fine, it's just old and discouraged. There are no types for this, both because it's deprecated and because we discourage passing raw bank account details server-side. If you want to move forward with that approach, you will have to explicitly suppress the type errors on that code.\r\n\r\nI'm going to close this out as this is more an integration support question and not really a bug with the library. If you have follow up questions, please work with our support team directly: https://support.stripe.com/contact", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1490#1", "question": "In stripe-node on Linux with Node 16.15, latest library version, and Stripe API version 2020-08-27, I am trying to create a customer bank account object using `customers.createSource` by passing bank account details as key/value pairs instead of passing a source ID, but I get a TypeScript typing issue and the documentation is vague about whether bank details can be passed directly. Should this work, and what should I do about the typing issue?\n\nCode:\n```JavaScript\ncustomers.createSource(\n  \"cus_...\",\n  { source: {\n    object: \"bank_account\",\n    account_number: \"110000000\",\n    routing_number: \"000123456789\",\n    country: \"US\",\n    currency: \"usd\",\n  },\n```\n\nExpected behavior: the method should create a bank object with the bank properties passed.", "answer": "Stripe recommends using the PaymentMethod API instead, specifically the ACH Debit integration docs (https://stripe.com/docs/payments/ach-debit), and collecting bank account details client-side because passing raw bank account details server-side is dangerous and increases security/compliance burden. The shown `createSource` approach works, but it is old and discouraged; there are no types for it because it is deprecated and Stripe discourages passing raw bank account details server-side. If you still use that approach, explicitly suppress the type errors.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://stripe.com/docs/payments/ach-debit"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://stripe.com/docs/payments/ach-debit", "https://support.stripe.com/contact"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-18T07:40:07.742517Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1294", "repo": "stripe/stripe-node", "number": 1294, "title": "NextJS - Error: construction of webhook event returned an error: No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload. Are you passing the raw request body you received from Stripe? https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node#webhook-signing", "url": "https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/1294", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2021-11-15T02:38:40Z", "closed_at": "2021-11-15T18:23:18Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "wicka-aus", "security_topic": "Stripe webhook signature verification raw body handling", "qa_summary": "The developer needed guidance on correctly accessing the raw request body to verify Stripe webhook signatures, and the answer referenced a dev.to article (https://dev.to/mahnuh/how-to-access-the-raw-body-of-a-stripe-webhook-request-in-nestjs-41kj) and Stripe's issue #341 as concrete sources.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c3", "question_author": "wicka-aus", "answer_author": "remi-stripe", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": ["#341"], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Security-relevant guidance on correct raw-body handling for webhook signature verification", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "wicka-aus", "timestamp": "2021-11-15T02:38:40Z", "body": "Hi \r\n\r\nI am pulling my hair out trying to pass raw JSON to NextJS 12 endpoint.\r\n\r\nI have tried sooo many solutions and I just cant get it to work.\r\n\r\nPlease help as I have wasted half a day on this and I am at a loss.\r\n\r\nCreate test webhook in stripe and passing in Secret Signing key from webhook test dashboard \"whsec ...\"\r\nI get a signature from the test webhook I trigger in the dashboard but I am constantly getting:\r\n\r\nError: construction of webhook event returned an error: No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload. Are you passing the raw request body you received from Stripe? https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node#webhook-signing\r\n\r\nThe code below is from Reddit, where some had a similar issue.\r\n\r\nI have tried all of these solutions here: [https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/13405](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/13405)\r\n\r\nSeems to be an issue in a lot of forums.\r\n\r\nPlease help.\r\n\r\nExhausted!\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport getRawBody from \"raw-body\";\r\n\r\nconst stripe = require(\"stripe\")(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY);\r\n\r\nconst webhookSecret = process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_ENDPOINT_SECRET;\r\n\r\nexport const config = {\r\n  api: {\r\n    bodyParser: false,\r\n  },\r\n};\r\n\r\nconst webhookHandler = async (req, res) => {\r\n  if (req.method === \"POST\") {\r\n    const rawBody = await getRawBody(req);\r\n\r\n    // Extract Stripe signature from header\r\n    const signature = req.headers[\"stripe-signature\"];\r\n    console.log(rawBody)\r\n    console.log(signature)\r\n    console.log(webhookSecret)\r\n\r\n    let event;\r\n\r\n    // Attempt to reconstruct and validate passed event\r\n    try {\r\n      event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(rawBody, signature, webhookSecret);\r\n    } catch (err) {\r\n      res\r\n        .status(400)\r\n        .send(\r\n          `Error: construction of webhook event returned an error: ${err.message}`,\r\n        );\r\n      return;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    console.log(\"success!\")\r\n\r\n    res.json({ received: true });\r\n  } else {\r\n\r\n    res.setHeader(\"Allow\", \"POST\");\r\n    res.status(405).end(\"Method Not Allowed\");\r\n    \r\n  }\r\n};\r\n\r\nexport default webhookHandler;\r\n\r\n```", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "wicka-aus", "timestamp": "2021-11-15T08:23:27Z", "body": "Also trying code below and having the same issue\r\n\r\nSo I create my webhooks in test mode in the dashboard and click 'Signing Secret' to reveal 'whsec_...' I add this to my env variables.\r\n\r\nI can log this secret, the Stripe signature and I receive the req.body but I also hit the error \"No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload.\"\r\n\r\nPlease help resolve this issue?\r\n\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport { buffer } from 'micro';\r\nimport Cors from 'micro-cors';\r\n\r\nconst stripe = require(\"stripe\")(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY);\r\n\r\nconst webhookSecret = process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_ENDPOINT_SECRET;\r\n\r\n// Stripe requires the raw body to construct the event.\r\nexport const config = {\r\n  api: {\r\n    bodyParser: false,\r\n  },\r\n};\r\n\r\nconst cors = Cors({\r\n  allowMethods: ['POST', 'HEAD'],\r\n});\r\n\r\nconst webhookHandler = async (req, res) => {\r\n  if (req.method === 'POST') {\r\n    const buf = await buffer(req);\r\n    const sig = req.headers['stripe-signature'];\r\n\r\n    let event;\r\n\r\n    try {\r\n      event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(\r\n        buf.toString(),\r\n        sig,\r\n        webhookSecret\r\n      );\r\n    } catch (err) {\r\n      console.log(`\u274c Error message: ${err.message}`);\r\n      res.status(400).send(`Webhook Error: ${err.message}`);\r\n      return;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n\r\n    if (event.type === 'payment_intent.succeeded') {\r\n      const paymentIntent = event.data.object;\r\n      console.log(`\ud83d\udcb0 PaymentIntent status: ${paymentIntent.status}`);\r\n    } else if (event.type === 'payment_intent.payment_failed') {\r\n      const paymentIntent = event.data.object;\r\n      console.log(\r\n        `\u274c Payment failed: ${paymentIntent.last_payment_error?.message}`\r\n      );\r\n    } else if (event.type === 'charge.succeeded') {\r\n      const charge = event.data.object;\r\n      console.log(`\ud83d\udcb5 Charge id: ${charge.id}`);\r\n    } else {\r\n      console.warn(`\ud83e\udd37\u200d\u2640\ufe0f Unhandled event type: ${event.type}`);\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    res.json({ received: true });\r\n  } else {\r\n    res.setHeader('Allow', 'POST');\r\n    res.status(405).end('Method Not Allowed');\r\n  }\r\n};\r\n\r\nexport default cors(webhookHandler);\r\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "TyrisDavidLopez", "timestamp": "2021-11-15T11:44:01Z", "body": "I had the same Issue, until read this article:\r\nhttps://dev.to/mahnuh/how-to-access-the-raw-body-of-a-stripe-webhook-request-in-nestjs-41kj\r\n\r\nthe problem is the express parser.\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "remi-stripe", "timestamp": "2021-11-15T18:23:18Z", "body": "@wicka-aus Adding to the recommendation above, there's also https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/341 which has numerous solutions for this problem that depend on your exact stack/version of frameworks/etc.\r\n\r\nGithub issues should be limited to bugs with the library itself and the issue right now is in your code and how to access the real raw body JSON. Whenever your code/framework/helpers change/touch the body, it will prevent webhook signature from working since we need the same exact payload we used to generate the signature when we want to verify it. I'm going to close this out but you can ask for help to our support team [here](https://support.stripe.com/contact) or on our Discord server for developers [here](https://stripe.com/go/developer-chat)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "wicka-aus", "timestamp": "2021-11-15T23:06:01Z", "body": "Thanks to the Stripe team, who pointed me to some code, this worked for me:\r\n\r\n```\r\nasync function buffer(readable) {\r\n  const chunks = [];\r\n  for await (const chunk of readable) {\r\n    chunks.push(typeof chunk === 'string' ? Buffer.from(chunk) : chunk);\r\n  }\r\n  return Buffer.concat(chunks);\r\n}\r\n\r\n\r\nconst webhookHandler = async (req, res) => {\r\n  if (req.method === 'POST') {\r\n    const buf = await buffer(req);\r\n   .....\r\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "andres-dejesus", "timestamp": "2022-01-31T20:47:25Z", "body": "@wicka-aus So what does the complete webhook file look like? What imports? Thanks.\r\nEdit: Nevermind got it to work was missing\r\n`export const config = {\r\n    api: {\r\n      bodyParser: false,\r\n    },\r\n  };`\r\n  in your example", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "adimaralimuddin", "timestamp": "2022-06-08T08:33:53Z", "body": "i have tried all of your suggestions above, but still nothings works. i'm really disappointed by this.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "adimaralimuddin", "timestamp": "2022-06-08T09:43:23Z", "body": "after scratching my head for a half day, i finally figured it out.\r\n\r\ninstall micro npm i micro\r\nmy code on /project/pages/api/stripe-webhook.js\r\n`import initStripe from \"stripe\";\r\nimport { buffer } from \"micro\";\r\n\r\nexport const config = { api: { bodyParser: false } };\r\n\r\nexport default async function handler(req, res) {\r\nconst stripe = initStripe(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY);\r\nconst signature = req.headers[\"stripe-signature\"];\r\nconst signingSecret =process.env.STRIPE_SIGNING_SECRET;\r\nconst reqBuffer = await buffer(req);\r\n\r\nlet event;\r\n\r\ntry {\r\nevent = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(reqBuffer, signature, signingSecret);\r\n} catch (error) {\r\nconsole.log(error);\r\nreturn res.status(400).send(webhook error: ${error.message});\r\n}\r\n\r\nconsole.log(\"event \", event);\r\n\r\nres.send({ received: true });\r\n}\r\n`", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c8", "author": "hemeda3", "timestamp": "2022-06-12T00:36:50Z", "body": "Thanks all this helped me, want to share with everybody many mistakes I made while testing this for first time : \r\n\r\n1-  disable body-parser\r\n\r\n```\r\nexport const config = {\r\n  api: {\r\n    bodyParser: false,\r\n  },\r\n};\r\n```\r\n2- after using micro.buffer,  pay attention to` rawBody.toString()` !!\r\n ` const rawBody = await buffer(req);`. and   `event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent( ***rawBody.toString()***, .signtaure..., ..secret. )`\r\n3- use correct webhook signing secret ( secret generated by CLI vs secret generated by Dashboard)\r\nin my case I was using this command \r\n```\r\n$ stripe listen --forward-to http://localhost:3000/api1/stripe/hooks > Ready! You are using Stripe API Version [2020-03-02].\r\n````\r\n```\r\n Your -->>> webhook signing secret is whsec_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx (^C to quit)\r\n\r\n```\r\nbut another mistake I made, that I was using `webhook test secret` generated by dashboard not the one generated by `CLI` :(  \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nHere is my code after getting correct secret\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\nimport Stripe from 'stripe';\r\nimport { buffer } from 'micro';\r\nconst stripe = new Stripe(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY);\r\n\r\nexport const config = {\r\n  api: {\r\n    bodyParser: false,\r\n  },\r\n};\r\n\r\nexport default async function handler(req, res) {\r\n  if (req.method === 'POST') {\r\n    let event;\r\n\r\n    try {\r\n      // 1. Retrieve the event by verifying the signature using the raw body and secret\r\n      const rawBody = await buffer(req);\r\n      const signature = req.headers['stripe-signature'];\r\n\r\n      event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(\r\n        rawBody.toString(),\r\n        signature,\r\n        process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET\r\n      );\r\n    } catch (err) {\r\n      console.log(`\u274c Error message: ${err.message}`);\r\n      res.status(400).send(`Webhook Error: ${err.message}`);\r\n      return;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    // Successfully constructed event\r\n    console.log('\u2705 Success:', event.id);\r\n\r\n    // 2. Handle event type (add business logic here)\r\n    if (event.type === 'checkout.session.completed') {\r\n      console.log(`\ud83d\udcb0  Payment received!`);\r\n    } else {\r\n      console.warn(`\ud83e\udd37\u200d\u2640\ufe0f Unhandled event type: ${event.type}`);\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    // 3. Return a response to acknowledge receipt of the event.\r\n    res.json({ received: true });\r\n  } else {\r\n    res.setHeader('Allow', 'POST');\r\n    res.status(405).end('Method Not Allowed');\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n\r\n````", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c9", "author": "Bulletninja", "timestamp": "2022-10-12T03:16:04Z", "body": "Tried all of this and doesn't seem to work. One thing that caught my attention is that @hemeda3 solution passes `rawBody.toString()` whereas:\r\n- https://www.codedaily.io/tutorials/Stripe-Webhook-Verification-with-NextJS\r\n- https://egghead.io/lessons/next-js-subscribe-to-stripe-webhooks-using-next-js-api-routes\r\nThese two use the buffer directly\r\nThis Vercel guide does convert to string: https://vercel.com/guides/getting-started-with-nextjs-typescript-stripe\r\n\r\nNone of them seem to work for me.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c10", "author": "AAlon", "timestamp": "2022-11-07T10:29:26Z", "body": "> @wicka-aus Adding to the recommendation above, there's also #341 which has numerous solutions for this problem that depend on your exact stack/version of frameworks/etc.\r\n> \r\n> Github issues should be limited to bugs with the library itself and the issue right now is in your code and how to access the real raw body JSON. Whenever your code/framework/helpers change/touch the body, it will prevent webhook signature from working since we need the same exact payload we used to generate the signature when we want to verify it. I'm going to close this out but you can ask for help to our support team [here](https://support.stripe.com/contact) or on our Discord server for developers [here](https://stripe.com/go/developer-chat)\r\n\r\nWhat a pathetic response. Your docs are lacking - show some ownership?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c11", "author": "RyelBanfield", "timestamp": "2022-11-07T19:18:03Z", "body": "Mine worked perfectly up until Friday.\r\n\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport { buffer } from \"micro\";\r\nimport { NextApiRequest, NextApiResponse } from \"next\";\r\n\r\nimport { env } from \"../../env/server.mjs\";\r\n\r\n// eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-var-requires\r\nconst stripe = require(\"stripe\")(env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY);\r\nconst webhookSecret = env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET;\r\n\r\nexport const config = {\r\n  api: {\r\n    bodyParser: false,\r\n  },\r\n};\r\n\r\nconst handler = async (req: NextApiRequest, res: NextApiResponse) => {\r\n  if (req.method === \"POST\") {\r\n    const sig = req.headers[\"stripe-signature\"] as string;\r\n\r\n    let event;\r\n\r\n    try {\r\n      event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(\r\n        await buffer(req),\r\n        sig,\r\n        webhookSecret\r\n      );\r\n      // eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any\r\n    } catch (err: any) {\r\n      return res.status(400).send(`Webhook Error: ${err.message}`);\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    switch (event.type) {\r\n      case \"checkout.session.completed\": {\r\n        break;\r\n      }\r\n      default: {\r\n        console.log(`Unhandled event type ${event.type}`);\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    res.json({ received: true });\r\n  } else {\r\n    res.setHeader(\"Allow\", \"POST\");\r\n    res.status(405).end(\"Method Not Allowed\");\r\n  }\r\n};\r\n\r\nexport default handler;\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\nI am so confused.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c12", "author": "jgreensmith", "timestamp": "2022-11-22T11:22:45Z", "body": "> Thanks all this helped me, want to share with everybody many mistakes I made while testing this for first time :\r\n> \r\n> 1- disable body-parser\r\n> \r\n> ```\r\n> export const config = {\r\n>   api: {\r\n>     bodyParser: false,\r\n>   },\r\n> };\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> 2- after using micro.buffer, pay attention to` rawBody.toString()` !! ` const rawBody = await buffer(req);`. and `event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent( ***rawBody.toString()***, .signtaure..., ..secret. )` 3- use correct webhook signing secret ( secret generated by CLI vs secret generated by Dashboard) in my case I was using this command\r\n> \r\n> ```\r\n> $ stripe listen --forward-to http://localhost:3000/api1/stripe/hooks > Ready! You are using Stripe API Version [2020-03-02].\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> ```\r\n>  Your -->>> webhook signing secret is whsec_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx (^C to quit)\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> but another mistake I made, that I was using `webhook test secret` generated by dashboard not the one generated by `CLI` :(\r\n> \r\n> Here is my code after getting correct secret\r\n> \r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> import Stripe from 'stripe';\r\n> import { buffer } from 'micro';\r\n> const stripe = new Stripe(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY);\r\n> \r\n> export const config = {\r\n>   api: {\r\n>     bodyParser: false,\r\n>   },\r\n> };\r\n> \r\n> export default async function handler(req, res) {\r\n>   if (req.method === 'POST') {\r\n>     let event;\r\n> \r\n>     try {\r\n>       // 1. Retrieve the event by verifying the signature using the raw body and secret\r\n>       const rawBody = await buffer(req);\r\n>       const signature = req.headers['stripe-signature'];\r\n> \r\n>       event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(\r\n>         rawBody.toString(),\r\n>         signature,\r\n>         process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET\r\n>       );\r\n>     } catch (err) {\r\n>       console.log(`\u274c Error message: ${err.message}`);\r\n>       res.status(400).send(`Webhook Error: ${err.message}`);\r\n>       return;\r\n>     }\r\n> \r\n>     // Successfully constructed event\r\n>     console.log('\u2705 Success:', event.id);\r\n> \r\n>     // 2. Handle event type (add business logic here)\r\n>     if (event.type === 'checkout.session.completed') {\r\n>       console.log(`\ud83d\udcb0  Payment received!`);\r\n>     } else {\r\n>       console.warn(`\ud83e\udd37\u200d\u2640\ufe0f Unhandled event type: ${event.type}`);\r\n>     }\r\n> \r\n>     // 3. Return a response to acknowledge receipt of the event.\r\n>     res.json({ received: true });\r\n>   } else {\r\n>     res.setHeader('Allow', 'POST');\r\n>     res.status(405).end('Method Not Allowed');\r\n>   }\r\n> }\r\n> ```\r\n\r\npart 3 of this fixed it for me!", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c13", "author": "th3brink", "timestamp": "2023-05-22T15:39:43Z", "body": "I mistakenly used the webhook id and not the Signing Secret for `process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET`. \r\n\r\nYou will find the secret on the webhook detail page\r\n<img width=\"448\" alt=\"Screenshot 2023-05-22 093719\" src=\"https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/assets/1435543/e4c3c84e-c437-4b82-8fac-68225ef6aa86\">\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c14", "author": "user061281", "timestamp": "2023-05-25T20:55:07Z", "body": "I happen to have the same error using expressjs v4.17.0 no matter what I try.\r\n\r\n`async function buffer(readable) {\r\n  const chunks = [];\r\n  for await (const chunk of readable) {\r\n    chunks.push(typeof chunk === 'string' ? Buffer.from(chunk) : chunk);\r\n  }\r\n  return Buffer.concat(chunks);\r\n}\r\n\r\napp.post('/hooks/webhook', express.raw({type: 'application/json'}), async (request, response) => {\r\n  const sig = request.headers['stripe-signature'];\r\n  const buf = await buffer(request);\r\n  let event;\r\n\r\n  try {\r\n    event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(buf.toString(), sig, endpointSecret);\r\n  } catch (err) {\r\n    response.status(400).send(`Webhook Error: ${err.message}`);\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  // Handle the event\r\n  switch (event.type) {\r\n    case 'payment_intent.succeeded':\r\n      const paymentIntent = event.data.object;\r\n      console.log('PaymentIntent was successful!');\r\n      break;\r\n    case 'payment_method.attached':\r\n      const paymentMethod = event.data.object;\r\n      console.log('PaymentMethod was attached to a Customer!');\r\n      break;\r\n      // ... handle other event types\r\n    default:\r\n      console.log(`Unhandled event type ${event.type}`);\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  // Return a response to acknowledge receipt of the event\r\n  response.json({received: true});\r\n});\r\n`\r\n\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c15", "author": "user061281", "timestamp": "2023-05-27T09:36:28Z", "body": "After a lot of trial end error I have finally made it work. The issue is where you declare the endpoint in the app.... It has to be before any express.json() usages. So, in my case app is : \r\n\r\n`\r\nconst cors = require(\"cors\");\r\nconst corsOptions = {\r\n  origin: \"*\",\r\n  methods: \"GET,PUT,POST,DELETE\",\r\n};\r\n\r\nconst app = express();\r\napp.use(cors(corsOptions));\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\napp.use('/hooks', hookRouter);\r\napp.use(express.json());\r\n...\r\n`\r\n\r\nThe rest routes are below app.use(express.json())\r\n\r\nRouter is: \r\n\r\n`\r\nrouter.post('/webhook', express.raw({type: '*/*'}), async function (request, response) {\r\n    await web.webhooksMethods(request, response);\r\n});\r\n`\r\n\r\nand then the code is:\r\n\r\n`\r\n\r\nconst endpointSecret = process.env.WEBHOOK_SECRET;\r\n\r\nmodule.exports.webhooksMethods = async (request, response) => {\r\n\r\n    console.log(\"~~~~~~~REQUEST PARAMS~~~~~~~~~~\")\r\n    console.log(request.params);\r\n    console.log(\"~~~~~~~REQUEST PARAMS~~~~~~~~~~\")\r\n    let signature = request.headers['stripe-signature']\r\n\r\n\r\n    let event;\r\n\r\n    try {\r\n        event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(request.body.toString(), signature, endpointSecret);\r\n        console.log(event)\r\n    }\r\n    catch (err) {\r\n        console.log(err)\r\n       // response.status(400).send(`Webhook Error: ${err.message}`);\r\n    }\r\n\r\n        event = JSON.parse(request.body.toString());\r\n        switch (event.type) {\r\n\r\n\r\n            case 'payment_intent.requires_action':\r\n                console.log('webhook_payment_intent.requires_action')\r\n                const paymentIntentRequiresAction = event.data.object;\r\n                console.log(paymentIntentRequiresAction)\r\n                // Then define and call a function to handle the event payment_intent.succeeded\r\n                break;\r\n.....\r\n\r\n`\r\n\r\nI have currently commented out the response inside the catch block for debugging purposes.\r\n\r\nHope this helps anyone who faces the same issue and saves him/her some time. Express version I use is  4.17.1\r\n\r\nRemember to use the secret your local terminal prints to you and not the secret from the webhook on stripe dashboard.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c16", "author": "Megajin", "timestamp": "2023-07-01T20:41:38Z", "body": "Hey everyone!\r\n\r\nI am sure, that I have the right solution. You don't need any packages at all.\r\nThe Problem was to create a working buffer out of the request. \r\n\r\nYou have use `req.text()` and transform that into a Buffer with `Buffer.from()`.\r\n\r\nHere is a full example:\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport { type NextRequest, NextResponse } from 'next/server';\r\nimport Stripe from 'stripe';\r\n\r\n// export const config = { api: { bodyParser: false } };\r\n\r\nexport async function POST(req: NextRequest) {\r\n  const stripe = new Stripe(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY || '', {\r\n    apiVersion: '2022-11-15',\r\n  });\r\n  const sig = req.headers.get('stripe-signature') || '';\r\n  const signingSecret = process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SIG || '';\r\n\r\n  // Read the request body as text\r\n  const reqText = await req.text();\r\n  // Convert the text to a buffer\r\n  const reqBuffer = Buffer.from(reqText);\r\n\r\n  let event;\r\n\r\n  try {\r\n    event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(reqBuffer, sig, signingSecret);\r\n    // eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any\r\n  } catch (err: any) {\r\n    return new NextResponse(`Webhook Error: ${err.message}`, {\r\n      status: 400,\r\n    });\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  // Handle the event just an example!\r\n  switch (event.type) {\r\n    case 'product.created':\r\n      const productCreated = event.data.object;\r\n      // Then define and call a function to handle the event product.created\r\n      break;\r\n    case 'product.deleted':\r\n      const productDeleted = event.data.object;\r\n      // Then define and call a function to handle the event product.deleted\r\n      break;\r\n    case 'product.updated':\r\n      const productUpdated = event.data.object;\r\n      // Then define and call a function to handle the event product.updated\r\n      break;\r\n    // ... handle other event types\r\n    default:\r\n      console.log(`Unhandled event type ${event.type}`);\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  return NextResponse.json({ received: true });\r\n}\r\n\r\n```\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c17", "author": "richardsondx", "timestamp": "2023-11-27T22:13:26Z", "body": "> I mistakenly used the webhook id and not the Signing Secret for `process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET`.\r\n> \r\n> You will find the secret on the webhook detail page <img alt=\"Screenshot 2023-05-22 093719\" width=\"448\" src=\"https://private-user-images.githubusercontent.com/1435543/239958557-e4c3c84e-c437-4b82-8fac-68225ef6aa86.png?jwt=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9..u869fjq1aD8OJt-CfZBrZiBivfS9MCPanNpRjha1z6I\">\r\n\r\nThat was it for me. Thanks! ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c18", "author": "Danieldo1", "timestamp": "2024-02-21T15:52:03Z", "body": "> Hey everyone!\r\n> \r\n> I am sure, that I have the right solution. You don't need any packages at all. The Problem was to create a working buffer out of the request.\r\n> \r\n> You have use `req.text()` and transform that into a Buffer with `Buffer.from()`.\r\n> \r\n> Here is a full example:\r\n> \r\n> ```\r\n> import { type NextRequest, NextResponse } from 'next/server';\r\n> import Stripe from 'stripe';\r\n> \r\n> // export const config = { api: { bodyParser: false } };\r\n> \r\n> export async function POST(req: NextRequest) {\r\n>   const stripe = new Stripe(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY || '', {\r\n>     apiVersion: '2022-11-15',\r\n>   });\r\n>   const sig = req.headers.get('stripe-signature') || '';\r\n>   const signingSecret = process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SIG || '';\r\n> \r\n>   // Read the request body as text\r\n>   const reqText = await req.text();\r\n>   // Convert the text to a buffer\r\n>   const reqBuffer = Buffer.from(reqText);\r\n> \r\n>   let event;\r\n> \r\n>   try {\r\n>     event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(reqBuffer, sig, signingSecret);\r\n>     // eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any\r\n>   } catch (err: any) {\r\n>     return new NextResponse(`Webhook Error: ${err.message}`, {\r\n>       status: 400,\r\n>     });\r\n>   }\r\n> \r\n>   // Handle the event just an example!\r\n>   switch (event.type) {\r\n>     case 'product.created':\r\n>       const productCreated = event.data.object;\r\n>       // Then define and call a function to handle the event product.created\r\n>       break;\r\n>     case 'product.deleted':\r\n>       const productDeleted = event.data.object;\r\n>       // Then define and call a function to handle the event product.deleted\r\n>       break;\r\n>     case 'product.updated':\r\n>       const productUpdated = event.data.object;\r\n>       // Then define and call a function to handle the event product.updated\r\n>       break;\r\n>     // ... handle other event types\r\n>     default:\r\n>       console.log(`Unhandled event type ${event.type}`);\r\n>   }\r\n> \r\n>   return NextResponse.json({ received: true });\r\n> }\r\n> ```\r\n\r\nAMAZING ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c19", "author": "tsykin", "timestamp": "2024-08-30T21:19:58Z", "body": "I faced this erron when building stripe webhook with Next.js 14 app router.\r\nIn order to fix the issue, make sure to:\r\n1. Use `request.text()` method for converting request body to string\r\n2. Check whether you are passing correct webhook secret to `constructEvent()` function\r\n\r\nmy completed webhook (make sure to add logic for every event type):\r\n```ts\r\nimport { NextResponse } from 'next/server';\r\nimport Stripe from 'stripe';\r\nimport { env } from '@/utils/utilities';\r\nimport { stripe } from '@/lib/stripe';\r\n\r\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\r\n  let event: Stripe.Event | null = null;\r\n\r\n  try {\r\n    // Read the raw request body as text\r\n    const requestText = await request.text();\r\n    // Retrieve the Stripe signature from the headers\r\n    const sig = request.headers.get('stripe-signature');\r\n    const signingSecret = process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET as string;\r\n    if (!sig) {\r\n      console.error('Stripe signature is null');\r\n      return NextResponse.json({ error: 'Webhook error' }, { status: 400 });\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(requestText, sig, signingSecret);\r\n  } catch (error) {\r\n    if (error instanceof Error) {\r\n      console.error('Webhook signature verification failed:', error);\r\n      return NextResponse.json({ error: 'Webhook error' }, { status: 400 });\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  // Handle the event\r\n  if (event) {\r\n    switch (event.type) {\r\n      case 'checkout.session.completed':\r\n        const completedSession = event.data.object;\r\n        // Handle completed checkout session\r\n        break;\r\n      case 'checkout.session.async_payment_failed':\r\n        const failedSession = event.data.object;\r\n        // Handle failed checkout session\r\n        break;\r\n      default:\r\n        // Unexpected event type\r\n        console.log('Unhandled event type', event.type);\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  return NextResponse.json({ received: true }, { status: 200 });\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1294#1", "question": "In NextJS 12, how can I pass the raw JSON body to a Stripe webhook endpoint so `stripe.webhooks.constructEvent` succeeds? I created a test webhook in the Stripe dashboard, revealed the test webhook Signing Secret (`whsec_...`), put it in my env var, and when I trigger the test webhook I can log the Stripe signature, the signing secret, and the request body, but I constantly get:\n\n`Error: construction of webhook event returned an error: No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload. Are you passing the raw request body you received from Stripe? https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node#webhook-signing`\n\nI tried many solutions, including https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/13405. One version of my code is:\n\n```js\nimport getRawBody from \"raw-body\";\n\nconst stripe = require(\"stripe\")(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY);\n\nconst webhookSecret = process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_ENDPOINT_SECRET;\n\nexport const config = {\n  api: {\n    bodyParser: false,\n  },\n};\n\nconst webhookHandler = async (req, res) => {\n  if (req.method === \"POST\") {\n    const rawBody = await getRawBody(req);\n\n    // Extract Stripe signature from header\n    const signature = req.headers[\"stripe-signature\"];\n    console.log(rawBody)\n    console.log(signature)\n    console.log(webhookSecret)\n\n    let event;\n\n    // Attempt to reconstruct and validate passed event\n    try {\n      event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(rawBody, signature, webhookSecret);\n    } catch (err) {\n      res\n        .status(400)\n        .send(\n          `Error: construction of webhook event returned an error: ${err.message}`,\n        );\n      return;\n    }\n\n    console.log(\"success!\")\n\n    res.json({ received: true });\n  } else {\n\n    res.setHeader(\"Allow\", \"POST\");\n    res.status(405).end(\"Method Not Allowed\");\n    \n  }\n};\n\nexport default webhookHandler;\n\n```\n\nI also tried this and got the same error:\n\n```js\nimport { buffer } from 'micro';\nimport Cors from 'micro-cors';\n\nconst stripe = require(\"stripe\")(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY);\n\nconst webhookSecret = process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_ENDPOINT_SECRET;\n\n// Stripe requires the raw body to construct the event.\nexport const config = {\n  api: {\n    bodyParser: false,\n  },\n};\n\nconst cors = Cors({\n  allowMethods: ['POST', 'HEAD'],\n});\n\nconst webhookHandler = async (req, res) => {\n  if (req.method === 'POST') {\n    const buf = await buffer(req);\n    const sig = req.headers['stripe-signature'];\n\n    let event;\n\n    try {\n      event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(\n        buf.toString(),\n        sig,\n        webhookSecret\n      );\n    } catch (err) {\n      console.log(`\u274c Error message: ${err.message}`);\n      res.status(400).send(`Webhook Error: ${err.message}`);\n      return;\n    }\n\n\n    if (event.type === 'payment_intent.succeeded') {\n      const paymentIntent = event.data.object;\n      console.log(`\ud83d\udcb0 PaymentIntent status: ${paymentIntent.status}`);\n    } else if (event.type === 'payment_intent.payment_failed') {\n      const paymentIntent = event.data.object;\n      console.log(\n        `\u274c Payment failed: ${paymentIntent.last_payment_error?.message}`\n      );\n    } else if (event.type === 'charge.succeeded') {\n      const charge = event.data.object;\n      console.log(`\ud83d\udcb5 Charge id: ${charge.id}`);\n    } else {\n      console.warn(`\ud83e\udd37\u200d\u2640\ufe0f Unhandled event type: ${event.type}`);\n    }\n\n    res.json({ received: true });\n  } else {\n    res.setHeader('Allow', 'POST');\n    res.status(405).end('Method Not Allowed');\n  }\n};\n\nexport default cors(webhookHandler);\n```\n\nPlease help resolve why Stripe says no signatures match the expected signature for the payload.", "answer": "The problem is not a bug in `stripe-node`; it is in how the app/framework code is accessing the real raw JSON body. Webhook verification requires the exact same payload Stripe used to generate the signature, so if the framework, helpers, or body parsers change or touch the body, signature verification will fail. The maintainer pointed to stripe-node issue #341 for stack/version-specific solutions, and another commenter pointed to an article noting the parser problem.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["stripe-node issue #341", "https://dev.to/mahnuh/how-to-access-the-raw-body-of-a-stripe-webhook-request-in-nestjs-41kj"], "answer_grounded_in": "c3", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/341", "https://stripe.com/go/developer-chat", "https://support.stripe.com/contact"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T19:29:33.525068Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1264", "repo": "stripe/stripe-node", "number": 1264, "title": "PaymentIntentCreateParams.PaymentMethodData does not support 'card' type", "url": "https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/1264", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2021-10-03T04:38:04Z", "closed_at": "2021-10-03T17:06:49Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "willbt", "security_topic": "PCI compliance rationale for API type definitions", "qa_summary": "The developer asked if using a token with payment_method_data type='card' is safe and why the TypeScript interface lacks the 'card' type, and the maintainer explained that raw card details require higher PCI compliance, referencing Stripe\u2019s security guide, and confirmed that casting to any is the supported workaround.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "willbt", "answer_author": "remi-stripe", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Security-relevant guidance on PCI-driven restrictions for handling raw card data in Stripe SDK", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "willbt", "timestamp": "2021-10-03T04:38:04Z", "body": "Hello, \r\n\r\nI used the payment_method attribute in the paymentIntents.create function and received this error: \r\n\r\n`A token may not be passed in as a PaymentMethod. Instead, use payment_method_data with type=card and card[token]=tok_xxxx\"`\r\n\r\n\r\nSo I tried to use the payment_method_data and set the type as 'card' as below: \r\npayment_method_data: { type: 'card', card: { token: 'tok_xxxxx' } },\r\n\r\nHowever, with TypeScript this doesn't work as the interface  PaymentIntentCreateParams.PaymentMethodData does not support the 'card' type.  \r\n\r\nI solved by casting to any before passing to .paymentIntents.create and it works. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n```\r\nconst stripeRequest = {\r\n      amount: request.amount,\r\n      currency: request.currency_code,\r\n      application_fee_amount: 0,\r\n      payment_method_types: ['card'],\r\n      confirm: true,\r\n      capture_method: 'manual',\r\n      payment_method_data: { type: 'card', card: { token: 'tok_xxxx' } },\r\n    };\r\n\r\n    const res = await this._stripe.paymentIntents.create(stripeRequest as any);\r\n```\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "remi-stripe", "timestamp": "2021-10-03T17:06:49Z", "body": "@willbt Thanks for the report. We avoid typing the `card` hash because it'd also contain types for passing raw card details server-side which requires a higher level of [PCI compliance](https://stripe.com/docs/security/guide#validating-pci-compliance). Passing a Token id (tok_123) like you do is definitely supported but it's seen more as a temporary migration path than a canonical integration path as you should be confirming the PaymentIntent client-side instead of server-side on creation. We don't have plans to add types for that scenario but casting as any should solve the issue as you found!", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1264#1", "question": "In stripe-node with TypeScript, I used the `payment_method` attribute in `paymentIntents.create` and received this error:\n\n`A token may not be passed in as a PaymentMethod. Instead, use payment_method_data with type=card and card[token]=tok_xxxx\"`\n\nSo I tried to use `payment_method_data` and set the type as `card`:\n\n`payment_method_data: { type: 'card', card: { token: 'tok_xxxxx' } },`\n\nHowever, TypeScript reports that the interface `PaymentIntentCreateParams.PaymentMethodData` does not support the `card` type. Casting to `any` before passing to `.paymentIntents.create` works:\n\n```ts\nconst stripeRequest = {\n      amount: request.amount,\n      currency: request.currency_code,\n      application_fee_amount: 0,\n      payment_method_types: ['card'],\n      confirm: true,\n      capture_method: 'manual',\n      payment_method_data: { type: 'card', card: { token: 'tok_xxxx' } },\n    };\n\n    const res = await this._stripe.paymentIntents.create(stripeRequest as any);\n```\n\nWhy does `PaymentIntentCreateParams.PaymentMethodData` not support `type: 'card'` with `card[token]`, and is there a supported way to do this?", "answer": "The maintainers intentionally avoid typing the `card` hash because it would also include types for passing raw card details server-side, which requires a higher level of PCI compliance. Passing a token ID such as `tok_123` is supported, but Stripe views it as a temporary migration path rather than the canonical integration path; the recommended approach is to confirm the PaymentIntent client-side instead of server-side on creation. They do not plan to add types for this scenario, and casting as `any` is the workaround.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["Stripe PCI compliance guide: https://stripe.com/docs/security/guide#validating-pci-compliance", "stripe-node PaymentIntentCreateParams.PaymentMethodData typing behavior"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://stripe.com/docs/security/guide#validating-pci-compliance"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T19:32:12.802058Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1254", "repo": "stripe/stripe-node", "number": 1254, "title": "Webhook validate signing error: No signature found matching the expected signature for payload. | express", "url": "https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/1254", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2021-09-22T15:23:23Z", "closed_at": "2021-09-23T03:31:39Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "Jonathan-Hofmann", "security_topic": "raw body handling for Stripe webhook signature verification", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know that Stripe webhook signature verification requires the raw request body, and the answer provides a concrete fix by skipping express.json middleware for the webhook endpoint and using express.raw as documented in Stripe/Express docs.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c4", "question_author": "Jonathan-Hofmann", "answer_author": "Jonathan-Hofmann", "answerer_role": "op_self", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Security-relevant raw-body handling issue affecting webhook signature verification integrity", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "Jonathan-Hofmann", "timestamp": "2021-09-22T15:23:23Z", "body": "# Another 'Webhook validate signing' issue! Whoooohooo \ud83e\udd73 \r\n\r\nHi there!\r\nI am trying to built a Backend Server for a Web project with Google Cloud App Engine. \r\n\r\n## What do I want to achieve:\r\nI want to verify all my received WebHooks from Stripe.\r\n\r\n## What is my Problem:\r\nCurrently I always get the same error message.\r\n\r\n```\r\n\u274c Error message: No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload. Are you passing the raw request body you received from Stripe? https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node#webhook-signing\r\n```\r\n\r\nI tried lot's of ways getting the raw body from the request and passing it to the `stripe.webhooks.constructEvent` Method.\r\nBut all failed... \r\n\r\n## My Code to reproduce\r\n\r\n```js\r\napp.post('/webhook', bodyParser.raw({type: 'application/json'}), (req, res) => {\r\n  \r\n  const raw = Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(req.body), 'base64').toString('utf8');\r\n  console.log(raw);\r\n  const sig = req.headers['stripe-signature'];\r\n  const endpointSecret = 'whsec_*********';\r\n  let event;\r\n\r\n  try {\r\n    event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(raw, sig, endpointSecret);\r\n  } catch (err) {\r\n    // On error, log and return the error message\r\n    console.log(`\u274c Error message: ${err.message}`);\r\n    return res.status(400).send(`Webhook Error: ${err.message}`);\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  // Successfully constructed event\r\n  console.log('\u2705 Success:', event.id);\r\n\r\n  // Return a response to acknowledge receipt of the event\r\n  res.json({received: true});\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\nAs you can see it is nearly identical to the code from the Stripe Docs.\r\n\r\nI am not sure if App Engine, from Google Cloud, is parsing the request body before I can even touch it. \r\nMy only reference point is that if I `console.log` out the complete request, the `body` part is always already parsed as a JSON, at least it seems like it.\r\n\r\nThis is what I get when I do ```console.log(req)```:\r\n```\r\n  { ... },\r\n  body: {\r\n    id: 'evt_1JcW8OKBPpELGMIU63zjXLPM',\r\n    object: 'event',\r\n    api_version: '2020-08-27',\r\n    created: 1632319755,\r\n    data: { object: [Object] },\r\n    livemode: false,\r\n    pending_webhooks: 1,\r\n    request: { id: 'req_mjJqRWVdi4t0Hb', idempotency_key: null },\r\n    type: 'invoice.paid'\r\n},\r\n{ ... }\r\n```\r\n\r\n## What I have tried so far\r\nI tried many things.\r\n\r\n- ```JSON.stringify(req.body)``` \r\nResult:\r\n```\r\n\u274c Error message: No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload. Are you passing the raw request body you received from Stripe? https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node#webhook-signing\r\n```\r\n\r\n- ```Buffer.from(req.body, 'base64').toString('utf8');```\r\nResult:\r\n```\r\n\u274c Error message: No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload. Are you passing the raw request body you received from Stripe? https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node#webhook-signing\r\n```\r\n\r\n- ```Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(req.body), 'base64').toString('utf8');``` (same as in 'My Code to reproduce')\r\nResult:\r\n```\r\n\u274c Error message: No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload. Are you passing the raw request body you received from Stripe? https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node#webhook-signing\r\n```\r\n\r\n- I have also tried the 'simple middleware' idea from [jlomas-stripe](https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/341#issuecomment-307492777). But still:\r\nResult:\r\n```\r\n\u274c Error message: No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload. Are you passing the raw request body you received from Stripe? https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node#webhook-signing\r\n```\r\n\r\n- ```req.body.raw``` \r\nResult:\r\n```\r\nundefined\r\n```\r\n\r\n- ```req.body.rawBody``` \r\nResult:\r\n```\r\nundefined\r\n```\r\n\r\n- ```req.rawBody``` \r\nResult:\r\n```\r\nundefined\r\n```\r\n\r\n...\r\n\r\nAt the end, as you can see, I got very frustrated \u2639\ufe0f  ...\r\n\r\nIs this a Bug or is there a solution to fix it?\r\n\r\nI have **only** tested it with the Stripe CLI.\r\n\r\n| Name        | Version           | \r\n| ------------- |:-------------:| \r\n| NodeJS      | v. 14 | \r\n| express      | v. 4.17.1      |   \r\n| stripe | v. 8.176.0      |    \r\n\r\nThank you in advance for helping :D\r\nGreetings from Hamburg, Germany", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "remi-stripe", "timestamp": "2021-09-23T03:31:39Z", "body": "@Jonathan-Hofmann thanks for reaching out! Unfortunately, as you've found there are numerous different ways to access the raw body of a request (required for webhook signatures to work) for that environment and that is completely specific to your environment. It can't be a stripe-node bug which is what github issues are limited to.\r\n\r\nLooking around there are some answers like [this one SO question](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/68680900/express-4-17-get-raw-body-for-one-endpoint) that mentions using `express.raw()` which you haven't tried.\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, if that doesn't work, I think you should contact our support team instead since this is more an integration question. You can contact support [here](https://support.stripe.com/contact/email) or talk to other developers on our Discord community server [here](https://stripe.com/go/developer-chat). They will be better equipped to help you debug your code directly.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "Jonathan-Hofmann", "timestamp": "2021-09-23T11:05:08Z", "body": "Thanks for the quick answer. \r\nI now tried using `express.raw()` but still it doesn't work.\r\n\r\nI did post a question on discord - hopefully getting some more help there...\r\n\r\nThanks again for your time :D", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "MNorgren", "timestamp": "2021-11-01T16:05:08Z", "body": "Any update on this? I am also using the express.raw that is specified in the docs and I am receiving the same error", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "Jonathan-Hofmann", "timestamp": "2021-11-02T15:11:43Z", "body": "Hi, @MNorgren !\r\n\r\nAfter a long debugging session with one very nice Stripe Discord Admin from the Stripe Dev Server the problem was actually very simple. \r\n\r\nI did use a bodyParser like this: `app.use(express.json())` and this middleware did converted the body into a JSON format before I could get the raw body.\r\n\r\nAnd Stripe **needs** the raw body to verify the WebHook.\r\n\r\nAnd so now what I have done is the following:\r\n```javascript\r\napp.use((req, res, next) => {\r\n  if (req.originalUrl === '/webhook') {\r\n    next(); // Do nothing with the body because I need it in a raw state.\r\n  } else {\r\n    express.json()(req, res, next);  // ONLY do express.json() if the received request is NOT a WebHook from Stripe.\r\n  }\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\nHope this helps!\r\nGreetings from Hamburg.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "MNorgren", "timestamp": "2021-11-02T16:58:45Z", "body": "@Jonathan-Hofmann  - Thank you, that also looks like it would work!\r\n\r\nI also found a work-around by using the `express.raw({type: 'application/json'})` as specified in the docs, but rather than using `request.body`, I had to use `request.rawBody`.\r\n\r\nMy express route ended up looking like this:\r\n\r\n```js\r\n  app.post('/stripe/webhook', express.raw({type: 'application/json'}), asyncHandler(async (req: Request, res: Response, ) => {\r\n\r\n    const stripeSignature = req.headers['stripe-signature'];\r\n    if(stripeSignature == null) { throw new UnknownError('No stripe signature found!');  }\r\n\r\n    const stripePayload = (req as any).rawBody || req.body;\r\n    const stripeSecret = `whsec_*********`;\r\n    const event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(stripePayload, stripeSignature?.toString(), stripeSecret);\r\n\r\n    // Handle Event...\r\n    const response = myService.handleEvent(event)\r\n    res.send(response );\r\n  }));\r\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "guenounrub", "timestamp": "2022-05-18T16:22:07Z", "body": "@Jonathan-Hofmann \r\nThank you so much for your solution ! \r\nAfter a whole day of debugging this error, i resolved it.\r\nThanks again\r\n\r\nGreetings from Paris", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "Einote", "timestamp": "2022-08-13T08:19:22Z", "body": "> Hi, @MNorgren !\r\n> \r\n> After a long debugging session with one very nice Stripe Discord Admin from the Stripe Dev Server the problem was actually very simple.\r\n> \r\n> I did use a bodyParser like this: `app.use(express.json())` and this middleware did converted the body into a JSON format before I could get the raw body.\r\n> \r\n> And Stripe **needs** the raw body to verify the WebHook.\r\n> \r\n> And so now what I have done is the following:\r\n> \r\n> ```js\r\n> app.use((req, res, next) => {\r\n>   if (req.originalUrl === '/webhook') {\r\n>     next(); // Do nothing with the body because I need it in a raw state.\r\n>   } else {\r\n>     express.json()(req, res, next);  // ONLY do express.json() if the received request is NOT a WebHook from Stripe.\r\n>   }\r\n> });\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> Hope this helps! Greetings from Hamburg.\r\n\r\nI love you, sincerely", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c8", "author": "dulanjanabandara", "timestamp": "2022-11-28T11:09:05Z", "body": "> Hi, @MNorgren !\r\n> \r\n> After a long debugging session with one very nice Stripe Discord Admin from the Stripe Dev Server the problem was actually very simple.\r\n> \r\n> I did use a bodyParser like this: `app.use(express.json())` and this middleware did converted the body into a JSON format before I could get the raw body.\r\n> \r\n> And Stripe **needs** the raw body to verify the WebHook.\r\n> \r\n> And so now what I have done is the following:\r\n> \r\n> ```js\r\n> app.use((req, res, next) => {\r\n>   if (req.originalUrl === '/webhook') {\r\n>     next(); // Do nothing with the body because I need it in a raw state.\r\n>   } else {\r\n>     express.json()(req, res, next);  // ONLY do express.json() if the received request is NOT a WebHook from Stripe.\r\n>   }\r\n> });\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> Hope this helps! Greetings from Hamburg.\r\n\r\nThis worked. I worked to solve this whole day and finally found your solution. Thank you very much! Greetings from Sri Lanka!", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c9", "author": "carlos00027", "timestamp": "2023-01-26T23:16:23Z", "body": "> Hi, @MNorgren !\r\n> \r\n> After a long debugging session with one very nice Stripe Discord Admin from the Stripe Dev Server the problem was actually very simple.\r\n> \r\n> I did use a bodyParser like this: `app.use(express.json())` and this middleware did converted the body into a JSON format before I could get the raw body.\r\n> \r\n> And Stripe **needs** the raw body to verify the WebHook.\r\n> \r\n> And so now what I have done is the following:\r\n> \r\n> ```js\r\n> app.use((req, res, next) => {\r\n>   if (req.originalUrl === '/webhook') {\r\n>     next(); // Do nothing with the body because I need it in a raw state.\r\n>   } else {\r\n>     express.json()(req, res, next);  // ONLY do express.json() if the received request is NOT a WebHook from Stripe.\r\n>   }\r\n> });\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> Hope this helps! Greetings from Hamburg.\r\n\r\nthanks thanks from Colombia:)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c10", "author": "devbyray", "timestamp": "2023-01-27T19:32:02Z", "body": "> Hi, @MNorgren !\r\n> \r\n> After a long debugging session with one very nice Stripe Discord Admin from the Stripe Dev Server the problem was actually very simple.\r\n> \r\n> I did use a bodyParser like this: `app.use(express.json())` and this middleware did converted the body into a JSON format before I could get the raw body.\r\n> \r\n> And Stripe **needs** the raw body to verify the WebHook.\r\n> \r\n> And so now what I have done is the following:\r\n> \r\n> ```js\r\n> app.use((req, res, next) => {\r\n>   if (req.originalUrl === '/webhook') {\r\n>     next(); // Do nothing with the body because I need it in a raw state.\r\n>   } else {\r\n>     express.json()(req, res, next);  // ONLY do express.json() if the received request is NOT a WebHook from Stripe.\r\n>   }\r\n> });\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> Hope this helps! Greetings from Hamburg.\r\n\r\nThis fixed my issue \ud83d\udc4d", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c11", "author": "gabrielcarreira", "timestamp": "2023-02-14T21:56:20Z", "body": "> Hi, @MNorgren !\r\n> \r\n> After a long debugging session with one very nice Stripe Discord Admin from the Stripe Dev Server the problem was actually very simple.\r\n> \r\n> I did use a bodyParser like this: `app.use(express.json())` and this middleware did converted the body into a JSON format before I could get the raw body.\r\n> \r\n> And Stripe **needs** the raw body to verify the WebHook.\r\n> \r\n> And so now what I have done is the following:\r\n> \r\n> ```js\r\n> app.use((req, res, next) => {\r\n>   if (req.originalUrl === '/webhook') {\r\n>     next(); // Do nothing with the body because I need it in a raw state.\r\n>   } else {\r\n>     express.json()(req, res, next);  // ONLY do express.json() if the received request is NOT a WebHook from Stripe.\r\n>   }\r\n> });\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> Hope this helps! Greetings from Hamburg.\r\n\r\nu saved my life, thanks!!", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c12", "author": "lediokoci1", "timestamp": "2023-03-25T23:18:41Z", "body": "Another alternative solution. Since we need to access only the raw request body we can alter the request object to have a raw body property. Since we are using json body parser we can set this in the middleware like this:\r\n```\r\napp.use(\r\n  express.json({\r\n    verify: (req, res, buf) => {\r\n      req.rawBody = buf;\r\n    },\r\n    limit: '10kb',\r\n  })\r\n);\r\n```\r\n```\r\napp.post('/webhook', (req,res,next)=>{\r\n(req, res, next) => {\r\n  const signature = req.headers['stripe-signature'];\r\n  try {\r\n    const event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(\r\n      req.rawBody,\r\n      signature,\r\n      process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET\r\n    );\r\n\r\n    if (event.type === 'checkout.session.completed') {\r\n     // sth you want to run\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    res.status(200).json({ recived: true });\r\n  } catch (err) {\r\n    res.status(400).send(`Webhook error: ${err.message}`);\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n});\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c13", "author": "iltonbarbosa", "timestamp": "2023-09-22T23:07:01Z", "body": "I was have this same error, but in PHP.\r\n\r\n<?php\r\nrequire_once '../vendor/autoload.php';\r\n\r\n\\Stripe\\Stripe::setApiKey('sk_live_xxxxxxxxxxxxxx');\r\n\r\n$endpoint_secret = 'whsec_xxxxxxxxxxxxx';\r\n\t\t\t\t   \r\n$payload = @file_get_contents('php://input');\r\n$sig_header = $_SERVER['HTTP_STRIPE_SIGNATURE'];\r\n$event = null;\r\n\r\ntry {\r\n  $event = \\Stripe\\Webhook::constructEvent($payload, $sig_header, $endpoint_secret);\r\n} catch(\\UnexpectedValueException $e) {\r\n  // Invalid payload\r\n  http_response_code(400);\r\n  \r\n  exit();\r\n} catch(\\Stripe\\Exception\\SignatureVerificationException $e) {\r\n  // Invalid signature\r\n    http_response_code(401);\r\n  echo json_encode(['Error verifying webhook signature: ' => $e->getMessage()]);\r\n  exit();\r\n}", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c14", "author": "andresmv94", "timestamp": "2023-10-09T23:28:03Z", "body": "> Hi, @MNorgren !\r\n> \r\n> After a long debugging session with one very nice Stripe Discord Admin from the Stripe Dev Server the problem was actually very simple.\r\n> \r\n> I did use a bodyParser like this: `app.use(express.json())` and this middleware did converted the body into a JSON format before I could get the raw body.\r\n> \r\n> And Stripe **needs** the raw body to verify the WebHook.\r\n> \r\n> And so now what I have done is the following:\r\n> \r\n> ```js\r\n> app.use((req, res, next) => {\r\n>   if (req.originalUrl === '/webhook') {\r\n>     next(); // Do nothing with the body because I need it in a raw state.\r\n>   } else {\r\n>     express.json()(req, res, next);  // ONLY do express.json() if the received request is NOT a WebHook from Stripe.\r\n>   }\r\n> });\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> Hope this helps! Greetings from Hamburg.\r\n\r\nThanks for the idea but for me I had to add diferent code before the next();\r\n\r\n```\r\napp.use((req, res, next) => {\r\n  // console.log(\"req.originalUrl ==> \", req.originalUrl);\r\n  if (req.originalUrl === \"/webhooks/stripe\") {\r\n    // Capture the raw request body as a Buffer\r\n    const chunks: Buffer[] = [];\r\n    req.on(\"data\", (chunk) => {\r\n      chunks.push(chunk);\r\n    });\r\n\r\n    req.on(\"end\", () => {\r\n      // Concatenate the chunks to get the raw request body\r\n      (req as any).rawBody = Buffer.concat(chunks);\r\n      next(); // Continue processing the request\r\n    });\r\n  } else {\r\n    express.json()(req, res, next); // ONLY do express.json() if the received request is NOT a WebHook from Stripe.\r\n  }\r\n});\r\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c15", "author": "ardaorkin", "timestamp": "2023-11-20T04:53:32Z", "body": "HI all! I have a NestJS app and I am obtaning raw body at application level like this:\r\n```javascript\r\n//main.ts\r\nconst app = await NestFactory.create(AppModule, { rawBody: true });\r\n```\r\nIt works when I use my own credentials at test mode. But when I deploy the code to the production where I use another stripe account credentials with live mode, it throw this error when a payment is received:\r\n**No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload**\r\n\r\nI couldn't find any solution for days. Can you please help me?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c16", "author": "Noushad-web", "timestamp": "2023-12-05T09:17:42Z", "body": "I faced a similar issue and tried various approaches, including using body parser and exploring other options. Eventually, I discovered the Stripe GitHub examples repository, and its examples helped me resolve my issue.\r\n[github examples](https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/blob/master/examples/webhook-signing/express/main.ts)\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c17", "author": "sujayroro", "timestamp": "2023-12-20T06:46:33Z", "body": "@ardaorkin I was using same webhook endpoint for both live and test mode and getting test mode errors in production", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1254#1", "question": "I am building a backend server for a web project on Google Cloud App Engine and want to verify all received Stripe webhooks, but with the Stripe CLI I always get this error from `stripe.webhooks.constructEvent`: \n\n```\n\u274c Error message: No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload. Are you passing the raw request body you received from Stripe? https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node#webhook-signing\n```\n\nMy route is:\n\n```js\napp.post('/webhook', bodyParser.raw({type: 'application/json'}), (req, res) => {\n  \n  const raw = Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(req.body), 'base64').toString('utf8');\n  console.log(raw);\n  const sig = req.headers['stripe-signature'];\n  const endpointSecret = 'whsec_*********';\n  let event;\n\n  try {\n    event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(raw, sig, endpointSecret);\n  } catch (err) {\n    // On error, log and return the error message\n    console.log(`\u274c Error message: ${err.message}`);\n    return res.status(400).send(`Webhook Error: ${err.message}`);\n  }\n\n  // Successfully constructed event\n  console.log('\u2705 Success:', event.id);\n\n  // Return a response to acknowledge receipt of the event\n  res.json({received: true});\n});\n```\n\nIf I `console.log(req)`, the `body` already looks parsed as JSON:\n\n```\n  { ... },\n  body: {\n    id: 'evt_1JcW8OKBPpELGMIU63zjXLPM',\n    object: 'event',\n    api_version: '2020-08-27',\n    created: 1632319755,\n    data: { object: [Object] },\n    livemode: false,\n    pending_webhooks: 1,\n    request: { id: 'req_mjJqRWVdi4t0Hb', idempotency_key: null },\n    type: 'invoice.paid'\n},\n{ ... }\n```\n\nI have tried `JSON.stringify(req.body)`, `Buffer.from(req.body, 'base64').toString('utf8');`, `Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(req.body), 'base64').toString('utf8');`, the simple middleware idea from https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/341#issuecomment-307492777, and checking `req.body.raw`, `req.body.rawBody`, and `req.rawBody`, but they either give the same signature error or `undefined`. Environment: NodeJS v14, express v4.17.1, stripe v8.176.0. Is this a stripe-node bug or is there a solution to fix it?", "answer": "After a long debugging session with one very nice Stripe Discord Admin from the Stripe Dev Server the problem was actually very simple. \n\nI did use a bodyParser like this: `app.use(express.json())` and this middleware did converted the body into a JSON format before I could get the raw body.\n\nAnd Stripe **needs** the raw body to verify the WebHook.\n\nAnd so now what I have done is the following:\n```javascript\napp.use((req, res, next) => {\n  if (req.originalUrl === '/webhook') {\n    next(); // Do nothing with the body because I need it in a raw state.\n  } else {\n    express.json()(req, res, next);  // ONLY do express.json() if the received request is NOT a WebHook from Stripe.\n  }\n});\n```", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["stripe-node webhook-signing documentation", "Stack Overflow question https://stackoverflow.com/questions/68680900/express-4-17-get-raw-body-for-one-endpoint"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1,c4", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": [], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-15T21:00:58.418085Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1224", "repo": "stripe/stripe-node", "number": 1224, "title": "Stripe.PaymentIntent type is incompatible with stripe-js version of PaymentIntent", "url": "https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/1224", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2021-08-20T22:04:09Z", "closed_at": "2021-08-20T23:10:15Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "imdavidmin", "security_topic": "absence of built-in redaction for PaymentIntent data", "qa_summary": "The developer asked if copying a full server\u2011side Stripe.PaymentIntent to the client could expose sensitive data and how to handle the type incompatibility; the maintainer answered that client\u2011side cannot expand the customer field for security reasons, warned about leaking sensitive info in last_payment_error, and referenced the Stripe\u2011node and stripe\u2011js type definitions as concrete sources.", "question_comment_id": "c6", "answer_comment_id": "c7", "question_author": "imdavidmin", "answer_author": "remi-stripe", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "security-relevant guidance on preventing sensitive PaymentIntent data exposure to client-side applications", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "imdavidmin", "timestamp": "2021-08-20T22:04:09Z", "body": "Currently there are two type errors on copying a Stripe.PaymentIntent object (full privilege with secret key) into a client side PaymentIntent (publishable key)\r\n\r\nFirst is last_payment_error:\r\n```\r\nType 'import(\"stripe\").Stripe.PaymentIntent.LastPaymentError' is not assignable to type 'import(\"@stripe/stripe-js\").PaymentIntent.LastPaymentError'.\r\n  The types of 'payment_method.customer' are incompatible between these types.\r\n    Type 'string | Customer' is not assignable to type 'string'.\r\n      Type 'Customer' is not assignable to type 'string'.ts(2322)\r\n```\r\n\r\nSecond is payment_method:\r\n```\r\nType 'string | PaymentMethod' is not assignable to type 'string'.\r\n  Type 'PaymentMethod' is not assignable to type 'string'.ts(2322)\r\n```", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "remi-stripe", "timestamp": "2021-08-20T22:26:48Z", "body": "@imdavidmin I'm sorry but I'm struggling to parse the report here with the details you shared. Would you be able to provide more context about what you're doing? For example, what is your code around the place where the error happens in your code. Where does the PaymentIntent resource come from (client-side or server-side) and could you provide a truncated response/JSON for the relevant parts for me to investigate further?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "imdavidmin", "timestamp": "2021-08-20T22:30:41Z", "body": "Hi,\r\n\r\nMy client side code sends a request to the server to validate a basket. The server checks all the pricing is correct then creates a PaymentIntent object on the server side using a POST fetch. The response from that fetch is the full Stripe.PaymentIntent type (for example, including the `metadata` field in the response JSON). Returning this response back to the client will expose too much info, so I'm using the reducer function below to strip out the fields that are not available for publishable key.\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport { PaymentIntent } from '@stripe/stripe-js'\r\nimport Stripe from 'stripe'\r\n\r\n// pi parameter should be of Stripe.PaymentIntent type - the server side PaymentIntent type definition\r\n// But Stripe.PaymentIntent is incompatible with @stripe/stripe-js version of PaymentIntent.\r\n// The below code results in Typescript error.\r\n\r\nfunction reduceStripePIToPublishableKeyPermissions(pi: Stripe.PaymentIntent): PaymentIntent {\r\n    return {\r\n        id: pi.id,\r\n        amount: pi.amount,\r\n        client_secret: pi.client_secret,\r\n        currency: pi.currency,\r\n        description: pi.description,\r\n        last_payment_error: pi.last_payment_error,\r\n        next_action: pi.next_action,\r\n        payment_method: pi.payment_method,\r\n        payment_method_types: pi.payment_method_types,\r\n        receipt_email: pi.receipt_email,\r\n        setup_future_usage: pi.setup_future_usage,\r\n        shipping: pi.shipping,\r\n        status: pi.status,\r\n        object: pi.object,\r\n        canceled_at: pi.canceled_at,\r\n        cancellation_reason: pi.cancellation_reason,\r\n        capture_method: pi.capture_method,\r\n        confirmation_method: pi.confirmation_method,\r\n        created: pi.created,\r\n        livemode: pi.livemode\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nHope this clears it up.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "remi-stripe", "timestamp": "2021-08-20T22:36:16Z", "body": "@imdavidmin Thanks for the extra details. Just to make sure I understand, the issue here is unrelated to stripe-node and its types and is instead related to Stripe.js, our client-side library, and its own types? And you get an error because you are doing your own logic to truncate/redact the JSON yourself server-side, unrelated to stripe-node?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "imdavidmin", "timestamp": "2021-08-20T22:41:58Z", "body": "@remi-stripe Well the type definition will need to change either on the stripe-node side or the stripe-js side. I don't know what business logic you guys want to have. Both PaymentIntent definitions are addressing the same JSON response from the REST API. \r\n\r\nI don't see how this is my input of my own logic as I'm simply copying like for like. The PaymentIntent definition of the client-side should be a subset of the server side definition.\r\n\r\nSure, I can do a work around by passing only the PaymentIntent ID back to the client side, and then use the client-side SDK to retrieve it again from Stripe. That seems like a silly extra step to add in just because the type definition was incompatible from the library. The actual data without type checking is the same.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "remi-stripe", "timestamp": "2021-08-20T22:54:13Z", "body": "@imdavidmin From what I can tell, the problem is that server-side the `customer` property on `PaymentMethod` can be a `string` **or** a `Customer` object. This is due to the [Expand feature](https://stripe.com/docs/expand) which can be used server-side (and not client-side).\r\n\r\nSo when you retrieve the `PaymentIntent`, the `last_payment_error` can have the `PaymentMethod` object in it, and that could technically have the whole `Customer` object in `customer` because the types are identical to the top-level `PaymentMethod` object which it describes. This is reflected by the types in `stripe-node` for that reason [here](https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/blob/master/types/2020-08-27/PaymentIntents.d.ts#L273)\r\n\r\nClient-side though, the `customer` property can never be anything else than a `string` on `PaymentMethod` since it can't be expanded for security reasons. This means that inside `last_payment_error`, the type definition points to a `PaymentMethod` too but that one only supports `string` as defined [here](https://github.com/stripe/stripe-js/blob/master/types/api/PaymentMethods.d.ts#L30)\r\n\r\nThis does mean that, since you're the one turning a server-side PaymentIntent into a client-side PaymentIntent, you'll need to force the type for that specific part of the JSON (and possibly others) explicitly. You could also ignore `last_payment_error` in the returned property since it could have sensitive information such as the entire `Charge` object.\r\n\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "imdavidmin", "timestamp": "2021-08-20T22:59:16Z", "body": "Thanks @remi-stripe \r\n\r\nI had a further read and seems like for most operations on the front-end, the client only needs the client secret, and not other attributes in a PaymentIntent. I'm trying to use the PaymentIntent object as a single source of truth so that the displayed amount, currency etc on the UI matches what the PI will charge. \r\n\r\nIn this case, I guess Stripe's preferred way is for me to pass from the server the client secret only, and then the client makes additional queries to Stripe's server as needed? There is no built in redaction function on the stripe-node library, I assume?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "remi-stripe", "timestamp": "2021-08-20T23:10:14Z", "body": "Correct, there's no built-in redaction in stripe-node and it's not something we would add for now unfortunately. You could retrieve the PaymentIntent server-side with the publishable key I guess though it'd be the same as doing it client-side after passing the client_secret back.\r\n\r\nI do understand wanting to pass more than just `client_secret`, like amount/currency, but I'd recommend doing this as your own object instead between your server-side code and client-side code.\r\n\r\nI'm going to close as we've resolved the confusion. Don't hesitate to ask for help from developers on our Discord server: https://stripe.com/go/developer-chat", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1224#1", "question": "In a Stripe integration using `stripe-node` on the server and `@stripe/stripe-js` on the client, I create a server-side `Stripe.PaymentIntent` after validating a basket, then wanted to strip it down to the client-side `PaymentIntent` shape. The TypeScript errors I hit were:\n\n```\nType 'import(\"stripe\").Stripe.PaymentIntent.LastPaymentError' is not assignable to type 'import(\"@stripe/stripe-js\").PaymentIntent.LastPaymentError'.\n  The types of 'payment_method.customer' are incompatible between these types.\n    Type 'string | Customer' is not assignable to type 'string'.\n      Type 'Customer' is not assignable to type 'string'.ts(2322)\n```\n\nand:\n\n```\nType 'string | PaymentMethod' is not assignable to type 'string'.\n  Type 'PaymentMethod' is not assignable to type 'string'.ts(2322)\n```\n\nMy reducer looked like this:\n\n```\nimport { PaymentIntent } from '@stripe/stripe-js'\nimport Stripe from 'stripe'\n\n// pi parameter should be of Stripe.PaymentIntent type - the server side PaymentIntent type definition\n// But Stripe.PaymentIntent is incompatible with @stripe/stripe-js version of PaymentIntent.\n// The below code results in Typescript error.\n\nfunction reduceStripePIToPublishableKeyPermissions(pi: Stripe.PaymentIntent): PaymentIntent {\n    return {\n        id: pi.id,\n        amount: pi.amount,\n        client_secret: pi.client_secret,\n        currency: pi.currency,\n        description: pi.description,\n        last_payment_error: pi.last_payment_error,\n        next_action: pi.next_action,\n        payment_method: pi.payment_method,\n        payment_method_types: pi.payment_method_types,\n        receipt_email: pi.receipt_email,\n        setup_future_usage: pi.setup_future_usage,\n        shipping: pi.shipping,\n        status: pi.status,\n        object: pi.object,\n        canceled_at: pi.canceled_at,\n        cancellation_reason: pi.cancellation_reason,\n        capture_method: pi.capture_method,\n        confirmation_method: pi.confirmation_method,\n        created: pi.created,\n        livemode: pi.livemode\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAfter reading more, it seems most frontend operations only need the client secret rather than other `PaymentIntent` attributes. I wanted to use the `PaymentIntent` as a single source of truth so displayed amount, currency, etc. match what the PaymentIntent will charge. Is Stripe's preferred approach to pass only the client secret from the server and have the client make additional Stripe queries as needed? Also, is there any built-in redaction function in `stripe-node` for turning a server-side `PaymentIntent` into a publishable-key-safe/client-side object?", "answer": "There is no built-in redaction function in `stripe-node`, and the maintainer said they would not add one for now. They suggested that retrieving the PaymentIntent server-side with the publishable key would be equivalent to doing it client-side after passing back the `client_secret`. If you want to pass fields such as amount or currency, the recommendation was to define and return your own object between server-side and client-side code.", "knowledge_type": "parametric", "grounding_sources": [], "answer_grounded_in": "c7", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://stripe.com/go/developer-chat"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T19:34:36.846362Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1161", "repo": "stripe/stripe-node", "number": 1161, "title": "\"Stripe.PaymentIntentCreateParams.PaymentMethodData.Type\" does not contain \"card\"", "url": "https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/1161", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2021-05-24T15:45:41Z", "closed_at": "2021-05-24T18:58:57Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "yilmazbingo", "security_topic": "PCI compliance requirement for server-side card data handling", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know whether sending raw card details server\u2011side (type 'card') is allowed and what security/compliance implications it has; the maintainer answered that it is by design, requires higher PCI compliance and recommends using client\u2011side Elements, citing Stripe\u2019s PCI compliance guide and Elements documentation.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "yilmazbingo", "answer_author": "remi-stripe", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "security-relevant PCI compliance design decision restricting server-side raw card data handling", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "yilmazbingo", "timestamp": "2021-05-24T15:45:41Z", "body": "    \"stripe\": \"^8.149.0\"\r\n   node version: v14.15.1\r\n\r\nThis is the type definiton of \"Stripe.PaymentIntentCreateParams.PaymentMethodData.Type\"\r\n\r\n```js\r\n type Type =\r\n          | 'acss_debit'\r\n          | 'afterpay_clearpay'\r\n          | 'alipay'\r\n          | 'au_becs_debit'\r\n          | 'bacs_debit'\r\n          | 'bancontact'\r\n          | 'eps'\r\n          | 'fpx'\r\n          | 'giropay'\r\n          | 'grabpay'\r\n          | 'ideal'\r\n          | 'oxxo'\r\n          | 'p24'\r\n          | 'sepa_debit'\r\n          | 'sofort';\r\n      }\r\n```\r\n\r\nI checked the docs to see if something changed, but it shows \"card\" on enum: \r\n\r\n[stripe-docs-enum-type](https://stripe.com/docs/api/payment_methods/object)\r\n\r\nHere is my code:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nstripeClient.paymentIntents\r\n    .create({\r\n      amount,\r\n      currency: \"USD\",\r\n      payment_method_types: [\"card\"],\r\n      // Type '\"card\"' is not assignable to type 'Type'\r\n      payment_method_data: { type: \"card\", card: { token } },\r\n      confirm: true,\r\n    })\r\n    .then((paymentIntent) => {\r\n      response.json(paymentIntent);\r\n    })\r\n    .catch((error) => {\r\n      console.log(error.message);\r\n      response.status(400);\r\n      response.send(\"Something went wrong with your payment\");\r\n    });\r\n};\r\n```", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "remi-stripe", "timestamp": "2021-05-24T18:58:57Z", "body": "@yilmazbingo This is by design on our end. Sending raw card details server-side changes your PCI compliance requirements and are harmful to the vast majority of developers and businesses out there. We strongly discourage doing this and instead recommend that you collect card details securely client-side via [Elements](https://stripe.com/docs/stripe-js#elements) instead.\r\n\r\nUltimately though, this works if you are already meeting the higher [PCI compliance requirements](https://stripe.com/docs/security/guide#validating-pci-compliance) and you can pass card details, it will just be untyped to avoid mistaken adoption by developers.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1161#1", "question": "In `stripe-node` version `^8.149.0` on Node `v14.15.1`, `Stripe.PaymentIntentCreateParams.PaymentMethodData.Type` does not include `\"card\"` in its TypeScript definition:\n\n```js\n type Type =\n          | 'acss_debit'\n          | 'afterpay_clearpay'\n          | 'alipay'\n          | 'au_becs_debit'\n          | 'bacs_debit'\n          | 'bancontact'\n          | 'eps'\n          | 'fpx'\n          | 'giropay'\n          | 'grabpay'\n          | 'ideal'\n          | 'oxxo'\n          | 'p24'\n          | 'sepa_debit'\n          | 'sofort';\n      }\n```\n\nThe Stripe docs for the PaymentMethod object enum show `card`: https://stripe.com/docs/api/payment_methods/object\n\nMy code gets a TypeScript error, `Type '\"card\"' is not assignable to type 'Type'`:\n\n```js\nstripeClient.paymentIntents\n    .create({\n      amount,\n      currency: \"USD\",\n      payment_method_types: [\"card\"],\n      // Type '\"card\"' is not assignable to type 'Type'\n      payment_method_data: { type: \"card\", card: { token } },\n      confirm: true,\n    })\n    .then((paymentIntent) => {\n      response.json(paymentIntent);\n    })\n    .catch((error) => {\n      console.log(error.message);\n      response.status(400);\n      response.send(\"Something went wrong with your payment\");\n    });\n};\n```\n\nWhy is `card` missing from `Stripe.PaymentIntentCreateParams.PaymentMethodData.Type`, and how should I handle creating a card payment method here?", "answer": "This is by design. Stripe discourages sending raw card details server-side because it changes PCI compliance requirements, and recommends collecting card details securely client-side using [Elements](https://stripe.com/docs/stripe-js#elements) instead. If you already meet the higher [PCI compliance requirements](https://stripe.com/docs/security/guide#validating-pci-compliance), passing card details still works, but it is intentionally left untyped to avoid mistaken adoption.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://stripe.com/docs/stripe-js#elements", "https://stripe.com/docs/security/guide#validating-pci-compliance"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://stripe.com/docs/security/guide#validating-pci-compliance", "https://stripe.com/docs/stripe-js#elements"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T19:37:28.274713Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1110", "repo": "stripe/stripe-node", "number": 1110, "title": "Consider retrying on 429 Too Many Requests?", "url": "https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/1110", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2021-01-20T22:11:57Z", "closed_at": "2021-01-21T03:29:29Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "jli", "security_topic": "automatic retry on rate limit (429) errors", "qa_summary": "The developer asked whether the SDK should automatically retry on 429 Too Many Requests (a rate\u2011limit security control), and the maintainer answered that it does not retry to avoid hiding underlying problems, citing Stripe\u2019s rate\u2011limit documentation (https://stripe.com/docs/rate-limits).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "jli", "answer_author": "paulasjes-stripe", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "jli", "timestamp": "2021-01-20T22:11:57Z", "body": "I expected that setting `maxNetworkRetries` would result in retries when rate limited (429 Too Many Requests errors), but in testing, I noticed this isn't the case. Would the team consider adding 429 as a condition that gets retried automatically (e.g. [here, in _shouldRetry](https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/blob/v8.131.1/lib/StripeResource.js#L251))?\r\n\r\nI think the typical \"right\" thing to do when rate limited is automatic retries with exponential backoff, which is why it would be nice to have it be part of the built-in retry mechanism (though having a higher max delay than 2s might also be nice in this particular use case as well).\r\n\r\nThanks!", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "paulasjes-stripe", "timestamp": "2021-01-21T03:29:28Z", "body": "The reason we don't automatically retry on 429 errors is that getting that error usually means there's something up with your application. It's actually quite difficult to reach Stripe's rate limit with normal traffic, so getting that error usually means you need to review your application to make sure you aren't making unnecessary requests.\r\n\r\nGetting rate limited shouldn't be solved by automatically retrying within the SDK, as that will obfuscate the actual underlying problem.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "jli", "timestamp": "2021-01-22T14:56:30Z", "body": "Thanks @paulasjes-stripe, that makes sense!\r\n\r\nFWIW, what prompted this is that my team gets alerted for errors in our staging environment which uses the Stripe test environment, which I know has lower rate limits[1]. (The error occasionally gets triggered during server startup, when many processes spin up at the same time and make a Stripe API call as part of initialization.)\r\n\r\nI guess I'll find some other workaround for our staging environment. Thanks!\r\n\r\n\r\n[1] per the Stripe error message: `Testmode request rate limit exceeded, the rate limits in testmode are lower than livemode. You can learn more about rate limits here https://stripe.com/docs/rate-limits.`\r\n\u00a0\r\n\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "remi-stripe", "timestamp": "2021-01-22T18:09:25Z", "body": "@jli The way we approach rate limits is that we expect the vast majority of integrations to never hit this, even at scale. In Test mode the rate limits are 25 requests per second which is extremely high overall for a test suite. If we were retrying the requests automatically for you, you might not even notice that your test suite is making that many requests to our API per second (which is a pretty high load for our servers and yours).\r\n\r\nIn this world, we would expect no test integrations to go over a few of rps other than random peaks. Otherwise you should actively look into adding mocks to your tests and not hit the API directly in test suites!", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "theredcat", "timestamp": "2021-09-29T10:15:09Z", "body": "@remi-stripe @paulasjes-stripe IMHO You should at least provide an option `retryOnRateLimit` because you have small clients, but you have big clients too : @ work we can trigger 1000+ payment in a few seconds because we are doing time-limited auctions and our clients often program all their items to end at a specific time (often Sunday evening)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "remi-stripe", "timestamp": "2021-09-30T04:08:15Z", "body": "@theredcat the large clients are even more reasons not to retry automatically or provide that option in my opinion. Rate limits are something you need to handle carefully and how to handle them depends on your own approach and business models and a \"permanent retry\" for all your requests is likely a bad idea. We implement various logic in rate limiting, dropping reads before writes for example and prioritizing some requests over others internally and most advanced/large users should do the same on their end when they start hitting rate limits, on top of alerting for it.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "acomagu", "timestamp": "2022-11-05T11:37:52Z", "body": "I created auto retry mechanism by httpClient shim.\r\n\r\n```typescript\r\nclass StripeHttpClient implements Stripe.HttpClient {\r\n  baseHttpClient = Stripe.createNodeHttpClient();\r\n  retryCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\r\n  async makeRequest(\r\n    host: string,\r\n    port: string | number,\r\n    path: string,\r\n    method: 'GET' | 'POST' | 'PUT' | 'DELETE',\r\n    headers: Record<string, string>,\r\n    requestData: string | null,\r\n    protocol: Stripe.HttpProtocol,\r\n    timeout: number,\r\n  ): Promise<Stripe.HttpClientResponse> {\r\n    const originalIdempotencyKey = headers['Idempotency-Key'];\r\n    let idempotencyKey: string | undefined;\r\n    if (originalIdempotencyKey) {\r\n      if (this.retryCounts[originalIdempotencyKey] !== undefined) {\r\n        this.retryCounts[originalIdempotencyKey] = (this.retryCounts[originalIdempotencyKey] ?? 0) + 1;\r\n        idempotencyKey = `${originalIdempotencyKey}-rate-limit-retry-${this.retryCounts[originalIdempotencyKey]}`;\r\n      } else {\r\n        this.retryCounts[originalIdempotencyKey] = 0;\r\n        idempotencyKey = originalIdempotencyKey;\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    const resp = await this.baseHttpClient.makeRequest(\r\n      host,\r\n      port,\r\n      path,\r\n      method,\r\n      headers,\r\n      requestData,\r\n      protocol,\r\n      timeout,\r\n    );\r\n    if (resp.getStatusCode() === 429) {\r\n      console.log('retried');\r\n      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 500));\r\n      return this.makeRequest(\r\n        host,\r\n        port,\r\n        path,\r\n        method,\r\n        idempotencyKey ? { ...headers, 'Idempotency-Key': idempotencyKey } : headers,\r\n        requestData,\r\n        protocol,\r\n        timeout,\r\n      );\r\n    }\r\n    return resp;\r\n  }\r\n  getClientName() {\r\n    return this.baseHttpClient.getClientName();\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n\r\nconst stripe = new Stripe('...', {\r\n  apiVersion: '2020-08-27',\r\n  maxNetworkRetries: 2,\r\n  httpClient: new StripeHttpClient(),\r\n});\r\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c7", "author": "isaachinman", "timestamp": "2023-07-24T08:08:21Z", "body": "Here's a more lightweight approach, using `p-throttle`:\r\n\r\n```tsx\r\nimport pThrottle from 'p-throttle'\r\nimport { Stripe } from 'stripe'\r\n\r\nconst httpClient = Stripe.createNodeHttpClient()\r\n\r\nhttpClient.makeRequest = pThrottle({\r\n  interval: 1000,\r\n  limit: 100,\r\n})(httpClient.makeRequest)\r\n\r\nexport const stripeClient = new Stripe(apiKeys, { httpClient })\r\n```", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1110#1", "question": "In stripe-node v8.131.1, I expected that setting `maxNetworkRetries` would result in retries when rate limited with HTTP 429 Too Many Requests, but in testing I noticed this is not the case. Would the Stripe team consider adding 429 as a condition that gets retried automatically in `_shouldRetry` (for example, `lib/StripeResource.js` around line 251 in v8.131.1)? I think the typical right thing to do when rate limited is automatic retries with exponential backoff, so it would be nice to have this in the built-in retry mechanism, though a higher max delay than 2s might also be useful for this use case.", "answer": "Stripe does not automatically retry 429 errors because hitting rate limits usually indicates an issue in the application, such as making unnecessary requests, and SDK retries would hide the underlying problem. The maintainers expect most integrations not to hit rate limits; in test mode they cited a limit of 25 requests per second and suggested using mocks rather than hitting the API heavily in test suites. They also did not favor a `retryOnRateLimit` option for large clients, because rate-limit handling should be application-specific, with prioritization and alerting rather than blanket retries.", "knowledge_type": "parametric", "grounding_sources": [], "answer_grounded_in": "c1, c3, c5", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": [], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T19:38:27.809067Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1067", "repo": "stripe/stripe-node", "number": 1067, "title": "Webhook signing does not work express JS", "url": "https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/1067", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2020-11-16T22:49:43Z", "closed_at": "2020-11-17T18:06:03Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "kondricz", "security_topic": "using correct webhook signing secret for Stripe webhook verification", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know that the third argument to Stripe's constructEvent must be the webhook signing secret (whsec_\u2026) instead of the Stripe secret key, as explained in Stripe's webhook signature verification documentation.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "kondricz", "answer_author": "richardm-stripe", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Security-relevant (webhook signature verification correctness and credential misuse risk)", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "kondricz", "timestamp": "2020-11-16T22:49:43Z", "body": "Hello everyone,\r\n\r\n\r\nOne more ticket about webhooks...\r\nObviously I researched most of the existing github and stackoverflow tickets, but I keep receiving the very same error message. \r\n\r\n```\r\n\u274c Error message: No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload. Are you passing the raw request  body you received from Stripe? https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node#webhook-signing\r\n--\r\n```\r\n\r\nI am pretty sure it is familiar for many of You \ud83d\ude04. But with this screenshot, it gets really interesting:\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/30053409/99317154-daa53480-2865-11eb-968a-40bd1403cc17.png)\r\n\r\n- Right side => WEBHOOK test payload from stripe dashboard\r\n- Left side => incoming WH payload logged (after converting it from Buffer)\r\n\r\nIn the code, everything else is commented out but the raw data parser:\r\n\r\n```\r\n  // appObject.use(cors({ origin: true }));\r\n  // appObject.use((req, res, next) => (req.originalUrl === routes.WH_STRIPE ? next() : bodyParser.json()(req, res, next)));\r\n  // appObject.use((req, res, next) => (req.originalUrl === routes.WH_STRIPE ? next() : bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: false })(req, res, next)));\r\n  // appObject.use(limiter);\r\n  // appObject.use(fileMiddleware);\r\n  // appObject.use(morgan('combined'));\r\n  appObject.use(bodyParser.raw({ type: '*/*' }));\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd the `constructEvent` method:\r\n\r\n```\r\nconst constructEvent = async (req, res, next) => {\r\n  if (!provider) {\r\n    init();\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  const raw = Buffer.from(req.body, 'base64').toString('utf8');\r\n  console.log(raw);\r\n\r\n  try {\r\n    const stripeEvent = provider.webhooks.constructEvent(\r\n      raw,\r\n      req.headers['stripe-signature'],\r\n      process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY\r\n    );\r\n    console.log('EVENT success: ', stripeEvent);\r\n    res.locals.stripeEvent = stripeEvent;\r\n    return next();\r\n  } catch (err) {\r\n    console.log(`\u274c Error message: ${err.message}`);\r\n    return next(serverStatuses.STATUS_ERROR);\r\n  }\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\nAny idea? I tried to exclude every aspect, but already giving it up.\r\n\r\nI am using GCP functions, but my local environment is plain express JS.\r\nThank You for your time and effort in advance,\r\n\r\nPS: You guys are making a great product! \ud83d\udcb8 \r\n\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "richardm-stripe", "timestamp": "2020-11-16T22:59:58Z", "body": "Hi @kondricz. Something that sticks out to me is the `process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY`,\r\n```javascript\r\n    const stripeEvent = provider.webhooks.constructEvent(\r\n      raw,\r\n      req.headers['stripe-signature'],\r\n      process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY\r\n    );\r\n```\r\n\r\nYou usually want to pass a webhook signing key (begins with `whsec_`) to constructEvent (see [here](https://stripe.com/docs/webhooks/signatures#verify-official-libraries)), not your stripe secret key, which is different.\r\n\r\nIf that's not it, I recommend contacting Stripe support (https://support.stripe.com/contact). I don't *think* this is a bug in stripe-node, and support can help you get to the root of the problem a bit more interactively than we can here.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "kondricz", "timestamp": "2020-11-17T12:36:49Z", "body": "Thank You, it makes sense. I was so focused about the incoming request's body, that I did not see that the third argument is no the SECRET_KEY but another type of secret. I will confirm You, if it solves the problem.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1067#1", "question": "In a Node/Express webhook handler for Stripe, I keep getting this error from `constructEvent`: \n\n```\n\u274c Error message: No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload. Are you passing the raw request  body you received from Stripe? https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node#webhook-signing\n--\n```\n\nI compared the Stripe Dashboard webhook test payload with the incoming webhook payload logged after converting it from a Buffer and they appear to match. I am using GCP Functions, but my local environment is plain Express JS. In the code, everything else is commented out except the raw data parser:\n\n```\n  // appObject.use(cors({ origin: true }));\n  // appObject.use((req, res, next) => (req.originalUrl === routes.WH_STRIPE ? next() : bodyParser.json()(req, res, next)));\n  // appObject.use((req, res, next) => (req.originalUrl === routes.WH_STRIPE ? next() : bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: false })(req, res, next)));\n  // appObject.use(limiter);\n  // appObject.use(fileMiddleware);\n  // appObject.use(morgan('combined'));\n  appObject.use(bodyParser.raw({ type: '*/*' }));\n```\n\nAnd my `constructEvent` method is:\n\n```\nconst constructEvent = async (req, res, next) => {\n  if (!provider) {\n    init();\n  }\n\n  const raw = Buffer.from(req.body, 'base64').toString('utf8');\n  console.log(raw);\n\n  try {\n    const stripeEvent = provider.webhooks.constructEvent(\n      raw,\n      req.headers['stripe-signature'],\n      process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY\n    );\n    console.log('EVENT success: ', stripeEvent);\n    res.locals.stripeEvent = stripeEvent;\n    return next();\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(`\u274c Error message: ${err.message}`);\n    return next(serverStatuses.STATUS_ERROR);\n  }\n};\n```\n\nAny idea what could be causing webhook signature verification to fail?", "answer": "Something that sticks out to me is the `process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY`,\n```javascript\n    const stripeEvent = provider.webhooks.constructEvent(\n      raw,\n      req.headers['stripe-signature'],\n      process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY\n    );\n```\n\nYou usually want to pass a webhook signing key (begins with `whsec_`) to constructEvent (see [here](https://stripe.com/docs/webhooks/signatures#verify-official-libraries)), not your stripe secret key, which is different.\n\nIf that's not it, I recommend contacting Stripe support (https://support.stripe.com/contact). I don't *think* this is a bug in stripe-node, and support can help you get to the root of the problem a bit more interactively than we can here.", "knowledge_type": "parametric", "grounding_sources": [], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://stripe.com/docs/webhooks/signatures#verify-official-libraries", "https://support.stripe.com/contact"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-16T03:47:24.548821Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1043", "repo": "stripe/stripe-node", "number": 1043, "title": "Stripe webhook not working with firebase functions", "url": "https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/1043", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2020-10-14T14:51:10Z", "closed_at": "2020-10-14T15:37:18Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "Pldesch", "security_topic": "Stripe webhook signature verification failure due to payload modification", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know why Stripe webhook signature verification fails and was directed to Stripe\u2019s node SDK issue #341 (see https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/341 and comment 617193575) for fixes.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "Pldesch", "answer_author": "remi-stripe", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation", "external_reference"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": ["https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/341", "https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/341#issuecomment-617193575"]}, "human_note": "security-relevant (webhook payload integrity and signature verification correctness)", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "Pldesch", "timestamp": "2020-10-14T14:51:10Z", "body": "Hi,\r\nI'm setting up a stripe webhook to fulfill my order with stripe after a payment while hosting it with firebase functions.\r\nWhen developing on a localhost everything works fine until I publish it in a firebase function where it always sends me a [400] error. Creating the payment intent works fine in the same firebase function but that one doesn't.\r\n\r\nThe error message I get is : No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload. Are you passing the raw request body you received from Stripe? https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node#webhook-signing\r\n\r\nCode of my express backend :\r\n\r\n```app.post(\r\n  \"/stripe/webhook\",\r\n  bodyParser.raw({ type: \"application/json\" }),\r\n  async (request, response) => {\r\n    const payload = request.body;\r\n    const sig = request.headers[\"stripe-signature\"];\r\n    let event;\r\n\r\n    try {\r\n      event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(payload, sig, endpointSecret);\r\n    } catch (err) {\r\n      return response.status(400).send(`Webhook Error: ${err.message}`);\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    // Handle the checkout.session.completed event\r\n    if (event.type === \"checkout.session.completed\") {\r\n      const session = event.data.object;\r\n      oldSession = await stripe.checkout.sessions.listLineItems(\r\n        session.id,\r\n        function (err, lineItems) {\r\n          fulfillOrder(session, lineItems);\r\n        }\r\n      );\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    response.status(200);\r\n  }\r\n);", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "remi-stripe", "timestamp": "2020-10-14T15:37:18Z", "body": "@Pldesch The error indicates that we couldn't find a matching signature. This usually happens when the payload that we signed is different from the payload you are trying to verify, often because your framework/server is modifying the payload trying to be helpful.\r\nThere are unfortunately many possible root causes which depends a lot on your exact setup. What I usually recommend is to look into this issue https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/341 which has many different fixes from users based on their own integration path. Especially this one: https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/341#issuecomment-617193575\r\n\r\nOtherwise, I'd recommend reaching out to our support team and providing more details, especially the exact payload you are receiving/parsing: https://support.stripe.com/contact", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/1043#1", "question": "I'm setting up a Stripe webhook to fulfill my order after a payment, hosted with Firebase Functions. When developing on localhost everything works fine, but after publishing to a Firebase Function the webhook always returns a 400. Creating the PaymentIntent works fine in the same Firebase Function.\n\nThe error message is: `No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload. Are you passing the raw request body you received from Stripe? https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node#webhook-signing`\n\nMy Express backend code is:\n\n```app.post(\n  \"/stripe/webhook\",\n  bodyParser.raw({ type: \"application/json\" }),\n  async (request, response) => {\n    const payload = request.body;\n    const sig = request.headers[\"stripe-signature\"];\n    let event;\n\n    try {\n      event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(payload, sig, endpointSecret);\n    } catch (err) {\n      return response.status(400).send(`Webhook Error: ${err.message}`);\n    }\n\n    // Handle the checkout.session.completed event\n    if (event.type === \"checkout.session.completed\") {\n      const session = event.data.object;\n      oldSession = await stripe.checkout.sessions.listLineItems(\n        session.id,\n        function (err, lineItems) {\n          fulfillOrder(session, lineItems);\n        }\n      );\n    }\n\n    response.status(200);\n  }\n);\n```\n\nWhy is Stripe webhook signature verification failing in Firebase Functions, and how should I troubleshoot or fix it?", "answer": "he error indicates that we couldn't find a matching signature. This usually happens when the payload that we signed is different from the payload you are trying to verify, often because your framework/server is modifying the payload trying to be helpful.\nThere are unfortunately many possible root causes which depends a lot on your exact setup. What I usually recommend is to look into this issue https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/341 which has many different fixes from users based on their own integration path. Especially this one: https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/341#issuecomment-617193575", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/341", "https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/341#issuecomment-617193575"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/341", "https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/341#issuecomment-617193575", "https://support.stripe.com/contact"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T19:39:51.823715Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/935", "repo": "stripe/stripe-node", "number": 935, "title": "'Invalid JSON Received from the Stipe API' - stripe.oauth.token", "url": "https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/935", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2020-06-29T17:10:16Z", "closed_at": "2020-06-30T09:02:05Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "Bendrog", "security_topic": "misuse of Stripe OAuth authorization code parameter", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know that the `code` parameter must be the OAuth authorization code (starting with `ac_`), not the client ID, as documented in Stripe\u2019s OAuth guide, and the maintainer referenced that documentation.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "Bendrog", "answer_author": "cjavilla-stripe", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "security-relevant (OAuth credential misuse involving authorization code handling)", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "Bendrog", "timestamp": "2020-06-29T17:10:16Z", "body": "### Environment\r\n**Node:** Version 12.16.2\r\n**Stripe:** Version 8.67.0 \r\n_______\r\n### The Code\r\n\r\nI used the code directly from the [Documentation](https://stripe.com/docs/connect/express-accounts#connect-users)\r\n\r\nI tried multiple ways but still stuck here. Is it a probem from the API or do I just miss-use it ?\r\n\r\n**Controller**\r\n```javascript\r\n    async getAuthStripe(ctx) {\r\n        const stripe = require('stripe')(process.env.STRIPE_SK_TEST);\r\n\r\n        const response = await stripe.oauth.token({\r\n            grant_type: 'authorization_code',\r\n            code: process.env.STRIPE_CLIENT_ID,\r\n          });\r\n          console.log(response);\r\n\r\n        return response.stripe_user_id;\r\n    }\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe code breakes before the `console.log()`, and there is no way to log this response\r\n**Console Error**\r\n```\r\nerror Error: Invalid JSON received from the Stripe API\r\n```\r\n\r\n_______\r\nPlease only file issues here that you believe represent actual bugs or feature requests for the Stripe Node library.\r\n\r\nIf you're having general trouble with your Stripe integration, please reach out to support using the form at https://support.stripe.com/ (preferred) or via email to support@stripe.com.\r\n\r\nIf you are reporting a bug, please include your Node version and the version of the Stripe Node library you're using, as well as any other details that may be helpful in reproducing the problem.\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "cjavilla-stripe", "timestamp": "2020-06-29T17:52:11Z", "body": "Hey @Bendrog Thanks for question. I'm surprised you're seeing that error, but also. The value for `code` here should not be the Connect Client ID, but rather the authorization code returned in the OAuth flow. That would not be stored in environment variables, would start with `ac_` and is passed back to you via the redirect URI as shown in Step 3 of the guide you've linked.\r\n\r\nI just tried to reproduce this with the same version of  stripe-node and was unable.\r\n\r\nCan you try wrapping the call to token in a try/catch block?\r\n\r\n```js\r\n  try {\r\n    const response = await stripe.oauth.token({\r\n\tgrant_type: 'authorization_code',\r\n\tcode: 'ac_xxx', // this is from the query string parameters on your redirect_uri\r\n    });\r\n    console.log(response);\r\n    var connected_account_id = response.stripe_user_id;\r\n  } catch(e) {\r\n   console.log(e);\r\n  }\r\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "Bendrog", "timestamp": "2020-06-30T09:05:35Z", "body": "I completely miss understood how to use the API. \r\nThanks for your fast answer.\r\n\r\nI am thinking of not doing anything with this code returned from _STRIPE_ after being redirected, and get the stripe_user_id from a webhook", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/935#1", "question": "I'm using Node 12.16.2 and stripe-node 8.67.0. I used code directly from the Stripe Connect Express Accounts documentation, but `stripe.oauth.token` fails before `console.log(response)` with `Error: Invalid JSON received from the Stripe API`. Is this an API problem or am I misusing it?\n\nController:\n```javascript\n    async getAuthStripe(ctx) {\n        const stripe = require('stripe')(process.env.STRIPE_SK_TEST);\n\n        const response = await stripe.oauth.token({\n            grant_type: 'authorization_code',\n            code: process.env.STRIPE_CLIENT_ID,\n          });\n          console.log(response);\n\n        return response.stripe_user_id;\n    }\n```\n\nConsole error:\n```\nerror Error: Invalid JSON received from the Stripe API\n```", "answer": "The `code` value should not be the Connect Client ID. It should be the authorization code returned by the OAuth flow, passed back to your redirect URI in Step 3 of the linked guide; it starts with `ac_` and would not be stored in environment variables. The maintainer could not reproduce the error with the same stripe-node version and suggested wrapping the call in a try/catch while using the returned `ac_xxx` code.", "knowledge_type": "parametric", "grounding_sources": [], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": [], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T19:48:56.474202Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/932", "repo": "stripe/stripe-node", "number": 932, "title": "\"stripe.webhooks.constructEvent\" always throwing \"No signatures found...\" (Using feathersJs)", "url": "https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/932", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2020-06-27T16:50:07Z", "closed_at": "2020-07-02T15:39:06Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "DEVtrust", "security_topic": "Stripe webhook signature verification requires raw request body", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to know that Stripe webhook signature verification requires the raw, unparsed request body rather than the parsed JSON, and the answer cites Stripe\u2019s webhook\u2011signing example in the stripe-node repository as the concrete source.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "DEVtrust", "answer_author": "paulasjes-stripe", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Security-relevant (webhook signature verification integrity depends on raw request body preservation)", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "DEVtrust", "timestamp": "2020-06-27T16:50:07Z", "body": "I am always getting below error with stripe.webhooks.constructEvent function:\r\n\"No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload. Are you passing the raw request body you received from Stripe? https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node#webhook-signing.\r\n\r\nI am using feathersJs web-framework. And trying to verify the stripe webhook signature and following this document https://stripe.com/docs/webhooks/signatures.\r\nI wrote a hook and pass all required parameters in stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(hook.data, sig, endpointSecret), hook code is below:\r\n``` \r\n// Use this hook to manipulate incoming or outgoing data.\r\n// For more information on hooks see: http://docs.feathersjs.com/api/hooks.html\r\n\u200b\r\nmodule.exports = function (options = {}) {\r\n  // eslint-disable-line no-unused-vars\r\n  return function stripeWebhookValidation(hook) {\r\n    let stripeOptions = hook.app.get(\"stripe\");\r\n    const stripe = require(\"stripe\")(stripeOptions.publicKey);\r\n    const { GeneralError } = require(\"@feathersjs/errors\");\r\n    const endpointSecret = stripeOptions.customerEndpointSecret;\r\n    const sig = hook.params.headers[\"stripe-signature\"];\r\n    try {\r\n      //1st parameter of constructEvent need to be req.body\r\n      stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(hook.data, sig, endpointSecret);\r\n    } catch (err) {\r\n      throw new GeneralError(\r\n        `Error getting events -----------: ${err.message}`\r\n      );\r\n    }\r\n    return Promise.resolve(hook);\r\n  };\r\n```\r\n\r\n- **Node Version** : v11.10.1\r\n- **Stripe node library version**: 8.67.0\r\n- **Problem Replication steps:** Send a test event to a webhook endpoint ({host}/stripe-customer) and verifying the stripe signature.", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "paulasjes-stripe", "timestamp": "2020-06-29T01:45:36Z", "body": "The most likely thing happening here is that your framework is parsing the body as JSON. `constructEvent` requires the raw, unparsed body you receive from the request to verify the signature. \r\n\r\nIf you log out `hook.data`, you'll probably get a JSON blob. To verify you have the raw body you'd get something like `<Buffer 28 72 10...`.\r\n\r\nI'm not familiar with feathersJS, but you should look into preventing the framework from parsing the body for this particular endpoint, as we do in this example using express: https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/blob/master/examples/webhook-signing/node-express/express.js#L20", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "remi-stripe", "timestamp": "2020-07-02T15:39:06Z", "body": "Going to close out for now, but let us know if you're still stuck! You can also talk to our support team directly and they can help investigate the issue: https://support.stripe.com/contact", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "stripe/stripe-node/issue/932#1", "question": "In a FeathersJS app, `stripe.webhooks.constructEvent` always throws: `No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload. Are you passing the raw request body you received from Stripe? https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node#webhook-signing.`\n\nI am trying to verify a Stripe webhook signature following https://stripe.com/docs/webhooks/signatures. I wrote a hook and pass all required parameters as `stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(hook.data, sig, endpointSecret)`:\n\n``` \n// Use this hook to manipulate incoming or outgoing data.\n// For more information on hooks see: http://docs.feathersjs.com/api/hooks.html\n\u200b\nmodule.exports = function (options = {}) {\n  // eslint-disable-line no-unused-vars\n  return function stripeWebhookValidation(hook) {\n    let stripeOptions = hook.app.get(\"stripe\");\n    const stripe = require(\"stripe\")(stripeOptions.publicKey);\n    const { GeneralError } = require(\"@feathersjs/errors\");\n    const endpointSecret = stripeOptions.customerEndpointSecret;\n    const sig = hook.params.headers[\"stripe-signature\"];\n    try {\n      //1st parameter of constructEvent need to be req.body\n      stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(hook.data, sig, endpointSecret);\n    } catch (err) {\n      throw new GeneralError(\n        `Error getting events -----------: ${err.message}`\n      );\n    }\n    return Promise.resolve(hook);\n  };\n```\n\nEnvironment:\n- Node Version: v11.10.1\n- Stripe node library version: 8.67.0\n- Replication steps: send a test event to a webhook endpoint `({host}/stripe-customer)` and verify the Stripe signature.\n\nWhy is `constructEvent` failing with this signature error, and what should I change?", "answer": "The most likely cause is that FeathersJS is parsing the request body as JSON before it reaches `constructEvent`. `constructEvent` requires the raw, unparsed request body from Stripe; if you log `hook.data` and see a JSON blob, it is parsed, while the raw body would look like a Buffer such as `<Buffer 28 72 10...`. Prevent the framework from parsing the body for this webhook endpoint, similar to the linked Express webhook-signing example.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/blob/master/examples/webhook-signing/node-express/express.js#L20"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/blob/master/examples/webhook-signing/node-express/express.js#L20"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T19:52:24.734040Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "urllib3/urllib3/issue/3745", "repo": "urllib3/urllib3", "number": 3745, "title": "Bug Report", "url": "https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/3745", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-12-18T14:39:15Z", "closed_at": "2026-01-07T16:54:29Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "D47A", "security_topic": "redirect draining decompresses attacker-controlled bodies", "qa_summary": "The reporter asked whether the redirect\u2011draining behavior that decompresses attacker\u2011controlled bodies is a security issue, and the maintainer confirmed it is a valid security issue fixed in version 2.6.3 (see GHSA-38jv-5279-wg99).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "D47A", "answer_author": "illia-v", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["advisory", "code"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": ["GHSA-38jv-5279-wg99"], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": ["2.6.3"], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Confirmed security vulnerability with advisory and fix (redirect draining + attacker-controlled decompression).", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "D47A", "timestamp": "2025-12-18T14:39:15Z", "body": "_Environment used for validation_: Python 3.12 virtualenv at `.venv` with `urllib3==2.6.2` (latest on PyPI) plus optional `h2==4.3.0`.\n\n## Findings\n\n### 1. Unlimited decompression can exhaust client memory (High)\n- **Location:** `src/urllib3/response.py:619`-`src/urllib3/response.py:1110`\n- **Impact:** When `Accept-Encoding` is enabled (default), `HTTPResponse.read()` always fully inflates compressed bodies without any upper bound on the decoded size. A malicious peer can send a very small gzip/brotli/zstd payload that expands to gigabytes, leading to client-side memory exhaustion or process termination, independent of the advertised `Content-Length`.\n- **Details:** `_decode()` forces `max_length=-1` when `flush_decoder` is true (line 637) and directly feeds all incoming bytes to the decoder (line 641) while `read()` keeps appending to `_decoded_buffer` until the requested amount is satisfied or EOF (line 1097 onward). There is no limit tied to `Content-Length`, streaming hint, or caller-provided cap, so any compressed response is eagerly expanded into RAM.\n- **Proof of Concept:**\n  ```python\n  import gzip, http.server, socketserver, threading, urllib3\n\n  payload = gzip.compress(b\"A\" * (50 * 1024 * 1024))  # 50 MB after inflate\n\n  class Handler(http.server.BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n      def do_GET(self):\n          self.send_response(200)\n          self.send_header(\"Content-Encoding\", \"gzip\")\n          self.send_header(\"Content-Length\", str(len(payload)))\n          self.end_headers()\n          self.wfile.write(payload)\n      def log_message(self, *args):\n          pass\n\n  server = socketserver.TCPServer((\"127.0.0.1\", 0), Handler)\n  threading.Thread(target=server.serve_forever, daemon=True).start()\n\n  http = urllib3.PoolManager()\n  resp = http.request(\"GET\", f\"http://127.0.0.1:{server.server_address[1]}/\")\n  print(len(resp.data))  # prints 52428800, showing the inflated size held in memory\n\n  server.shutdown()\n  ```\n  Increasing the payload size proportionally increases instantaneous memory usage in the urllib3 client process.\n- **Recommendation:** Introduce configurable limits for the decoded body size (e.g., cap based on `Content-Length`, a new `max_decoded_bytes` parameter, or stop inflating once a threshold is hit) and allow streaming decoders to hand off chunks directly to the caller without buffering everything in `_decoded_buffer`.\n\n### 2. `Retry-After` header induces unbounded client sleep (Medium)\n- **Location:** `src/urllib3/util/retry.py:308-339`\n- **Impact:** When `Retry.respect_retry_after_header` (True by default) is set, urllib3 trusts any `Retry-After` header and performs `time.sleep(retry_after)` with the parsed value. A malicious origin or intermediary can respond with `Retry-After: 31536000` to block the caller\u2019s worker thread for a year, bypassing request-level timeouts and causing denial of service across thread pools.\n- **Details:** `sleep_for_retry()` parses either a delta seconds or HTTP-date (lines 308-323) and, if present, unconditionally calls `time.sleep(retry_after)` (line 338) before reattempting the request. There is no upper bound, no linkage to the configured timeout, and no way to abort the sleep other than thread interruption.\n- **Proof of Concept:**\n  ```python\n  import http.server, socketserver, threading, time\n  import urllib3\n\n  class Handler(http.server.BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n      def do_GET(self):\n          self.send_response(503)\n          self.send_header(\"Retry-After\", \"5\")  # attacker controls this value\n          self.end_headers()\n      def log_message(self, *args):\n          pass\n\n  server = socketserver.TCPServer((\"127.0.0.1\", 0), Handler)\n  threading.Thread(target=server.serve_forever, daemon=True).start()\n\n  retries = urllib3.util.retry.Retry(total=1, respect_retry_after_header=True)\n  http = urllib3.PoolManager()\n  start = time.time()\n  try:\n      http.request(\"GET\", f\"http://127.0.0.1:{server.server_address[1]}/\", retries=retries)\n  except urllib3.exceptions.MaxRetryError:\n      pass\n  print(round(time.time() - start, 2))  # ~5.0 seconds, equal to Retry-After header\n\n  server.shutdown()\n  ```\n  Raising the header to hours or days will stall the thread for the same duration.\n- **Recommendation:** Cap `Retry-After` sleeps to a configurable maximum (defaulting to a few seconds) or tie the sleep to the original request timeout; allow users to opt out or enforce asynchronous cancellation.\n\n### 3. HTTP/2 responses are always fully buffered despite `preload_content=False` (Medium)\n- **Location:** `src/urllib3/http2/connection.py:226-266` and `src/urllib3/http2/connection.py:324-347`\n- **Impact:** The experimental HTTP/2 backend accumulates the entire response body in a `bytearray` (line 230) and materializes it into `HTTP2Response.data` regardless of the caller\u2019s `preload_content` flag. There is no streaming API, so any large HTTP/2 download forces full buffering, opening the door to memory exhaustion when a remote server (or CDN) sends multi-GB responses. Dependent services expecting streaming semantics (e.g., `stream=True` in requests) silently lose back-pressure when HTTP/2 is enabled.\n- **Details:** `HTTP2Connection.getresponse()` reads until `StreamEnded`, appending each `DataReceived` chunk to `data` (lines 248-255). The resulting bytes are stored in `_data` inside `HTTP2Response` (line 331), and methods such as `.stream()` are not implemented. The passed `preload_content` flag from `HTTPSConnectionPool.urlopen()` is ignored entirely for HTTP/2 connections.\n- **Proof of Concept:**\n  ```python\n  import urllib3, urllib3.http2\n\n  urllib3.http2.inject_into_urllib3()\n  http = urllib3.PoolManager(headers={\"accept-encoding\": \"identity\"})\n  resp = http.request(\n      \"GET\",\n      \"https://nghttp2.org/httpbin/stream-bytes/10240\",\n      preload_content=False,\n  )\n  print(resp.version_string, len(resp.data))  # -> HTTP/2 10240\n  urllib3.http2.extract_from_urllib3()\n  ```\n  Despite `preload_content=False`, the full payload is buffered in `resp.data`. Increasing the endpoint size quickly spikes RAM.\n- **Recommendation:** Honor `preload_content=False` by exposing a streaming HTTP/2 response object (e.g., forward `DataReceived` frames to an iterator) or block HTTP/2 upgrades unless the caller explicitly opts in to buffering. At a minimum, document the limitation and provide safeguards (max buffered bytes) to prevent accidental memory exhaustion.\n\n### 4. Redirect draining eagerly decompresses attacker-controlled bodies (High)\n- **Location:** `src/urllib3/poolmanager.py:500`-`src/urllib3/poolmanager.py:503` and `src/urllib3/response.py:793`-`src/urllib3/response.py:801`\n- **Impact:** When a response triggers a redirect, `PoolManager` always calls `response.drain_conn()` before following the `Location`. `drain_conn()` simply invokes `HTTPResponse.read()` with default arguments, which eagerly inflates any `Content-Encoding` on the response body. An attacker can reply with `302 Found`, a tiny compressed payload that expands to hundreds of megabytes, and a benign `Location`. The client must fully decompress the unused body before the redirect is honored, leading to CPU and memory exhaustion without the application ever touching the response object.\n- **Details:** The redirect logic at `poolmanager.py:500-503` calls `response.drain_conn()` unconditionally. `drain_conn()` in turn calls `self.read()` (response.py:799-800), which performs the same unlimited decompression described in Finding 1. Because redirects are handled internally, even a single GET to an attacker-controlled URL can force the client to inflate arbitrary amounts of data just to reach the next hop.\n- **Proof of Concept:**\n  ```python\n  import gzip, http.server, socketserver, threading, time, urllib3\n\n  payload = gzip.compress(b\"A\" * (200 * 1024 * 1024))  # ~200 MB once inflated\n\n  class Handler(http.server.BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n      def do_GET(self):\n          if self.path == \"/next\":\n              self.send_response(200); self.end_headers(); self.wfile.write(b\"ok\")\n              return\n          self.send_response(302)\n          self.send_header(\"Location\", \"/next\")\n          self.send_header(\"Content-Encoding\", \"gzip\")\n          self.send_header(\"Content-Length\", str(len(payload)))\n          self.end_headers()\n          self.wfile.write(payload)\n      def log_message(self, *args): pass\n\n  srv = socketserver.TCPServer((\"127.0.0.1\", 0), Handler)\n  threading.Thread(target=srv.serve_forever, daemon=True).start()\n\n  http = urllib3.PoolManager()\n  start = time.time()\n  resp = http.request(\"GET\", f\"http://127.0.0.1:{srv.server_address[1]}/\")\n  print(resp.status, resp.data, \"elapsed\", round(time.time() - start, 2), \"s\")\n  srv.shutdown()\n  ```\n  Even though the redirect target is only \u201cok\u201d, the client spends noticeable time decompressing the 200\u202fMB payload. Scaling the compressed bomb quickly leads to out-of-memory conditions.\n- **Recommendation:** Avoid decompressing or even reading redirect bodies entirely\u2014most clients ignore them. At minimum, cap the number of bytes that `drain_conn()` will pull (both raw and decoded) or skip draining compressed redirect bodies by closing the socket instead of decompressing attacker data.\n\n### 5. HTTP proxy redirects always drop `Authorization`/`Cookie` headers (Medium)\n- **Location:** `src/urllib3/poolmanager.py:602`-`src/urllib3/poolmanager.py:616` and `src/urllib3/poolmanager.py:477`-`src/urllib3/poolmanager.py:487`\n- **Impact:** When using a plain HTTP proxy (no CONNECT), urllib3 mis-identifies every redirect as \u201ccross-host\u201d because `ProxyManager.connection_from_host()` builds pools keyed on the proxy host (line 614). Consequently `conn.is_same_host(redirect_location)` always returns `False`, so the redirect logic strips every header listed in `Retry.remove_headers_on_redirect`\u2014including `Authorization`, `Cookie`, and any user-provided entries\u2014even if the redirect stays on the exact same origin. Authenticated HTTP workflows behind proxies therefore lose credentials or session cookies after the first redirect and often fall into infinite login loops.\n- **Details:** For HTTP destinations, `connection_from_host()` ignores the target host and instead returns a pool for `self.proxy.host` (`poolmanager.py:614-616`). Later, `PoolManager.urlopen()` removes \u201cunsafe\u201d headers whenever `not conn.is_same_host(redirect_location)` (`poolmanager.py:477-487`). Because `conn.host` is the proxy, not the origin, this condition is always true and every redirect is treated as cross-origin.\n- **Proof of Concept:**\n  ```python\n  import urllib3\n  proxy = urllib3.ProxyManager(\"http://proxy.local:8080\")\n  conn = proxy.connection_from_host(\"example.com\", 80, \"http\")\n  print(\"pool host:\", conn.host)  # -> proxy.local\n  print(\"same host redirect?\",\n        conn.is_same_host(\"http://example.com/secure\"))  # -> False\n  ```\n  The above means cookies/Authorization headers will be stripped before following even a relative redirect to `/secure`. Users behind mandatory HTTP proxies cannot stay authenticated on any site that issues redirects (OAuth, SSO, login flows).\n- **Recommendation:** Track the original destination host separately from the proxy host when evaluating `is_same_host` for redirects, or short-circuit the removal logic when a forwarding proxy is in use so headers are only stripped when the *destination* host truly changes.\n\n### 6. Enabling HTTP/2 globally makes proxy usage fail outright (Low)\n- **Location:** `src/urllib3/http2/connection.py:94`-`src/urllib3/http2/connection.py:103` and `src/urllib3/http2/__init__.py:30`-`src/urllib3/http2/__init__.py:42`\n- **Impact:** Calling `urllib3.http2.inject_into_urllib3()` replaces every `HTTPSConnection` with `HTTP2Connection`, which explicitly raises `NotImplementedError` whenever `proxy` or `proxy_config` is supplied. Any deployment that uses proxies (corporate networks, tests that rely on mitmproxy, etc.) immediately loses outbound HTTPS support once HTTP/2 is injected, effectively taking the client offline.\n- **Details:** `HTTP2Connection.__init__` rejects all proxy kwargs (line 94) and the injector rewires `HTTPSConnectionPool.ConnectionCls` globally to `HTTP2Connection` (http2/__init__.py lines 30-36). Proxied requests therefore crash before any network traffic is attempted.\n- **Proof of Concept:**\n  ```python\n  import urllib3, urllib3.http2\n\n  urllib3.http2.inject_into_urllib3()\n  proxy = urllib3.ProxyManager(\"http://127.0.0.1:3128/\")\n  try:\n      proxy.request(\"GET\", \"https://example.com/\", timeout=urllib3.util.Timeout(1))\n  except Exception as exc:\n      print(type(exc).__name__, exc)\n  finally:\n      urllib3.http2.extract_from_urllib3()\n  # -> NotImplementedError: Proxies aren't supported with HTTP/2\n  ```\n- **Recommendation:** Either keep HTTP/1.1 as a fallback ALPN option when proxies are present or block the injection entirely when proxy settings are detected so that enabling HTTP/2 cannot silently break every proxied request.\n\n## Summary\n\n| #   | Title                                                         | Location                                                                        | Severity |\n| --- | ------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n| 1   | Unlimited decompression grows unbounded client memory         | `src/urllib3/response.py:619-1110`                                              | High     |\n| 2   | `Retry-After` header can stall threads indefinitely           | `src/urllib3/util/retry.py:308-339`                                             | Medium   |\n| 3   | HTTP/2 responses ignore streaming hints and buffer entirely   | `src/urllib3/http2/connection.py:226-347`                                       | Medium   |\n| 4   | Redirect draining decompresses attacker-controlled bodies     | `src/urllib3/poolmanager.py:500-503`, `src/urllib3/response.py:793-801`         | High     |\n| 5   | HTTP proxies treat every redirect as cross-host and drop auth | `src/urllib3/poolmanager.py:477-487`, `src/urllib3/poolmanager.py:602-616`      | Medium   |\n| 6   | Global HTTP/2 injection disables all proxied HTTPS traffic    | `src/urllib3/http2/connection.py:94-103`, `src/urllib3/http2/__init__.py:30-42` | Low      |\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "illia-v", "timestamp": "2026-01-07T16:54:29Z", "body": "Hi @D47A,\n\nThanks for the report! However, we'd appreciate if you reported potential security vulnerabilities privately using our security contact mentioned in [our Security Policy](https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/security/policy).\n\n> 1. Unlimited decompression can exhaust client memory\n\nWe are aware of the issue, but we do not consider it a vulnerability.\nurllib3's users are advised to use [the streaming API](https://urllib3.readthedocs.io/en/stable/advanced-usage.html#streaming-and-i-o)  when dealing with responses of large or unknown length including responses from untrusted sources.\n\nFYI, we applied a security hardening to the streaming API in the recent 2.6.0 release, check GHSA-2xpw-w6gg-jr37.\n\nWhile unlimited decompression by the non-streaming API is not a security issue due to the described reasons, we consider adding parameters for configuring the maximum allowed response size as a new feature.\n\n\n> 2. `Retry-After` header induces unbounded client sleep\n\nDuplicate of #3743, fixed in 2.6.3\n\n\n> 3. HTTP/2 responses are always fully buffered despite `preload_content=False`\n\nIt is fine to report HTTP/2 issues publicly because it is an experimental and uncomplete functionality.\n#3300 exists for adding support for streaming HTTP/2 responses.\n\n\n> 4. Redirect draining eagerly decompresses attacker-controlled bodies\n\nThis is a valid security issue, fixed in 2.6.3, see GHSA-38jv-5279-wg99\n\n\n> 5. HTTP proxy redirects always drop `Authorization`/`Cookie` headers\n\nI am not inclined to accept this as a security issue since the confidential information is not passed to third parties, but the issue does sound like a bug.\n\n\n> 6. Enabling HTTP/2 globally makes proxy usage fail outright\n\n#3299 exists for adding support for proxies with HTTP/2.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "urllib3/urllib3/issue/3745#1", "question": "In urllib3/urllib3, using Python 3.12 in a virtualenv with `urllib3==2.6.2` from PyPI plus optional `h2==4.3.0`, I found several potential security/DoS issues and would like maintainers to triage them and advise on fixes/remediation.\n\n### 1. Unlimited decompression can exhaust client memory (High)\n- **Location:** `src/urllib3/response.py:619`-`src/urllib3/response.py:1110`\n- **Impact:** When `Accept-Encoding` is enabled (default), `HTTPResponse.read()` always fully inflates compressed bodies without any upper bound on the decoded size. A malicious peer can send a very small gzip/brotli/zstd payload that expands to gigabytes, leading to client-side memory exhaustion or process termination, independent of the advertised `Content-Length`.\n- **Details:** `_decode()` forces `max_length=-1` when `flush_decoder` is true (line 637) and directly feeds all incoming bytes to the decoder (line 641) while `read()` keeps appending to `_decoded_buffer` until the requested amount is satisfied or EOF (line 1097 onward). There is no limit tied to `Content-Length`, streaming hint, or caller-provided cap, so any compressed response is eagerly expanded into RAM.\n- **Proof of Concept:**\n  ```python\n  import gzip, http.server, socketserver, threading, urllib3\n\n  payload = gzip.compress(b\"A\" * (50 * 1024 * 1024))  # 50 MB after inflate\n\n  class Handler(http.server.BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n      def do_GET(self):\n          self.send_response(200)\n          self.send_header(\"Content-Encoding\", \"gzip\")\n          self.send_header(\"Content-Length\", str(len(payload)))\n          self.end_headers()\n          self.wfile.write(payload)\n      def log_message(self, *args):\n          pass\n\n  server = socketserver.TCPServer((\"127.0.0.1\", 0), Handler)\n  threading.Thread(target=server.serve_forever, daemon=True).start()\n\n  http = urllib3.PoolManager()\n  resp = http.request(\"GET\", f\"http://127.0.0.1:{server.server_address[1]}/\")\n  print(len(resp.data))  # prints 52428800, showing the inflated size held in memory\n\n  server.shutdown()\n  ```\n  Increasing the payload size proportionally increases instantaneous memory usage in the urllib3 client process.\n- **Recommendation:** Introduce configurable limits for the decoded body size, such as a cap based on `Content-Length`, a new `max_decoded_bytes` parameter, or stopping inflation once a threshold is hit, and allow streaming decoders to hand off chunks directly to the caller without buffering everything in `_decoded_buffer`.\n\n### 2. `Retry-After` header induces unbounded client sleep (Medium)\n- **Location:** `src/urllib3/util/retry.py:308-339`\n- **Impact:** When `Retry.respect_retry_after_header` (True by default) is set, urllib3 trusts any `Retry-After` header and performs `time.sleep(retry_after)` with the parsed value. A malicious origin or intermediary can respond with `Retry-After: 31536000` to block the caller\u2019s worker thread for a year, bypassing request-level timeouts and causing denial of service across thread pools.\n- **Details:** `sleep_for_retry()` parses either a delta seconds or HTTP-date (lines 308-323) and, if present, unconditionally calls `time.sleep(retry_after)` (line 338) before reattempting the request. There is no upper bound, no linkage to the configured timeout, and no way to abort the sleep other than thread interruption.\n- **Proof of Concept:**\n  ```python\n  import http.server, socketserver, threading, time\n  import urllib3\n\n  class Handler(http.server.BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n      def do_GET(self):\n          self.send_response(503)\n          self.send_header(\"Retry-After\", \"5\")  # attacker controls this value\n          self.end_headers()\n      def log_message(self, *args):\n          pass\n\n  server = socketserver.TCPServer((\"127.0.0.1\", 0), Handler)\n  threading.Thread(target=server.serve_forever, daemon=True).start()\n\n  retries = urllib3.util.retry.Retry(total=1, respect_retry_after_header=True)\n  http = urllib3.PoolManager()\n  start = time.time()\n  try:\n      http.request(\"GET\", f\"http://127.0.0.1:{server.server_address[1]}/\", retries=retries)\n  except urllib3.exceptions.MaxRetryError:\n      pass\n  print(round(time.time() - start, 2))  # ~5.0 seconds, equal to Retry-After header\n\n  server.shutdown()\n  ```\n  Raising the header to hours or days will stall the thread for the same duration.\n- **Recommendation:** Cap `Retry-After` sleeps to a configurable maximum, defaulting to a few seconds, or tie the sleep to the original request timeout; allow users to opt out or enforce asynchronous cancellation.\n\n### 3. HTTP/2 responses are always fully buffered despite `preload_content=False` (Medium)\n- **Location:** `src/urllib3/http2/connection.py:226-266` and `src/urllib3/http2/connection.py:324-347`\n- **Impact:** The experimental HTTP/2 backend accumulates the entire response body in a `bytearray` (line 230) and materializes it into `HTTP2Response.data` regardless of the caller\u2019s `preload_content` flag. There is no streaming API, so any large HTTP/2 download forces full buffering, opening the door to memory exhaustion when a remote server or CDN sends multi-GB responses. Dependent services expecting streaming semantics, such as `stream=True` in requests, silently lose back-pressure when HTTP/2 is enabled.\n- **Details:** `HTTP2Connection.getresponse()` reads until `StreamEnded`, appending each `DataReceived` chunk to `data` (lines 248-255). The resulting bytes are stored in `_data` inside `HTTP2Response` (line 331), and methods such as `.stream()` are not implemented. The passed `preload_content` flag from `HTTPSConnectionPool.urlopen()` is ignored entirely for HTTP/2 connections.\n- **Proof of Concept:**\n  ```python\n  import urllib3, urllib3.http2\n\n  urllib3.http2.inject_into_urllib3()\n  http = urllib3.PoolManager(headers={\"accept-encoding\": \"identity\"})\n  resp = http.request(\n      \"GET\",\n      \"https://nghttp2.org/httpbin/stream-bytes/10240\",\n      preload_content=False,\n  )\n  print(resp.version_string, len(resp.data))  # -> HTTP/2 10240\n  urllib3.http2.extract_from_urllib3()\n  ```\n  Despite `preload_content=False`, the full payload is buffered in `resp.data`. Increasing the endpoint size quickly spikes RAM.\n- **Recommendation:** Honor `preload_content=False` by exposing a streaming HTTP/2 response object, such as forwarding `DataReceived` frames to an iterator, or block HTTP/2 upgrades unless the caller explicitly opts in to buffering. At a minimum, document the limitation and provide safeguards, such as max buffered bytes, to prevent accidental memory exhaustion.\n\n### 4. Redirect draining eagerly decompresses attacker-controlled bodies (High)\n- **Location:** `src/urllib3/poolmanager.py:500`-`src/urllib3/poolmanager.py:503` and `src/urllib3/response.py:793`-`src/urllib3/response.py:801`\n- **Impact:** When a response triggers a redirect, `PoolManager` always calls `response.drain_conn()` before following the `Location`. `drain_conn()` simply invokes `HTTPResponse.read()` with default arguments, which eagerly inflates any `Content-Encoding` on the response body. An attacker can reply with `302 Found`, a tiny compressed payload that expands to hundreds of megabytes, and a benign `Location`. The client must fully decompress the unused body before the redirect is honored, leading to CPU and memory exhaustion without the application ever touching the response object.\n- **Details:** The redirect logic at `poolmanager.py:500-503` calls `response.drain_conn()` unconditionally. `drain_conn()` in turn calls `self.read()` (response.py:799-800), which performs the same unlimited decompression described in Finding 1. Because redirects are handled internally, even a single GET to an attacker-controlled URL can force the client to inflate arbitrary amounts of data just to reach the next hop.\n- **Proof of Concept:**\n  ```python\n  import gzip, http.server, socketserver, threading, time, urllib3\n\n  payload = gzip.compress(b\"A\" * (200 * 1024 * 1024))  # ~200 MB once inflated\n\n  class Handler(http.server.BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n      def do_GET(self):\n          if self.path == \"/next\":\n              self.send_response(200); self.end_headers(); self.wfile.write(b\"ok\")\n              return\n          self.send_response(302)\n          self.send_header(\"Location\", \"/next\")\n          self.send_header(\"Content-Encoding\", \"gzip\")\n          self.send_header(\"Content-Length\", str(len(payload)))\n          self.end_headers()\n          self.wfile.write(payload)\n      def log_message(self, *args): pass\n\n  srv = socketserver.TCPServer((\"127.0.0.1\", 0), Handler)\n  threading.Thread(target=srv.serve_forever, daemon=True).start()\n\n  http = urllib3.PoolManager()\n  start = time.time()\n  resp = http.request(\"GET\", f\"http://127.0.0.1:{srv.server_address[1]}/\")\n  print(resp.status, resp.data, \"elapsed\", round(time.time() - start, 2), \"s\")\n  srv.shutdown()\n  ```\n  Even though the redirect target is only \u201cok\u201d, the client spends noticeable time decompressing the 200 MB payload. Scaling the compressed bomb quickly leads to out-of-memory conditions.\n- **Recommendation:** Avoid decompressing or even reading redirect bodies entirely\u2014most clients ignore them. At minimum, cap the number of bytes that `drain_conn()` will pull, both raw and decoded, or skip draining compressed redirect bodies by closing the socket instead of decompressing attacker data.\n\n### 5. HTTP proxy redirects always drop `Authorization`/`Cookie` headers (Medium)\n- **Location:** `src/urllib3/poolmanager.py:602`-`src/urllib3/poolmanager.py:616` and `src/urllib3/poolmanager.py:477`-`src/urllib3/poolmanager.py:487`\n- **Impact:** When using a plain HTTP proxy with no CONNECT, urllib3 mis-identifies every redirect as \u201ccross-host\u201d because `ProxyManager.connection_from_host()` builds pools keyed on the proxy host (line 614). Consequently `conn.is_same_host(redirect_location)` always returns `False`, so the redirect logic strips every header listed in `Retry.remove_headers_on_redirect`\u2014including `Authorization`, `Cookie`, and any user-provided entries\u2014even if the redirect stays on the exact same origin. Authenticated HTTP workflows behind proxies therefore lose credentials or session cookies after the first redirect and often fall into infinite login loops.\n- **Details:** For HTTP destinations, `connection_from_host()` ignores the target host and instead returns a pool for `self.proxy.host` (`poolmanager.py:614-616`). Later, `PoolManager.urlopen()` removes \u201cunsafe\u201d headers whenever `not conn.is_same_host(redirect_location)` (`poolmanager.py:477-487`). Because `conn.host` is the proxy, not the origin, this condition is always true and every redirect is treated as cross-origin.\n- **Proof of Concept:**\n  ```python\n  import urllib3\n  proxy = urllib3.ProxyManager(\"http://proxy.local:8080\")\n  conn = proxy.connection_from_host(\"example.com\", 80, \"http\")\n  print(\"pool host:\", conn.host)  # -> proxy.local\n  print(\"same host redirect?\",\n        conn.is_same_host(\"http://example.com/secure\"))  # -> False\n  ```\n  The above means cookies/Authorization headers will be stripped before following even a relative redirect to `/secure`. Users behind mandatory HTTP proxies cannot stay authenticated on any site that issues redirects (OAuth, SSO, login flows).\n- **Recommendation:** Track the original destination host separately from the proxy host when evaluating `is_same_host` for redirects, or short-circuit the removal logic when a forwarding proxy is in use so headers are only stripped when the destination host truly changes.\n\n### 6. Enabling HTTP/2 globally makes proxy usage fail outright (Low)\n- **Location:** `src/urllib3/http2/connection.py:94`-`src/urllib3/http2/connection.py:103` and `src/urllib3/http2/__init__.py:30`-`src/urllib3/http2/__init__.py:42`\n- **Impact:** Calling `urllib3.http2.inject_into_urllib3()` replaces every `HTTPSConnection` with `HTTP2Connection`, which explicitly raises `NotImplementedError` whenever `proxy` or `proxy_config` is supplied. Any deployment that uses proxies, such as corporate networks or tests that rely on mitmproxy, immediately loses outbound HTTPS support once HTTP/2 is injected, effectively taking the client offline.\n- **Details:** `HTTP2Connection.__init__` rejects all proxy kwargs (line 94) and the injector rewires `HTTPSConnectionPool.ConnectionCls` globally to `HTTP2Connection` (http2/__init__.py lines 30-36). Proxied requests therefore crash before any network traffic is attempted.\n- **Proof of Concept:**\n  ```python\n  import urllib3, urllib3.http2\n\n  urllib3.http2.inject_into_urllib3()\n  proxy = urllib3.ProxyManager(\"http://127.0.0.1:3128/\")\n  try:\n      proxy.request(\"GET\", \"https://example.com/\", timeout=urllib3.util.Timeout(1))\n  except Exception as exc:\n      print(type(exc).__name__, exc)\n  finally:\n      urllib3.http2.extract_from_urllib3()\n  # -> NotImplementedError: Proxies aren't supported with HTTP/2\n  ```\n\nAre these accepted as security vulnerabilities or bugs, and what fixes, workarounds, or existing tracking issues apply?", "answer": "Thanks for the report! However, we'd appreciate if you reported potential security vulnerabilities privately using our security contact mentioned in [our Security Policy](https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/security/policy).\n\n> 1. Unlimited decompression can exhaust client memory\n\nWe are aware of the issue, but we do not consider it a vulnerability.\nurllib3's users are advised to use [the streaming API](https://urllib3.readthedocs.io/en/stable/advanced-usage.html#streaming-and-i-o)  when dealing with responses of large or unknown length including responses from untrusted sources.\n\nFYI, we applied a security hardening to the streaming API in the recent 2.6.0 release, check GHSA-2xpw-w6gg-jr37.\n\nWhile unlimited decompression by the non-streaming API is not a security issue due to the described reasons, we consider adding parameters for configuring the maximum allowed response size as a new feature.\n\n\n> 2. `Retry-After` header induces unbounded client sleep\n\nDuplicate of #3743, fixed in 2.6.3\n\n\n> 3. HTTP/2 responses are always fully buffered despite `preload_content=False`\n\nIt is fine to report HTTP/2 issues publicly because it is an experimental and uncomplete functionality.\n#3300 exists for adding support for streaming HTTP/2 responses.\n\n\n> 4. Redirect draining eagerly decompresses attacker-controlled bodies\n\nThis is a valid security issue, fixed in 2.6.3, see GHSA-38jv-5279-wg99\n\n\n> 5. HTTP proxy redirects always drop `Authorization`/`Cookie` headers\n\nI am not inclined to accept this as a security issue since the confidential information is not passed to third parties, but the issue does sound like a bug.\n\n\n> 6. Enabling HTTP/2 globally makes proxy usage fail outright\n\n#3299 exists for adding support for proxies with HTTP/2.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/security/policy", "urllib3 streaming API documentation", "urllib3 2.6.0", "GHSA-2xpw-w6gg-jr37", "issue #3743", "urllib3 2.6.3", "issue #3300", "GHSA-38jv-5279-wg99", "issue #3299"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["#3299", "#3300", "#3743", "GHSA-2XPW-W6GG-JR37", "GHSA-38JV-5279-WG99", "https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/security/policy", "https://urllib3.readthedocs.io/en/stable/advanced-usage.html#streaming-and-i-o"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-15T21:18:23.108850Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "urllib3/urllib3/issue/3665", "repo": "urllib3/urllib3", "number": 3665, "title": "Python 3.13: `[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Missing Authority Key Identifier (_ssl.c:1032)`", "url": "https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/3665", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-08-29T17:36:37Z", "closed_at": "2025-08-30T07:39:39Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "benz0li", "security_topic": "SSL verification failure due to stricter X509 checks in Python 3.13", "qa_summary": "The developer needed a workaround for the new strict X509 verification in Python\u202f3.13 that caused SSL verification failures, and the answer shows disabling VERIFY_X509_STRICT (and VERIFY_X509_PARTIAL_CHAIN) via an SSL context, citing python/cpython PR\u202f#112389 as the source.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c2", "question_author": "benz0li", "answer_author": "nuclearpidgeon", "answerer_role": "contributor", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "Security-relevant (TLS certificate validation / X.509 verification behavior), with a mitigation tied to Python 3.13 verification changes.", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "benz0li", "timestamp": "2025-08-29T17:36:37Z", "body": "### Subject\n\n```python\nimport requests\nrequests.get('https://www.google.com')\n```\n\n```\nurllib3.exceptions.SSLError: [SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Missing Authority Key Identifier (_ssl.c:1032)\n\nThe above exception was the direct cause of the following exception:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/requests/adapters.py\", line 644, in send\n resp = conn.urlopen(\n method=request.method,\n ...<9 lines>...\n chunked=chunked,\n )\n File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 841, in urlopen\n retries = retries.increment(\n method, url, error=new_e, _pool=self, _stacktrace=sys.exc_info()[2]\n )\n File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/urllib3/util/retry.py\", line 519, in increment\n raise MaxRetryError(_pool, url, reason) from reason # type: ignore[arg-type]\n ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nurllib3.exceptions.MaxRetryError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='www.google.com', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: / (Caused by SSLError(SSLCertVerificationError(1, '[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Missing Authority Key Identifier (_ssl.c:1032)')))\n\nDuring handling of the above exception, another exception occurred:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n File \"<python-input-1>\", line 1, in <module>\n requests.get('https://www.google.com')\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/requests/api.py\", line 73, in get\n return request(\"get\", url, params=params, **kwargs)\n File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/requests/api.py\", line 59, in request\n return session.request(method=method, url=url, **kwargs)\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 589, in request\n resp = self.send(prep, **send_kwargs)\n File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 703, in send\n r = adapter.send(request, **kwargs)\n File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/requests/adapters.py\", line 675, in send\n raise SSLError(e, request=request)\nrequests.exceptions.SSLError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='www.google.com', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: / (Caused by SSLError(SSLCertVerificationError(1, '[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Missing Authority Key Identifier (_ssl.c:1032)')))\n```\n\nThe Linux machine is behind a corporate HTTP proxy with self-signed certificates.\n\nThe required Root CA Certificate has been added to the trust store, e.g. uploaded to `/usr/local/share/ca-certificates` followed by executing `update-ca-certificates`.\n\nFurthermore the following environment variables are set:\n\n```\nftp_proxy=\"ftp://<ftp-proxy-adress>:<ftp-proxy-port>/\"\nFTP_PROXY=\"ftp://<ftp-proxy-adress>:<ftp-proxy-port>/\"\nhttp_proxy=\"http://<http-proxy-adress>:<http-proxy-port>/\"\nHTTP_PROXY=\"http://<http-proxy-adress>:<http-proxy-port>/\"\nhttps_proxy=\"http://<http-proxy-adress>:<http-proxy-port>/\"\nHTTPS_PROXY=\"http://<http-proxy-adress>:<http-proxy-port>/\"\nno_proxy=\"127.0.0.1,localhost[,REDACTED]\"\nNO_PROXY=\"127.0.0.1,localhost[,REDACTED]\"\nCURL_CA_BUNDLE=\"/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt\"\nREQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE=\"/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt\"\nSSL_CERT_FILE=\"/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt\"\n```\n\n\u2139\ufe0f This setup works fine for Python 3.12 (using pip, urllib3, requests, etc.) but **not** for Python 3.13 (using pip it works, though).\n\n---\n\nThe Root CA Certificate contains the following lines:\n\n```\nCertificate:\n    Data:\n        ....\n        X509v3 extensions:\n            ...\n            X509v3 Basic Constraints: critical\n                CA:TRUE, pathlen:1\n            X509v3 Subject Key Identifier:\n                4E:32:71:1C:07:BB:1D:A8:A4:8A:F8:17:4B:B6:15:47:65:54:24:7E\n            X509v3 Authority Key Identifier:\n                4E:32:71:1C:07:BB:1D:A8:A4:8A:F8:17:4B:B6:15:47:65:54:24:7E\n    ...\n```\n\n### Environment\n\nDescribe your environment.\nAt least, paste here the output of:\n\n```python\nimport platform\nimport ssl\nimport urllib3\n\nprint(\"OS\", platform.platform())\nprint(\"Python\", platform.python_version())\nprint(ssl.OPENSSL_VERSION)\nprint(\"urllib3\", urllib3.__version__)\n```\n\n```python\n>>> import platform\n>>> import ssl\n>>> import urllib3\n>>>\n>>> print(\"OS\", platform.platform())\nOS Linux-6.8.0-71-generic-x86_64-with-glibc2.36\n>>> print(\"Python\", platform.python_version())\nPython 3.13.0a0\n>>> print(ssl.OPENSSL_VERSION)\nOpenSSL 3.0.17 1 Jul 2025\n>>> print(\"urllib3\", urllib3.__version__)\nurllib3 2.5.0\n```\n\n### Steps to Reproduce\n\nCan only be reproduced behind a (corporate) HTTP proxy with self-signed certificates.\n\n### Expected Behavior\n\nNo SSLError.\n\n### Actual Behavior\n\nSSLError.\n\n---\n\nRelated:\n\n* https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/3614", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "benz0li", "timestamp": "2025-08-29T17:42:35Z", "body": "The most important comments:\n\n* https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/138193#issuecomment-3229061767\n* https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/138193#issuecomment-3229536381\n* https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/138193#issuecomment-3237729425\n* https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/138193#issuecomment-3239014158", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "nuclearpidgeon", "timestamp": "2025-11-24T13:01:45Z", "body": "For practicality purposes for anyone else hitting this error: a decent downgrade-avoiding workaround if you can control the SSL context passed into urllib3 is to just disable the `VERIFY_X509_STRICT` verification flag that is now set by default (courtesy @vstinner on https://github.com/python/cpython/pull/112389#issuecomment-2024625491 ):\n\n```\n>>> import ssl\n\n>>> ctx = ssl.create_default_context()\n>>> ctx.verify_flags & ssl.VERIFY_X509_STRICT\n<VerifyFlags.VERIFY_X509_STRICT: 32>\n>>> ctx.verify_flags & ssl.VERIFY_X509_PARTIAL_CHAIN\n<VerifyFlags.VERIFY_X509_PARTIAL_CHAIN: 524288>\n\n>>> ctx.verify_flags &= ~(ssl.VERIFY_X509_STRICT | ssl.VERIFY_X509_PARTIAL_CHAIN)\n>>> ctx.verify_flags & ssl.VERIFY_X509_STRICT\n<VerifyFlags.VERIFY_DEFAULT: 0>\n>>> ctx.verify_flags & ssl.VERIFY_X509_PARTIAL_CHAIN\n<VerifyFlags.VERIFY_DEFAULT: 0>\n```\n\nfrom here you can pass this `ctx` into a urllib3 PoolManager via:\n```py\npool = urllib3.PoolManager(ssl_context=ctx)\n```\n\nAs per the other linked issues, python/cpython/pull/112389 introduced the change causing the AKI validation behaviour by tightening the flags used in OpenSSL - it seems it got merged in to Python v3.13.0a5, so presumably it affects all 3.13 versions of Python.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "urllib3/urllib3/issue/3665#1", "question": "In urllib3 2.5.0 on Python 3.13.0a0 with OpenSSL 3.0.17, why does a simple request through a corporate HTTP proxy with self-signed certificates fail with `Missing Authority Key Identifier`, and is there a workaround that avoids downgrading Python?\n\nReproducer:\n\n```python\nimport requests\nrequests.get('https://www.google.com')\n```\n\nError:\n\n```\nurllib3.exceptions.SSLError: [SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Missing Authority Key Identifier (_ssl.c:1032)\n\nThe above exception was the direct cause of the following exception:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/requests/adapters.py\", line 644, in send\n resp = conn.urlopen(\n method=request.method,\n ...<9 lines>...\n chunked=chunked,\n )\n File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 841, in urlopen\n retries = retries.increment(\n method, url, error=new_e, _pool=self, _stacktrace=sys.exc_info()[2]\n )\n File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/urllib3/util/retry.py\", line 519, in increment\n raise MaxRetryError(_pool, url, reason) from reason # type: ignore[arg-type]\n ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nurllib3.exceptions.MaxRetryError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='www.google.com', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: / (Caused by SSLError(SSLCertVerificationError(1, '[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Missing Authority Key Identifier (_ssl.c:1032)')))\n\nDuring handling of the above exception, another exception occurred:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n File \"<python-input-1>\", line 1, in <module>\n requests.get('https://www.google.com')\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/requests/api.py\", line 73, in get\n return request(\"get\", url, params=params, **kwargs)\n File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/requests/api.py\", line 59, in request\n return session.request(method=method, **kwargs)\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 589, in request\n resp = self.send(prep, **send_kwargs)\n File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/requests/sessions.py\", line 703, in send\n r = adapter.send(request, **kwargs)\n File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/requests/adapters.py\", line 675, in send\n raise SSLError(e, request=request)\nrequests.exceptions.SSLError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='www.google.com', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: / (Caused by SSLError(SSLCertVerificationError(1, '[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Missing Authority Key Identifier (_ssl.c:1032)')))\n```\n\nThe machine is Linux behind a corporate HTTP proxy with self-signed certificates. The required Root CA Certificate was added to the trust store, e.g. uploaded to `/usr/local/share/ca-certificates` followed by `update-ca-certificates`. These environment variables are set:\n\n```\nftp_proxy=\"ftp://<ftp-proxy-adress>:<ftp-proxy-port>/\"\nFTP_PROXY=\"ftp://<ftp-proxy-adress>:<ftp-proxy-port>/\"\nhttp_proxy=\"http://<http-proxy-adress>:<http-proxy-port>/\"\nHTTP_PROXY=\"http://<http-proxy-adress>:<http-proxy-port>/\"\nhttps_proxy=\"http://<http-proxy-adress>:<http-proxy-port>/\"\nHTTPS_PROXY=\"http://<http-proxy-adress>:<http-proxy-port>/\"\nno_proxy=\"127.0.0.1,localhost[,REDACTED]\"\nNO_PROXY=\"127.0.0.1,localhost[,REDACTED]\"\nCURL_CA_BUNDLE=\"/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt\"\nREQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE=\"/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt\"\nSSL_CERT_FILE=\"/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt\"\n```\n\nThis setup works for Python 3.12 using pip, urllib3, requests, etc., and pip works on Python 3.13, but requests/urllib3 do not. The Root CA Certificate contains:\n\n```\nCertificate:\n    Data:\n        ....\n        X509v3 extensions:\n            ...\n            X509v3 Basic Constraints: critical\n                CA:TRUE, pathlen:1\n            X509v3 Subject Key Identifier:\n                4E:32:71:1C:07:BB:1D:A8:A4:8A:F8:17:4B:B6:15:47:65:54:24:7E\n            X509v3 Authority Key Identifier:\n                4E:32:71:1C:07:BB:1D:A8:A4:8A:F8:17:4B:B6:15:47:65:54:24:7E\n    ...\n```\n\nEnvironment:\n\n```python\n>>> import platform\n>>> import ssl\n>>> import urllib3\n>>>\n>>> print(\"OS\", platform.platform())\nOS Linux-6.8.0-71-generic-x86_64-with-glibc2.36\n>>> print(\"Python\", platform.python_version())\nPython 3.13.0a0\n>>> print(ssl.OPENSSL_VERSION)\nOpenSSL 3.0.17 1 Jul 2025\n>>> print(\"urllib3\", urllib3.__version__)\nurllib3 2.5.0\n```\n\nIt can only be reproduced behind a corporate HTTP proxy with self-signed certificates. Expected behavior is no SSLError; actual behavior is SSLError. Related issue: https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/3614. Additional related CPython comments were linked: https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/138193#issuecomment-3229061767, https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/138193#issuecomment-3229536381, https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/138193#issuecomment-3237729425, and https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/138193#issuecomment-3239014158.", "answer": "For practicality purposes for anyone else hitting this error: a decent downgrade-avoiding workaround if you can control the SSL context passed into urllib3 is to just disable the `VERIFY_X509_STRICT` verification flag that is now set by default (courtesy @vstinner on https://github.com/python/cpython/pull/112389#issuecomment-2024625491 ):\n\n```\n>>> import ssl\n\n>>> ctx = ssl.create_default_context()\n>>> ctx.verify_flags & ssl.VERIFY_X509_STRICT\n<VerifyFlags.VERIFY_X509_STRICT: 32>\n>>> ctx.verify_flags & ssl.VERIFY_X509_PARTIAL_CHAIN\n<VerifyFlags.VERIFY_X509_PARTIAL_CHAIN: 524288>\n\n>>> ctx.verify_flags &= ~(ssl.VERIFY_X509_STRICT | ssl.VERIFY_X509_PARTIAL_CHAIN)\n>>> ctx.verify_flags & ssl.VERIFY_X509_STRICT\n<VerifyFlags.VERIFY_DEFAULT: 0>\n>>> ctx.verify_flags & ssl.VERIFY_X509_PARTIAL_CHAIN\n<VerifyFlags.VERIFY_DEFAULT: 0>\n```\n\nfrom here you can pass this `ctx` into a urllib3 PoolManager via:\n```py\npool = urllib3.PoolManager(ssl_context=ctx)\n```\n\nAs per the other linked issues, python/cpython/pull/112389 introduced the change causing the AKI validation behaviour by tightening the flags used in OpenSSL - it seems it got merged in to Python v3.13.0a5, so presumably it affects all 3.13 versions of Python.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://github.com/python/cpython/pull/112389#issuecomment-2024625491", "python/cpython#112389", "Python v3.13.0a5"], "answer_grounded_in": "c2", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://github.com/python/cpython/pull/112389#issuecomment-2024625491"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T19:58:06.801616Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "urllib3/urllib3/issue/3632", "repo": "urllib3/urllib3", "number": 3632, "title": "(Caused by SSLError(SSLError(1, '[SSL: SSLV3_ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE] sslv3 alert handshake failure (_ssl.c:1006)'))). Getting this error when using an HTTPS proxy with urllib3 version 2.5.0", "url": "https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/3632", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2025-06-25T03:17:55Z", "closed_at": "2025-06-30T21:57:49Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "JatinMaheriya", "security_topic": "HTTPS proxy SSL handshake failure after urllib3 upgrade", "qa_summary": "The developer needed guidance on fixing SSL handshake failures when using an HTTPS proxy with urllib3\u202fv2.5.0, and the maintainer answered to use `proxy_ssl_context` and linked to a PR comment as the concrete source (https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/pull/3633#issuecomment-3016885365).", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "JatinMaheriya", "answer_author": "illia-v", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "security-relevant due to HTTPS proxy TLS/SSL configuration and SSLContext handling, even though it is primarily a configuration issue rather than a vulnerability.", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "JatinMaheriya", "timestamp": "2025-06-25T03:17:55Z", "body": "### Subject\n**HTTPS Proxy Fails with SSL Handshake Error in urllib3 v2.5.0 (Works Fine in v1.26.20)**\n* I'm using the urllib3 library as a dependency of the requests package. After upgrading to urllib3 version 2.5.0, I encounter the following SSL error when attempting to use an HTTPS proxy:\n**Caused by SSLError(SSLError(1, '[SSL: SSLV3_ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE] sslv3 alert handshake failure (_ssl.c:1006)'))**\n* However, the HTTP proxy works correctly with urllib3 v2.5.0.\n* Previously, with urllib3 version 1.26.20, both HTTP and HTTPS proxies worked without any issues.\n\n### Environment\n\n* Python: 3.11.5\n* urllib3: 2.5.0\n* requests: 2.31.0\n* OpenSSL: 1.1.1k FIPS  25 Mar 2021\n* **OpenSSL (proxy): 1.0.2k-fips  26 Jan 2017**\n\n### Steps to Reproduce\n\n1. Set up the environment as described above.\n2. Replace the proxy and URL placeholders in the code snippet below with valid values.\n3. Run the following script:\n```\nimport requests\n\nproxies = {\n    \"http\":  \"http://username:password@proxy_ip:proxy_port\",\n    \"https\": \"http://username:password@proxy_ip:proxy_port\"\n}\n\nurl = \"https://example.com\"\n\ntry:\n    response = requests.get(url, proxies=proxies)\n    print(\"Status Code:\", response.status_code)\n    print(\"Response Body:\", response.text)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(\"An error occurred:\", e)\n```\n\n### Expected Behavior\n\nThe request should complete successfully when using an HTTPS proxy with the urllib3 v2.5.0, just like it does in urllib3 v1.26.20.\n\n### Actual Behavior\n\nThe request fails with the following SSL handshake error:\n\n**(Caused by SSLError(SSLError(1, '[SSL: SSLV3_ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE] sslv3 alert handshake failure (_ssl.c:1006)')))** \n\n### Additional Notes\n* Downgrading back to urllib3==1.26.20 resolves the issue.\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "illia-v", "timestamp": "2025-06-30T21:57:49Z", "body": "The error is related to your specific (possibly insecure) proxy server configuration. Please use urllib3's `proxy_ssl_context` if you need to configure a custom `SSLContext` for this use case.\n\nSee my other comment too https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/pull/3633#issuecomment-3016885365", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "urllib3/urllib3/issue/3632#1", "question": "I'm using urllib3 as a dependency of requests. After upgrading to urllib3 2.5.0, HTTPS proxy usage fails with an SSL handshake error, while HTTP proxy usage works. With urllib3 1.26.20, both HTTP and HTTPS proxies worked.\n\nEnvironment:\n- Python: 3.11.5\n- urllib3: 2.5.0\n- requests: 2.31.0\n- OpenSSL: 1.1.1k FIPS 25 Mar 2021\n- OpenSSL on the proxy: 1.0.2k-fips 26 Jan 2017\n\nReproduction code:\n```python\nimport requests\n\nproxies = {\n    \"http\":  \"http://username:password@proxy_ip:proxy_port\",\n    \"https\": \"http://username:password@proxy_ip:proxy_port\"\n}\n\nurl = \"https://example.com\"\n\ntry:\n    response = requests.get(url, proxies=proxies)\n    print(\"Status Code:\", response.status_code)\n    print(\"Response Body:\", response.text)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(\"An error occurred:\", e)\n```\n\nThe request fails with:\n\n`Caused by SSLError(SSLError(1, '[SSL: SSLV3_ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE] sslv3 alert handshake failure (_ssl.c:1006)'))`\n\nDowngrading to `urllib3==1.26.20` resolves the issue. Why is this happening with urllib3 2.5.0, and how should I configure it for this HTTPS proxy use case?", "answer": "The maintainer said the error is related to the reporter's specific, possibly insecure proxy server configuration. They advised using urllib3's `proxy_ssl_context` to configure a custom `SSLContext` for this use case, and pointed to another comment (https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/pull/3633#issuecomment-3016885365) for more context.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["urllib3 proxy_ssl_context", "https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/pull/3633#issuecomment-3016885365"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/pull/3633#issuecomment-3016885365"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-17T19:59:46.951935Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "urllib3/urllib3/issue/3465", "repo": "urllib3/urllib3", "number": 3465, "title": "Certificate failed to validate for AWS S3 site", "url": "https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/3465", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2024-08-31T00:56:56Z", "closed_at": "2024-09-02T12:34:39Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "mmulich", "security_topic": "hostname verification failure for S3 bucket with underscore", "qa_summary": "The developer needed to understand why urllib3 reports a hostname mismatch for an S3 bucket containing an underscore and how to work around the SSL verification issue, with the answer citing OpenSSL issue\u202f12566 and providing concrete code examples to disable hostname checking or use urllib3's assert_hostname.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c2", "question_author": "mmulich", "answer_author": "pquentin", "answerer_role": "contributor", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "mmulich", "timestamp": "2024-08-31T00:56:56Z", "body": "### Subject\r\n\r\nThe site `https://cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com/` when requested by urllib3 results in: `urllib3.exceptions.MaxRetryError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: / (Caused by SSLError(SSLCertVerificationError(1, \"[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Hostname mismatch, certificate is not valid for 'cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com'. (_ssl.c:1007)\")))`\r\n\r\n### Environment\r\n\r\n```\r\nOS Linux-6.10.0-linuxkit-aarch64-with-glibc2.35\r\nPython 3.10.12\r\nOpenSSL 3.0.2 15 Mar 2022\r\nurllib3 2.2.2\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Steps to Reproduce\r\n\r\n```py\r\nimport urllib3\r\nurl = 'https://cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com/'\r\nresp = urllib3.request('GET', url)\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Expected Behavior\r\n\r\nThe request should complete. All indications from various browsers and `openssl s_client -connect cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com:443` validate the certificates.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>See also, output from curl</summary>\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ curl --verbose https://cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com > /dev/null\r\n...snip...\r\n* SSL connection using TLSv1.3 / TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256\r\n* ALPN, server accepted to use http/1.1\r\n* Server certificate:\r\n*  subject: CN=*.s3.amazonaws.com\r\n*  start date: Apr 22 00:00:00 2024 GMT\r\n*  expire date: Apr  7 23:59:59 2025 GMT\r\n*  subjectAltName: host \"cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com\" matched cert's \"*.s3.amazonaws.com\"\r\n*  issuer: C=US; O=Amazon; CN=Amazon RSA 2048 M01\r\n*  SSL certificate verify ok.\r\n...snip...\r\n```\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### Actual Behavior\r\n\r\nThe code fails with `certificate verify failed: Hostname mismatch, certificate is not valid for 'cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com'.`. The _Common Name_ in the certificate is `*.s3.amazonaws.com`, so I fail to see the hostname mismatch issue.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Full traceback</summary>\r\n\r\n```pytb\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 466, in _make_request\r\n    self._validate_conn(conn)\r\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 1095, in _validate_conn\r\n    conn.connect()\r\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connection.py\", line 652, in connect\r\n    sock_and_verified = _ssl_wrap_socket_and_match_hostname(\r\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connection.py\", line 805, in _ssl_wrap_socket_and_match_hostname\r\n    ssl_sock = ssl_wrap_socket(\r\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 465, in ssl_wrap_socket\r\n    ssl_sock = _ssl_wrap_socket_impl(sock, context, tls_in_tls, server_hostname)\r\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 509, in _ssl_wrap_socket_impl\r\n    return ssl_context.wrap_socket(sock, server_hostname=server_hostname)\r\n  File \"/usr/lib/python3.10/ssl.py\", line 513, in wrap_socket\r\n    return self.sslsocket_class._create(\r\n  File \"/usr/lib/python3.10/ssl.py\", line 1100, in _create\r\n    self.do_handshake()\r\n  File \"/usr/lib/python3.10/ssl.py\", line 1371, in do_handshake\r\n    self._sslobj.do_handshake()\r\nssl.SSLCertVerificationError: [SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Hostname mismatch, certificate is not valid for 'cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com'. (_ssl.c:1007)\r\n\r\nDuring handling of the above exception, another exception occurred:\r\n\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 789, in urlopen\r\n    response = self._make_request(\r\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 490, in _make_request\r\n    raise new_e\r\nurllib3.exceptions.SSLError: [SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Hostname mismatch, certificate is not valid for 'cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com'. (_ssl.c:1007)\r\n\r\nThe above exception was the direct cause of the following exception:\r\n\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 1, in <module>\r\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/__init__.py\", line 193, in request\r\n    return _DEFAULT_POOL.request(\r\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/_request_methods.py\", line 136, in request\r\n    return self.request_encode_url(\r\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/_request_methods.py\", line 183, in request_encode_url\r\n    return self.urlopen(method, url, **extra_kw)\r\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/poolmanager.py\", line 443, in urlopen\r\n    response = conn.urlopen(method, u.request_uri, **kw)\r\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 873, in urlopen\r\n    return self.urlopen(\r\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 873, in urlopen\r\n    return self.urlopen(\r\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 873, in urlopen\r\n    return self.urlopen(\r\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 843, in urlopen\r\n    retries = retries.increment(\r\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/util/retry.py\", line 519, in increment\r\n    raise MaxRetryError(_pool, url, reason) from reason  # type: ignore[arg-type]\r\nurllib3.exceptions.MaxRetryError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: / (Caused by SSLError(SSLCertVerificationError(1, \"[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Hostname mismatch, certificate is not valid for 'cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com'. (_ssl.c:1007)\")))\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "keepworking", "timestamp": "2024-09-02T05:43:53Z", "body": "It also relative with https://github.com/openssl/openssl/issues/12566\r\n\r\nThe underscore is available to domain name, but not available to host name\r\n\r\nSo, if you need to pass the host name verification then you can disable it on ssl context\r\n\r\nexample follows\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport ssl\r\nfrom urllib3 import PoolManager\r\n\r\ncontext = ssl.create_default_context()\r\ncontext.check_hostname = False\r\n\r\nwith PoolManager(ssl_context=context) as pool:\r\n  pool.request('GET','https://cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com/')\r\n```", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "pquentin", "timestamp": "2024-09-02T12:34:39Z", "body": "Disabling `check_hostname` isn't a good workaround here. While you can't rely on OpenSSL to verify the non-conforming hostname, you can use `assert_hostname` instead, which will use our own hostname checking implementation:\r\n\r\n```python3\r\nimport urllib3\r\n\r\nwith urllib3.PoolManager(assert_hostname=\"cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com\") as http:\r\n    resp = http.request(\"GET\", \"https://cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com/\")\r\n    print(resp)\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd next time you choose a bucket name, try to avoid underscores!", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "sigmavirus24", "timestamp": "2024-09-02T12:59:05Z", "body": "To be fair, openssl also enforces a limit on a DNS name in the list of SANs when generating a CSR. That limit is the defined max length of a common name but it does not apply in the specifications to SANs. So openssl applying validations that are invalid isn't strictly new or surprising. ", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "mmulich", "timestamp": "2024-09-03T20:47:17Z", "body": "@pquentin Thank you for the workaround!\r\n\r\nAnd for anyone else that might come across this from the `requests` API perspective. Here is a quick and dirty solution that incorporates this workaround.\r\n\r\n```py\r\nfrom requests.adapters import HTTPAdapter as BaseHTTPAdapter\r\nfrom requests.sessions import Session as BaseSession\r\nfrom urllib3.util import parse_url\r\n\r\n\r\nclass HTTPSAdapter(BaseHTTPAdapter):\r\n    def send(self, request, stream=False, timeout=None, verify=True, cert=None, proxies=None):\r\n        _, _, host, _, _, _, _ = parse_url(request.url)\r\n        # Re-initialize the PoolManager with the hostname assertion.\r\n        # https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/3465#issuecomment-2324655893\r\n        self.init_poolmanager(\r\n             self._pool_connections,\r\n             self._pool_maxsize,\r\n             block=self._pool_block,\r\n             assert_hostname=host,\r\n         )\r\n        return super().send(request, stream=stream, timeout=timeout, verify=verify, cert=cert, proxies=proxies)\r\n\r\n\r\nclass Session(BaseSession):\r\n    def __init__(self, *args, **kwargs):\r\n        super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)\r\n        # Override the base adapter to use our adapter\r\n        self.mount('https://', HTTPSAdapter())\r\n```", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "urllib3/urllib3/issue/3465#1", "question": "In urllib3 2.2.2 on Linux-6.10.0-linuxkit-aarch64-with-glibc2.35, Python 3.10.12, OpenSSL 3.0.2 15 Mar 2022, why does requesting the AWS S3 URL `https://cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com/` fail hostname validation even though browsers, `openssl s_client -connect cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com:443`, and curl validate the certificate?\n\nReproduction:\n\n```py\nimport urllib3\nurl = 'https://cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com/'\nresp = urllib3.request('GET', url)\n```\n\nExpected: the request should complete. Curl reports:\n\n```text\n* Server certificate:\n*  subject: CN=*.s3.amazonaws.com\n*  start date: Apr 22 00:00:00 2024 GMT\n*  expire date: Apr  7 23:59:59 2025 GMT\n*  subjectAltName: host \"cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com\" matched cert's \"*.s3.amazonaws.com\"\n*  issuer: C=US; O=Amazon; CN=Amazon RSA 2048 M01\n*  SSL certificate verify ok.\n```\n\nActual error:\n\n```text\nurllib3.exceptions.MaxRetryError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: / (Caused by SSLError(SSLCertVerificationError(1, \"[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Hostname mismatch, certificate is not valid for 'cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com'. (_ssl.c:1007)\")))\n```\n\nFull traceback:\n\n```pytb\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 466, in _make_request\n    self._validate_conn(conn)\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 1095, in _validate_conn\n    conn.connect()\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connection.py\", line 652, in connect\n    sock_and_verified = _ssl_wrap_socket_and_match_hostname(\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connection.py\", line 805, in _ssl_wrap_socket_and_match_hostname\n    ssl_sock = ssl_wrap_socket(\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 465, in ssl_wrap_socket\n    ssl_sock = _ssl_wrap_socket_impl(sock, context, tls_in_tls, server_hostname)\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/util/ssl_.py\", line 509, in _ssl_wrap_socket_impl\n    return ssl_context.wrap_socket(sock, server_hostname=server_hostname)\n  File \"/usr/lib/python3.10/ssl.py\", line 513, in wrap_socket\n    return self.sslsocket_class._create(\n  File \"/usr/lib/python3.10/ssl.py\", line 1100, in _create\n    self.do_handshake()\n  File \"/usr/lib/python3.10/ssl.py\", line 1371, in do_handshake\n    self._sslobj.do_handshake()\nssl.SSLCertVerificationError: [SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Hostname mismatch, certificate is not valid for 'cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com'. (_ssl.c:1007)\n\nDuring handling of the above exception, another exception occurred:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 789, in urlopen\n    response = self._make_request(\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 490, in _make_request\n    raise new_e\nurllib3.exceptions.SSLError: [SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Hostname mismatch, certificate is not valid for 'cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com'. (_ssl.c:1007)\n\nThe above exception was the direct cause of the following exception:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 1, in <module>\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/__init__.py\", line 193, in request\n    return _DEFAULT_POOL.request(\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/_request_methods.py\", line 136, in request\n    return self.request_encode_url(\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/_request_methods.py\", line 183, in request_encode_url\n    return self.urlopen(method, url, **extra_kw)\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/poolmanager.py\", line 443, in urlopen\n    response = conn.urlopen(method, u.request_uri, **kw)\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 873, in urlopen\n    return self.urlopen(\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 873, in urlopen\n    return self.urlopen(\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 873, in urlopen\n    return self.urlopen(\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/connectionpool.py\", line 843, in urlopen\n    retries = retries.increment(\n  File \"/app/env/lib/python3.10/site-packages/urllib3/util/retry.py\", line 519, in increment\n    raise MaxRetryError(_pool, url, reason) from reason  # type: ignore[arg-type]\nurllib3.exceptions.MaxRetryError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: / (Caused by SSLError(SSLCertVerificationError(1, \"[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Hostname mismatch, certificate is not valid for 'cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com'. (_ssl.c:1007)\")))\n```\n\nWhat is the recommended workaround without disabling hostname verification?", "answer": "Disabling `check_hostname` isn't a good workaround here. While you can't rely on OpenSSL to verify the non-conforming hostname, you can use `assert_hostname` instead, which will use our own hostname checking implementation:\n\n```python3\nimport urllib3\n\nwith urllib3.PoolManager(assert_hostname=\"cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com\") as http:\n    resp = http.request(\"GET\", \"https://cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com/\")\n    print(resp)\n```\n\nAnd next time you choose a bucket name, try to avoid underscores!", "knowledge_type": "parametric", "grounding_sources": [], "answer_grounded_in": "c2", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://cs_sample_feed.s3.amazonaws.com/"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-15T20:41:11.783553Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "urllib3/urllib3/issue/3102", "repo": "urllib3/urllib3", "number": 3102, "title": "OpenSSL ERRORS on windows 10 desktops ==> require the \"official solution\"", "url": "https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/3102", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2023-08-03T21:42:09Z", "closed_at": "2023-08-04T07:52:32Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "DaddyLee02", "security_topic": "compatibility of urllib3 v2.0 with OpenSSL on legacy Python 3.7", "qa_summary": "The developer needed a solution for the OpenSSL version error, and the maintainer answered that upgrading to a supported Python version (e.g., 3.7.17) resolves it, citing the Python versions documentation.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "DaddyLee02", "answer_author": "pquentin", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": ["3.7.17"], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": ["https://devguide.python.org/versions/"]}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "DaddyLee02", "timestamp": "2023-08-03T21:42:09Z", "body": "I get the following error when trying to run some code that connects to a secure web-api\r\n|~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\r\n**ImportError: urllib3 v2.0 only supports OpenSSL 1.1.1+, currently the 'ssl' module is compiled with 'OpenSSL 1.1.0i  14 Aug 2018'. See: https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/2168**\r\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\r\nI am running this on a windows 10 PC - there is no relevant advice in the \"migration guide\" located at:\r\n \"https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/2168 \" \r\n- my python version is 3.7.1\r\n-", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "pquentin", "timestamp": "2023-08-04T07:52:32Z", "body": "Hello, Python 3.7 has reached end-of-life and is no longer receiving security updates: https://devguide.python.org/versions/. Your specific version (3.7.1) is five years old now. If you can't switch to a more recent version right away, at least try installing 3.7.17 instead. In any case, this is not a bug in urllib3 itself, so I'll close this issue. You can reach out to [our community Discord](https://discord.gg/urllib3) if you have more questions.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "DaddyLee02", "timestamp": "2023-08-04T11:56:23Z", "body": "Hi,\r\nThis IS a bug; I had to force an install of a \"lower\" version (1.21.1) to\r\nget it to work.\r\nForcing users to upgrade when they are not ready is NOT good\r\nadvice/support. You introduced unnecessary complexities on the SSL layer\r\nand then refuse to help ?!\r\nThanks for nothing !!\r\n\r\nOn Fri, 4 Aug 2023, 08:52 Quentin Pradet, ***@***.***> wrote:\r\n\r\n> Hello, Python 3.7 has reached end-of-life and is no longer receiving\r\n> security updates: https://devguide.python.org/versions/. Your specific\r\n> version (3.7.1) is five years old now. If you can't switch to a more recent\r\n> version right away, at least try installing 3.7.17 instead. In any case,\r\n> this is not a bug in urllib3 itself, so I'll close this issue. You can\r\n> reach out to our community Discord <https://discord.gg/urllib3> if you\r\n> have more questions.\r\n>\r\n> \u2014\r\n> Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\r\n> <https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/3102#issuecomment-1665173499>,\r\n> or unsubscribe\r\n> <https://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/BBWSEJGJEJIVJ2EB24SNQZLXTSS4ZANCNFSM6AAAAAA3DLQ7CQ>\r\n> .\r\n> You are receiving this because you authored the thread.Message ID:\r\n> ***@***.***>\r\n>\r\n", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "sigmavirus24", "timestamp": "2023-08-04T13:54:08Z", "body": "@DaddyLee02 I strongly encourage you to read two things very closely before you continue here. \r\n\r\n1. [The License](https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/blob/main/LICENSE.txt) which provides no guarantees that things won't break for you and provides no guarantee of support. To be clear, urllib3 `IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND` (quote from the license, capitalization that of the license)\r\n2. [The Code of Conduct](https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md) which is what we expect of ourselves and those participating in the community (i.e., you).\r\n\r\nWe've given you sound advice. Your frustration is valid and understandable, but you're making accusations without demonstrating empathy or trying to understand why. Before you come back, please read and reflect on those two files. Even while we can no longer support your exact use case, Quentin offered you an avenue for help that is above and beyond what is required of us, so we're not trying to ignore you, we're merely telling you that what you want is not something we will put effort into supporting.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "DaddyLee02", "timestamp": "2023-08-06T08:27:22Z", "body": "I hear you sir,\r\nDo note that I resolved the issue by downgrading my urllib3 version.\r\n\r\nThanks for all the hard work over the years \ud83d\udc4d\ud83c\udffe\ud83d\udc4f\ud83c\udffe\ud83d\udc4d\ud83c\udffe\r\n\r\nCheers!\r\n\r\nOn Fri, 4 Aug 2023, 14:54 Ian Stapleton Cordasco, ***@***.***>\r\nwrote:\r\n\r\n> @DaddyLee02 <https://github.com/DaddyLee02> I strongly encourage you to\r\n> read two things very closely before you continue here.\r\n>\r\n>    1. The License\r\n>    <https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/blob/main/LICENSE.txt> which\r\n>    provides no guarantees that things won't break for you and provides no\r\n>    guarantee of support. To be clear, urllib3 IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\",\r\n>    WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND (quote from the license, capitalization\r\n>    that of the license)\r\n>    2. The Code of Conduct\r\n>    <https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md>\r\n>    which is what we expect of ourselves and those participating in the\r\n>    community (i.e., you).\r\n>\r\n> We've given you sound advice. Your frustration is valid and\r\n> understandable, but you're making accusations without demonstrating empathy\r\n> or trying to understand why. Before you come back, please read and reflect\r\n> on those two files. Even while we can no longer support your exact use\r\n> case, Quentin offered you an avenue for help that is above and beyond what\r\n> is required of us, so we're not trying to ignore you, we're merely telling\r\n> you that what you want is not something we will put effort into supporting.\r\n>\r\n> \u2014\r\n> Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\r\n> <https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/3102#issuecomment-1665646773>,\r\n> or unsubscribe\r\n> <https://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/BBWSEJBW6N7IOXZUPVLUVI3XTT5IXANCNFSM6AAAAAA3DLQ7CQ>\r\n> .\r\n> You are receiving this because you were mentioned.Message ID:\r\n> ***@***.***>\r\n>\r\n", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "urllib3/urllib3/issue/3102#1", "question": "When trying to run code that connects to a secure web API on a Windows 10 PC, urllib3 v2.0 fails with:\n\n```text\nImportError: urllib3 v2.0 only supports OpenSSL 1.1.1+, currently the 'ssl' module is compiled with 'OpenSSL 1.1.0i  14 Aug 2018'. See: https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/2168\n```\n\nI am using Python 3.7.1, and I do not see relevant advice for Windows 10 desktops in the migration guide at https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/2168. What is the official solution for this OpenSSL error?", "answer": "Python 3.7 has reached end-of-life and is no longer receiving security updates: https://devguide.python.org/versions/. Your specific version (3.7.1) is five years old now. If you can't switch to a more recent version right away, at least try installing 3.7.17 instead. In any case, this is not a bug in urllib3 itself, so I'll close this issue. You can reach out to [our community Discord](https://discord.gg/urllib3) if you have more questions.", "knowledge_type": "grounded", "grounding_sources": ["https://devguide.python.org/versions/"], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://devguide.python.org/versions/", "https://discord.gg/urllib3"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-15T19:32:29.833158Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "urllib3/urllib3/issue/2809", "repo": "urllib3/urllib3", "number": 2809, "title": "Disable warnings as a context manager", "url": "https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/2809", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2022-11-15T12:20:33Z", "closed_at": "2023-01-02T20:57:14Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "mgrazebrook", "security_topic": "selective suppression of InsecureRequestWarning in Python", "qa_summary": "The developer wanted a way to temporarily suppress the InsecureRequestWarning for a single API call, and the answer directs them to use Python\u2019s warnings.catch_warnings() as documented in the official warnings library.", "question_comment_id": "c0", "answer_comment_id": "c1", "question_author": "mgrazebrook", "answer_author": "sethmlarson", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["documentation"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "mgrazebrook", "timestamp": "2022-11-15T12:20:33Z", "body": "### Context\r\n\r\nIn a  development context, I'm making insecure API requests. I'd like to disable just one warning (InsecureRequestWarning in this case) for just that call - after all, other warnings may indicate bugs!\r\n\r\nIt would be nicer to be able to disable only one warning for only one call in a context manager.\r\n\r\n### Alternatives\r\n\r\nI can disable all or one warning. I can reset all warnings but not, I think, only one. If the warning I want to have temporarily disabled is already filtered, I can't easily revert to that state.\r\n\r\n### Duplicate\r\n\r\nI did some rough keyword searching on your issue list so apologies if I missed something.\r\n\r\n### Contribution\r\n\r\nWould you be willing to submit a PR?\r\n_(Help can be provided if you need assistance submitting a PR)_\r\nCould be fun, I'm a decent programmer and know git but would like support to ensure I work within the project style and integrate nicely with docs.", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "sethmlarson", "timestamp": "2023-01-02T20:57:14Z", "body": "You can suppress individual warnings in Python using [`warnings.catch_warnings()`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/warnings.html#warnings.catch_warnings). Hope this covers your use case.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "urllib3/urllib3/issue/2809#1", "question": "In urllib3, in a development context where I'm making insecure API requests, I'd like to disable just one warning (`InsecureRequestWarning`) for just one call, because other warnings may indicate bugs. I know I can disable all warnings or one warning globally, and reset all warnings, but I don't think I can reset only one; if the warning I want temporarily disabled is already filtered, I can't easily revert to that state. Is there a way to disable only `InsecureRequestWarning` for only one call as a context manager, or would urllib3 be interested in adding such a context-manager feature?", "answer": "The maintainer said individual warnings can be suppressed in Python using `warnings.catch_warnings()` and linked to the Python documentation for it.", "knowledge_type": "parametric", "grounding_sources": [], "answer_grounded_in": "c1", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": ["https://docs.python.org/3/library/warnings.html#warnings.catch_warnings"], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-16T03:36:43.015684Z", "review_note": ""}
{"id": "urllib3/urllib3/issue/2802", "repo": "urllib3/urllib3", "number": 2802, "title": "`util.parse_url` allows invalid characters in reg-names", "url": "https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/issues/2802", "state": "closed", "created_at": "2022-11-13T04:40:33Z", "closed_at": "2023-04-23T18:44:27Z", "labels": [], "reporter": "kenballus", "security_topic": "percent-encoding vs rejection of invalid characters in URL parsing", "qa_summary": "Developer asked whether invalid characters in URL reg-names should be percent\u2011encoded or cause a parse failure; the maintainer referenced the WHATWG URL spec (e.g., https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#forbidden-host-code-point) indicating they should reject such characters.", "question_comment_id": "c5", "answer_comment_id": "c6", "question_author": "kenballus", "answer_author": "sethmlarson", "answerer_role": "maintainer", "artifacts_needed": ["code", "documentation", "issue_tracker"], "hard_facts": {"cve_ids": [], "ghsa_ids": [], "cwe_ids": [], "osv_ids": [], "fixed_versions": [], "fix_prs": [], "fix_commits": [], "advisory_urls": []}, "human_note": "", "llm_confidence": 0.9025, "comments": [{"id": "c0", "author": "kenballus", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T04:40:33Z", "body": "### `util.parse_url` allows invalid characters in reg-names.\r\n\r\nRFC3986 defines reg-name as follows:\r\n`reg-name = *( unreserved / pct-encoded / sub-delims )`\r\n`unreserved = ALPHA / DIGIT / \"-\" / \".\" / \"_\" / \"~\"`\r\n`sub-delims = \"!\" / \"$\" / \"&\" / \"'\" / \"(\" / \")\" / \"*\" / \"+\" / \",\" / \";\" / \"=\"`\r\n`pct-encoded = \"%\" HEXDIG HEXDIG`\r\n\r\nCurrently, `urllib3.util.parse_url` accepts reg-names containing characters outside those enumerated above.\r\nThe incorrectly accepted ASCII characters are `[\\x00-\\x20]`, `\"`, `<`, `>`, `^`, `` ` ``, `{`, `|`, `}`, and `\\x7f`.\r\n\r\n### Environment\r\n```python3\r\n>>> print(\"OS\", platform.platform())\r\nOS Linux-6.0.7-arch1-1-x86_64-with-glibc2.36\r\n>>> print(\"Python\", platform.python_version())\r\nurllib3 2.0.0.dev0\r\n>>> print(\"urllib3\", urllib3.__version__)\r\nPython 3.10.8\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Steps to Reproduce\r\n\r\n```python3\r\n>>> import urllib3\r\n>>> urllib3.util.parse_url(\"http://|\") # Whatever happens, \"|\" should not end up in a reg-name.\r\nUrl(scheme='http', auth=None, host='|', port=None, path=None, query=None, fragment=None)\r\n>>> # But it does!\r\n```", "role": "body"}, {"id": "c1", "author": "kenballus", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T17:08:40Z", "body": "WHATWG also disallows many of these characters. Quoting the WHATWG spec:\r\n\r\n\"If input contains a [forbidden host code point](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#forbidden-host-code-point), [validation error](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#validation-error), return failure.\"\r\n\"A forbidden host code point is U+0000 NULL, U+0009 TAB, U+000A LF, U+000D CR, U+0020 SPACE, U+0023 (#), U+002F (/), U+003A (:), U+003C (<), U+003E (>), U+003F (?), U+0040 (@), U+005B ([), U+005C (\\), U+005D (]), U+005E (^), or U+007C (|).\"\r\n(If you see any of these characters, the parse should fail)\r\n\r\n\"If input contains a [code point](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point) that is not a [URL code point](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#url-code-points) and not U+0025 (%), [validation error](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#validation-error).\"\r\n\"The URL code points are [ASCII alphanumeric](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-alphanumeric), U+0021 (!), U+0024 ($), U+0026 (&), U+0027 ('), U+0028 LEFT PARENTHESIS, U+0029 RIGHT PARENTHESIS, U+002A (*), U+002B (+), U+002C (,), U+002D (-), U+002E (.), U+002F (/), U+003A (:), U+003B (;), U+003D (=), U+003F (?), U+0040 (@), U+005F (_), U+007E (~), and [code points](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point) in the range U+00A0 to U+10FFFD, inclusive, excluding [surrogates](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#surrogate) and [noncharacters](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#noncharacter).\"\r\n(If you see any characters other than these characters, they will later be percent-encoded.)", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c2", "author": "kenballus", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T17:42:07Z", "body": "> 3986 isn't the end of the line. WHAT WG supports Unicode ignoring 3987\r\n\r\nThe docs say \"This parser is RFC 3986 and RFC 6874 compliant,\" so I would assume that RFC compliance trumps WHATWG compliance. Is that a safe assumption to make?\r\n\r\nI'm not sure what Unicode has to do with this; this is about incorrectly accepted ASCII characters. The PR currently breaks Unicode, but that's pretty easily fixable, I just need to know which Unicode URL spec to comply with (RFC3987 or WHATWG).", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c3", "author": "sethmlarson", "timestamp": "2022-11-19T00:13:32Z", "body": "@kenballus Yeah that's a safe assumption, we implement RFC 3986 instead of WHATWG. Why have you closed your PR btw?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c4", "author": "kenballus", "timestamp": "2022-11-22T10:50:38Z", "body": "I closed the PR because I made it from the main branch of my fork and it had an extraneous fix built in. Working on making a series of PRs, each from separate branches, that fix a few different things.\r\n\r\nCurrently traveling for Thanksgiving, but will submit in the next few days.", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c5", "author": "kenballus", "timestamp": "2023-01-29T00:39:50Z", "body": "If someone tries to encode a URL containing an invalid character (e.g. `'\\x00'`) in a reg-name, should we automatically percent-encode it, or should we reject the parse?", "role": "comment"}, {"id": "c6", "author": "sethmlarson", "timestamp": "2023-04-23T18:44:27Z", "body": "Sorry for taking so long to get to this. I'm going to make a call that we continue doing what we're doing and percent-encode everything that's outside the valid ranges for each component. Can discuss more but I think it's better to make a call rather than delay indefinitely.", "role": "comment"}], "qa_pairs": [{"qid": "urllib3/urllib3/issue/2802#1", "question": "In urllib3 2.0.0.dev0 on Linux/Python 3.10.8, `urllib3.util.parse_url` currently accepts reg-names containing ASCII characters outside RFC3986's `reg-name = *( unreserved / pct-encoded / sub-delims )`, such as `|`:\n\n```python3\n>>> import urllib3\n>>> urllib3.util.parse_url(\"http://|\") # Whatever happens, \"|\" should not end up in a reg-name.\nUrl(scheme='http', auth=None, host='|', port=None, path=None, query=None, fragment=None)\n>>> # But it does!\n```\n\nThe incorrectly accepted ASCII characters include `[\u0000-\u001f]`, space, `\"`, `<`, `>`, `^`, `` ` ``, `{`, `|`, `}`, and `\\x7f`. If someone tries to encode a URL containing an invalid character, for example `'\\x00'`, in a reg-name, should urllib3 automatically percent-encode it, or should it reject the parse?", "answer": "The maintainer decided that urllib3 should continue percent-encoding everything outside the valid ranges for each URL component rather than rejecting the parse.", "knowledge_type": "parametric", "grounding_sources": [], "answer_grounded_in": "c6", "leak_flags": []}], "answer_in_thread_refs": [], "leak_flags": [], "normalizer_model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "reviewed_at": "2026-06-15T19:24:09.682817Z", "review_note": ""}
